{"MINDMAP_V2_NOTES_N2":{"n2-001||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ものの · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものの","patternReading":"ものの","coreMeaning":"Although... but (P happened, Q is unexpected)","slug":"core","definition":"～ものの expresses **although P, Q**, where P is an event/action that **has happened** or an **established fact**, while Q is the **opposite of the original expectation**, often conveying **disappointment or regret**. This is a **formal written** pattern and is not used in casual conversation.","nuance":"Unlike neutral けど/が, ものの implies: **I did/had P, and yet Q happened—not as I expected**. The speaker emphasizes the **incompleteness** of the result. It can describe both the speaker and others.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"パソコン","m":"computer"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買った","r":"かった","m":"bought"},{"t":"ものの","r":"ものの","m":"although...but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"忙しくて","r":"いそがしくて","m":"busy"},{"t":"まだ","m":"still"},{"t":"一度も","r":"いちども","m":"even once"},{"t":"使って","r":"つかって","m":"use"},{"t":"いない","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I bought a new computer, but I have been too busy to use it even once.","scene":"Bought a new laptop but never used it"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"能力","r":"のうりょく","m":"ability"},{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"申し込んだ","r":"もうしこんだ","m":"registered"},{"t":"ものの","r":"ものの","m":"although...but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"studying"},{"t":"する","r":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"全く","r":"まったく","m":"any at all"},{"t":"取れない","r":"とれない","m":"cannot find"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I registered for the Japanese-Language Proficiency Test, but I have no time to study at all.","scene":"Regretting registering without being able to study"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-001||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"～ものの · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものの","patternReading":"ものの","coreMeaning":"Although... but (P happened, Q is unexpected)","slug":"forms","definition":"～ものの attaches directly to the **plain form (普通形)** of verbs and adjectives. The **た-form** is common because it emphasizes that P has happened. A **な-adjective** must retain **な**; a **noun** must use **である** in formal writing.","nuance":"Do not use the polite form (ます) before ものの. Negative forms are also possible: 行かなかったものの, 高くないものの.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"V-た / V-る + ものの","note":"The た-form is common because it emphasizes that P happened."},{"lhs":"い-形","rhs":"Aい + ものの","note":"Example: 高いものの"},{"lhs":"な-形","rhs":"Aな + ものの","note":"な is required; do not attach directly."},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"N + である + ものの","note":"Formal written style."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"便利","r":"べんり","m":"convenient"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"ものの","r":"ものの","m":"although...but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"少し","r":"すこし","m":"a little"},{"t":"高い","r":"たかい","m":"expensive"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This shop is convenient, but it is a little expensive.","scene":"Commenting on a convenient shop"},{"tokens":[{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"である","r":"である","m":"be"},{"t":"ものの","r":"ものの","m":"although...but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ほとんど","m":"hardly"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"しない","m":"do not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although he is a student, he hardly studies.","scene":"Criticizing a lazy friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-001||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and contexts","sub":"～ものの · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものの","patternReading":"ものの","coreMeaning":"Although... but (P happened, Q is unexpected)","slug":"usage","definition":"～ものの suits **formal written contexts** such as news, reports, essays, and speeches. Use it to present an **unexpected result**, often with a sense of **regret**.","nuance":"In everyday conversation, Japanese speakers use **けど / でも / のに** instead. Using ものの while drinking with friends sounds like reading from a newspaper.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"システム","m":"system"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"導入","r":"どうにゅう","m":"introduce"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"ものの","r":"ものの","m":"although...but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"社員","r":"しゃいん","m":"employees"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"まだ","m":"still"},{"t":"浸透して","r":"しんとうして","m":"spread to"},{"t":"いない","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although the new system has been introduced, employees still have not adopted it.","scene":"Internal company report on software rollout"},{"tokens":[{"t":"景気","r":"けいき","m":"economy"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"回復","r":"かいふく","m":"recovery"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"ものの","r":"ものの","m":"although...but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"賃金","r":"ちんぎん","m":"wages"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"上昇","r":"じょうしょう","m":"rise"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"依然として","r":"いぜんとして","m":"still"},{"t":"鈍い","r":"にぶい","m":"sluggish"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although the economy has recovered, wage growth remains sluggish.","scene":"Economic news commentary"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-001||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"～ものの · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものの","patternReading":"ものの","coreMeaning":"Although... but (P happened, Q is unexpected)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare ものの with other easily confused “although...but” patterns. The key point: ものの = **P has happened, Q defies expectations, written style**.","nuance":"Each pattern has its own focus; choosing the wrong one changes the nuance.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"医者","r":"いしゃ","m":"doctor"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"運動","r":"うんどう","m":"exercise"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"ように"},{"t":"言われた","r":"いわれた","m":"was told"},{"t":"ものの","r":"ものの","m":"although...but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"なかなか","m":"still"},{"t":"習慣","r":"しゅうかん","m":"habit"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"ならない","r":"ならない","m":"does not become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The doctor told me to exercise, but I still cannot make it a habit.","scene":"Talking about failing to keep exercising"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜にもかかわらず","point":"にもかかわらず emphasizes that Q happens despite P. ものの emphasizes that P is established, yet Q defies expectations and often expresses regret. Example: 雨が降っているにもかかわらず、試合は続行された。"},{"vs":"〜とはいえ","point":"とはいえ ACKNOWLEDGES that P is true → then states a limitation Q, without regret. ものの emphasizes an UNEXPECTED result. Example: 春とはいえ、朝晩はまだ冷え込む。"},{"vs":"〜のに","point":"のに is used in CASUAL speech and expresses strong frustration or regret; ものの is WRITTEN and does not directly reveal strong emotion."}]}},"n2-001||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"～ものの · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものの","patternReading":"ものの","coreMeaning":"Although... but (P happened, Q is unexpected)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner errors with ものの: using it for simple comparison, omitting な, using a Q that is not contrasting, and attaching it incorrectly to nouns.","nuance":"If Q is the expected result, the sentence loses its contrast and ものの is inappropriate.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ジム","m":"gym"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"入会","r":"にゅうかい","m":"join"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"ものの","r":"ものの","m":"although...but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"忙しくて","r":"いそがしくて","m":"busy"},{"t":"月","r":"つき","m":"month"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"一度","r":"いちど","m":"once"},{"t":"しか"},{"t":"行けて","r":"いけて","m":"manage to go"},{"t":"いない","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I joined a gym, but I am so busy that I can only go once a month.","scene":"Telling a friend about rarely going to the gym"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 弟は背が高いものの、私は低い。 → ✅ 弟は背が高い**が**、私は低い。 (ものの is not for simple contrast between two subjects; use が)","❌ この店は便利ものの、高い。 → ✅ この店は便利**な**ものの、高い。 (Missing な after a な-adjective)","❌ 友達と飲みに行ったものの、楽しかった。 → ✅ 友達と飲みに行ったものの、**あまり楽しくなかった**。 (Q must contrast with expectations)","❌ 彼は学生ものの、働いている。 → ✅ 彼は学生**である**ものの、働いている。 (A noun must connect with である)"],"compare":null}},"n2-001||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～ものの · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものの","patternReading":"ものの","coreMeaning":"Although... but (P happened, Q is unexpected)","slug":"memory","definition":"**Memory tip**: **もの** = a thing you **ALREADY HAVE** in your hands → **ものの** = “I was already holding it, and yet...” → P happened, but Q is disappointing.","nuance":"If one condition is missing (P happened, Q is unexpected, written style), switch to けど / にもかかわらず / とはいえ.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-001||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～ものの · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものの","patternReading":"ものの","coreMeaning":"Although... but (P happened, Q is unexpected)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases with ものの in news and formal texts: た-form verbs + ものの, and な-adjectives + なものの.","nuance":"In JLPT questions, phrases such as 導入したものの, 申し込んだものの, and 便利なものの appear frequently.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"制度","r":"せいど","m":"system"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"導入","r":"どうにゅう","m":"introduce"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"ものの","r":"ものの","m":"although...but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"まだ","m":"still"},{"t":"効果","r":"こうか","m":"effect"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"見えない","r":"みえない","m":"cannot see"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although the new system has been introduced, its effects are still not visible.","scene":"Discussing a new company policy"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-002||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～とはいえ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とはいえ","patternReading":"とはいえ","coreMeaning":"Although... but (acknowledge P, state limitation Q)","slug":"core","definition":"～とはいえ acknowledges that clause P is true, while also presenting a limitation, condition, or reality Q that keeps P from being completely true as it appears. Closest meanings are “Although...,” “That may be true, but...”. This is a concessive pattern: P is true, but Q must be considered before drawing a conclusion.","nuance":"This pattern is formal to semi-formal: it works in writing (news, commentary) and careful speech (meetings, presentations). It is more formal than けど/でも but milder than にもかかわらず. Speakers use it to adjust the listener’s understanding rather than completely deny P.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","r":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"春","r":"はる","m":"spring"},{"t":"とはいえ","r":"とはいえ","m":"although... but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"朝晩","r":"あさばん","m":"mornings and nights"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"まだ","r":"まだ","m":"still"},{"t":"冷え込む","r":"ひえこむ","m":"turn cold"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although spring has arrived, mornings and nights are still cold.","scene":"Mid-March: the days are warmer, but nights are still cold."},{"tokens":[{"t":"コロナ","m":"COVID"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"落ち着いた","r":"おちついた","m":"settled down"},{"t":"とはいえ","r":"とはいえ","m":"although... but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"油断","r":"ゆだん","m":"carelessness"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"できない","r":"できない","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although COVID has calmed down, we still cannot let our guard down.","scene":"The outbreak is declining, but people are reminded to stay careful."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-002||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & connecting forms","sub":"～とはいえ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とはいえ","patternReading":"とはいえ","coreMeaning":"Although... but (acknowledge P, state limitation Q)","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes the connecting forms of ～とはいえ. The pattern follows V plain form, い-adjectives, な-adjectives (drop だ), and nouns (drop だ). It can also begin a sentence as a conjunction meaning “that may be true, but...”.","nuance":"After N or a な-adjective, **だ is usually omitted**: 春とはいえ, 便利とはいえ. Keeping だ is grammatical but sounds slightly more emphatic. In the sentence-opening form, とはいえ must follow a sentence-ending mark (。); it cannot follow an unfinished clause.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"＋ とはいえ","note":"Example: 行くとはいえ、行ったとはいえ"},{"lhs":"い形","rhs":"＋ とはいえ","note":"Example: 寒いとはいえ"},{"lhs":"な形語幹","rhs":"＋ (だ)とはいえ","note":"だ is usually omitted: 便利とはいえ"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"＋ (だ)とはいえ","note":"だ is usually omitted: 春とはいえ"},{"lhs":"Sentence opening","rhs":"。とはいえ、…","note":"Used as a sentence-linking conjunction"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"終わった","r":"おわった","m":"ended"},{"t":"。"},{"t":"とはいえ","r":"とはいえ","m":"that may be true, but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"報告書","r":"ほうこくしょ","m":"report"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"作成","r":"さくせい","m":"preparation"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"残っている","r":"のこっている","m":"remains"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The meeting is over. That may be true, but the report still needs to be prepared.","scene":"After a meeting, mentioning unfinished work."},{"tokens":[{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"とはいえ","r":"とはいえ","m":"although... but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"社会","r":"しゃかい","m":"society"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ルール","m":"rules"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"守る","r":"まもる","m":"follow"},{"t":"べきだ","r":"べきだ","m":"should"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although they are students, they should still follow society’s rules.","scene":"Discussing young people’s responsibilities in society."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-002||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～とはいえ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とはいえ","patternReading":"とはいえ","coreMeaning":"Although... but (acknowledge P, state limitation Q)","slug":"usage","definition":"This branch describes the contexts in which ～とはいえ is used. The pattern is common in news writing, presentations, business emails, and interviews. It suits situations where you acknowledge one true point but add a limitation, making the statement more balanced.","nuance":"In serious conversation with friends, this pattern is possible but sounds fairly formal. In short LINE messages, Japanese speakers choose でも/けど instead of とはいえ. Q need not be negative: 子どもとはいえ、約束を守ることは大切だ is advice, not criticism.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","r":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"5月","r":"ごがつ","m":"May"},{"t":"とはいえ","r":"とはいえ","m":"although... but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"北海道","r":"ほっかいどう","m":"Hokkaido"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"雪","r":"ゆき","m":"snow"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降る","r":"ふる","m":"fall"},{"t":"こと","r":"こと","m":"the fact"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"there are"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although it is already May, it sometimes still snows in Hokkaido.","scene":"Discussing regional weather differences in Japan."},{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"とはいえ","r":"とはいえ","m":"although... but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"約束","r":"やくそく","m":"promise"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"守る","r":"まもる","m":"keep"},{"t":"こと","r":"こと","m":"the act of"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"大切","r":"たいせつ","m":"important"},{"t":"だ","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although they are children, it is still important to teach them to keep promises.","scene":"Debating children’s education."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-002||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing ものの・といっても","sub":"～とはいえ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とはいえ","patternReading":"とはいえ","coreMeaning":"Although... but (acknowledge P, state limitation Q)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes ～とはいえ from three easily confused patterns: ～とはいうものの, ～といっても, and ～ものの. The key differences are register and the nuance of Q.","nuance":"～とはいえ is the most neutral choice: it works in both speech and writing. ～とはいうものの is more formal and limited to writing. ～といっても is more casual and often downplays the statement. ～ものの emphasizes a result that contrasts with the action taken.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"便利","r":"べんり","m":"convenient"},{"t":"とはいえ","r":"とはいえ","m":"although... but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"値段","r":"ねだん","m":"price"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"高い","r":"たかい","m":"high"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although it is convenient, the price is high.","scene":"Commenting on a technology product."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～とはいうものの","point":"Nearly the same meaning, but VERY formal and limited to written news, magazines, and similar contexts."},{"vs":"～といっても","point":"More casual and spoken; often downplays or revises a claim (“I say..., but really it’s only...” )."},{"vs":"～ものの","point":"Emphasizes a result opposite to the action taken and is writing-oriented; とはいえ presents a limitation or adjustment, not necessarily a complete reversal."}]}},"n2-002||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～とはいえ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とはいえ","patternReading":"とはいえ","coreMeaning":"Although... but (acknowledge P, state limitation Q)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"This branch lists common mistakes learners make with ～とはいえ. The first is adding な after a noun; the second is using とはいえ for cause and effect instead of ので.","nuance":"To avoid mistakes, remember: とはいえ = “P is true, but Q limits it,” NOT “because P, Q.” If Q is an inevitable result of P, use ので/から.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"春","r":"はる","m":"spring"},{"t":"とはいえ","r":"とはいえ","m":"although... but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"まだ","r":"まだ","m":"still"},{"t":"寒い","r":"さむい","m":"cold"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although it is spring, it is still cold.","scene":"The correct form instead of 春なとはいえ."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 春なとはいえ、まだ寒い。 → ✅ 春とはいえ、まだ寒い。 (After N, simply drop だ; do not add な)","❌ 雨が降ったとはいえ、試合が中止された。 → ✅ 雨が降ったので、試合が中止された。 (P and Q show cause and effect, so とはいえ is incorrect)","❌ 春とはいえ、無理をしなさい。 → ✅ 春とはいえ、無理は禁物だ。 (Q should not use a command, request, or invitation)"],"compare":null}},"n2-002||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～とはいえ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とはいえ","patternReading":"とはいえ","coreMeaning":"Although... but (acknowledge P, state limitation Q)","slug":"memory","definition":"A memory aid for ～とはいえ: break it into **とは + 言え** = “even if you say...”. Imagine someone affirming P, you nod “that’s true,” and then add “but...”—that is exactly how とはいえ works.","nuance":"Also remember: with N and な-adjectives, **drop だ**—春とはいえ, 便利とはいえ. The pattern can also begin a sentence as a conjunction: “That may be true, but...”.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-002||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common collocations","sub":"～とはいえ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とはいえ","patternReading":"とはいえ","coreMeaning":"Although... but (acknowledge P, state limitation Q)","slug":"phrases","definition":"This branch introduces words and phrases commonly used with ～とはいえ in exams and everyday life: 油断はできない, 依然として, 課題, 冷え込む, and others.","nuance":"These phrases often form Q—the limiting part—in sentences with とはいえ. For example, 油断はできない appears frequently in health and economic notices.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"healthy"},{"t":"とはいえ","r":"とはいえ","m":"although... but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"油断","r":"ゆだん","m":"carelessness"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"禁物","r":"きんもつ","m":"forbidden"},{"t":"だ","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although you are healthy, you must not let your guard down.","scene":"A reminder to take care of one’s health."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-003||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～に限り · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に限り","patternReading":"にかぎり","coreMeaning":"Only ~ (official, objective restriction)","slug":"core","definition":"〜に限り means **only ~ may**, restricting something to a specific case or group. It has a **formal written** tone and often appears in notices, signs, and rules. The speaker sets an objective boundary without complaining or expressing emotion.","nuance":"Formal written style; neutral. Used in notices from shops, organizations, and institutions. Unlike に限って, which is often conversational and expresses annoyance or surprise such as “of all times, it had to be...,” に限り has no unlucky-emotion nuance.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"会員","r":"かいいん","m":"member"},{"t":"に限り","r":"にかぎり","m":"only ~ may"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"施設","r":"しせつ","m":"facility"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ご利用","r":"ごりよう","m":"use"},{"t":"いただけます","m":"may use (hon.)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only members may use this facility.","scene":"Notice posted at a gym entrance"},{"tokens":[{"t":"本日","r":"ほんじつ","m":"today"},{"t":"に限り","r":"にかぎり","m":"only ~ may"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"全品","r":"ぜんぴん","m":"all products"},{"t":"20"},{"t":"パーセント","m":"percent"},{"t":"引き","r":"ひき","m":"off"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"なります","m":"become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Today only, all products are 20% off.","scene":"Sign outside an electronics store"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-003||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & connections","sub":"～に限り · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に限り","patternReading":"にかぎり","coreMeaning":"Only ~ (official, objective restriction)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure of ～に限り is simple: **Noun + に限り + following clause**. It works as a limiting particle and does not conjugate. Distinguish it from に限らず, which **expands** the range: “not only ~, but also...”.","nuance":"Register: formal written notices. It is not used in casual conversation for “only me.” With the same meaning of “only,” に限り is more formal than だけ and sounds like a firm rule.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"に限り + predicate","note":"Limits person/condition N"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"に限らず + predicate","note":"Opposite form: not only N, but also..."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先着","r":"せんちゃく","m":"first arrivals"},{"t":"50"},{"t":"名","r":"めい","m":"people"},{"t":"に限り","r":"にかぎり","m":"only ~ may"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"無料","r":"むりょう","m":"free"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The first 50 people enter for free.","scene":"Promotion notice on a store website"},{"tokens":[{"t":"関係者","r":"かんけいしゃ","m":"authorized persons"},{"t":"に限り","r":"にかぎり","m":"only ~ may"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"入場","r":"にゅうじょう","m":"entry"},{"t":"できます","m":"can"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only authorized persons may enter.","scene":"Sign at a backstage entrance"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-003||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～に限り · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に限り","patternReading":"にかぎり","coreMeaning":"Only ~ (official, objective restriction)","slug":"usage","definition":"～に限り announces a **rule-based restriction**: only during a certain time, for a certain group, or under a certain condition. It is common on signs, advertisements, company rules, and promotions.","nuance":"The tone is **neutral and objective**, not blaming. To say “whenever A happens, B goes wrong” — such as a train being late when you are in a hurry — use に限って, not に限り.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"に限り","r":"にかぎり","m":"only ~ may"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"イベント","m":"event"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"無料","r":"むりょう","m":"free"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Children may attend this event for free.","scene":"Children's Day flyer at a mall"},{"tokens":[{"t":"本日","r":"ほんじつ","m":"today"},{"t":"に限り","r":"にかぎり","m":"only ~ may"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"入場","r":"にゅうじょう","m":"entry"},{"t":"無料","r":"むりょう","m":"free"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Today only, admission is free.","scene":"Sign at a museum entrance"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-003||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast: だけ・のみ・に限り・に限って","sub":"～に限り · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に限り","patternReading":"にかぎり","coreMeaning":"Only ~ (official, objective restriction)","slug":"compare","definition":"Learners often confuse **に限り** with words that also mean “only.” The key point: に限り marks an **official, objective rule**; だけ is neutral in any context; のみ is more formal than だけ and common on signs and in legal writing; に限って is **conversational and emotional** — for example, “whenever I need it, it...”.","nuance":"Compare **communication purpose**, not “strength”: use に限り for a rule and に限って for an unfortunate coincidence or complaint. に限らず has the opposite meaning: “not only ~, but also...”.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"students"},{"t":"に限り","r":"にかぎり","m":"only ~ may"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"料金","r":"りょうきん","m":"ticket price"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"半額","r":"はんがく","m":"half price"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なります","m":"become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Students get half-price movie tickets.","scene":"Student discount notice at a cinema"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜だけ","point":"だけ is neutral in speech and writing; に限り is formal and rule-oriented."},{"vs":"〜のみ","point":"のみ is formal and written, close to “only” in documents; に限り more clearly marks a special restriction."},{"vs":"〜に限って","point":"に限って is conversational and emotional (complaint/surprise/protection); に限り is neutral and emotionless."},{"vs":"〜に限らず","point":"に限らず expands: “not only ~”; に限り narrows: “only ~”."}]}},"n2-003||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～に限り · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に限り","patternReading":"にかぎり","coreMeaning":"Only ~ (official, objective restriction)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The two most common errors are **using に限り for a complaint** (use に限って) and **using に限って in an official notice** (use に限り). Learners also translate “only” directly without noticing that に限り requires an objective rule or notice context.","nuance":"Do not say “急ぐ時に限り” when expressing bad luck; use に限って. Do not say “明日に限って休みです” for a cold official notice; use に限り.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"会員","r":"かいいん","m":"members"},{"t":"に限り","r":"にかぎり","m":"only ~ may"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"無料","r":"むりょう","m":"free"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"入場","r":"にゅうじょう","m":"entry"},{"t":"できます","m":"can"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only members may enter for free.","scene":"Rule printed on a museum ticket"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 急ぐ時に限り、電車が遅れる。 → ✅ 急ぐ時に限って、電車が遅れる。","❌ 明日に限って、仕事が休みです。(notice) → ✅ 明日に限り、業務を休止いたします。"],"compare":null}},"n2-003||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～に限り · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に限り","patternReading":"にかぎり","coreMeaning":"Only ~ (official, objective restriction)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember that **限（かぎ）** means **limit**. Thus, **に限り** marks a limit applying to this person or time—like a sign. **に限って** targets an unwanted moment and adds annoyance. **に限らず** means “not limited to ~,” so it expands the range.","nuance":"Use the context to choose quickly: a notice or rule → に限り; an exclamation like “why did it happen now?” → に限って; ず → expansion.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-003||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common set phrases","sub":"～に限り · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に限り","patternReading":"にかぎり","coreMeaning":"Only ~ (official, objective restriction)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases recur in JLPT questions and everyday life: **本日に限り** (today only), **会員に限り** (members only), **先着◯名に限り** (the first ◯ people only), and **子どもに限り** (children only). Memorizing the chunks helps you remember the structure.","nuance":"They are all notice or website formulas. **先着◯名に限り** is common in promotions; **関係者に限り** often appears at theater doors and office buildings.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先着","r":"せんちゃく","m":"first arrivals"},{"t":"50"},{"t":"名","r":"めい","m":"people"},{"t":"に限り","r":"にかぎり","m":"only ~ may"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"無料","r":"むりょう","m":"free"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The first 50 people get in for free.","scene":"Free-gift notice at a convenience store"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-004||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～に限って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に限って","patternReading":"にかぎって","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Whenever it is ~ (with annoyance or surprise)","slug":"core","definition":"〜に限って expresses a **special limitation** colored by the speaker's **subjective emotion**. It often conveys complaint, surprise, sarcasm, or defense, unlike the neutral tone of に限り. The main ideas are “of all times, ~...” and “that particular person definitely would not...”.","nuance":"This is natural **spoken language** for conversation, not announcements or rules. With うちの子に限って, the speaker defends their child: “My child would never do that.” With 急ぐ時に限って, the speaker complains about bad luck.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"急ぐ","r":"いそぐ","m":"hurry"},{"t":"時","r":"とき","m":"time"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"限って","r":"かぎって","m":"whenever"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"遅れる","r":"おくれる","m":"be delayed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Whenever I'm in a hurry, the train is delayed.","scene":"Complaining to a coworker about a delayed train"},{"tokens":[{"t":"うち","m":"my home"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"子","r":"こ","m":"child"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"限って","r":"かぎって","m":"only ... would"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"そんな","m":"such"},{"t":"こと","m":"thing"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"はず","m":"expectation"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ない","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My child would never do anything like that.","scene":"Defending your child after someone suspects them"},{"tokens":[{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"日","r":"ひ","m":"day"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"限って","r":"かぎって","m":"of all days"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"目覚まし時計","r":"めざましどけい","m":"alarm clock"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"鳴らなかった","r":"ならなかった","m":"did not ring"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Of all days, my alarm didn't ring on the day of the exam.","scene":"Recounting bad luck on exam day"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-004||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & connective forms","sub":"～に限って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に限って","patternReading":"にかぎって","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Whenever it is ~ (with annoyance or surprise)","slug":"forms","definition":"に限って follows a **noun** or **noun phrase**, such as 時 or 日. In speech, **V + に限って** is common with verbs nominalized by 時, as in 急ぐ時に限って and 雨の日に限って. The variant **に限らず** means **expansion** and has the opposite meaning.","nuance":"Do not confuse に限り with に限って. Both follow nouns, but **に限り** is emotionless and used in rules or announcements, while **に限って** expresses complaint or defense in conversation.","structure":[{"lhs":"N + に限って","rhs":"述語","note":"Spoken; emphasizes the target with emotion"},{"lhs":"N/V時 + に限って","rhs":"述語","note":"Common phrase: 急ぐ時に限って"},{"lhs":"N + に限り","rhs":"述語","note":"Formal; limitation without emotion"},{"lhs":"に限らず","rhs":"述語","note":"Not only ~ but also; expands scope"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"日","r":"ひ","m":"day"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"限って","r":"かぎって","m":"whenever"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"傘","r":"かさ","m":"umbrella"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"忘れる","r":"わすれる","m":"forget"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Whenever it rains, I forget my umbrella.","scene":"Complaining about forgetting an umbrella on rainy days"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"限って","r":"かぎって","m":"he in particular"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"そんな","m":"such"},{"t":"こと","m":"thing"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ない","m":"not exist"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He, of all people, would never do such a thing.","scene":"Defending him against gossip"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-004||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～に限って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に限って","patternReading":"にかぎって","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Whenever it is ~ (with annoyance or surprise)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜に限って has two main uses: **① complaint/surprise** – “something bad happens right at this moment”; **② defense** – “that particular person definitely would not.” Both are used in **everyday speech**, not official announcements.","nuance":"The tone is strong, close to “just when I need it, ...”. In the defensive use, the speaker has absolute trust in the person or thing, sometimes naively or subjectively.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"大事な","r":"だいじな","m":"important"},{"t":"朝","r":"あさ","m":"morning"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"限って","r":"かぎって","m":"of all times"},{"t":"寝坊する","r":"ねぼうする","m":"oversleep"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Of all mornings, I oversleep on an important one.","scene":"Oversleeping on the morning of an important meeting"},{"tokens":[{"t":"うち","m":"my home"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"子","r":"こ","m":"child"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"限って","r":"かぎって","m":"my child in particular"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"嘘","r":"うそ","m":"lie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"つく","m":"tell"},{"t":"はず","m":"expectation"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ない","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My child would never lie.","scene":"A mother firmly defends her child after a teacher reports a lie"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-004||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with だけ・のみ・に限り","sub":"～に限って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に限って","patternReading":"にかぎって","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Whenever it is ~ (with annoyance or surprise)","slug":"compare","definition":"〜に限って is most often confused with **に限り**, **だけ**, and **のみ**. The key difference is **emotional tone and context**: に限って is spoken and emotional, while the others are mostly neutral or formal.","nuance":"If the sentence expresses complaint, surprise, or defense, use に限って. For notices, rules, or conditions, use に限り, のみ, or だけ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"限って","r":"かぎって","m":"he in particular"},{"t":"それ","m":"that"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ない","m":"not exist"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He, of all people, would never do that.","scene":"Defending him when no one believes him"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～だけ","point":"Simple and neutral; works in any situation. に限って adds surprise or complaint."},{"vs":"～のみ","point":"Formal and written; emphasizes limitation. に限って is emotional spoken language."},{"vs":"～に限り","point":"Same root 限る but neutral; used in rules and notices. に限って is emotional and spoken."},{"vs":"～に限らず","point":"Opposite: expands scope, “not only ~ but also ...”."}]}},"n2-004||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～に限って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に限って","patternReading":"にかぎって","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Whenever it is ~ (with annoyance or surprise)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse **に限り** and **に限って** because they share the root 限る. Remember: complaints, surprise, or defense → に限って; rules and notices → に限り. Also, do not mechanically translate it as “only” and lose the emotion.","nuance":"Choosing the wrong form makes the sentence unnatural or completely inappropriate: spoken language may sound overly formal, while writing may sound too casual.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"急ぐ","r":"いそぐ","m":"hurry"},{"t":"時","r":"とき","m":"time"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"限って","r":"かぎって","m":"whenever"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"遅れる","r":"おくれる","m":"be delayed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Whenever I'm in a hurry, the train is delayed.","scene":"A standard spoken example with に限って"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 急ぐ時に限り、電車が遅れる。 → ✅ 急ぐ時に限って、電車が遅れる。","❌ 明日に限って、業務を休止いたします。 → ✅ 明日に限り、業務を休止いたします。","❌ 彼に限って嘘をつく。 → ✅ 彼に限って嘘をつくはずがない。 (when defending him)","❌ 日本に限って、この問題がある。 → ✅ 日本に限らず、世界中で起きている問題だ。 (when expanding the scope)"],"compare":null}},"n2-004||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～に限って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に限って","patternReading":"にかぎって","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Whenever it is ~ (with annoyance or surprise)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine **に限り** as a cold sign outside a store: “本日に限り、20%オフ”. **に限って** is an irritated voice saying: “Of all times, this happens now!” The same 限 expresses two completely different attitudes.","nuance":"If the subject is complaining or defending someone, choose に限って. If the sentence looks like a notice or rule, choose に限り.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-004||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases & collocations","sub":"～に限って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に限って","patternReading":"にかぎって","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Whenever it is ~ (with annoyance or surprise)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases in exams and conversation include **急ぐ時に限って**, **雨の日に限って**, **うちの子に限って**, **試験の日に限って**, and **こんな時に限って**. Memorizing them helps you respond quickly.","nuance":"Most express the complaint “whenever...” except **うちの子に限って**, which usually defends one's child.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"こんな","m":"like this"},{"t":"時","r":"とき","m":"time"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"限って","r":"かぎって","m":"of all times"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降ってきた","r":"ふってきた","m":"started raining"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Of all times, it starts raining now.","scene":"It starts raining just after leaving home"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-005||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"～をもって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をもって","patternReading":"をもって","coreMeaning":"By means of ~ / as of ~ (means or end point)","slug":"core","definition":"〜をもって is a **formal written grammar pattern** with two main meanings: (1) **means/basis** — doing something *by means of* something with spiritual value, such as an attitude or ability; (2) **end point** — marking when something ends, often with verbs such as 終了する and 閉店する. Both uses sound **ceremonial and solemn**, not conversational.","nuance":"をもって is highly **formal**, typical of official documents or speeches. Compared with で (by means of), it carries much more weight, emphasizing 'by this very thing, not another.' It is therefore not used for ordinary transport such as buses or trains. Register: formal writing or polite ceremonial speech.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"誠意","r":"せいい","m":"sincerity"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もって","m":"with/by"},{"t":"交渉","r":"こうしょう","m":"negotiation"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We will negotiate with sincere intent.","scene":"In an essay or speech about conduct."},{"tokens":[{"t":"本日","r":"ほんじつ","m":"today"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もって","m":"as of"},{"t":"閉店","r":"へいてん","m":"close"},{"t":"いたします","m":"do (polite)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As of today, the store will close.","scene":"A notice posted by a closing store."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-005||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & connecting forms","sub":"～をもって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をもって","patternReading":"をもって","coreMeaning":"By means of ~ / as of ~ (means or end point)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is simple: **noun + をもって**. There is no special conjugation or connecting change. With a time expression such as 本日 or 今月末, it means 'as of that point.' The polite form **をもちまして** often replaces をもって in direct announcements.","nuance":"をもちまして is the honorific form of をもって, used when addressing customers or superiors, or in speeches. It appears only in highly formal contexts.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"をもって + [動詞]","note":"means; followed by an action"},{"lhs":"Time N","rhs":"をもって + [終了表現]","note":"end point"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"をもちまして + [終了表現]","note":"polite form; end-point meaning"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"実力","r":"じつりょく","m":"ability"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もって","m":"by"},{"t":"証明","r":"しょうめい","m":"prove"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"みせる","m":"show"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will prove it through my ability.","scene":"A determined statement in a meeting."},{"tokens":[{"t":"本日","r":"ほんじつ","m":"today"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もちまして","m":"as of (polite)"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"閉店","r":"へいてん","m":"close"},{"t":"いたします","m":"do (polite)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As of today, our store will close.","scene":"A manager's direct notice to customers."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-005||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～をもって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をもって","patternReading":"をもって","coreMeaning":"By means of ~ / as of ~ (means or end point)","slug":"usage","definition":"Distinguish the two meanings by **context**: if the following clause describes an action or state (交渉する, 証明する, 取り組む), をもって means means; if it announces an ending (終了する, 閉店する, 退任する), it marks the end point. This pattern often appears in company notices, opening or closing ceremonies, and posted announcements.","nuance":"At a closing ceremony, **これをもって** is a familiar formula meaning 'we hereby declare this concluded.' For an end point, をもって can be replaced by で, but をもって sounds much more formal.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もって","m":"hereby"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"式典","r":"しきてん","m":"ceremony"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"終了","r":"しゅうりょう","m":"end"},{"t":"いたします","m":"do (polite)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We hereby declare the ceremony concluded.","scene":"Closing remarks at a formal ceremony."},{"tokens":[{"t":"今月末","r":"こんげつまつ","m":"end of this month"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もって","m":"as of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"業務","r":"ぎょうむ","m":"work"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"終了","r":"しゅうりょう","m":"end"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"なります","m":"become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This work will end at the end of this month.","scene":"An internal company notice about ending a work area."},{"tokens":[{"t":"全力","r":"ぜんりょく","m":"full effort"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もって","m":"with"},{"t":"取り組む","r":"とりくむ","m":"work on"},{"t":"こと","m":"act"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"約束","r":"やくそく","m":"promise"},{"t":"します","m":"do (polite)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I promise to tackle it with all my strength.","scene":"A commitment at a project kickoff meeting."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-005||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with で / によって","sub":"～をもって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をもって","patternReading":"をもって","coreMeaning":"By means of ~ / as of ~ (means or end point)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish をもって from two easily confused patterns: **で** (neutral means) and **によって** (cause or dependence). をもって is more formal than either and is not used in everyday situations.","nuance":"To say 'by train,' use で, because をもって requires a means with symbolic value, such as sincerity or ability.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"誠意","r":"せいい","m":"sincerity"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もって","m":"with"},{"t":"交渉","r":"こうしょう","m":"negotiation"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We will negotiate with sincere intent.","scene":"Emphasizing spiritual rather than physical means."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"で","point":"で is used for ordinary means (電車, バス); をもって is used for spiritual/value-based means (誠意, 実力) or a formal end point."},{"vs":"によって","point":"によって emphasizes cause or dependence; をもって marks means or an end point and cannot mean 'depending on.'"}]}},"n2-005||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～をもって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をもって","patternReading":"をもって","coreMeaning":"By means of ~ / as of ~ (means or end point)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make two mistakes: (1) using をもって for ordinary transportation, and (2) using をもちまして with an overly casual following clause. Also, をもって does **not** state ordinary objective causes such as weather or accidents; use ため or によって instead.","nuance":"Remember: をもって does not mean ordinary 'by'; it means 'by this very valuable means.'","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"本日","r":"ほんじつ","m":"today"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もって","m":"as of"},{"t":"閉店","r":"へいてん","m":"close"},{"t":"いたします","m":"do (polite)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As of today, the store will close.","scene":"A correct end-point use of をもって with a formal clause."}],"pitfalls":["❌ バスをもって学校へ行く。 → ✅ バスで学校へ行く。 (Use で for ordinary transportation)","❌ 今日をもちまして、よく頑張りました。 → ✅ 本日をもちまして、皆様のご尽力に感謝いたします。 (をもちまして requires a formal following clause)","❌ 雨をもって、試合が中止になった。 → ✅ 雨のため、試合が中止になった。 (をもって is not used for ordinary objective causes such as weather)"],"compare":null}},"n2-005||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"～をもって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をもって","patternReading":"をもって","coreMeaning":"By means of ~ / as of ~ (means or end point)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine that you are 'holding' (持って) something in your hand. If it is a **means**—an attitude or ability—you mean 'by ~'; if it is a **time point**—today or month-end—you mark an end point with 'as of ~.'","nuance":"をもちまして is the polite version and is used only for end points. Picture an MC saying: これをもちまして、本日のイベントを終了いたします.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-005||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～をもって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をもって","patternReading":"をもって","coreMeaning":"By means of ~ / as of ~ (means or end point)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Fixed phrases with をもって often appear in JLPT questions and official documents: 誠意をもって, 実力をもって, 全力をもって, 本日をもって, これをもって. Memorizing them helps you recognize the pattern quickly in reading passages.","nuance":"Among them, **本日をもって/をもちまして** and **これをもって** are closely associated with ending announcements. Read them aloud as set phrases.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"本日","r":"ほんじつ","m":"today"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もって","m":"as of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"サービス","m":"service"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"終了","r":"しゅうりょう","m":"end"},{"t":"いたします","m":"do (polite)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As of today, this service will be discontinued.","scene":"A service discontinuation notice."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-006||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"～をもちまして · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をもちまして","patternReading":"をもちまして","coreMeaning":"By ~ / As of ~ (formal announcements)","slug":"core","definition":"～をもちまして is the **polite / highly formal** form of ～をもって. It has **two meanings**: ① “by/with ~,” indicating a valued means or attitude; ② “as of/from ~,” marking an end point. It is used in administrative notices, speeches, and closing ceremonies.","nuance":"Very formal register: written language or formal speeches. Not used in conversation with friends. Meaning ② is more common, especially in 本日をもちまして. Meaning ① often goes with abstract nouns such as 誠意、実力、全力.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"本日","r":"ほんじつ","m":"today"},{"t":"をもちまして","m":"as of/by"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"当店","r":"とうてん","m":"this store"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"閉店","r":"へいてん","m":"close"},{"t":"いたします","m":"humbly do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As of today, our store will close.","scene":"Notice posted at a store that is closing"},{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"をもちまして","m":"as of/by"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"式典","r":"しきてん","m":"ceremony"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"終了","r":"しゅうりょう","m":"end"},{"t":"いたします","m":"humbly do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We will conclude the ceremony here.","scene":"Host announces the ceremony’s closing"},{"tokens":[{"t":"皆様","r":"みなさま","m":"everyone"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ご参加","r":"ごさんか","m":"participation"},{"t":"をもちまして","m":"thanks to/by"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"本大会","r":"ほんたいかい","m":"this event"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"無事に","r":"ぶじに","m":"successfully"},{"t":"終了する","r":"しゅうりょうする","m":"finish"},{"t":"ことができました","m":"was able to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Thanks to your participation, the event ended successfully.","scene":"Thanks given at a closing ceremony"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-006||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"～をもちまして · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をもちまして","patternReading":"をもちまして","coreMeaning":"By ~ / As of ~ (formal announcements)","slug":"forms","definition":"In form, をもちまして = をもって + ます/まして. Both share **two meanings**; をもちまして is more polite. Meaning ① connects a noun for means or attitude to an action verb; meaning ② connects a time noun to an expression of ending.","nuance":"Use をもちまして in official documents or formal notices for a safe, polite tone. をもって can be used in neutral written language.","structure":[{"lhs":"N (means/attitude)","rhs":"をもって / をもちまして","note":"Connects to an action, meaning “by/with ~”"},{"lhs":"N (time point)","rhs":"をもって / をもちまして","note":"Connects to an ending, meaning “as of ~”"},{"lhs":"N + をもちまして","rhs":"+ formal notice / thanks","note":"The most common polite form in ceremonies"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"誠意","r":"せいい","m":"sincerity"},{"t":"をもちまして","m":"with"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"対応","r":"たいおう","m":"handle"},{"t":"してまいります","m":"will humbly do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We will handle the matter with sincere commitment.","scene":"Company’s formal written reply to a customer"},{"tokens":[{"t":"今月末","r":"こんげつまつ","m":"the end of this month"},{"t":"をもちまして","m":"as of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"業務","r":"ぎょうむ","m":"work"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"終了","r":"しゅうりょう","m":"end"},{"t":"となります","m":"will become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This work will end at the end of this month.","scene":"Internal company notice"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-006||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～をもちまして · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をもちまして","patternReading":"をもちまして","coreMeaning":"By ~ / As of ~ (formal announcements)","slug":"usage","definition":"Practical usage falls into two contexts: (1) saying “by/with ~” to carry out an action; (2) announcing a time point as an endpoint. In exams, identify whether the following clause describes an action or an ending.","nuance":"本日をもちまして is almost a set opening for notices about closing or ending something. It is much more formal than 本日で.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"本日","r":"ほんじつ","m":"today"},{"t":"をもちまして","m":"as of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"サービス","m":"service"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"終了","r":"しゅうりょう","m":"end"},{"t":"いたします","m":"humbly do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As of today, this service will be discontinued.","scene":"Website notice ending a service"},{"tokens":[{"t":"来月末","r":"らいげつまつ","m":"the end of next month"},{"t":"をもちまして","m":"as of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"プロジェクト","m":"project"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"終了","r":"しゅうりょう","m":"end"},{"t":"いたします","m":"humbly do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This project will end at the end of next month.","scene":"Manager announces project completion"},{"tokens":[{"t":"皆様","r":"みなさま","m":"everyone"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ご支援","r":"ごしえん","m":"support"},{"t":"をもちまして","m":"thanks to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"プロジェクト","m":"project"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"完了","r":"かんりょう","m":"complete"},{"t":"いたしました","m":"humbly did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Thanks to everyone’s support, the project has been completed.","scene":"Thank-you email after project completion"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-006||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with で / によって","sub":"～をもちまして · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をもちまして","patternReading":"をもちまして","coreMeaning":"By ~ / As of ~ (formal announcements)","slug":"compare","definition":"Learners often confuse をもちまして with で because both can mean “by.” The key difference is that **で** is neutral for any means, while **をもちまして** is formal and reserved for something with **symbolic weight**.","nuance":"Do not use をもちまして for transportation such as buses or trains. In writing, によって can express either cause or means, while をもちまして has a ceremonial tone.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"誠意","r":"せいい","m":"sincerity"},{"t":"をもちまして","m":"with"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"交渉","r":"こうしょう","m":"negotiations"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"臨みます","r":"のぞみます","m":"approach"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We will enter the negotiations with sincere commitment.","scene":"Statement at a negotiation meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"で","point":"で is used for ordinary means: 電車で行く. をもちまして is used for valued means such as 誠意 or 実力 and has a formal tone."},{"vs":"によって","point":"によって emphasizes an objective cause or means: 努力によって達成する. をもちまして is ceremonial and rarely marks a natural cause or process."}]}},"n2-006||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～をもちまして · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をもちまして","patternReading":"をもちまして","coreMeaning":"By ~ / As of ~ (formal announcements)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"There are two major traps: (1) using をもちまして for ordinary physical means; (2) mixing registers by pairing this highly formal phrase with a casual ending.","nuance":"Keep the level of formality consistent throughout the sentence. をもちまして does not mean “together with someone”; it marks a means or a time point.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"本日","r":"ほんじつ","m":"today"},{"t":"をもちまして","m":"as of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"閉店","r":"へいてん","m":"close"},{"t":"いたします","m":"humbly do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Our store will close as of today.","scene":"Official store notice"}],"pitfalls":["❌ バスをもって学校へ行く。 → ✅ バスで学校へ行く。 (Use で, not をもって/をもちまして, for vehicles)","❌ 本日をもちまして、閉店するよ！ → ✅ 本日をもちまして、閉店いたします。 (The register must match)","❌ 友達をもちまして来ました。 → ✅ 友達と来ました。 (をもちまして does not mean “together with”)"],"compare":null}},"n2-006||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～をもちまして · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をもちまして","patternReading":"をもちまして","coreMeaning":"By ~ / As of ~ (formal announcements)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember two words: **BY** and **ENDING**. If the noun before it is a time expression, it marks an ending. If it is an attitude or ability, it marks a means. をもちまして = をもって + まして → polite.","nuance":"Memory line: “本日をもちまして means an ending is coming.” When you hear it in a store, company, or ceremony, expect an important announcement.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-006||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～をもちまして · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をもちまして","patternReading":"をもちまして","coreMeaning":"By ~ / As of ~ (formal announcements)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear almost as **set formulas** in official announcements. Memorize them to recognize and use the correct register quickly.","nuance":"For the means meaning, common phrases include 誠意をもちまして and 全力をもちまして. For the ending meaning, common phrases include 本日をもちまして and 今月末をもちまして.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"をもちまして","m":"as of/with"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"本日","r":"ほんじつ","m":"today"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"会","r":"かい","m":"meeting"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"終了","r":"しゅうりょう","m":"end"},{"t":"いたします","m":"humbly do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We hereby conclude today’s meeting.","scene":"Chair announces the meeting’s closing"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-007||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～や否や · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～や否や","patternReading":"やいなや","coreMeaning":"As soon as ~, ~ immediately (without delay)","slug":"core","definition":"〜や否や describes **two actions occurring almost simultaneously**: the second action erupts immediately after the first action is completed. This is a **formal written pattern**, common in literature, sports reports, and news articles.","nuance":"Avoid it in everyday conversation unless you want to sound stiff. It feels **sudden and dramatic**, emphasizing the speed of the reaction. Compared with とたんに, や否や is more literary and objective.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ベル","r":"ベル","m":"bell"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"鳴る","r":"なる","m":"ring"},{"t":"や否や","r":"やいなや","m":"as soon as"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"生徒たち","r":"せいとたち","m":"students"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"教室","r":"きょうしつ","m":"classroom"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飛び出した","r":"とびだした","m":"rushed out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As soon as the bell rang, the students rushed out of the classroom.","scene":"A dismissal scene: the bell rings and students pour out"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"席","r":"せき","m":"seat"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"座る","r":"すわる","m":"sit down"},{"t":"や否や","r":"やいなや","m":"as soon as"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"眠ってしまった","r":"ねむってしまった","m":"fell asleep"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As soon as he sat down, he fell asleep.","scene":"Recounting how a friend fell asleep upon getting home"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-007||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～や否や · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～や否や","patternReading":"やいなや","coreMeaning":"As soon as ~, ~ immediately (without delay)","slug":"forms","definition":"Standard structure: **V辞書形 + や否や + past-tense clause (Vた)**. Although the first action is complete, the plain dictionary form is still required; the second clause describes an event that happened, so it usually uses Vた.","nuance":"This pattern is stiff and formal. Do not use た-form in the first clause or present/future form in the second. This is the most common point of error.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vる（五段）","rhs":"＋ や否や ＋ Vた","note":"書くや否や、読み始めた。"},{"lhs":"Vる（一段）","rhs":"＋ や否や ＋ Vた","note":"食べるや否や、席を立った。"},{"lhs":"Vる（する/来る）","rhs":"＋ や否や ＋ Vた","note":"するや否や / 来るや否や"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"食べる","r":"たべる","m":"eat"},{"t":"や否や","r":"やいなや","m":"as soon as"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"出かけた","r":"でかけた","m":"went out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As soon as he finished eating, he went out.","scene":"Recounting someone rushing out after eating"},{"tokens":[{"t":"立ち上がる","r":"たちあがる","m":"stand up"},{"t":"や否や","r":"やいなや","m":"as soon as"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"叫んだ","r":"さけんだ","m":"shouted"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As soon as he stood up, he shouted.","scene":"An instant reaction in a story"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-007||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～や否や · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～や否や","patternReading":"やいなや","coreMeaning":"As soon as ~, ~ immediately (without delay)","slug":"usage","definition":"The typical context is **reporting**: a writer or reporter describes a second action appearing immediately after the first action is completed. The two events are tightly linked, with **no time gap**.","nuance":"The speaker takes the role of an **observer**, not a direct participant. Therefore, this pattern is rarely used with commands, invitations, or polite honorific forms.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"match"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"終わる","r":"おわる","m":"end"},{"t":"や否や","r":"やいなや","m":"as soon as"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ファンたち","r":"ファンたち","m":"fans"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"グラウンド","r":"グラウンド","m":"field"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"押し寄せた","r":"おしよせた","m":"rushed onto"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As soon as the match ended, the fans rushed onto the field.","scene":"A sports report after the final whistle"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ドア","r":"ドア","m":"door"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開く","r":"あく","m":"open"},{"t":"や否や","r":"やいなや","m":"as soon as"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"猫","r":"ねこ","m":"cat"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"飛び出した","r":"とびだした","m":"dashed out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As soon as the door opened, the cat dashed out.","scene":"A story about a cat suddenly darting out"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-007||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing it from とたんに・すると","sub":"～や否や · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～や否や","patternReading":"やいなや","coreMeaning":"As soon as ~, ~ immediately (without delay)","slug":"compare","definition":"All three patterns describe actions occurring in sequence. They differ in **style and degree of surprise**: や否や is formal and dramatic; とたん is casual and often has an unpleasant result; すると is neutral and simply continues the narrative.","nuance":"Use や否や in exam writing or formal reports. Use とたん when telling a friend about a sudden mishap. Use すると for a simple neutral sequence of events.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ドア","r":"ドア","m":"door"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開く","r":"あく","m":"open"},{"t":"や否や","r":"やいなや","m":"as soon as"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"猫","r":"ねこ","m":"cat"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"飛び出した","r":"とびだした","m":"dashed out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As soon as the door opened, the cat dashed out.","scene":"Emphasizing speed—the cat dashed out as the door opened"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜たとたん（に）","point":"や否や = written, dramatic; とたん = conversational, sudden, often negative. E.g.: ドアが開くや否や猫が飛び出した ／ ドアを開けたとたん、猫が飛び出した."},{"vs":"〜すると","point":"や否や = two events almost touch; すると = neutral continuation, with a possible gap. E.g.: ドアが開くや否や、猫が飛び出した ／ ドアを開けた。すると、雨が降っていた."},{"vs":"〜なり","point":"なり is also written/literary like や否や, but is more everyday and less formal. E.g.: 帰ってくるなり、泣き出した ／ 帰ってくるや否や、泣き出した."}]}},"n2-007||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～や否や · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～や否や","patternReading":"やいなや","coreMeaning":"As soon as ~, ~ immediately (without delay)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often put the first clause in the past because they think 'just finished.' However, や否や **requires V辞書形** in the first clause; the second clause carries the past information.","nuance":"Also, avoid や否や in texts or everyday conversation; it sounds very stiff. Use とたん instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"食べる","r":"たべる","m":"eat"},{"t":"や否や","r":"やいなや","m":"as soon as"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"外","r":"そと","m":"outside"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"飛び出した","r":"とびだした","m":"rushed out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As soon as he finished eating, he rushed outside.","scene":"A correct replacement for the incorrect sentence"}],"pitfalls":["❌ ベルが鳴ったや否や、走り出した。 → ✅ ベルが鳴るや否や、走り出した。 (The first clause must use V辞書形)","❌ 友達に会うや否や、話しかけた。（casual conversation） → ✅ 友達に会ったとたん、話しかけた。 (や否や is too formal for conversation)","❌ 起きるや否や、走ります。 → ✅ 起きるや否や、走り出した。 (The second clause usually uses Vた)"],"compare":null}},"n2-007||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～や否や · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～や否や","patternReading":"やいなや","coreMeaning":"As soon as ~, ~ immediately (without delay)","slug":"memory","definition":"Mnemonic: **否 = 'not'**, so や否や suggests 'before you can even realize it'—the next action has already happened. Picture a soccer report: the whistle has barely sounded when fans rush onto the field.","nuance":"Do not look for this pattern in casual conversation; it belongs in **written reports, news, and narration**. This tip helps you spot it quickly in reading passages.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-007||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～や否や · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～や否や","patternReading":"やいなや","coreMeaning":"As soon as ~, ~ immediately (without delay)","slug":"phrases","definition":"In exams and news reports, や否や often combines with vivid verbs such as **鳴る, 終わる, 開く** in the first clause and **飛び出す, 押し寄せる, 倒れる** in the second.","nuance":"These combinations form familiar descriptive sequences. Memorizing them as chunks helps you fill in blanks and read faster.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ドア","r":"ドア","m":"door"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開く","r":"あく","m":"open"},{"t":"や否や","r":"やいなや","m":"as soon as"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"猫","r":"ねこ","m":"cat"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"飛び出した","r":"とびだした","m":"dashed out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As soon as the door opened, the cat dashed out.","scene":"The familiar chunk '開くや否や + 飛び出す'"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-008||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ゆえに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ゆえに","patternReading":"ゆえに","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~; due to ~ (formal written cause)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ゆえに expresses a **classical, formal cause**, commonly used in written language, literature, and speeches. It emphasizes a **direct cause** leading to a result and creates a reflective tone. In modern Japanese, everyday “because” is usually expressed with から or ので.","nuance":"The tone is **formal, archaic, and bookish**. In speech, it sounds **dramatic** and unnatural. Formality: ゆえに > ので > から. It suits memoirs, essays, and philosophy.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"無知","r":"むち","m":"ignorance"},{"t":"である","m":"is"},{"t":"ゆえに","r":"ゆえに","m":"precisely because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"恐れ","r":"おそれ","m":"fear"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"知らない","r":"しらない","m":"does not know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because of ignorance, one does not know fear.","scene":"A quotation or philosophical passage"},{"tokens":[{"t":"若さ","r":"わかさ","m":"youth"},{"t":"ゆえに","r":"ゆえに","m":"precisely because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"多くの","r":"おおくの","m":"many"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"mistakes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"犯して","r":"おかして","m":"commit"},{"t":"しまった","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because I was young, I made many mistakes.","scene":"A memoir reflecting on the past"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-008||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～ゆえに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ゆえに","patternReading":"ゆえに","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~; due to ~ (formal written cause)","slug":"forms","definition":"ゆえに follows **many word forms**: verbs in plain form, い-adjectives, な-adjectives (with な/である), and nouns (with の/である). To **nominalize the cause**, use **N + ゆえの + N**.","nuance":"This structure is entirely **written and formal** and has no separate polite variant. A noun may come directly before it (N + ゆえに) or with の/である (Nのゆえに / Nであるゆえに).","structure":[{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"ゆえに","note":"Example: 知らないゆえに、失敗した (because I did not know, I failed)"},{"lhs":"いA","rhs":"ゆえに","note":"Example: 若いゆえに、思い込みが強い (because they are young, they are often opinionated)"},{"lhs":"なAな / である","rhs":"ゆえに","note":"Example: 有名なゆえに / 有名であるゆえに"},{"lhs":"Nの / である","rhs":"ゆえに","note":"Example: 学生ゆえに / 学生であるゆえに"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"ゆえの + N","note":"Example: 若さゆえの失敗 (a mistake caused by youth)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"ゆえに","r":"ゆえに","m":"because of"},{"t":"成功","r":"せいこう","m":"success"},{"t":"した","m":"achieved"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of the effort, success was achieved.","scene":"A formal comment on an achievement"},{"tokens":[{"t":"有名","r":"ゆうめい","m":"famous"},{"t":"である","m":"is"},{"t":"ゆえに","r":"ゆえに","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"プレッシャー","m":"pressure"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"大きい","r":"おおきい","m":"great"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because they are famous, the pressure is also great.","scene":"An interview or essay about a celebrity"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-008||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & contexts","sub":"～ゆえに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ゆえに","patternReading":"ゆえに","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~; due to ~ (formal written cause)","slug":"usage","definition":"ゆえに commonly appears in **formal writing**: memoirs, essays, quotations, and journalism. It emphasizes a decisive cause with a somewhat philosophical tone.","nuance":"It is not used in conversation. When you encounter it in a book, understand that the author is creating a **formal, classical atmosphere**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"人気","r":"にんき","m":"popularity"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"ゆえに","r":"ゆえに","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"プレッシャー","m":"pressure"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"大きい","r":"おおきい","m":"great"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because they are popular, the pressure is also great.","scene":"A newspaper article or artist interview"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"成功","r":"せいこう","m":"success"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"ゆえの","r":"ゆえの","m":"due to"},{"t":"もの","m":"thing"},{"t":"だ","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"His success is something achieved through effort.","scene":"A written evaluative statement"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-008||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Comparison with other connectors","sub":"～ゆえに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ゆえに","patternReading":"ゆえに","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~; due to ~ (formal written cause)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare ゆえに with other causal expressions to see **where it belongs in the system**.","nuance":"ゆえに is at the **formal/classical** extreme; each expression has its own nuance.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"若い","r":"わかい","m":"young"},{"t":"ゆえに","r":"ゆえに","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"よく","m":"often"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failures"},{"t":"する","m":"make"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because he is young, he often makes mistakes.","scene":"A philosophical observation in writing"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"から","point":"Common conversational connector; neutral and subjective. ゆえに is classical written language."},{"vs":"ので","point":"Polite and objective, used in speech and writing; ゆえに is more literary."},{"vs":"ばこそ","point":"Stresses the sole, often positive cause; ゆえ simply states a direct cause."}]}},"n2-008||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～ゆえに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ゆえに","patternReading":"ゆえに","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~; due to ~ (formal written cause)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners may use ゆえに in everyday speech because they think it sounds formal. In reality, it is **too archaic** and can sound amusing.","nuance":"In personal blogs, messages, apologies, and workplace conversation, choose **から/ので**. ゆえに suits essays, speeches, and literature.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"国境","r":"こっきょう","m":"border"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"越えた","r":"こえた","m":"crossed"},{"t":"愛","r":"あい","m":"love"},{"t":"ゆえに","r":"ゆえに","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"二人","r":"ふたり","m":"two people"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"結ばれた","r":"むすばれた","m":"were united"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because their love crossed borders, the two were brought together.","scene":"A literary line or film commentary"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 眠いゆえに、授業中寝てしまいました。 → ✅ 眠かったので、授業中寝てしまいました。","❌ 彼は日本人ゆえに、日本語が上手だ。 → ✅ 彼は日本人なので、日本語が上手だ。","❌ 病気ゆえに、会社を休みます。 → ✅ 病気なので、会社を休みます。","❌ 学生ゆえに、お金がない。 → ✅ 学生なので、お金がない。"],"compare":null}},"n2-008||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～ゆえに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ゆえに","patternReading":"ゆえに","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~; due to ~ (formal written cause)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember ゆえ as “because of that FACT” in literary language, with a weighty, formal feeling. For philosophical writing, memoirs, or maxims, prefer ゆえに. For conversation, eliminate it immediately.","nuance":"This tip helps you avoid confusion in reading comprehension and vocabulary questions.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-008||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～ゆえに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ゆえに","patternReading":"ゆえに","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~; due to ~ (formal written cause)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are common fixed phrases with ゆえに in exams and news articles. Knowing them helps you recognize and translate the pattern quickly.","nuance":"Most of these phrases occur in written language and have a generalizing, philosophical tone.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"若さ","r":"わかさ","m":"youth"},{"t":"ゆえの","r":"ゆえの","m":"caused by"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"mistakes"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"成長","r":"せいちょう","m":"growth"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"糧","r":"かて","m":"nourishment"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なる","m":"become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Mistakes caused by youth can nourish growth.","scene":"A maxim or piece of advice"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-009||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ゆえ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ゆえ","patternReading":"ゆえ","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~, due to ~ — direct cause (classical written style)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ゆえ expresses a **direct cause** leading to a result, meaning 'precisely because ~' or 'due to ~'. This is a **classical, written** expression found in maxims, literature, and essays. In everyday conversation, use から or ので.","nuance":"It feels **formal and philosophical**, almost separate from everyday speech. Using ゆえ with friends sounds **dramatic** or bookish. **ゆえの** has a **summative, reflective** tone and suits statements about experience.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"若さ","r":"わかさ","m":"youth"},{"t":"ゆえに","r":"ゆえに","m":"precisely because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"多くの","r":"おおくの","m":"many"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"mistakes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"犯して","r":"おかして","m":"commit"},{"t":"しまった","r":"しまった","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because I was young, I made many mistakes.","scene":"Memoir reflecting on the past."},{"tokens":[{"t":"無知","r":"むち","m":"ignorance"},{"t":"である","r":"である","m":"be"},{"t":"ゆえに","r":"ゆえに","m":"precisely because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"恐れ","r":"おそれ","m":"fear"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"知らない","r":"しらない","m":"not know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because of ignorance, one does not know fear.","scene":"A maxim in a philosophy book."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-009||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & conjugation","sub":"～ゆえ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ゆえ","patternReading":"ゆえ","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~, due to ~ — direct cause (classical written style)","slug":"forms","definition":"How different word types connect to ゆえ: **verbs and i-adjectives** use the plain form; **na-adjectives** can use な or である; **nouns** use の or である. The form **N + ゆえの + N** creates a causal noun phrase and is **very common on the N2 exam**.","nuance":"Using である makes the sentence even **more formal**. ゆえの does not take に; it directly links two nouns as 'A caused by B'.","structure":[{"lhs":"V・いA（plain form）","rhs":"＋ ゆえに","note":"知らないゆえに／若いゆえに"},{"lhs":"なA ＋ な／である","rhs":"＋ ゆえに","note":"有名なゆえに／有名であるゆえに"},{"lhs":"N ＋ の／である","rhs":"＋ ゆえに","note":"学生のゆえに／学生であるゆえに"},{"lhs":"N ＋ ゆえの","rhs":"＋ N","note":"若さゆえの失敗"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"人気","r":"にんき","m":"popularity"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"ゆえに","r":"ゆえに","m":"precisely because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"プレッシャー","m":"pressure"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"大きい","r":"おおきい","m":"great"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because she is popular, the pressure is also great.","scene":"An interview with a popular singer."},{"tokens":[{"t":"若さ","r":"わかさ","m":"youth"},{"t":"ゆえの","r":"ゆえの","m":"due to"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"mistakes"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"成長","r":"せいちょう","m":"growth"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"糧","r":"かて","m":"nourishment"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なる","r":"なる","m":"become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Mistakes caused by youth become nourishment for growth.","scene":"A maxim in a self-help book."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-009||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & contexts","sub":"～ゆえ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ゆえ","patternReading":"ゆえ","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~, due to ~ — direct cause (classical written style)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜ゆえに is used in **formal writing**: memoirs, maxims, news articles, and speeches. It suits causes with a **fundamental quality** (youth, fame, ignorance) rather than minor everyday reasons.","nuance":"Do not use it in texts, conversation, or everyday apologies. If ゆえ appears on an exam, the context is usually **written language** or a **journalistic quotation**. ゆえの is especially common in patterns like '〜ゆえのN'.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"有名人","r":"ゆうめいじん","m":"famous person"},{"t":"である","r":"である","m":"be"},{"t":"ゆえに","r":"ゆえに","m":"precisely because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"自由","r":"じゆう","m":"free"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"not have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because he is famous, he has no free time.","scene":"A star's comments in a magazine."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"成功","r":"せいこう","m":"success"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"ゆえの","r":"ゆえの","m":"due to"},{"t":"もの","r":"もの","m":"thing"},{"t":"だ","r":"だ","m":"be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"His success is the result of his efforts.","scene":"Comment on a colleague's success."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-009||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare: から・ので・ばこそ","sub":"～ゆえ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ゆえ","patternReading":"ゆえ","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~, due to ~ — direct cause (classical written style)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare ゆえ with three commonly confused near-synonyms: から, ので, and ばこそ. The key differences are **register** and **emphasis**.","nuance":"ゆえ = classical written style; から = spoken; ので = polite spoken and written; ばこそ = 'precisely because P, Q', often praising or affirming value.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"である","r":"である","m":"be"},{"t":"ゆえに","r":"ゆえに","m":"precisely because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"料金","r":"りょうきん","m":"fee"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"半額","r":"はんがく","m":"half price"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なる","r":"なる","m":"become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because they are students, the admission fee is half price.","scene":"A museum notice (written style)."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜から","point":"から is used in **conversation** for subjective reasons; ゆえ is **classical written style**. '眠いから' is natural, while '眠いゆえに' sounds theatrical."},{"vs":"〜ので","point":"ので is polite and objective, usable in speech and writing; ゆえ is **never** used in conversation, even politely."},{"vs":"〜ばこそ","point":"ばこそ emphasizes a **sole positive cause** (Q has value precisely because of P); ゆえ simply states a cause without praise or gratitude."}]}},"n2-009||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～ゆえ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ゆえ","patternReading":"ゆえ","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~, due to ~ — direct cause (classical written style)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with ゆえ include **using it in conversation** and **using the wrong connecting form**.","nuance":"Because ゆえ appears in formal writing, mistakes often come from **using it in spoken contexts** or **omitting の/である** before ゆえ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"若さ","r":"わかさ","m":"youth"},{"t":"ゆえの","r":"ゆえの","m":"due to"},{"t":"もの","r":"もの","m":"thing"},{"t":"だ","r":"だ","m":"be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"His failure was caused by his youth.","scene":"An employee assessment."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 眠いゆえに、授業中寝てしまいました。 → ✅ 眠かったので、授業中寝てしまいました。 (Use ので in conversation)","❌ 忙しいゆえに、行けなかった。 (to a friend) → ✅ 忙しかったから、行けなかった。","❌ 若さゆえ失敗 (missing の) → ✅ 若さゆえの失敗","❌ Mispronouncing it as ゆえい → ✅ Read it briefly: ゆえ"],"compare":null}},"n2-009||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～ゆえ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ゆえ","patternReading":"ゆえ","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~, due to ~ — direct cause (classical written style)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember ゆえ as 'because of that KEY cause' → **weighty and formal**. In essays, maxims, and memoirs, use ゆえ; in casual conversation, drop ゆえ and use から/ので.","nuance":"Remember the N2 exam favorite: **N + ゆえの + N**. The more you use である before ゆえ, the more formal the sentence becomes.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-009||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～ゆえ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ゆえ","patternReading":"ゆえ","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~, due to ~ — direct cause (classical written style)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed noun phrases with ゆえ are common in exams and written language. Learning them in pairs makes them easier to remember.","nuance":"Most follow the pattern **N + ゆえの + N**, clearly showing the 'result' caused by noun N.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"有名人","r":"ゆうめいじん","m":"famous person"},{"t":"ゆえの","r":"ゆえの","m":"due to"},{"t":"プレッシャー","m":"pressure"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"想像","r":"そうぞう","m":"imagination"},{"t":"以上","r":"いじょう","m":"beyond"},{"t":"だ","r":"だ","m":"be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The pressure of being famous is greater than imagined.","scene":"An actor's comments in a newspaper."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-010||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ばこそ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばこそ","patternReading":"ばこそ","coreMeaning":"Only because ~ (the sole, positive reason)","slug":"core","definition":"**～ばこそ** presents P as both a **condition** and the sole, most important reason for Q. The speaker uses it to praise, defend, or stress that Q is deserved. Unlike an ordinary reason, P in ばこそ **always has a positive, valued nuance**.","nuance":"Semi-formal and emotional; common in thank-you letters, speeches, and lyrics. **Stronger and more literary than だからこそ**; modern speakers prefer だからこそ in daily conversation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あなた","r":"あなた","m":"you"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"こと","r":"こと","m":"matter"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"思えばこそ","r":"おもえばこそ","m":"only because I care"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"厳しい","r":"きびしい","m":"harsh"},{"t":"こと","r":"こと","m":"things"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"言っています","r":"いっています","m":"am saying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is only because I care about you that I say such harsh things.","scene":"An adult explains strict criticism to a student."},{"tokens":[{"t":"努力すればこそ","r":"どりょくすればこそ","m":"only through effort"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"成功","r":"せいこう","m":"success"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"得られる","r":"えられる","m":"can be achieved"},{"t":"のだ","r":"のだ","m":"that is why"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is only through effort that success can be achieved.","scene":"An encouraging line in a speech."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-010||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～ばこそ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばこそ","patternReading":"ばこそ","coreMeaning":"Only because ~ (the sole, positive reason)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure consists of the **ば-form** of V/A + **こそ**. For nouns and な-adjectives, use **であれば** (formal and slightly old-fashioned). The pattern always appears in the **reason clause**, followed by result Q.","nuance":"Not used in casual conversation; it sounds formal and somewhat literary.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vば形","rhs":"+ こそ","note":"Example: 思えばこそ、信じていればこそ"},{"lhs":"いA（〜ければ）","rhs":"+ こそ","note":"Example: うれしければこそ"},{"lhs":"なA/N（であれば）","rhs":"+ こそ","note":"Example: 本物であればこそ"},{"lhs":"[P]ばこそ、","rhs":"[Q]のだ","note":"Q is usually a positive or highly valued result"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"本物","r":"ほんもの","m":"genuine item"},{"t":"であればこそ","r":"であればこそ","m":"only because it is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"長く","r":"ながく","m":"for a long time"},{"t":"愛される","r":"あいされる","m":"is loved"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is only because it is genuine that it remains loved for so long.","scene":"Discussing genuine goods versus imitations."},{"tokens":[{"t":"うれしければこそ","r":"うれしければこそ","m":"only because I am happy"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"涙","r":"なみだ","m":"tears"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"出る","r":"でる","m":"come out"},{"t":"のだ","r":"のだ","m":"that is why"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is only because I am happy that I shed tears.","scene":"Explaining the emotion of reuniting with family."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-010||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"～ばこそ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばこそ","patternReading":"ばこそ","coreMeaning":"Only because ~ (the sole, positive reason)","slug":"usage","definition":"Three main situations: **defending** strict words out of care, **praising** success achieved through effort, and **explaining a decision** to delegate work because of trust.","nuance":"P is always framed as something ‘worth valuing’ so Q is not misunderstood. Do not use it for bad or neutral causes.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"信頼して","r":"しんらいして","m":"trusting"},{"t":"いればこそ","r":"いればこそ","m":"only because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"任せた","r":"まかせた","m":"delegated"},{"t":"のです","r":"のです","m":"that is why"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is only because I trust you that I assigned you this work.","scene":"A boss reassures an employee given an important task."},{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"将来","r":"しょうらい","m":"future"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"思えばこそ","r":"おもえばこそ","m":"only because I care about"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"厳しく","r":"きびしく","m":"strictly"},{"t":"接しています","r":"せっしています","m":"am treating"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is only because I care about my child’s future that I am so strict.","scene":"Parents explain why they do not indulge their child."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-010||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with だからこそ・ゆえに","sub":"～ばこそ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばこそ","patternReading":"ばこそ","coreMeaning":"Only because ~ (the sole, positive reason)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare ばこそ with two similar patterns: だからこそ and ゆえに. All can be translated as ‘because,’ but their nuance and register differ.","nuance":"ばこそ = だからこそ but **more formal and literary**; ゆえに is **neutral and does not imply a positive evaluation**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"信頼して","r":"しんらいして","m":"trusting"},{"t":"いればこそ","r":"いればこそ","m":"only because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"任せた","r":"まかせた","m":"delegated"},{"t":"のです","r":"のです","m":"that is why"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is only because I trust you that I entrusted it to you.","scene":"A boss speaks to an employee given a task."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"だからこそ","point":"Similar and common in conversation. ばこそ sounds formal and literary; だからこそ is more natural."},{"vs":"ゆえに","point":"ゆえに simply states a direct cause and is NEUTRAL. ばこそ emphasizes P as the SOLE + POSITIVE reason."}]}},"n2-010||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～ばこそ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばこそ","patternReading":"ばこそ","coreMeaning":"Only because ~ (the sole, positive reason)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The most common mistake is using ばこそ for **every kind of cause**, including negative ones. This pattern is used only when P is **good and valued**.","nuance":"If P is a negative or neutral reason, use から, ので, or ために instead. Reserve ばこそ for a positive, valued cause; this keeps its emphatic praise or defense natural and avoids making ordinary misfortunes sound worthy of praise.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あなた","r":"あなた","m":"you"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"思えばこそ","r":"おもえばこそ","m":"only because I care about"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"言っている","r":"いっている","m":"am saying"},{"t":"んだ","r":"んだ","m":"that is why"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is only because I care about you that I am saying this.","scene":"An older brother explains after his younger brother gets angry."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 眠ければこそ、授業中寝てしまった。 → ✅ 眠かったから、授業中寝てしまった。","❌ 失敗すればこそ、悔しかった。 → ✅ 失敗したからこそ、悔しかった。","❌ 雨が降ればこそ、試合は中止になった。 → ✅ 雨が降ったから、試合は中止になった。","❌ 忙しければこそ、手伝えなかった。 → ✅ 忙しかったから、手伝えなかった。"],"compare":null}},"n2-010||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip: ば+こそ","sub":"～ばこそ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばこそ","patternReading":"ばこそ","coreMeaning":"Only because ~ (the sole, positive reason)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember it by splitting the form: **ば** (if/because) + **こそ** (precisely, uniquely) → ‘ONLY BECAUSE of this good thing.’ Picture a hand pointing directly at the positive reason.","nuance":"Always test it: If replacing it with だからこそ still works and P is positive, ばこそ may work. If P is negative, reject it immediately.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-010||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～ばこそ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばこそ","patternReading":"ばこそ","coreMeaning":"Only because ~ (the sole, positive reason)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Fixed phrases often seen on tests: **信じていればこそ**, **思えばこそ**, **愛すればこそ**, and **本物であればこそ**.","nuance":"These phrases often appear when expressing strong emotion, defending an action, or affirming value.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"愛すればこそ","r":"あいすればこそ","m":"only because I love"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"許せる","r":"ゆるせる","m":"can forgive"},{"t":"のです","r":"のです","m":"that is why"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is only because I love you that I can forgive you.","scene":"Reflecting on love within a family."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-011||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ないことには · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないことには","patternReading":"ないことには","coreMeaning":"Unless ~, it is impossible (an essential prerequisite)","slug":"core","definition":"～ないことには expresses P as an essential prerequisite: without P, Q cannot happen or be carried out. Q is always negative. The speaker’s feeling is: “To achieve Q, you must do P first; there is no other way.” This pattern is common at work and in everyday life.","nuance":"This pattern strongly emphasizes necessity, more than なければ/ないと. It works in both speech and writing and sounds natural in formal writing. P is often an action that has not yet been done, so the sentence implicitly urges the listener to do P.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"まず","m":"first"},{"t":"予算","r":"よさん","m":"budget"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"確保し","r":"かくほし","m":"secure"},{"t":"ないことには","m":"unless...impossible"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"プロジェクト","m":"project"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"始まらない","r":"はじまらない","m":"cannot start"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Unless we secure the budget first, the project cannot start.","scene":"A manager stresses the budget in a company meeting."},{"tokens":[{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"でないことには","m":"unless...impossible"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何も","r":"なにも","m":"anything"},{"t":"できない","m":"cannot do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Without good health, you cannot do anything.","scene":"An older person advises younger family members about health."},{"tokens":[{"t":"話さ","r":"はなさ","m":"talk"},{"t":"ないことには","m":"unless...impossible"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"始まらない","r":"はじまらない","m":"cannot begin"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Unless we talk about it, nothing can begin.","scene":"After an argument, one person realizes they must talk."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-011||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"～ないことには · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないことには","patternReading":"ないことには","coreMeaning":"Unless ~, it is impossible (an essential prerequisite)","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: Vない形 / いAくない / なA・Nでない + ことには + a negative clause. The following clause is always negative or means “cannot” or “is difficult to.”","nuance":"Polite and past forms do not directly apply to this pattern. The structure itself expresses a present or future condition.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（ない形）","rhs":"ことには + [否定文]","note":"Example: 行かないことには始まらない"},{"lhs":"いA（くない）","rhs":"ことには + [否定文]","note":"Example: 安くないことには売れない"},{"lhs":"なA/N + でない","rhs":"ことには + [否定文]","note":"Example: 会員でないことには入れない"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"安く","r":"やすく","m":"inexpensive"},{"t":"ないことには","m":"unless...impossible"},{"t":"売れない","r":"うれない","m":"cannot sell"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Unless it is inexpensive, it will not sell.","scene":"A seller comments on the product price."},{"tokens":[{"t":"会員","r":"かいいん","m":"member"},{"t":"でないことには","m":"unless...impossible"},{"t":"入れない","r":"はいれない","m":"cannot enter"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Unless you are a member, you cannot enter.","scene":"A club employee speaks to a visitor at the entrance."},{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"足り","r":"たり","m":"be enough"},{"t":"ないことには","m":"unless...impossible"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何も","r":"なにも","m":"anything"},{"t":"できない","m":"cannot do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Unless there is enough time, we cannot do anything.","scene":"Students discuss how to finish an assignment."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-011||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～ないことには · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないことには","patternReading":"ないことには","coreMeaning":"Unless ~, it is impossible (an essential prerequisite)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to emphasize something that must be in place first. At work, common examples include needing a budget or data to start, or needing to try something to know its quality.","nuance":"This is a formal pattern, but it is also used in daily conversation with friends for emphasis. It is not overly casual and remains polite when speaking to a superior.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"現地","r":"げんち","m":"site"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"訪問し","r":"ほうもんし","m":"visit"},{"t":"ないことには","m":"unless...impossible"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"正確な","r":"せいかくな","m":"accurate"},{"t":"情報","r":"じょうほう","m":"information"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"得られない","r":"えられない","m":"cannot obtain"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Unless we visit the site, we cannot obtain accurate information.","scene":"Reporters discuss visiting the site to cover a story."},{"tokens":[{"t":"自分","r":"じぶん","m":"oneself"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"経験し","r":"けいけんし","m":"experience"},{"t":"ないことには","m":"unless...impossible"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"本当","r":"ほんとう","m":"true"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"大変さ","r":"たいへんさ","m":"difficulty"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"わからない","m":"cannot understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Unless you experience it yourself, you cannot understand how hard it really is.","scene":"An older sibling advises someone starting adult life."},{"tokens":[{"t":"読ま","r":"よま","m":"read"},{"t":"ないことには","m":"unless...impossible"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"感想","r":"かんそう","m":"impressions"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"書けない","r":"かけない","m":"cannot write"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Unless you read it, you cannot write your impressions.","scene":"A teacher assigns reading; a student complains it is difficult."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-011||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with なければ・ないと","sub":"～ないことには · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないことには","patternReading":"ないことには","coreMeaning":"Unless ~, it is impossible (an essential prerequisite)","slug":"compare","definition":"These three negative-condition patterns differ as follows: なければ expresses an ordinary condition and Q may be affirmative; ないと often connects to a negative result; ないことには emphasizes P as an essential prerequisite, and Q is always negative.","nuance":"ないことには is more formal and stronger than the other two patterns. It sounds logical and persuasive.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"本人","r":"ほんにん","m":"person concerned"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"会わ","r":"あわ","m":"meet"},{"t":"ないことには","m":"unless...impossible"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何も","r":"なにも","m":"anything"},{"t":"決められない","r":"きめられない","m":"cannot decide"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Unless we meet the person concerned, we cannot decide anything.","scene":"Everyone waits for a decision in a meeting."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜なければ","point":"なければ simply means “if not P, Q,” and Q may be affirmative. ないことには emphasizes P as an essential step and Q is always negative. Example: 運動しなければ太る (affirmative) cannot use ないことには."},{"vs":"〜ないと","point":"ないと often describes a negative consequence such as 怒られる or 間に合わない, without a prerequisite nuance. ないことには emphasizes “without P, Q cannot begin or be achieved.”"}]}},"n2-011||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～ないことには · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないことには","patternReading":"ないことには","coreMeaning":"Unless ~, it is impossible (an essential prerequisite)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The three most common mistakes learners make with ～ないことには are: **1)** making the following clause affirmative; **2)** using it for natural cause-and-effect, such as “if you do not eat, you die”; **3)** using じゃない instead of でない with nouns and na-adjectives.","nuance":"Mistake 1 is the most serious: Q after ～ないことには must be negative. If Q is affirmative, such as きっとできる, the sentence is grammatically wrong. Mistake 2 uses this pattern for natural cause and effect, which calls for なければ. Mistake 3 uses じゃない with nouns instead of でない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"実際に","r":"じっさいに","m":"in reality"},{"t":"見","r":"み","m":"see"},{"t":"ないことには","m":"unless...impossible"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"判断","r":"はんだん","m":"judgment"},{"t":"できない","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Unless you see it in person, you cannot judge it.","scene":"A consultant needs to see the product in person"}],"pitfalls":["❌ やってみないことには、きっとできる。 → ✅ やってみないことには、できるかどうかわからない。 (Q must be negative or uncertain)","❌ 食べないことには死んでしまう。 → ✅ 食べなければ死んでしまう。 (Natural cause and effect, not an essential prerequisite for an action)","❌ 学生ないことには、入れない。 → ✅ 学生でないことには / 学生じゃないことには、入れない。 (With N/なA, add で/じゃ before ないことには)","❌ 予算を確保しないことには、開始できる。 → ✅ 予算を確保しないことには、開始できない。 (The following clause must be negative)"],"compare":null}},"n2-011||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"～ないことには · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないことには","patternReading":"ないことには","coreMeaning":"Unless ~, it is impossible (an essential prerequisite)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember ～ないことには with an image of a key. P is the key and Q is the door. Without the key (P), the door (Q) cannot open. Thus remember: P is an unfinished action and Q must be negative.","nuance":"Use this mnemonic: “To get Q, do P first; without P, no Q.” Whenever you see the pattern, ask: Has P happened yet? Is Q negative?","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-012||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"～につき · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～につき","patternReading":"につき","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ / Per ~ (official notices)","slug":"core","definition":"～につき expresses a **reason** (because of) or a **rate/unit** (per). It appears in **official notices, signs, invoices, and contracts**. The key point: it is a **one-way statement from an organization or authority** to readers, and is not used in everyday conversation.","nuance":"The tone is **dry and formal**, with no emotion or extra explanation. It is more formal and official than から/ので. It often appears at the beginning of a notice, stating the reason before the consequence or rule.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"工事","r":"こうじ","m":"construction"},{"t":"につき","r":"につき","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"通行止め","r":"つうこうどめ","m":"road closed"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"なって","r":"なって","m":"become"},{"t":"おります","r":"おります","m":"(honorific)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of construction, the road is closed.","scene":"Notice beside a construction site"},{"tokens":[{"t":"お一人様","r":"おひとりさま","m":"per customer"},{"t":"につき","r":"につき","m":"per"},{"t":"一点","r":"いってん","m":"one item"},{"t":"限り","r":"かぎり","m":"limited to"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"なって","r":"なって","m":"become"},{"t":"おります","r":"おります","m":"(honorific)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Limited to one item per customer.","scene":"Supermarket notice limiting purchases"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-012||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and structure","sub":"～につき · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～につき","patternReading":"につき","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ / Per ~ (official notices)","slug":"forms","definition":"～につき always follows a **pure noun** (N + につき). It has no conjugated forms like verb patterns. For the reason meaning, N is usually an event noun such as 工事, 都合, 諸事情, or 棚卸し. For the rate meaning, N is a unit noun such as 一人, 一泊, 一つ, or 一時間.","nuance":"Because it is a **fixed expression**, it does not change for tense or negation. Politeness depends on the rest of the sentence, such as おります or いたします.","structure":[{"lhs":"N (event: 工事・都合・諸事情…)","rhs":"につき","note":"Expresses a REASON — common in notices and official documents."},{"lhs":"N (unit: 一人・一泊・一つ…)","rhs":"につき","note":"Expresses a RATE — per unit."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"都合","r":"つごう","m":"circumstances"},{"t":"につき","r":"につき","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"本日","r":"ほんじつ","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"臨時休業","r":"りんじきゅうぎょう","m":"temporary closure"},{"t":"いたします","r":"いたします","m":"(honorific) close"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Due to circumstances, we will be temporarily closed today.","scene":"Notice posted on a shop window"},{"tokens":[{"t":"一泊","r":"いっぱく","m":"one night"},{"t":"につき","r":"につき","m":"per"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"サービス料","r":"サービスりょう","m":"service fee"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"別途","r":"べっと","m":"separately"},{"t":"かかります","r":"かかります","m":"is charged"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The service fee is charged separately per night.","scene":"Hotel invoice stating service terms"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-012||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～につき · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～につき","patternReading":"につき","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ / Per ~ (official notices)","slug":"usage","definition":"～につき appears in **public signs, closure notices, official documents, and invoices** for two purposes: stating an important reason or setting a rate. It is **extremely formal** and is not used in conversation or text messages.","nuance":"With ～につき, readers understand that the statement is an official decision and not open to discussion. Notices are therefore often very brief: '工事につき通行止め' — with no further explanation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"諸事情","r":"しょじじょう","m":"various reasons"},{"t":"につき","r":"につき","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"イベント","r":"イベント","m":"event"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし","m":"cancel"},{"t":"させて","r":"させて","m":"let do"},{"t":"いただきます","r":"いただきます","m":"(honorific) ask"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Due to various circumstances, we must cancel the event.","scene":"Official concert cancellation notice"},{"tokens":[{"t":"お客様","r":"おきゃくさま","m":"customer"},{"t":"一人","r":"ひとり","m":"one person"},{"t":"につき","r":"につき","m":"per"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"プレゼント","r":"プレゼント","m":"gift"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"一つ","r":"ひとつ","m":"one"},{"t":"差し上げます","r":"さしあげます","m":"give (honorific)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Each customer will receive one gift.","scene":"In-store promotion"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-012||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with ため / によって / あたり","sub":"～につき · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～につき","patternReading":"につき","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ / Per ~ (official notices)","slug":"compare","definition":"～につき is close to ～ため（に） and ～によって for reasons, but differs in **register and nuance**. For rates, it is often confused with ～あたり. Choosing the right expression makes the sentence natural and stylistically appropriate.","nuance":"～につき is a **one-way notice** with no explanation and no focus on who caused something. ～ため（に） works in both speech and writing and clearly states a cause. ～によって often emphasizes an agent or means. ～あたり is the common spoken expression for rates.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"棚卸し","r":"たなおろし","m":"inventory check"},{"t":"につき","r":"につき","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"臨時休業","r":"りんじきゅうぎょう","m":"temporary closure"},{"t":"させて","r":"させて","m":"let do"},{"t":"いただきます","r":"いただきます","m":"(honorific) ask"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Due to inventory checking, we will temporarily close.","scene":"Notice outside a supermarket"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～ため（に）","point":"〜につき is used in brief signs/documents ('工事につき通行止め'); 〜ため works in speech and writing and explains the reason: '工事のため、通行止めになっています。'"},{"vs":"～によって","point":"〜によって emphasizes an agent or means ('事故によって道が閉鎖された'); 〜につき simply announces the reason without focusing on the agent."},{"vs":"～あたり","point":"In conversation, 'per' is usually 〜あたり: '一つあたり百円'; 〜につき is too formal and suits documents."}]}},"n2-012||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"～につき · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～につき","patternReading":"につき","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ / Per ~ (official notices)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make three mistakes: (1) attaching につき to an adjective or verb; (2) using につき in conversation instead of から/ので; and (3) using につき for everyday spoken rates instead of あたり.","nuance":"Always check: **is the word before につき a noun?** If not, convert it to a noun, such as 忙しい→多忙, or use another pattern. When **speaking with friends**, use から/ので instead of につき.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"多忙","r":"たぼう","m":"busy"},{"t":"につき","r":"につき","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"返信","r":"へんしん","m":"reply"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"遅れました","r":"おくれました","m":"was late"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Due to a busy schedule, my email reply was delayed.","scene":"Formal work email apologizing for a late reply"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 忙しいにつき、返信が遅れました。 → ✅ 多忙につき、返信が遅れました。 (忙しい is an い-adjective; change it to the noun 多忙)","❌ 頭痛につき、今日休むね。(speaking to a close friend) → ✅ 頭痛だから、今日休むね。 (につき is for official writing/notices, not casual conversation)","❌ 一つにつきに百円 → ✅ 一つにつき百円 (do not add the particle に after につき)"],"compare":null}},"n2-012||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～につき · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～につき","patternReading":"につき","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ / Per ~ (official notices)","slug":"memory","definition":"～につき is written with the kanji に付き, whose original meaning is **to attach to** (付く). It **attaches directly to a noun** to form a notice-like expression. Imagine a sign attached to a post: official notices can use につき.","nuance":"Quick memory tip: when traveling in Japan, **if you see a notice containing につき, expect a reason or rule**. Connect it with official and administrative contexts.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-012||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common fixed phrases","sub":"～につき · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～につき","patternReading":"につき","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ / Per ~ (official notices)","slug":"phrases","definition":"N + につき phrases are **fixed** and recur often in real life. Memorizing them helps you quickly understand signs, contracts, and notices.","nuance":"These phrases are short and usually appear before a comma or directly before a rule. 工事につき is especially common in traffic contexts; 都合につき and 諸事情につき avoid giving detailed reasons.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"工事","r":"こうじ","m":"construction"},{"t":"につき","r":"につき","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"通行止め","r":"つうこうどめ","m":"road closed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of construction, the road is closed.","scene":"Short sign on a Japanese street"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-013||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～に反して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に反して","patternReading":"にはんして","coreMeaning":"Contrary to ~; against expectations, wishes, or rules","slug":"core","definition":"～に反して describes a result that goes against an expectation, wish, rule, or prediction. It presents an objective assessment, not an apology or concession. The base verb 反する means “to go against” or “to violate.”","nuance":"Formal and often used in writing or careful speech. It is stronger and more clearly oppositional than と違って: に反して means “completely contrary,” while と違って simply means “different.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"予想","r":"よそう","m":"prediction"},{"t":"に反して","r":"にはんして","m":"contrary to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"新製品","r":"しんせいひん","m":"new product"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"売れ行き","r":"うれゆき","m":"sales"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"好調","r":"こうちょう","m":"strong"},{"t":"だった","r":"だった","m":"was"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Contrary to predictions, the new product sold very well.","scene":"Economic news on new-product sales"},{"tokens":[{"t":"期待","r":"きたい","m":"expectations"},{"t":"に反して","r":"にはんして","m":"contrary to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"チーム","m":"team"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"一回戦","r":"いっかいせん","m":"first round"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"敗退","r":"はいたい","m":"was eliminated"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"しまった","r":"しまった","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Contrary to expectations, the team was eliminated in the first round.","scene":"Post-match commentary on an early elimination"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-013||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and forms","sub":"～に反して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に反して","patternReading":"にはんして","coreMeaning":"Contrary to ~; against expectations, wishes, or rules","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes the forms of ～に反して. It must follow a noun, never a verb or adjective. に反した and に反する come before a noun as modifiers.","nuance":"The classic form is N + に反して, used at the start of a clause describing a contrary result. に反した + N emphasizes a completed violation; に反する + N is more formal and common in laws and regulations.","structure":[{"lhs":"N（予想・期待・意志・規則・命令）","rhs":"＋ に反して","note":"A result contrary to N"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"＋ に反した ＋ N","note":"Modifier: violating / contrary to"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"＋ に反する ＋ N","note":"Formal present modifier"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"規則","r":"きそく","m":"rules"},{"t":"に反して","r":"にはんして","m":"against"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"スマートフォン","m":"smartphone"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"試験中","r":"しけんちゅう","m":"during the exam"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"使用","r":"しよう","m":"use"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"いた","r":"いた"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Against the rules, a smartphone was used during the exam.","scene":"School disciplinary notice"},{"tokens":[{"t":"規則","r":"きそく","m":"rules"},{"t":"に反した","r":"にはんした","m":"violating"},{"t":"行動","r":"こうどう","m":"conduct"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"厳しく","r":"きびしく","m":"strictly"},{"t":"処罰","r":"しょばつ","m":"punished"},{"t":"される","r":"される","m":"will be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Conduct that violates the rules will be strictly punished.","scene":"Company rules"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-013||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"～に反して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に反して","patternReading":"にはんして","coreMeaning":"Contrary to ~; against expectations, wishes, or rules","slug":"usage","definition":"This branch covers three uses of ～に反して: (1) an unexpected result, (2) violation of a rule or command, and (3) going against someone’s wishes or will. It objectively records a contrast.","nuance":"Suited to news, reports, official documents, and essays. In everyday conversation it sounds stiff; speakers often use と違って or のに. It works in formal emails and meetings.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"親","r":"おや","m":"parents"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"意志","r":"いし","m":"will"},{"t":"に反して","r":"にはんして","m":"against"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"芸術","r":"げいじゅつ","m":"art"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"道","r":"みち","m":"path"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"選んだ","r":"えらんだ","m":"chose"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Against her parents’ wishes, she chose a career in the arts.","scene":"A girl choosing art against her family’s wishes"},{"tokens":[{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"予想","r":"よそう","m":"prediction"},{"t":"に反して","r":"にはんして","m":"contrary to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"株価","r":"かぶか","m":"stock price"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"上がった","r":"あがった","m":"rose"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Contrary to the company’s prediction, the stock price rose.","scene":"Stock-market analysis"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-013||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Comparison with ~","sub":"～に反して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に反して","patternReading":"にはんして","coreMeaning":"Contrary to ~; against expectations, wishes, or rules","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes ～に反して from commonly confused patterns. In short: に反して = completely contrary; と違って = simply different; に背いて = intentionally betray; にもかかわらず = concession about a fact.","nuance":"They are not always interchangeable. に反して focuses on a result contrary to a prediction or rule; に背いて means intentional betrayal and often goes with 約束, 誓い, or 期待.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"予想","r":"よそう","m":"prediction"},{"t":"に反して","r":"にはんして","m":"contrary to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"降らなかった","r":"ふらなかった","m":"did not fall"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Contrary to the forecast, it did not rain.","scene":"Weather report: rain was forecast, but skies stayed clear"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜と違って","point":"と違って states a neutral DIFFERENCE; に反して states OPPOSITION to an expectation. 兄と違って、私は運動が苦手だ (に反して cannot be used)."},{"vs":"〜に背いて","point":"に背いて emphasizes INTENTIONAL violation of a promise or trust; に反して describes an objective contrary result. 約束に背いて彼女は去っていった (betrayal) ≠ 約束に反して… (against a promise)."},{"vs":"〜にもかかわらず","point":"にもかかわらず connects a REAL obstacle with a result and needs no expected noun; に反して requires N. 天気が悪かったにもかかわらず… (not: 天気が悪いに反して…)."}]}},"n2-013||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～に反して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に反して","patternReading":"にはんして","coreMeaning":"Contrary to ~; against expectations, wishes, or rules","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors with ～に反して. The main error is attaching it after a verb or adjective, although it only follows nouns; another is redundantly combining 反対 with 反して.","nuance":"Learners often translate it as “although” and use it incorrectly. The preceding element must be a noun expressing an expectation or rule. For “although it is raining,” use にもかかわらず.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"規則","r":"きそく","m":"rules"},{"t":"に反した","r":"にはんした","m":"violating"},{"t":"行動","r":"こうどう","m":"conduct"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"決して","r":"けっして","m":"never"},{"t":"許されない","r":"ゆるされない","m":"permitted"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Conduct that violates the rules will never be tolerated.","scene":"Company rules"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 天気が悪いに反して、遠足は楽しかった。 → ✅ 天気が悪かったにもかかわらず、遠足は楽しかった。 (に反して requires a noun for an expectation; do not use it after an adjective).","❌ 友達の反対に反して、彼は留学を決意した。 → ✅ 友達の反対を押し切って、彼は留学を決意した。 (反対に反して repeats 反 and has the wrong meaning).","❌ Casual speech: 予想に反して、あの店まずかった。 → ✅ 予想と違って、あの店まずかった。 (に反して is too formal for ordinary conversation)."],"compare":null}},"n2-013||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"～に反して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に反して","patternReading":"にはんして","coreMeaning":"Contrary to ~; against expectations, wishes, or rules","slug":"memory","definition":"A quick mnemonic based on the kanji and association. 反 (HAN) is the root of 反対 (opposition) and 反する (to oppose), so に反して inherently means “to go against.”","nuance":"Remember the key nouns: 予想・期待・意志・規則. When one appears before に反して, understand that the expected outcome has been reversed.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-013||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～に反して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に反して","patternReading":"にはんして","coreMeaning":"Contrary to ~; against expectations, wishes, or rules","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases with ～に反して. These collocations occur frequently in JLPT questions and real-world texts.","nuance":"Memorizing them helps you respond quickly in reading questions. 予想に反して and 期待に反して are especially common in economic news.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"予想","r":"よそう","m":"prediction"},{"t":"に反して","r":"にはんして","m":"contrary to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"store"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"大盛況","r":"だいせいきょう","m":"very busy"},{"t":"だった","r":"だった","m":"was"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Contrary to predictions, the store was packed with customers.","scene":"Comment on a newly opened shop"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-014||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～にかかわらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にかかわらず","patternReading":"にかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Regardless of ~; independent of the condition (neutral)","slug":"core","definition":"〜にかかわらず expresses that regardless of any condition or state, the result does not change. It has a neutral, objective tone: it simply states that the condition does not affect the result, without expressing emotional concession.","nuance":"This is formal grammar used in notices, regulations, and recruitment. Compared with ～ても, it is more objective and emphasizes “the same no matter what” rather than an emotional reaction. Japanese speakers rarely use it in casual conversation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"天候","r":"てんこう","m":"weather"},{"t":"にかかわらず","r":"にかかわらず","m":"regardless of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"match"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"予定通り","r":"よていどおり","m":"as planned"},{"t":"行われます","r":"おこなわれます","m":"will be held"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Regardless of the weather, the sports match will be held as scheduled.","scene":"School announcement"},{"tokens":[{"t":"経験","r":"けいけん","m":"experience"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"有無","r":"うむ","m":"presence or absence"},{"t":"にかかわらず","r":"にかかわらず","m":"regardless of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ご応募","r":"ごおうぼ","m":"apply"},{"t":"いただけ","r":"いただけ","m":"can"},{"t":"ます"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Regardless of experience, you may apply.","scene":"Company recruitment notice"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-014||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and connection","sub":"～にかかわらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にかかわらず","patternReading":"にかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Regardless of ~; independent of the condition (neutral)","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes the connection patterns for 〜にかかわらず. It can follow a noun or a pair of opposing forms or clauses, such as VるかVないか and AいかAくないか.","nuance":"With nouns, do not add の. With verb pairs, use **Vるか Vないか** to emphasize both possibilities. With adjective pairs, use **Aいか Aくないか**.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"＋ にかかわらず","note":"A noun showing a condition or state, often an opposing pair."},{"lhs":"Vるか / Vないか","rhs":"＋ にかかわらず","note":"Use Vるか Vないか to mean “whether one does it or not.”"},{"lhs":"Aいか / Aくないか","rhs":"＋ にかかわらず","note":"An opposing adjective pair; less common than nouns."},{"lhs":"Nの 有無 / 多少 / 良否","rhs":"＋ にかかわらず","note":"Natural compound pairs: 有無 (presence/absence), 多少 (more/less), 良否 (good/bad)."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"男女","r":"だんじょ","m":"men and women"},{"t":"にかかわらず","r":"にかかわらず","m":"regardless of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この"},{"t":"スポーツ","m":"sport"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"楽しめ","r":"たのしめ","m":"can enjoy"},{"t":"ます"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Regardless of gender, anyone can enjoy this sport.","scene":"Sports-club advertisement"},{"tokens":[{"t":"参加する","r":"さんかする","m":"participate"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"しない","r":"しない","m":"not participate"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"にかかわらず","r":"にかかわらず","m":"regardless of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"返信","r":"へんしん","m":"reply"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"お願い","r":"おねがい","m":"request"},{"t":"し"},{"t":"ます"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Whether you participate or not, please reply.","scene":"School email to parents"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-014||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and context","sub":"～にかかわらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にかかわらず","patternReading":"にかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Regardless of ~; independent of the condition (neutral)","slug":"usage","definition":"The most natural setting is an official notice: schools, companies, and recruitment rules. Japanese speakers often use it with phrases such as 有無・いかん・男女 to say that no person or thing is excluded.","nuance":"The style is formal and objective. For casual speech, switch to ～ても/～でも. The pattern also works in company emails, speeches, and administrative documents.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"学歴","r":"がくれき","m":"educational background"},{"t":"にかかわらず","r":"にかかわらず","m":"regardless of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"誰でも","r":"だれでも","m":"anyone"},{"t":"応募","r":"おうぼ","m":"apply"},{"t":"でき","r":"でき","m":"can"},{"t":"ます"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Regardless of educational background, anyone can apply.","scene":"Company careers page"},{"tokens":[{"t":"性別","r":"せいべつ","m":"gender"},{"t":"にかかわらず","r":"にかかわらず","m":"regardless of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"採用","r":"さいよう","m":"hire"},{"t":"いたし","r":"いたし","m":"do (humble)"},{"t":"ます"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Regardless of gender, we hire applicants.","scene":"Formal recruitment policy"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-014||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"～にかかわらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にかかわらず","patternReading":"にかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Regardless of ~; independent of the condition (neutral)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare 〜にかかわらず with similar patterns. The key point is that にかかわらず says a condition does not change the result, while にもかかわらず emphasizes that a real obstacle is overcome and the result still occurs.","nuance":"In written language, を問わず is similar but leans toward “applies to everyone,” while にかかわらず leans toward “the condition does not matter.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨天","r":"うてん","m":"rainy weather"},{"t":"にかかわらず","r":"にかかわらず","m":"regardless of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"match"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"実施","r":"じっし","m":"hold"},{"t":"し"},{"t":"ます"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Regardless of rain, the match will be held.","scene":"Official match announcement"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～にもかかわらず","point":"にかかわらず = the result stays the same regardless of a condition; にもかかわらず = a result occurs despite a real obstacle (concessive)."},{"vs":"～を問わず","point":"を問わず emphasizes “applies to everyone”; にかかわらず emphasizes “the condition has no effect.”"},{"vs":"～ても","point":"ても is casual and specifically concessive; にかかわらず is formal and makes a general statement."}]}},"n2-014||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"～にかかわらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にかかわらず","patternReading":"にかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Regardless of ~; independent of the condition (neutral)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors include connecting a verb in plain form directly to the pattern and confusing にかかわらず with にもかかわらず.","nuance":"To say “even though it is raining, ...” as a real obstacle, use にもかかわらず or ～ても. にかかわらず is suited to general statements about conditions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"経験","r":"けいけん","m":"experience"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"有無","r":"うむ","m":"presence or absence"},{"t":"にかかわらず","r":"にかかわらず","m":"regardless of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"応募","r":"おうぼ","m":"apply"},{"t":"でき","r":"でき","m":"can"},{"t":"ます"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Regardless of experience, anyone can apply.","scene":"Correct pattern instead of direct verb connection"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 雨が降るにかかわらず、試合をします。 → ✅ 雨天にかかわらず、試合をします。","❌ 病気にかかわらず、彼は来た。 → ✅ 病気にもかかわらず、彼は来た。","❌ 参加するかしないにかかわらず、返信してください。 → ✅ 参加するかしないかにかかわらず、返信してください。"],"compare":null}},"n2-014||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～にかかわらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にかかわらず","patternReading":"にかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Regardless of ~; independent of the condition (neutral)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember the root: **かかわる = to be involved or related**; にかかわらず = **not involved with or affected by the condition**.","nuance":"Imagine the result as a **stone**. Rain and sunshine are only wind passing by; they cannot move the stone.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-014||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common collocations","sub":"～にかかわらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にかかわらず","patternReading":"にかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Regardless of ~; independent of the condition (neutral)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are highly common fixed expressions with 〜にかかわらず, appearing frequently in exams and real-world formal writing.","nuance":"Memorize the opposing pairs 有無・男女・多少・いかん. When you see one, simply place it before にかかわらず to form a natural sentence.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"結果","r":"けっか","m":"result"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"いかん","r":"いかん","m":"what it is"},{"t":"にかかわらず","r":"にかかわらず","m":"regardless of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"最善","r":"さいぜん","m":"best"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"尽くし","r":"つくし","m":"do one’s best"},{"t":"ます"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Regardless of the result, I will do my very best.","scene":"Meeting statement before a project"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-015||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～にもかかわらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にもかかわらず","patternReading":"にもかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Although ~, still ~ (strong concession)","slug":"core","definition":"〜にもかかわらず expresses that **Q still occurs despite P being a fact (often an obstacle)**. This is a strong concessive pattern: Q happens **regardless of** P, conveying persistence, surprise, or criticism. P is an established or ongoing fact, not a hypothesis.","nuance":"This is **formal written language** (news, notices, novels). It is stronger than のに, with a neutral to **mildly critical** tone—“even though P is obvious, Q still...”. It suits objective reporting of events.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"大雨","r":"おおあめ","m":"heavy rain"},{"t":"にもかかわらず","r":"にもかかわらず","m":"despite"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"match"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"予定通り","r":"よていどおり","m":"as scheduled"},{"t":"続行","r":"ぞっこう","m":"continue"},{"t":"された","r":"された","m":"was continued"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Despite the heavy rain, the match continued as scheduled.","scene":"A sports report says the match was not postponed by heavy rain."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"高熱","r":"こうねつ","m":"high fever"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある"},{"t":"にもかかわらず","r":"にもかかわらず","m":"despite"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"出勤","r":"しゅっきん","m":"go to work"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"きた"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Despite having a high fever, he still came to work.","scene":"Seeing a colleague with a high fever still come to work."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-015||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～にもかかわらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にもかかわらず","patternReading":"にもかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Although ~, still ~ (strong concession)","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern attaches to several forms: **N, V plain form, い-adjectives, and な-adjectives**. With a な-adjective, add **な** or **である**—“静か**な**にもかかわらず,” not “静かにもかかわらず.” Negative, past, and progressive forms also use the V plain form: なかったにもかかわらず, ていたにもかかわらず.","nuance":"This structure is formal and common in writing. With nouns and な-adjectives, do not omit “な” or “である”—a frequent learner error.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"にもかかわらず"},{"lhs":"V plain form","rhs":"にもかかわらず"},{"lhs":"い-adj. plain form","rhs":"にもかかわらず"},{"lhs":"な-adj. + な / である","rhs":"にもかかわらず"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"全員","r":"ぜんいん","m":"everyone"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"反対","r":"はんたい","m":"opposition"},{"t":"にもかかわらず","r":"にもかかわらず","m":"despite"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"社長","r":"しゃちょう","m":"president"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"計画","r":"けいかく","m":"plan"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"強行","r":"きょうこう","m":"force through"},{"t":"した"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Despite opposition from all the employees, the president forced the plan through.","scene":"Employees discuss their boss’s controversial decision."},{"tokens":[{"t":"環境","r":"かんきょう","m":"conditions"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"不便","r":"ふべん","m":"inconvenient"},{"t":"である","r":"である","m":"be"},{"t":"にもかかわらず","r":"にもかかわらず","m":"despite"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"多く","r":"おおく","m":"many"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"観光客","r":"かんこうきゃく","m":"tourists"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"訪れる","r":"おとずれる","m":"visit"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Despite the inconvenient conditions, many tourists visit.","scene":"A travel feature about a remote but popular destination."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-015||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～にもかかわらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にもかかわらず","patternReading":"にもかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Although ~, still ~ (strong concession)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜にもかかわらず **reports an event despite an obstacle**—in news, official notices, praise, or criticism. When P is unfavorable (rain, illness, opposition) yet Q occurs, this pattern is very natural.","nuance":"Avoid it in casual conversation or friendly messages—it sounds **stiff and bookish**. Use のに in those settings. にもかかわらず is acceptable in formal speech when objectively discussing someone else.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"寒い","r":"さむい","m":"cold"},{"t":"にもかかわらず","r":"にもかかわらず","m":"despite"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"たち"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"外","r":"そと","m":"outside"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"遊んで","r":"あそんで","m":"play"},{"t":"いた"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Despite the cold, the children were still playing outside.","scene":"An adult sees children playing outside in the cold."},{"tokens":[{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"実らなかった","r":"みのらなかった","m":"did not pay off"},{"t":"にもかかわらず","r":"にもかかわらず","m":"despite"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"諦めなかった","r":"あきらめなかった","m":"did not give up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although her efforts did not pay off, she still did not give up.","scene":"Praising someone’s persistence through hardship."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-015||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare: のに vs ながらも","sub":"～にもかかわらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にもかかわらず","patternReading":"にもかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Although ~, still ~ (strong concession)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch helps distinguish 〜にもかかわらず from two common patterns: **〜のに** and **〜ながらも**. All three mean “although,” but differ in register and nuance.","nuance":"〜のに is natural in **conversation**, expressing disappointment, reproach, or gratitude. 〜ながらも is used when **one subject** has two parallel states. 〜にもかかわらず is objective and formal.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"にもかかわらず","r":"にもかかわらず","m":"despite"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大勢","r":"おおぜい","m":"many"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ファン","r":"ファン","m":"fans"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"集まった","r":"あつまった","m":"gathered"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Despite the rain, a large crowd of fans gathered.","scene":"News about a fan event held in the rain."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜のに","point":"のに is common in conversation and conveys disappointment, reproach, or gratitude; にもかかわらず is formal and objective: 雨なのに来てくれた (moved reaction) vs 雨にもかかわらず来てくれた (objective report)."},{"vs":"〜ながらも","point":"ながらも requires the same subject and parallel states: 小さいながらも立派な家だ. にもかかわらず does not require the same subject; P is an independent obstacle."}]}},"n2-015||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～にもかかわらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にもかかわらず","patternReading":"にもかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Although ~, still ~ (strong concession)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners commonly make three mistakes with 〜にもかかわらず: **forgetting な with な-adjectives**, **using it for themselves in everyday conversation**, and **using it in casual contexts, making the sentence unnatural**.","nuance":"This pattern is mainly written language, so it sounds **odd and overly formal** in messages to friends. Save にもかかわらず for writing, reports, or objective comments about others.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"した"},{"t":"にもかかわらず","r":"にもかかわらず","m":"despite"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"結果","r":"けっか","m":"result"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"出なかった","r":"でなかった","m":"did not appear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Despite making an effort, there were no results.","scene":"Someone laments their work after trying hard."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 静かなにもかかわらず、勉強できない。 → ✅ 静かであるにもかかわらず、集中できない。（な-adjective requires である）","❌ 明日試験があるにもかかわらず、今夜は遊びに行く。（too formal for casual conversation） → ✅ 明日試験があるのに、今夜は遊びに行く。（use のに in casual talk because にもかかわらず is formal written language）","❌ 疲れたにもかかわらず、まだ寝ないの？（asking a close friend） → ✅ 疲れたのに、まだ寝ないの？"],"compare":null}},"n2-015||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～にもかかわらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にもかかわらず","patternReading":"にもかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Although ~, still ~ (strong concession)","slug":"memory","definition":"Break にもかかわらず into に + かかわらず + も: かかわる means “be related,” にかかわらず means “regardless / irrespective”; adding も **emphasizes despite**—“even though P is a fact, Q still happens.”","nuance":"Imagine も as a hand brushing aside every obstacle: no matter how serious P is (rain, illness, opposition), Q still occurs. も strengthens the feeling of “despite.”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-015||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～にもかかわらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にもかかわらず","patternReading":"にもかかわらず","coreMeaning":"Although ~, still ~ (strong concession)","slug":"phrases","definition":"In exams and news, 〜にもかかわらず often appears with nouns for obstacles: **大雨, 反対, 困難, 資金不足**. Learn these as chunks to remember natural usage.","nuance":"These phrases are formal and suit written language. When you see にもかかわらず in a reading passage, identify the obstacle represented by P immediately; this helps you translate quickly and accurately.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"資金不足","r":"しきんぶそく","m":"lack of funds"},{"t":"にもかかわらず","r":"にもかかわらず","m":"despite"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"工事","r":"こうじ","m":"construction"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"続けられた","r":"つづけられた","m":"was continued"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Despite the lack of funds, construction continued.","scene":"A local report on a construction project facing financial problems."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-016||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ながらも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ながらも","patternReading":"ながらも","coreMeaning":"Although ~, still ~ (same subject, two parallel states)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ながらも expresses **two states or traits existing at the same time** in one subject, with an apparent contradiction between them. It means **“although A, still B,”** where B is notable despite A. `ながら` originally means “while; both…and,” while `も` adds concession and emphasis.","nuance":"Usable in speech and writing, but it leans toward **descriptive, evaluative, and formal statements**. The speaker often comments from an outside perspective, modestly acknowledges something, or expresses admiration. It can convey **praise, regret, or admiration**, and sometimes mild criticism. It is lighter and more “internal” than にもかかわらず.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"小さい","r":"ちいさい","m":"small"},{"t":"ながらも","r":"ながらも","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"家","r":"いえ","m":"house"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"温かみ","r":"あたたかみ","m":"warmth"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"has"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although it is small, this house has a warm feeling.","scene":"Praising a small but cozy house"},{"tokens":[{"t":"知り","r":"しり","m":"knowing"},{"t":"ながらも","r":"ながらも","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"黙って","r":"だまって","m":"silently"},{"t":"いた","m":"stayed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although he knew, he remained silent.","scene":"Someone hides something despite knowing the truth"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-016||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and connections","sub":"～ながらも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ながらも","patternReading":"ながらも","coreMeaning":"Although ~, still ~ (same subject, two parallel states)","slug":"forms","definition":"The connection patterns for 〜ながらも — **not every form works the same way**. Attach it to **い-adjectives**, **な-adjectives (drop な)**, **nouns**, or **verbs in the ます-form (drop ます)**. With な-adjectives, **never add な**: 残念ながらも, not 残念なながらも. With verbs, it commonly appears with verbs of **awareness or emotion**, such as 知る, 思う, and 認める.","nuance":"It has a formal, written feel. Verb + ながらも describes awareness or emotion existing alongside the main action; it is not used for commands or future actions.","structure":[{"lhs":"い-adj. (〜い)","rhs":"〜ながらも","note":"小さいながらも、苦しいながらも"},{"lhs":"な-adj. (stem)","rhs":"〜ながらも","note":"不完全でありながらも (な-adjectives use であり + ながらも)"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"〜ながらも","note":"子供ながらも、素人ながらも"},{"lhs":"V-ます-form (drop ます)","rhs":"〜ながらも","note":"知りながらも、思いながらも"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"残念","r":"ざんねん","m":"regrettable"},{"t":"ながらも","r":"ながらも","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"今回","r":"こんかい","m":"this time"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"申請","r":"しんせい","m":"application"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"不採用","r":"ふさいよう","m":"not accepted"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"なりました","r":"なりました","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Regrettably, this application was not accepted this time.","scene":"A polite rejection email"},{"tokens":[{"t":"素人","r":"しろうと","m":"amateur"},{"t":"ながらも","r":"ながらも","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"his"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"演奏","r":"えんそう","m":"performance"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"観客","r":"かんきゃく","m":"audience"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"魅了した","r":"みりょうした","m":"enchanted"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although he was an amateur, his performance captivated the audience.","scene":"Commenting on an amateur performer"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-016||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～ながらも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ながらも","patternReading":"ながらも","coreMeaning":"Although ~, still ~ (same subject, two parallel states)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜ながらも is used to **praise** someone who exceeds a limitation (small but warm, amateur but skilled), **give a polite notice** (残念ながらも in a rejection letter), **make a modest self-assessment** (不十分ながらも), or express **mild criticism** (knowing but remaining silent). Common contexts include evaluating products or people, speeches, and formal emails.","nuance":"Its main register is **descriptive prose, speeches, and formal email**. It can also work in a serious personal blog or careful meeting. **Avoid it in casual conversation and LINE messages** — use けど or でも instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"不十分","r":"ふじゅうぶん","m":"insufficient"},{"t":"ながらも","r":"ながらも","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"最大限","r":"さいだいげん","m":"maximum"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しました","m":"made"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although it was insufficient, I made every effort.","scene":"Reporting project progress to a manager"},{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"ながらも","r":"ながらも","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"驚くほど","r":"おどろくほど","m":"surprisingly"},{"t":"冷静","r":"れいせい","m":"calm"},{"t":"だった","m":"was"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although he was a child, he was surprisingly calm.","scene":"Recalling a boy who handled a situation calmly"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-016||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing ものの・にもかかわらず","sub":"～ながらも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ながらも","patternReading":"ながらも","coreMeaning":"Although ~, still ~ (same subject, two parallel states)","slug":"compare","definition":"ながらも, ものの, and にもかかわらず can all mean “although…but,” but their core differences are: `ながらも` = **two parallel sides of the same subject**; `ものの` = **an action was completed, but the result differs from expectation**; `にもかかわらず` = **despite an obstacle, with no subject restriction**.","nuance":"When choosing, ask: Is A a state or trait of the same subject as B? If A is a completed action, use ものの. If A is an external objective event, use にもかかわらず.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"小さい","r":"ちいさい","m":"small"},{"t":"ながらも","r":"ながらも","m":"although"},{"t":"立派","r":"りっぱ","m":"beautiful"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"家","r":"いえ","m":"house"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although it is small, the house is beautiful.","scene":"Commenting on a small but sturdy, attractive house"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ものの","point":"A is a COMPLETED action, followed by an unexpected result: 買ったものの、使えない。(bought it, but cannot use it)"},{"vs":"〜にもかかわらず","point":"DESPITE an external obstacle; the subjects need not match: 大雨にもかかわらず試合続行。"}]}},"n2-016||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"～ながらも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ながらも","patternReading":"ながらも","coreMeaning":"Although ~, still ~ (same subject, two parallel states)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Two major errors are **illogical contrast** and confusion about the form or subject. ながらも is used only when A and B genuinely coexist in the same subject, and A must not contradict B.","nuance":"The logic error often appears when 残念 (regrettable) is paired with a positive result. The subject error comes from using an independent event such as rain as A.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"嬉しい","r":"うれしい","m":"happy"},{"t":"ながらも","r":"ながらも","m":"although"},{"t":"緊張","r":"きんちょう","m":"nervous"},{"t":"した","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was happy but also nervous.","scene":"A bride and groom describing wedding emotions"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 残念ながらも合格した。 → ✅ 残念ながら、不合格でした。/ 嬉しいながらも緊張した。 (passing cannot logically be regrettable)","❌ 残念なながらも → ✅ 不完全でありながらも (な-adj. uses であり + ながらも)","❌ 雨が降るながらも、試合をした。 → ✅ 雨が降るにもかかわらず、試合をした。 (rain ≠ same subject as playing the match)"],"compare":null}},"n2-016||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～ながらも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ながらも","patternReading":"ながらも","coreMeaning":"Although ~, still ~ (same subject, two parallel states)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: `ながら` originally means “while; both…and,” showing two things at once. `も` adds emphasis: “**both A and B**.” When two things coexist in one subject, the contrast makes sense. Remember a **small but precious stone** — 小さいながらも価値がある.","nuance":"Remember ながらも as **two sides reinforcing each other**, not absolute opposition. When you see “both…and; although…but, coexisting,” you can recognize it quickly.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-016||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～ながらも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ながらも","patternReading":"ながらも","coreMeaning":"Although ~, still ~ (same subject, two parallel states)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases include `残念ながらも`, `素人ながらも`, `小さいながらも`, `不十分ながらも`, and `子供ながらも`. They appear in rejection letters, evaluations, self-assessments, and exam questions.","nuance":"Learn these as **chunks** for faster reading and writing. `残念ながらも` is especially useful because it often begins a polite rejection.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"古い","r":"ふるい","m":"old"},{"t":"ながらも","r":"ながらも","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"道具","r":"どうぐ","m":"tool"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"よく","r":"よく","m":"often"},{"t":"使われている","r":"つかわれている","m":"is used"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although it is old, this tool is still widely used.","scene":"Commenting on an old but useful pair of scissors"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-017||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～を機に · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を機に","patternReading":"をきに","coreMeaning":"Using ~ as a turning point for change (deliberate, formal)","slug":"core","definition":"～を機に treats an important event as a turning point, from which the speaker deliberately changes or starts something new. It shows strong resolve and views the event as a springboard. This formal pattern is common in writing, announcements, and speeches.","nuance":"Compared with ～をきっかけに, ～を機に is much more deliberate and formal. The following clause usually describes a planned action with a clear goal. It is not used for trivial matters or casual conversation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"退職","r":"たいしょく","m":"retirement"},{"t":"を機に","r":"をきに","m":"using as an opportunity"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"長年","r":"ながねん","m":"many years"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"夢","r":"ゆめ","m":"dream"},{"t":"だった","m":"had been"},{"t":"海外","r":"かいがい","m":"overseas"},{"t":"移住","r":"いじゅう","m":"relocation"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"実現","r":"じつげん","m":"make real"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Using my retirement as an opportunity, I made my long-held dream of moving abroad a reality.","scene":"A retiree shares plans after leaving work"},{"tokens":[{"t":"結婚","r":"けっこん","m":"marriage"},{"t":"を機に","r":"をきに","m":"using as an opportunity"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"タバコ","m":"cigarettes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"やめる","r":"やめる","m":"quit"},{"t":"ことにした","r":"ことにした","m":"decided to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Using my marriage as an opportunity, I decided to quit smoking.","scene":"Sharing a resolve to change after starting a family"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-017||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～を機に · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を機に","patternReading":"をきに","coreMeaning":"Using ~ as a turning point for change (deliberate, formal)","slug":"forms","definition":"There are two main forms: N + を機に（して） and V-る/た + こと + を機に（して）. With a verb, add こと to nominalize it. The variant を機にして is more formal and common in official announcements.","nuance":"The following clause (Q) is always a decision or plan: 始める, 変える, 実現する, 見直す. The preceding event is a turning point such as marriage, retirement, founding, or transfer.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"を機に（して）","note":"N is a turning-point event: 退職, 結婚, 創立..."},{"lhs":"V-た + こと","rhs":"を機に（して）","note":"Nominalize the verb with こと"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"転職","r":"てんしょく","m":"change jobs"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"こと","m":"act of"},{"t":"を機に","r":"をきに","m":"using as an opportunity"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"分野","r":"ぶんや","m":"field"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"挑戦","r":"ちょうせん","m":"challenge"},{"t":"する","r":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ことにした","r":"ことにした","m":"decided to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After changing jobs, I decided to take on a new field.","scene":"Sharing a career plan after changing companies"},{"tokens":[{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"統合","r":"とうごう","m":"merger"},{"t":"を機にして","r":"をきにして","m":"using as an opportunity"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ロゴ","m":"logo"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"スローガン","m":"slogan"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"刷新","r":"さっしん","m":"renew"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Using the company merger as an opportunity, we renewed the logo and slogan.","scene":"An internal notice about post-merger changes"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-017||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & contexts","sub":"～を機に · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を機に","patternReading":"をきに","coreMeaning":"Using ~ as a turning point for change (deliberate, formal)","slug":"usage","definition":"～を機に is used in formal, official contexts such as speeches, company announcements, news reports, and program introductions. The following action is deliberate and planned, not random.","nuance":"In companies, you often hear: 創立50周年を機に、社名を変更いたしました. For a small personal matter, Japanese speakers use をきっかけに. ～を機に feels grand and deliberate.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"当社","r":"とうしゃ","m":"our company"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"創立","r":"そうりつ","m":"founding"},{"t":"50周年","r":"ごじゅうしゅうねん","m":"50th anniversary"},{"t":"を機に","r":"をきに","m":"using as an opportunity"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"社名","r":"しゃめい","m":"company name"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"変更","r":"へんこう","m":"change"},{"t":"いたしました","r":"いたしました","m":"respectfully changed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Using our 50th anniversary as an opportunity, our company changed its name.","scene":"An official company website announcement"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"プロジェクト","m":"project"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"終了","r":"しゅうりょう","m":"completion"},{"t":"を機に","r":"をきに","m":"using as an opportunity"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"チーム","m":"team"},{"t":"全体","r":"ぜんたい","m":"entire"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"体制","r":"たいせい","m":"structure"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見直す","r":"みなおす","m":"review"},{"t":"予定","r":"よてい","m":"plan"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Using the end of this project as an opportunity, we plan to review the team’s entire structure.","scene":"Speaking at a project wrap-up meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-017||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions","sub":"～を機に · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を機に","patternReading":"をきに","coreMeaning":"Using ~ as a turning point for change (deliberate, formal)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes ～を機に from easily confused opportunity expressions: ～をきっかけに, ～に伴って, and ～に際して. The key differences are deliberateness and the scale of the event.","nuance":"When choosing, ask: Is the event major? Am I deliberately changing something? If it is merely a trigger, use きっかけ. For parallel consequences, use に伴って. For an occasion before an action, use に際して.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"退職","r":"たいしょく","m":"retirement"},{"t":"を機に","r":"をきに","m":"using as an opportunity"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"立ち上げた","r":"たちあげた","m":"started"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Using retirement as an opportunity, I started a company.","scene":"Describing a career turning point"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～をきっかけに","point":"を機に: a major event + deliberate resolve, formal written style. をきっかけに: a trigger or cause, possibly accidental, natural in speech. 例: 彼女に会ったのをきっかけに日本語を勉強し始めた。(を機に is too formal here)"},{"vs":"～に伴って","point":"を機に: using an opportunity to make a deliberate change. に伴って: two events occur in parallel; no intention is needed. 例: 高齢化に伴って、医療費が増加している。(を機に does not fit because there is no deliberate choice)"},{"vs":"～に際して","point":"を機に: using an event as a turning point to begin something new. に際して: on the occasion of preparing to do something, emphasizing timing. 例: 出発に際して、お礼の挨拶を述べた。→ closer to “before,” not “change afterward”."}]}},"n2-017||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～を機に · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を機に","patternReading":"をきに","coreMeaning":"Using ~ as a turning point for change (deliberate, formal)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes learners make with ～を機に include using it for small events, following it with an unintended change, forgetting こと after a verb, or making casual conversations with friends sound theatrical.","nuance":"On tests, the trap is often choosing between を機に and をきっかけに. Check whether the event truly marks a turning point.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"新生活","r":"しんせいかつ","m":"new life"},{"t":"を機に","r":"をきに","m":"using as an opportunity"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"毎朝","r":"まいあさ","m":"every morning"},{"t":"ジョギング","m":"jogging"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"始めた","r":"はじめた","m":"started"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Using the start of my new life as an opportunity, I began jogging every morning.","scene":"Sharing a new habit with colleagues"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 雨が降ったのを機に、傘を買った。 → ✅ 雨が降ったのをきっかけに、傘を買った。(Rain is a small event, not a turning point)","❌ 新年を機に、毎日ゲームをした。 → ✅ 新年を機に、毎日運動することにした。(The next clause must be a deliberate, positive change)","❌ 転職するを機に、新しい分野に挑戦した。 → ✅ 転職したことを機に、新しい分野に挑戦した。(The verb must be V-た and nominalized with こと)","❌ 彼女に振られたのを機に、ジムに通い始めた。 → ✅ 彼女に振られたのをきっかけに、ジムに通い始めた。(For a personal romantic event, きっかけ sounds more natural)"],"compare":null}},"n2-017||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～を機に · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を機に","patternReading":"をきに","coreMeaning":"Using ~ as a turning point for change (deliberate, formal)","slug":"memory","definition":"The kanji 機 in を機に means “opportunity” or “chance,” as in 機会 and 好機. Imagine seizing the opportunity to create a turning point for yourself.","nuance":"Connect it with a major event and strong resolve. If either element is missing, choose another pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-017||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common collocations","sub":"～を機に · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を機に","patternReading":"をきに","coreMeaning":"Using ~ as a turning point for change (deliberate, formal)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Several fixed phrases commonly occur with ～を機に, especially in workplaces and on tests: 退職を機に, 結婚を機に, 転勤を機に, and 創立○周年を機に.","nuance":"The next clause often uses 変える, 始める, 決める, 実現する, 見直す, or 立て直す. Memorizing these phrases helps you answer exercises quickly.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"転勤","r":"てんきん","m":"transfer"},{"t":"を機に","r":"をきに","m":"using as an opportunity"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"車","r":"くるま","m":"car"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買う","r":"かう","m":"buy"},{"t":"ことにした","r":"ことにした","m":"decided to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Using my transfer as an opportunity, I decided to buy a car.","scene":"Sharing a decision after a work transfer"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-018||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～にあたって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にあたって","patternReading":"にあたって","coreMeaning":"On the occasion of ~; when embarking on an important undertaking","slug":"core","definition":"〜にあたって means **on the occasion of / when embarking on ~**. It usually precedes an important event such as graduation, signing a contract, or taking office. The following clause often expresses **preparation, a precaution, thanks**, or a **commitment**. The speaker feels they are “standing at the threshold” of the event.","nuance":"Formal, but usable in careful speech such as speeches and in writing. Not used in casual everyday conversation. Compared with に際して, it is slightly softer and more approachable. It always relates to a weighty action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"卒業","r":"そつぎょう","m":"graduation"},{"t":"にあたって","r":"にあたって","m":"on the occasion of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"先生方","r":"せんせいがた","m":"the teachers"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"感謝","r":"かんしゃ","m":"gratitude"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"述べたい","r":"のべたい","m":"want to express"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"思います","r":"おもいます","m":"I think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the occasion of graduation, I would like to express my gratitude to the teachers.","scene":"Speech at a graduation ceremony"},{"tokens":[{"t":"海外赴任","r":"かいがいふにん","m":"overseas assignment"},{"t":"にあたって","r":"にあたって","m":"before embarking on"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"現地","r":"げんち","m":"local"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"文化","r":"ぶんか","m":"culture"},{"t":"や"},{"t":"法律","r":"ほうりつ","m":"laws"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"事前に","r":"じぜんに","m":"in advance"},{"t":"調べて","r":"しらべて","m":"research"},{"t":"おくべきだ","r":"おくべきだ","m":"should do beforehand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before taking an overseas assignment, you should research the local culture and laws in advance.","scene":"Preparation advice before a long business assignment"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-018||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"～にあたって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にあたって","patternReading":"にあたって","coreMeaning":"On the occasion of ~; when embarking on an important undertaking","slug":"forms","definition":"にあたって follows **a noun naming an event** or a **dictionary-form verb (V-る)**. It does not connect to V-た or V-ない. は may be added for emphasis: 卒業にあたっては… In formal letters and speeches, use the variant **にあたりまして**.","nuance":"With N, the event is often a Sino-Japanese noun: 卒業, 開会, 入学, 契約締結. With V-る, the sentence focuses more clearly on an action. Adding は makes the sentence firmer and more formal, often appearing at the start of official notices.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"N + にあたって（は）","note":"Event noun: 卒業、開会、入学、契約締結…"},{"lhs":"V-る","rhs":"Vる + にあたって（は）","note":"Dictionary-form verb: 契約を結ぶ、渡航する、新事業を始める…"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"開会","r":"かいかい","m":"opening"},{"t":"にあたって","r":"にあたって","m":"on the occasion of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"注意事項","r":"ちゅういじこう","m":"points to note"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ご説明","r":"ごせつめい","m":"explain"},{"t":"します","m":"will do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the occasion of the opening, I would like to explain the points to note.","scene":"An MC's announcement at a seminar opening"},{"tokens":[{"t":"新プロジェクト","r":"しんプロジェクト","m":"new project"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"立ち上げる","r":"たちあげる","m":"launch"},{"t":"にあたって","r":"にあたって","m":"when launching"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"チームメンバー","m":"team members"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"募集します","r":"ぼしゅうします","m":"recruit"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When launching the new project, we are recruiting team members.","scene":"Internal company recruitment notice"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-018||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and contexts","sub":"～にあたって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にあたって","patternReading":"にあたって","coreMeaning":"On the occasion of ~; when embarking on an important undertaking","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern frequently appears at the **opening of speeches, official notices, and business letters**. The following clause usually gives **something to do, a precaution, a reminder, thanks, or a commitment** before or during the event. For maximum formality, use にあたりまして.","nuance":"This is formal grammar suited to work and ceremonial contexts. Do not use it in texts to friends or for everyday eating and other routines. In speech, use it only in a prepared address.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"このたび","m":"this time"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"契約締結","r":"けいやくていけつ","m":"contract signing"},{"t":"にあたりまして","r":"にあたりまして","m":"on the occasion of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"多大なる","r":"ただいなる","m":"tremendous"},{"t":"ご尽力","r":"ごじんりょく","m":"support"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"いただきました","m":"we received"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the occasion of signing this contract, we received tremendous support from you.","scene":"Business thank-you letter to a partner"},{"tokens":[{"t":"新事業","r":"しんじぎょう","m":"new project"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"始める","r":"はじめる","m":"start"},{"t":"にあたって","r":"にあたって","m":"when starting"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"市場調査","r":"しじょうちょうさ","m":"market research"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"十分に","r":"じゅうぶんに","m":"thoroughly"},{"t":"行う","r":"おこなう","m":"conduct"},{"t":"こと","m":"the act"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"重要だ","r":"じゅうようだ","m":"is important"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When starting a new project, it is important to conduct thorough market research.","scene":"Content of a business plan"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-018||compare":{"title":"⚖️ にあたって vs に際して vs に先立って","sub":"～にあたって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にあたって","patternReading":"にあたって","coreMeaning":"On the occasion of ~; when embarking on an important undertaking","slug":"compare","definition":"All three patterns can mean “on the occasion of / before an event,” but their **formality and focus differ**. にあたって emphasizes preparation when entering an event. に際して focuses more on the occasion and is common in writing. に先立って emphasizes an action done beforehand.","nuance":"にあたって is the softest of the three and can be used in a speech. に際して sounds formulaic and suits legal documents. に先立って always suggests that something is done before a particular time or event.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"入学","r":"にゅうがく","m":"school entry"},{"t":"にあたって","r":"にあたって","m":"on the occasion of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"学習計画","r":"がくしゅうけいかく","m":"study plan"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"立てて","r":"たてて","m":"make"},{"t":"おこう","m":"let's do beforehand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On entering school, make a study plan in advance.","scene":"Advice before the new school term"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～に際して","point":"に際して is more formal and more written; にあたって can be used in a speech. Example: 退職に際して… (document) / 卒業にあたって… (speech)."},{"vs":"～に先立って","point":"に先立って emphasizes “doing action A before event B” in time order; にあたって simply refers to an important occasion and does not require a prior action."},{"vs":"〜まえに / 〜とき","point":"まえに/とき are used for everyday actions; にあたって is reserved for major events such as graduation, signing a contract, or taking office."}]}},"n2-018||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～にあたって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にあたって","patternReading":"にあたって","coreMeaning":"On the occasion of ~; when embarking on an important undertaking","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often use にあたって for something **too minor** or follow it with a clause expressing **only emotion**. This pattern is not for everyday actions. If the event is not major, use まえに, とき, or ので.","nuance":"にあたって sounds ceremonial, so attaching it to “eating breakfast,” “washing hands,” or “buying a gift” is unnatural. The Q clause must also express a purposeful action requiring preparation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"契約","r":"けいやく","m":"contract"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"結ぶ","r":"むすぶ","m":"sign"},{"t":"にあたって","r":"にあたって","m":"when signing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"内容","r":"ないよう","m":"contents"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"よく","m":"carefully"},{"t":"確認","r":"かくにん","m":"check"},{"t":"すべきだ","m":"should"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When signing a contract, you should carefully check its contents.","scene":"Reminder before signing a contract"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 朝ごはんを食べるにあたって、手を洗った。 → ✅ 朝ごはんを食べるまえに、手を洗った。","❌ 新しいゲームにあたって、とても楽しみだ。 → ✅ 新しいゲームがとても楽しみだ。","❌ 明日友達に会うにあたって、プレゼントを買った。 → ✅ 明日友達に会うので、プレゼントを買った。"],"compare":null}},"n2-018||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips and imagery","sub":"～にあたって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にあたって","patternReading":"にあたって","coreMeaning":"On the occasion of ~; when embarking on an important undertaking","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: **当たる** means “to face” or “to come into contact with.” Imagine **standing at the threshold** of a major event such as graduation or signing a contract—**にあたって** is when you prepare mentally and do what is necessary. For an especially formal event, use **に際して**. To emphasize doing something beforehand, use **に先立って**.","nuance":"This tip helps you remember that にあたって always goes with **a preparatory action**, not merely a point in time. When you see a major event plus advice or a precaution, think of にあたって.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-019||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～に先立って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に先立って","patternReading":"にさきだって","coreMeaning":"Before ~ (a major event) — preparation or ceremony in sequence","slug":"core","definition":"〜に先立って describes action P performed **before** an important event Q takes place. It emphasizes **preparation or an opening ceremony**, and is common in **formal** contexts such as opening ceremonies, press releases, and diplomatic events.","nuance":"Formal written and spoken language; not used in daily conversation. It has a **solemn**, formal tone. Compared with **まえに**, **に先立って** is much more formal and strongly emphasizes sequence and preparation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"開会","r":"かいかい","m":"opening"},{"t":"に先立って","r":"にさきだって","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"黙祷","r":"もくとう","m":"silent prayer"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"捧げた","r":"ささげた","m":"offered"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before the opening ceremony, we observed a moment of silence.","scene":"At a memorial event's opening ceremony"},{"tokens":[{"t":"新製品","r":"しんせいひん","m":"new product"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"発売","r":"はつばい","m":"launch"},{"t":"に先立ち","r":"にさきだち","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"プレス","m":"press"},{"t":"向け","r":"むけ","m":"for"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"説明会","r":"せつめいかい","m":"briefing"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"開催した","r":"かいさいした","m":"held"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before launching the new product, we held a briefing for the press.","scene":"A company's press release"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-019||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"～に先立って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に先立って","patternReading":"にさきだって","coreMeaning":"Before ~ (a major event) — preparation or ceremony in sequence","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern connects to an **event noun** or **V-る + こと**. It does not change for tense or negation; it has two forms: **に先立って** and **に先立ち** (連用形).","nuance":"In formal writing, **に先立ち** often begins the preparation clause. Do not use it with た-form or ない-form verbs.","structure":[{"lhs":"N（event/式典）","rhs":"+ に先立って","note":"N is the main event; P is the preceding preparation"},{"lhs":"V-る","rhs":"+ こと + に先立って","note":"Dictionary-form verb; do not conjugate it"},{"lhs":"N / V-る + こと","rhs":"+ に先立ち","note":"Shortened form of に先立って, common in writing"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"契約","r":"けいやく","m":"contract"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"結ぶ","r":"むすぶ","m":"conclude"},{"t":"こと"},{"t":"に先立って","r":"にさきだって","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"担当者","r":"たんとうしゃ","m":"person in charge"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"決めた","r":"きめた","m":"decided"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before concluding the contract, we decided who would be in charge.","scene":"An internal meeting before signing a contract"},{"tokens":[{"t":"開会","r":"かいかい","m":"opening"},{"t":"に先立ち","r":"にさきだち","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ご来賓","r":"ごらいひん","m":"honored guests"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ご紹介","r":"ごしょうかい","m":"introduce (hon.)"},{"t":"いたします","r":"いたします","m":"will humbly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before the opening, I would like to introduce the honored guests.","scene":"An emcee speaking at a formal ceremony"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-019||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and contexts","sub":"～に先立って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に先立って","patternReading":"にさきだって","coreMeaning":"Before ~ (a major event) — preparation or ceremony in sequence","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern often appears in **official announcements**: before a major event, people perform a ceremony or conduct a check. P is always **preparation or ceremony**, while Q is the **main event**.","nuance":"The register is very formal. Do not use it for everyday actions such as eating or washing your hands. For ordinary “before” situations, use **まえに**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"に先立って","r":"にさきだって","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"注意事項","r":"ちゅういじこう","m":"points to note"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"説明します","r":"せつめいします","m":"will explain"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before the exam begins, I will explain the important points.","scene":"A proctor explains exam rules"},{"tokens":[{"t":"合併","r":"がっぺい","m":"merger"},{"t":"に先立って","r":"にさきだって","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"資産査定","r":"しさんさてい","m":"asset assessment"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"行われた","r":"おこなわれた","m":"was conducted"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before the merger, both companies' assets were assessed.","scene":"An economic report on a merger"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-019||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compared with similar patterns","sub":"～に先立って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に先立って","patternReading":"にさきだって","coreMeaning":"Before ~ (a major event) — preparation or ceremony in sequence","slug":"compare","definition":"All three patterns can mean “before,” but their **focus** and **register** differ. に先立って emphasizes sequence and preparation for a major event; にあたって emphasizes doing something on a special occasion; まえに expresses neutral time order.","nuance":"For a formal context with preparation or ceremony before a major event, use に先立って. To emphasize “on the occasion of” or “when undertaking” something, use にあたって. For ordinary “before” situations, use まえに.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"新製品","r":"しんせいひん","m":"new product"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"発売","r":"はつばい","m":"launch"},{"t":"に先立って","r":"にさきだって","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"記者会見","r":"きしゃかいけん","m":"press conference"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"行った","r":"おこなった","m":"held"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before launching the new product, we held a press conference.","scene":"A company prepares for a product launch"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～にあたって","point":"にあたって emphasizes an OCCASION or OPPORTUNITY and does not require preparation; に先立って emphasizes the ORDER before a major event."},{"vs":"～まえに","point":"まえに works in ANY context and is neutral; に先立って is limited to MAJOR, FORMAL events."}]}},"n2-019||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"～に先立って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に先立って","patternReading":"にさきだって","coreMeaning":"Before ~ (a major event) — preparation or ceremony in sequence","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often translate “before” too broadly and apply に先立って everywhere. In reality, this pattern requires a **major, official event Q** and a **preparatory or ceremonial action P**, not an emotion.","nuance":"In daily speech, まえに is more natural. In formal writing, check whether Q is an important event and P is genuinely preparatory.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"開会","r":"かいかい","m":"opening"},{"t":"に先立って","r":"にさきだって","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"準備確認","r":"じゅんびかくにん","m":"preparation check"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"行った","r":"おこなった","m":"conducted"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before the opening, we completed a final preparation check.","scene":"Organizers make a final check before opening"}],"pitfalls":["❌ ご飯を食べるに先立って、手を洗う。 → ✅ ご飯を食べるまえに、手を洗う。","❌ 開会に先立って、とても緊張した。 → ✅ 開会に先立って、準備確認を行った。"],"compare":null}},"n2-019||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～に先立って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に先立って","patternReading":"にさきだって","coreMeaning":"Before ~ (a major event) — preparation or ceremony in sequence","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture someone “going ahead” to perform an opening step before a conference begins. Associate **に先立って** with “performing a formal advance ceremony.”","nuance":"On a test, when the context is formal and you see **先**, immediately recall the sequence: preparation comes before the main event.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-019||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common collocations","sub":"～に先立って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に先立って","patternReading":"にさきだって","coreMeaning":"Before ~ (a major event) — preparation or ceremony in sequence","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common collocations with に先立って in JLPT questions and formal materials include 黙祷, 記者会見, 説明会, and 注意事項.","nuance":"Memorizing these collocations helps you quickly recognize the context and answer reading-comprehension questions accurately.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"試合開始","r":"しあいかいし","m":"match start"},{"t":"に先立って","r":"にさきだって","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"選手","r":"せんしゅ","m":"athletes"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"紹介","r":"しょうかい","m":"introduction"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"行いました","r":"おこないました","m":"gave"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before the match began, we introduced the athletes.","scene":"An emcee introduces the lineup before a final"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-020||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～において · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～において","patternReading":"において","coreMeaning":"At/in ~ (place, scope, field — formal)","slug":"core","definition":"`〜において` is a **formal compound particle** marking the **place, time, or scope/field** where something occurs. Basically, it is the **formal version of `で`**—instead of saying “at/in” normally, use `において` to create a **polite, objective, written** tone.","nuance":"`において` appears often in **official notices, reports, speeches, and legal documents**. It is **not used in everyday conversation**—using it with friends sounds very strange and stiff. `においては` emphasizes the context; `における` comes before a noun to modify it.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"式典","r":"しきてん","m":"ceremony"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"東京","r":"とうきょう","m":"Tokyo"},{"t":"において","r":"において","m":"at/in"},{"t":"執り行われた","r":"とりおこなわれた","m":"was held"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The ceremony was held in Tokyo.","scene":"Official notice about an event venue"},{"tokens":[{"t":"現代","r":"げんだい","m":"modern times"},{"t":"においては","r":"においては","m":"in the context of"},{"t":"情報技術","r":"じょうほうぎじゅつ","m":"information technology"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"不可欠","r":"ふかけつ","m":"essential"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In modern times, information technology is essential.","scene":"Research article on technology’s role"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-020||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and structure","sub":"～において · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～において","patternReading":"において","coreMeaning":"At/in ~ (place, scope, field — formal)","slug":"forms","definition":"The pattern includes: **N + において（は）**—N can be a **place, time, or field**; **N + においても**—“also in ~”; and **N + における + N2**—modifying the following noun. `において` always follows a noun; it does not follow a verb or adjective.","nuance":"In writing, `において` often follows **abstract nouns** such as 分野, 場面, and 現代. `における` works as an **attributive modifier** connecting to the following noun; `において` cannot be used directly there.","structure":[{"lhs":"N（place・time・field）","rhs":"において（は）","note":"Formal; marks the context where something occurs."},{"lhs":"N（place・time・field）","rhs":"においても","note":"Also in ~; adds another context."},{"lhs":"N₁","rhs":"における + N₂","note":"N₂ falls within N₁; modifies N₂."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"conference"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"大阪","r":"おおさか","m":"Osaka"},{"t":"において","r":"において","m":"in"},{"t":"開催された","r":"かいさいされた","m":"was held"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The conference was held in Osaka.","scene":"Administrative conference notice"},{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"における","r":"における","m":"in (modifier)"},{"t":"重要な","r":"じゅうような","m":"important"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"issue"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is an important issue in Japan.","scene":"Essay sentence using における"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-020||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and contexts","sub":"～において · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～において","patternReading":"において","coreMeaning":"At/in ~ (place, scope, field — formal)","slug":"usage","definition":"`において` is used in **three main situations**: ① **Place**—where an event is held; ② **time/context**—in which era or period; ③ **scope/field**—in which field or range. All three mean “at/in,” but with a formal tone.","nuance":"For places, it often occurs with verbs such as `行われる`, `開催される`, and `執り行われる` (ceremonially held). For time, common words include `現代`, `現在`, and `近年`. For fields, it often combines with `医療`, `教育`, `スポーツ`, `経済`, and so on.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"開会式","r":"かいかいしき","m":"opening ceremony"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"東京","r":"とうきょう","m":"Tokyo"},{"t":"において","r":"において","m":"in"},{"t":"行われた","r":"おこなわれた","m":"was held"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The opening ceremony was held in Tokyo.","scene":"News report on a sports event"},{"tokens":[{"t":"現代","r":"げんだい","m":"modern times"},{"t":"において","r":"において","m":"in"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"SNS","m":"social media"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"影響","r":"えいきょう","m":"influence"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"無視できない","r":"むしできない","m":"cannot be ignored"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In modern times, the influence of social media cannot be ignored.","scene":"Magazine commentary"},{"tokens":[{"t":"スポーツ","m":"sports"},{"t":"における","r":"における","m":"in (modifier)"},{"t":"公平性","r":"こうへいせい","m":"fairness"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"重要","r":"じゅうよう","m":"important"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Fairness in sports is important.","scene":"Essay on sports ethics"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-020||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with で","sub":"～において · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～において","patternReading":"において","coreMeaning":"At/in ~ (place, scope, field — formal)","slug":"compare","definition":"The key contrast is **`において` vs `で`**. `において` is the **formal** version of `で`, used in writing; `で` is the common, neutral choice for both speech and writing. `で` also marks **means, manner, and cause**—meanings that `において` does not have.","nuance":"When reading a sentence with `で`, you can “upgrade” it to `において` for a more formal tone—but only when `で` marks a place, time, or scope. If `で` marks a means such as a bus or train, **never** use `において`.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"規則","r":"きそく","m":"rule"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"すべて","m":"all"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"場面","r":"ばめん","m":"situations"},{"t":"において","r":"において","m":"in"},{"t":"適用される","r":"てきようされる","m":"is applied"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This rule applies in every situation.","scene":"Excerpt from a regulation"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"で","point":"Register: で is neutral/colloquial; において is formal/written. 会議は大阪で開かれた (usual) vs 会議は大阪において開催された (written)."},{"vs":"で","point":"Means: only で marks a means or manner. バスで行く (go by bus)—バスにおいて行く is impossible."},{"vs":"で","point":"Abstract scope: for a field or context, において is much more natural than で. 現代において vs 現代で sounds unacceptable."}]}},"n2-020||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～において · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～において","patternReading":"において","coreMeaning":"At/in ~ (place, scope, field — formal)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make two major errors: **using `において` for means** because they overgeneralize “at/in,” and **using `において` in conversation**, where `で` would be natural. Another error is using `において` before a noun; use the variant `における` instead.","nuance":"Always ask: **is this spoken or written language?** For conversation → `で`. For formal writing → `において`. And remember: before a noun modifier, `において` changes to `における`.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"issue"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"において","r":"において","m":"in"},{"t":"重要","r":"じゅうよう","m":"important"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This issue is important in Japan.","scene":"Formal sentence in a speech"}],"pitfalls":["❌ バスにおいて学校へ行く。 → ✅ バスで学校へ行く。 (`において` does not mark means)","❌ 友達と公園においてサッカーをした。（会話） → ✅ 友達と公園でサッカーをした。 (conversation uses で)","❌ 現代で情報技術が発展した。 → ✅ 現代において情報技術が発展した。 (abstract scope/context)","❌ 日本において少子化問題の対策 → ✅ 日本における少子化問題の対策 (use における before a noun)"],"compare":null}},"n2-020||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～において · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～において","patternReading":"において","coreMeaning":"At/in ~ (place, scope, field — formal)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick tip: **`において` = “formal で.”** When you see a sentence with `で`, try replacing it with `において`. If the meaning stays the same and the sentence becomes more formal, `において` is valid. The character `於` (おいて) means “at/in” in Classical Chinese, evoking something **placed in a formal context**.","nuance":"Also remember: **if it cannot be replaced by `で`, do not use `において`**. To modify a noun, use `における` instead of `において`.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-020||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～において · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～において","patternReading":"において","coreMeaning":"At/in ~ (place, scope, field — formal)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Remember these **set phrases** commonly used with `において`: `…において執り行われる` (be ceremonially held at...), `現代においては` (in modern times), `すべての場面において` (in every situation), and `…における問題` (issues in...).","nuance":"These phrases appear frequently in **JLPT questions** and **administrative documents**. Memorizing them helps you recognize the pattern quickly.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"本大会","r":"ほんたいかい","m":"this tournament"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"大阪","r":"おおさか","m":"Osaka"},{"t":"において","r":"において","m":"in"},{"t":"開催されます","r":"かいさいされます","m":"will be held"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This tournament will be held in Osaka.","scene":"Official event website notice"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-021||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～にわたって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にわたって","patternReading":"にわたって","coreMeaning":"Spanning ~, covering an entire broad range","slug":"core","definition":"〜にわたって describes something that **spans and covers an entire** range of time, space, or content. It comes from 渡る = to cross, so にわたって evokes an image of a bridge spanning the whole river: the event covers the range from beginning to end. The key condition is that the range must be **large enough**—many days, a wide area, or multiple fields—not something small.","nuance":"Formal and objective, often found in news, announcements, and research reports. It is unnatural in casual speech for something like 1時間にわたって. にわたって emphasizes **large scale** and broad coverage.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"conference"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"3日間","r":"みっかかん","m":"3 days"},{"t":"にわたって","r":"にわたって","m":"spanning"},{"t":"行われた","r":"おこなわれた","m":"was held"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The conference was held over three full days.","scene":"Internal notice about a national conference"},{"tokens":[{"t":"被害","r":"ひがい","m":"damage"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"全国","r":"ぜんこく","m":"the whole country"},{"t":"にわたって","r":"にわたって","m":"across"},{"t":"おり"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"復旧","r":"ふっきゅう","m":"restoration"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"かかりそうだ","r":"かかりそうだ","m":"seems to take"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The damage extends across the country, and restoration will likely take time.","scene":"News report on flood damage"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-021||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structures and forms","sub":"～にわたって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にわたって","patternReading":"にわたって","coreMeaning":"Spanning ~, covering an entire broad range","slug":"forms","definition":"Here are the main combinations of 〜にわたって: **N + にわたって** (predicate use), **N + にわたり** (formal written form), and **N + にわたる + N₂** (noun modification). The noun before the pattern is a **broad range**: time (2年間, 3日間), space (全国, 広範囲), or content (多岐, 様々な分野).","nuance":"Using **にわたり** instead of にわたって creates a more formal style, common in administrative documents, news, and official speeches. **にわたる** always comes before a noun and does not inflect for tense.","structure":[{"lhs":"N(time) +","rhs":"にわたって","note":"3日間・2年間・長年..."},{"lhs":"N(space) +","rhs":"にわたって","note":"全国・広範囲..."},{"lhs":"N(content) +","rhs":"にわたって","note":"多岐・様々な分野..."},{"lhs":"N₁ +","rhs":"にわたる + N₂","note":"長年にわたる交渉..."},{"lhs":"N +","rhs":"にわたり","note":"more formal written form"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"2年間","r":"にねんかん","m":"two years"},{"t":"にわたって","r":"にわたって","m":"spanning"},{"t":"調査","r":"ちょうさ","m":"investigation"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"行われた","r":"おこなわれた","m":"was conducted"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The investigation was conducted over a full two years.","scene":"Scientific report"},{"tokens":[{"t":"長年","r":"ながねん","m":"many years"},{"t":"にわたる","r":"にわたる","m":"spanning"},{"t":"研究","r":"けんきゅう","m":"research"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"成果","r":"せいか","m":"成果"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"These are the results of research conducted over many years.","scene":"Professor presenting a project"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-021||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～にわたって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にわたって","patternReading":"にわたって","coreMeaning":"Spanning ~, covering an entire broad range","slug":"usage","definition":"This branch shows **real usage contexts**: describing events lasting a long time, disasters spreading widely, and research covering many fields. The pattern is common in written sentences such as 会議は3日間にわたって行われた and 被害は全国にわたっている.","nuance":"Do not use it for short actions or narrow ranges. For a weather forecast meaning “from now through next week,” Japanese generally prefers にかけて.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"プロジェクト","m":"project"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"5年間","r":"ごねんかん","m":"five years"},{"t":"にわたって","r":"にわたって","m":"spanning"},{"t":"続けられて","r":"つづけられて","m":"continued"},{"t":"きた"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This project has continued for five full years.","scene":"Long-term project summary"},{"tokens":[{"t":"研究","r":"けんきゅう","m":"research"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"医学","r":"いがく","m":"medicine"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"工学","r":"こうがく","m":"engineering"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"多岐","r":"たき","m":"many fields"},{"t":"にわたって","r":"にわたって","m":"across"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The research spans many fields, from medicine to engineering.","scene":"Professor's introduction at a symposium"},{"tokens":[{"t":"工事","r":"こうじ","m":"construction"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"全線","r":"ぜんせん","m":"the entire route"},{"t":"にわたって","r":"にわたって","m":"along"},{"t":"行われた","r":"おこなわれた","m":"was carried out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Construction was carried out along the entire route.","scene":"Traffic announcement"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-021||compare":{"title":"⚖️ にかけて・をとおして compared","sub":"～にわたって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にわたって","patternReading":"にわたって","coreMeaning":"Spanning ~, covering an entire broad range","slug":"compare","definition":"This compares にわたって with two similar patterns that are often confused: にかけて and をとおして. The core contrast is **covering an entire range** (にわたって) vs **a continuous A→B span** (にかけて) vs **continuous regularity** (をとおして).","nuance":"In test questions, 全国, 3日間, 2年間, or 多岐 usually point to にわたって. For a weather forecast meaning “from now through next week,” にかけて is more natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"1週間","r":"いっしゅうかん","m":"one week"},{"t":"にわたって","r":"にわたって","m":"for"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降り続いた","r":"ふりつづいた","m":"continued to fall"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Rain continued to fall for an entire week.","scene":"Opening of a weather report"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜にかけて","point":"にかけて = a continuous range from A to B (e.g. 来週にかけて雨) — it need not cover every part. にわたって = covers a broad range in its entirety. Weather forecasts commonly use にかけて."},{"vs":"〜をとおして","point":"をとおして = throughout, continuously and regularly (e.g. 一年を通して). にわたって = spreads across a large scale and does not stress regular continuity."}]}},"n2-021||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"～にわたって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にわたって","patternReading":"にわたって","coreMeaning":"Spanning ~, covering an entire broad range","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with にわたって include using it for a period that is too short, using a specific place name instead of a broad range, and using a single point in time. The pattern requires a noun expressing a **broad range** and an event that **covers the entire range**.","nuance":"Learners often translate it mechanically as “throughout” and use it everywhere. In everyday conversation, Japanese often uses で, かけて, or nothing; にわたって has a formal written tone.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"1週間","r":"いっしゅうかん","m":"one week"},{"t":"にわたる","r":"にわたる","m":"spanning"},{"t":"出張","r":"しゅっちょう","m":"business trip"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"疲れた","r":"つかれた","m":"was tiring"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The business trip lasting a full week was exhausting.","scene":"Talking with a colleague after a business trip"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 1時間にわたって、映画を見た。 → ✅ 1時間かけて／1時間で映画を見た。 にわたって requires a sufficiently large range (several days, years, or a wide area).","❌ 北海道にわたって旅行した。 → ✅ 北海道各地にわたって旅行した。 One place name does not create a 'broad range'; add 各地・全域・全線...","❌ 明日にわたって会議がある。 → ✅ 明日会議がある。 'Tomorrow' is a point in time, not an extended period."],"compare":null}},"n2-021||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～にわたって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にわたって","patternReading":"にわたって","coreMeaning":"Spanning ~, covering an entire broad range","slug":"memory","definition":"Visual memory tip: 渡る = to cross. Imagine **a bridge spanning the entire river**—every part of the bridge covers the water. Thus にわたって means **covering an entire range**, not a small isolated interval.","nuance":"Use these clues together: a **LARGE** range + the idea of **FULL COVERAGE** → にわたって. A range from A to B → にかけて. Continuous regularity → をとおして.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-021||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases and collocations","sub":"～にわたって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にわたって","patternReading":"にわたって","coreMeaning":"Spanning ~, covering an entire broad range","slug":"phrases","definition":"Key fixed phrases include 多岐にわたって (across many fields), 長年にわたる (over many years), 広範囲にわたって (across a broad area), and 全線にわたって (along the entire route). In the JLPT, these phrases often appear in reading passages and fill-in-the-blank questions.","nuance":"Learn these as complete phrases rather than translating each word separately: 多岐 = many areas, 広範囲 = broad range, 長年 = many years. When these nouns appear, にわたって is often a likely match.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今回","r":"こんかい","m":"this time"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"地震","r":"じしん","m":"earthquake"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"広範囲","r":"こうはんい","m":"a broad area"},{"t":"にわたって","r":"にわたって","m":"across"},{"t":"被害","r":"ひがい","m":"damage"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"出た","r":"でた","m":"occurred"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This earthquake caused damage across a wide area.","scene":"Natural disaster news report"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-022||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ものを · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものを","patternReading":"ものを","coreMeaning":"Regret or mild reproach: P should have happened, but did not","slug":"core","definition":"～ものを expresses **regret or mild reproach**: P should have been done, but was not, leading to an unwanted result. The speaker looks back on what happened and sighs, “if only...”. This is a **subjective emotional** pattern, not an objective description.","nuance":"Used in both **spoken and written language**, especially casual conversation, diaries, blogs, and literature; **avoid** it in official documents and formal notices. Compared with ～のに, ～ものを sounds **more wistful and regretful** than angry. The sentence often **trails off** after ものを.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"素直に","r":"すなおに","m":"honestly"},{"t":"謝れば","r":"あやまれば","m":"if I apologized"},{"t":"よかった","m":"would have been good"},{"t":"ものを","m":"if only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"意地","r":"いじ","m":"stubbornness"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"張った","r":"はった","m":"held on"},{"t":"せいで","m":"because of"},{"t":"関係","r":"かんけい","m":"relationship"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"壊れた","r":"こわれた","m":"fell apart"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If only I had apologized honestly; my stubbornness caused the relationship to fall apart.","scene":"Recalling an argument caused by refusing to admit fault"},{"tokens":[{"t":"地図","r":"ちず","m":"map"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持ってくれば","r":"もってくれば","m":"if I had brought"},{"t":"よかった","m":"would have been good"},{"t":"ものを","m":"if only"},{"t":"…"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If only I had brought a map...","scene":"Muttering after realizing the map was left behind"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-022||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"～ものを · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものを","patternReading":"ものを","coreMeaning":"Regret or mild reproach: P should have happened, but did not","slug":"forms","definition":"Core structure: **Vば／Vたら ＋ よかったものを** expresses regret: “P should have been done.” For mild reproach, use **V辞書形／Vた ＋ ものを**, meaning “if only you had done P.” With adjectives and nouns, use **Aいければ／Aな・Nなら ＋ よかったものを**.","nuance":"Usually appears at the **end of a sentence** and may be followed by `…` to trail off. The rest need not be stated—the listener understands the emotion. The polite ます form is rarely used because this pattern is a personal lament.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vば／Vたら","rhs":"＋ よかったものを","note":"Regret: if P had happened, that would have been great"},{"lhs":"V辞書形／Vた","rhs":"＋ ものを","note":"Mild reproach: you should have just done P"},{"lhs":"Aい（→ければ）／Aな・N ＋ なら","rhs":"＋ よかったものを","note":"If only it had been P"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"earlier"},{"t":"言って","r":"いって","m":"tell"},{"t":"くれれば","r":"くれれば","m":"if you had told me"},{"t":"よかった","m":"would have been good"},{"t":"ものを","m":"if only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"なぜ","r":"なぜ","m":"why"},{"t":"黙って","r":"だまって","m":"stay silent"},{"t":"いた","m":"did"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"You should have told me sooner; why did you keep silent?","scene":"A manager mildly reproaches an employee for not reporting an issue"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"her"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"正直に","r":"しょうじきに","m":"honestly"},{"t":"話せば","r":"はなせば","m":"if I had told"},{"t":"よかった","m":"would have been good"},{"t":"ものを","m":"if only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"嘘","r":"うそ","m":"lie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ついて","r":"ついて","m":"tell"},{"t":"しまった","r":"しまった","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If only I had been honest with her; I ended up lying.","scene":"Regretting lying to one’s girlfriend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-022||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～ものを · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものを","patternReading":"ものを","coreMeaning":"Regret or mild reproach: P should have happened, but did not","slug":"usage","definition":"There are two main uses: **personal regret** and **mild reproach**. With regret, the speaker admits their own mistake: “If only I had...”. With reproach, the speaker shakes their head at someone else: “You should have...”.","nuance":"In conversation, ～ものを is often spoken softly and hesitantly—not harshly. It sounds more like a lament than criticism. In writing, it can appear in **casual emails, blogs, and diaries**, but **avoid** it in polite request documents.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"黙って","r":"だまって","m":"stay silent"},{"t":"いれば","r":"いれば","m":"if you just"},{"t":"いい","m":"would be good"},{"t":"ものを","m":"should have"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"つい","m":"carelessly"},{"t":"口","r":"くち","m":"mouth"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"滑らせた","r":"すべらせた","m":"let slip"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should have kept quiet, but I let it slip.","scene":"Regretting having revealed a secret"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","r":"もう","m":"another"},{"t":"少し","r":"すこし","m":"little"},{"t":"考えれば","r":"かんがえれば","m":"if I had thought"},{"t":"分かった","r":"わかった","m":"would have understood"},{"t":"ものを","m":"if only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"焦って","r":"あせって","m":"in haste"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"fail"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If only I had thought a little longer, I would have understood; I rushed and failed.","scene":"Criticizing oneself for making a hasty decision"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-022||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing vs ばよかった・のに","sub":"～ものを · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものを","patternReading":"ものを","coreMeaning":"Regret or mild reproach: P should have happened, but did not","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare ～ものを with commonly confused patterns: **～ばよかった** (pure regret, without reproach), **～のに** (stronger dissatisfaction or reproach), and **～ものの** (objective contrast without regret).","nuance":"～ものを sits in between: it expresses both **regret and mild reproach**, with a more delicate, wistful tone than ～のに. Remember: ものを often follows ば/たら + よかった, while ものの connects two contrasting clauses.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"本当","r":"ほんとう","m":"truth"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"こと","r":"こと","m":"matter"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"言えば","r":"いえば","m":"if I had said"},{"t":"よかった","m":"would have been good"},{"t":"ものを","m":"if only"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I should have told the truth.","scene":"Regretting having hidden the truth"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ばよかった","point":"ばよかった expresses regret about one’s own action; ものを can mildly reproach someone else (言えばよかった ↔ 言えばよかったものを)."},{"vs":"〜のに","point":"のに expresses stronger dissatisfaction or reproach; ものを is softer and more wistful (言ってくれればいいのに ↔ 言ってくれればよかったものを)."},{"vs":"〜ものの","point":"ものの shows objective contrast (“although...but”); ものを carries the speaker’s subjective regret or reproach."}]}},"n2-022||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～ものを · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものを","patternReading":"ものを","coreMeaning":"Regret or mild reproach: P should have happened, but did not","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Typical mistakes include using ものを for **objective contrast** (use のに/ものの), using it for a **positive result** (use ので/から), or using it in **overly formal writing**.","nuance":"Always ask: is there **regret or mild reproach**? If it is simply contrast or cause and effect, do not use ものを.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電話","r":"でんわ","m":"phone"},{"t":"すれば","r":"すれば","m":"if I had called"},{"t":"よかった","m":"would have been good"},{"t":"ものを","m":"if only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"メール","m":"email"},{"t":"して","r":"して","m":"send"},{"t":"誤解","r":"ごかい","m":"misunderstanding"},{"t":"された","r":"された","m":"was caused"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I should have called; the email caused a misunderstanding.","scene":"Regretting choosing email instead of calling"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 明日試験があるものを、遊んでいる。 → ✅ 明日試験があるのに、遊んでいる。","❌ 早く起きたものを、電車に乗れた。 → ✅ 早く起きたので、電車に乗れた。","❌ Using ものを in an official notice → ✅ Use ものを only for regret or mild reproach"],"compare":null}},"n2-022||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"～ものを · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものを","patternReading":"ものを","coreMeaning":"Regret or mild reproach: P should have happened, but did not","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: **もの = what should have happened** (physical or abstract); **を = object**. Together: “the thing one should have done but did not” → **regret/reproach**. When you hear ものを, immediately think “if only...”.","nuance":"Pair it with the image of a sigh after the event is over. Do not use it for reasons or objective facts—ものを expresses emotion, not logic.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-022||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～ものを · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものを","patternReading":"ものを","coreMeaning":"Regret or mild reproach: P should have happened, but did not","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases with ものを include **～ばよかったものを**, **～ていればいいものを**, and **～すればよかったものを**. They appear often in conversation, tests, and diary-style writing.","nuance":"When you hear ものを, the preceding phrase is often one of these patterns. Memorizing them helps you respond quickly in listening and reading.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"素直に","r":"すなおに","m":"honestly"},{"t":"謝れば","r":"あやまれば","m":"if I had apologized"},{"t":"よかった","m":"would have been good"},{"t":"ものを","m":"if only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"後悔","r":"こうかい","m":"regret"},{"t":"して","r":"して","m":"be doing"},{"t":"いる","r":"いる","m":"be in state"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If only I had apologized honestly; now I regret it.","scene":"Telling oneself this after being stubborn"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-023||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ことか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことか","patternReading":"ことか","coreMeaning":"How ~! What an ~! (an exclamation of strong emotion)","slug":"core","definition":"～ことか is an **exclamatory ending** expressing **very strong emotion**: surprise, excitement, regret, gratitude, or disappointment. It can mean “how much,” “how ~!”, or “I wonder how many times.” It often combines with **どれほど / どれだけ / なんと / いくら** to intensify the emotion. It **always comes at the end of the sentence**—no predicate follows. The subject is usually the speaker.","nuance":"It suits literature, memoirs, emotional speeches, and conversations expressing intense emotion. Its tone is **much stronger** than ～なあ. It is not used in neutral reports or ordinary conversation. It often comes with **！** or **…**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"合格通知","r":"ごうかくつうち","m":"acceptance notice"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"受け取った","r":"うけとった","m":"received"},{"t":"とき","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"どれほど","m":"how much"},{"t":"嬉しかった","r":"うれしかった","m":"was happy"},{"t":"ことか","m":"how much"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"How happy I was when I received my acceptance notice!","scene":"Intense emotion on receiving the acceptance notice."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"帰国","r":"きこく","m":"return home"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"どれほど","m":"how much"},{"t":"待ちわびた","r":"まちわびた","m":"waited longingly"},{"t":"ことか","m":"how much"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I waited so long for her to return home.","scene":"Looking back on waiting for her."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-023||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～ことか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことか","patternReading":"ことか","coreMeaning":"How ~! What an ~! (an exclamation of strong emotion)","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **［どれほど・どれだけ・なんと］ + V/いA/なA + ことか**. Verbs and い-adjectives often use the **past (-た)** form because the speaker is describing an experience. With な-adjectives, attach **な** to the stem before ことか. Nothing can continue after ことか—it is sentence-final.","nuance":"Do not use です/ます with ことか, because this is an exclamatory form. It is more formal than ～なあ but still strongly emotional.","structure":[{"lhs":"どれほど/どれだけ + Vた形","rhs":"ことか","note":"Example: どれほど心配したことか！"},{"lhs":"なんと + いA（かった）","rhs":"ことか","note":"Example: なんと美しかったことか！"},{"lhs":"なんと + なA + な","rhs":"ことか","note":"Example: なんと幸せなことか！"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"なんと","m":"how"},{"t":"美しい","r":"うつくしい","m":"beautiful"},{"t":"夕焼け","r":"ゆうやけ","m":"sunset"},{"t":"だった","m":"was"},{"t":"ことか","m":"how"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"How beautiful that sunset was!","scene":"Exclaiming while watching the sunset."},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"何度","r":"なんど","m":"how many times"},{"t":"立ち直ろう","r":"たちなおろう","m":"tried to recover"},{"t":"とした","m":"tried to"},{"t":"ことか","m":"how many times"},{"t":"…"}],"vi":"How many times I tried to recover from that failure…","scene":"Looking back in a memoir."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-023||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～ことか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことか","patternReading":"ことか","coreMeaning":"How ~! What an ~! (an exclamation of strong emotion)","slug":"usage","definition":"～ことか is used to **look back on** or **express strong emotion** about an event or experience. It suits **literature, emotional memoirs and speeches**, and conversations involving intense emotion. Do **not** use it in reports, notices, or neutral everyday conversation—in those cases, use ～なあ.","nuance":"In ordinary conversation, ～ことか sounds **more dramatic**. If you want a less theatrical tone, choose ～なあ. ～ことか also often appears with **！** or **…** to show an emotional outburst.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"無事","r":"ぶじ","m":"safe"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"よかった","m":"thank goodness"},{"t":"！"},{"t":"どれほど","m":"how much"},{"t":"心配した","r":"しんぱいした","m":"worried"},{"t":"ことか","m":"how much"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Thank goodness you’re safe! I was so worried!","scene":"Reuniting with a loved one after an accident."},{"tokens":[{"t":"なんと","m":"how"},{"t":"忙しい","r":"いそがしい","m":"busy"},{"t":"日々","r":"ひび","m":"days"},{"t":"だった","m":"were"},{"t":"ことか","m":"how"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"How busy those days were!","scene":"Looking back on a busy period."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-023||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with ～なあ・～ものだ","sub":"～ことか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことか","patternReading":"ことか","coreMeaning":"How ~! What an ~! (an exclamation of strong emotion)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare ～ことか with commonly confused exclamatory patterns. The key differences are **emotional intensity** and **context of use**.","nuance":"～ことか typically appears with **どれほど/なんと** and has a literary tone. ～なあ is natural and mild in conversation. ～ものだ emphasizes a nature, general truth, or customary pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どれほど","m":"how much"},{"t":"待った","r":"まった","m":"waited"},{"t":"ことか","m":"how much"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"How long I waited!","scene":"Angry yet relieved when a close friend arrives late."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～なあ","point":"～ことか: very strong emotion, literary, often with どれほど/なんと. ～なあ: mild emotion in ordinary conversation. Examples: 嬉しいなあ vs どれほど嬉しかったことか！"},{"vs":"～ものだ","point":"～ものだ: exclaims about a quality or general truth, e.g. よく頑張ったものだ (That was admirable). ～ことか: exclaims about emotional intensity, e.g. どれほど頑張ったことか (How hard I worked)."}]}},"n2-023||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～ことか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことか","patternReading":"ことか","coreMeaning":"How ~! What an ~! (an exclamation of strong emotion)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse ことか with ことが or add です/ます. Remember: ことか is an **exclamatory ending at the end of a sentence**, never used in an ordinary declarative sentence.","nuance":"A serious error is writing “ことかです”—ことか is already exclamatory and cannot take です. Learners also confuse it with ことが, the nominalizer structure, because both contain こと.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"なんと","m":"how"},{"t":"素晴らしい","r":"すばらしい","m":"wonderful"},{"t":"ことか","m":"how"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"How wonderful it is!","scene":"Praising an outstanding work."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 嬉しいことかです。 → ✅ どれほど嬉しかったことか！","❌ 毎日勉強することか大切だ。 → ✅ 毎日勉強することが大切だ。"],"compare":null}},"n2-023||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"～ことか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことか","patternReading":"ことか","coreMeaning":"How ~! What an ~! (an exclamation of strong emotion)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tip: **こと** = a thing or event, and **か** = a rhetorical question → *“Just how great was it!”*—an exclamation. The **emotion must be strong**; for an ordinary comment, do not use ことか.","nuance":"Pair it with **どれほど / なんと**: “how much” = どれほど～ことか. Picture someone holding their head and exclaiming with overwhelming emotion.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-023||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～ことか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことか","patternReading":"ことか","coreMeaning":"How ~! What an ~! (an exclamation of strong emotion)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed phrases frequently appear on exams and in literary writing: **どれほど～たことか** (how much), **なんと～ことか** (how ~!), and **何度～たことか** (how many times).","nuance":"These phrases usually occur at the end of a sentence looking back on the past, and they commonly use past-tense verbs for past experiences.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"何度","r":"なんど","m":"how many times"},{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"曲","r":"きょく","m":"song"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聴いた","r":"きいた","m":"listened to"},{"t":"ことか","m":"how many times"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"How many times I have listened to this song.","scene":"Listening to an old song and reminiscing."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-024||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～だけあって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～だけあって","patternReading":"だけあって","coreMeaning":"Indeed ~ / Living up to the reputation or foundation of P","slug":"core","definition":"～だけあって expresses the speaker's **high regard** when result Q matches what is expected from foundation P (experience, reputation, status). Meaning: 'indeed, because P, Q' or 'P was not for nothing.' P must be a **good, reliable foundation**; Q must be a **positive or deserved result**.","nuance":"Conveys **admiration** and positivity; works in speech and writing. It is stronger and more natural than から in praise. If Q is negative, use **だけに**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"プロ","m":"professional"},{"t":"だけあって","r":"だけあって","m":"as expected"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"早くて","r":"はやくて","m":"fast"},{"t":"正確","r":"せいかく","m":"accurate"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is indeed a professional; his work is fast and accurate.","scene":"A colleague handles work professionally"},{"tokens":[{"t":"さすが","r":"さすが","m":"as expected"},{"t":"老舗","r":"しにせ","m":"long-established brand"},{"t":"だけあって","r":"だけあって","m":"as expected"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"味","r":"あじ","m":"flavor"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"格別","r":"かくべつ","m":"exceptional"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As expected of a long-established brand, the flavor is exceptional.","scene":"Eating at a famous restaurant"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-024||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"～だけあって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～だけあって","patternReading":"だけあって","coreMeaning":"Indeed ~ / Living up to the reputation or foundation of P","slug":"forms","definition":"Attach the pattern directly to the **plain form** of V, A, or N. な-adjectives must retain な (有名なだけあって). Do not use the polite form before だけあって.","nuance":"The sentence can continue normally after the pattern; the following clause often contains an evaluation: 上手い, 丁寧だ, 人気だ.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"＋ だけあって","note":"Example: 長年練習しただけあって"},{"lhs":"い-A","rhs":"＋ だけあって","note":"Example: 高いだけあって"},{"lhs":"な-A","rhs":"な ＋ だけあって","note":"な is required: 有名なだけあって"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"＋ だけあって","note":"Example: プロだけあって"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"高い","r":"たかい","m":"expensive"},{"t":"だけあって","r":"だけあって","m":"as expected"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"品質","r":"ひんしつ","m":"quality"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"文句なし","r":"もんくなし","m":"flawless"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is expensive for a reason—the quality is flawless.","scene":"Reviewing an expensive product"},{"tokens":[{"t":"長年","r":"ながねん","m":"many years"},{"t":"研究","r":"けんきゅう","m":"research"},{"t":"してきた","r":"してきた","m":"have done"},{"t":"だけあって","r":"だけあって","m":"as expected"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"説明","r":"せつめい","m":"explanation"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"分かりやすい","r":"わかりやすい","m":"easy to understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After researching for years, it is no surprise that the explanations are so clear.","scene":"Praising an experienced lecturer"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-024||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～だけあって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～だけあって","patternReading":"だけあって","coreMeaning":"Indeed ~ / Living up to the reputation or foundation of P","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to **praise** or **positively evaluate** someone or something based on a clear foundation: profession, experience, reputation, or price. Q can be inferred from P and is unsurprising.","nuance":"This expresses **admiration** and is slightly more formal than 'so that's why.' It works in reviews, comments, and workplace conversations, but not for complaints.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"さすが","r":"さすが","m":"as expected"},{"t":"人気店","r":"にんきてん","m":"popular restaurant"},{"t":"だけあって","r":"だけあって","m":"as expected"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"anything"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べて","r":"たべて","m":"eat"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"美味しかった","r":"おいしかった","m":"was delicious"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As expected of a popular restaurant, everything we ate was delicious.","scene":"Reviewing a restaurant recommended by friends"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"老舗","r":"しにせ","m":"long-established brand"},{"t":"だけあって","r":"だけあって","m":"as expected"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"接客","r":"せっきゃく","m":"customer service"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"丁寧","r":"ていねい","m":"courteous"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This shop is indeed long-established; its customer service is very courteous.","scene":"Commenting after shopping"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-024||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing ～だけに","sub":"～だけあって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～だけあって","patternReading":"だけあって","coreMeaning":"Indeed ~ / Living up to the reputation or foundation of P","slug":"compare","definition":"The two patterns most often confused are ～だけに and ～だけのことはある. Key point: **だけあって** praises a good foundation; **だけに** makes Q stronger than usual, good or bad; **だけのことはある** says the effort was worthwhile.","nuance":"Both だけあって and だけに express 'P → Q,' but だけあって **does not take** negative P/Q, while だけに works in either direction. だけのことはある usually appears at the end to confirm the result.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"社長","r":"しゃちょう","m":"company president"},{"t":"だけあって","r":"だけあって","m":"as expected"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"決断","r":"けつだん","m":"decisions"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"速い","r":"はやい","m":"quick"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is indeed a company president; he makes decisions very quickly.","scene":"Praising the boss in a meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～だけに","point":"だけあって: good P → praised Q; だけに: P causes stronger Q, possibly bad. Example: プロだけに責任が重い."},{"vs":"～だけのことはある","point":"だけあって: lives up to P's reputation; だけのことはある: worth the effort. Example: 苦労しただけのことはある."}]}},"n2-024||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～だけあって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～だけあって","patternReading":"だけあって","coreMeaning":"Indeed ~ / Living up to the reputation or foundation of P","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often use だけあって with negative P/Q. In fact, **だけあって** is not used when P is a negative foundation; switch to **だけに**.","nuance":"This is a common JLPT grammar trap. If Q is negative, immediately question だけあって and consider だけに.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"長年","r":"ながねん","m":"many years"},{"t":"練習","r":"れんしゅう","m":"practice"},{"t":"した","m":"practiced"},{"t":"だけあって","r":"だけあって","m":"as expected"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ピアノ","m":"piano"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手い","r":"うまい","m":"skilled"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After practicing for years, she is indeed very good at piano.","scene":"Praising a classmate's playing"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼は失敗しただけあって、落ち込んでいる。 → ✅ 彼は失敗した**だけに**、落ち込んでいる。","❌ 彼は有名なだけあって、いつも監視されている。 → ✅ 彼は有名な**だけに**、いつも監視されている。","❌ 事故があっただけあって、みんな慎重だ。 → ✅ 事故があっただけに、みんな慎重だ。"],"compare":null}},"n2-024||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～だけあって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～だけあって","patternReading":"だけあって","coreMeaning":"Indeed ~ / Living up to the reputation or foundation of P","slug":"memory","definition":"Anchor it with **さすが～だけあって**—'a pro is a pro.' Remember だけ = 'only' and あって = 'have': only when good P exists does praiseworthy Q 'appear.' Compare だけに: it means 'THE MORE'—an intensified result.","nuance":"Don't treat it as a dry rule; remember the riddle: 'Skill brings quality.'","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-024||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～だけあって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～だけあって","patternReading":"だけあって","coreMeaning":"Indeed ~ / Living up to the reputation or foundation of P","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common JLPT set phrases: さすが～だけあって, プロだけあって, 老舗だけあって, 人気店だけあって, 長年～しただけあって.","nuance":"Register: concise and natural; often starts a positive evaluation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"さすが","r":"さすが","m":"as expected"},{"t":"プロ","m":"professional"},{"t":"だけあって","r":"だけあって","m":"as expected"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"違う","r":"ちがう","m":"different"},{"t":"ね","m":"right"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As expected of a professional, it is on another level, isn't it?","scene":"Watching a colleague work quickly and smoothly"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-025||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～だけに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～だけに","patternReading":"だけに","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~, Q is stronger than usual","slug":"core","definition":"～だけに expresses that **because P is a special, specific reason**, Q occurs at a **stronger or more noticeable level than usual**. The P–Q relationship is **proportional**: the more significant P is, the more pronounced Q becomes. Unlike だけあって, Q may have either a **positive or negative** nuance.","nuance":"This expression helps the speaker **justify or make sense of** an observation: “Since P is true, it makes sense that Q happened.” It sounds natural in speech and writing; slightly formal but not stiff. It suits comments, evaluations, and explanations. Avoid it in official notices; use につき instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"入場","r":"にゅうじょう","m":"admission"},{"t":"無料","r":"むりょう","m":"free"},{"t":"だけに","r":"だけに","m":"precisely because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会場","r":"かいじょう","m":"venue"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ものすごい","m":"extremely"},{"t":"混雑","r":"こんざつ","m":"crowded"},{"t":"だった"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because admission was free, the venue was extremely crowded.","scene":"After attending a free-entry event, you comment to friends."},{"tokens":[{"t":"長年","r":"ながねん","m":"many years"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"夢","r":"ゆめ","m":"dream"},{"t":"だった"},{"t":"だけに","r":"だけに","m":"precisely because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"した","m":"failed"},{"t":"とき","m":"when"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ショック","m":"shock"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"大きかった","r":"おおきかった","m":"great"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because it had been a lifelong dream, the shock of failing was even greater.","scene":"Failing the final interview after pursuing a dream for years."},{"tokens":[{"t":"期待","r":"きたい","m":"expectations"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"大きい","r":"おおきい","m":"great"},{"t":"だけに","r":"だけに","m":"precisely because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"プレッシャー","m":"pressure"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"大きい","r":"おおきい","m":"great"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because expectations are high, the pressure is high too.","scene":"Talking with a friend about a new assignment."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-025||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & connecting forms","sub":"～だけに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～だけに","patternReading":"だけに","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~, Q is stronger than usual","slug":"forms","definition":"Before ～だけに, use a **basic form without a complex particle**. With **V** and **い-A**, attach it directly. With **な-A**, use **な** (e.g. 有名なだけに). With **N**, attach it directly (e.g. プロだけに). Past and negative forms are also possible: だっただけに, なかっただけに.","nuance":"There is no special honorific form; for politeness, keep だけに and put the following clause in です/ます form. だけに never attaches directly to です/ます in the preceding clause as ～ですから does.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"V + だけに","note":"Vる・Vた・Vなかった are all possible"},{"lhs":"いA","rhs":"いA + だけに","note":"例：難しいだけに"},{"lhs":"なA","rhs":"なAな + だけに","note":"例：有名なだけに"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"N + だけに","note":"例：プロだけに"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"プロ","m":"professional"},{"t":"だけに","r":"だけに","m":"precisely because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"責任","r":"せきにん","m":"responsibility"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"重い","r":"おもい","m":"heavy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because they are professionals, their responsibility is heavy too.","scene":"A manager discusses the company’s professional team."},{"tokens":[{"t":"難しい","r":"むずかしい","m":"difficult"},{"t":"だけに","r":"だけに","m":"precisely because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"やりがい","m":"rewarding"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"ある"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because it is difficult, it is even more rewarding.","scene":"Considering whether to accept a difficult project."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-025||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"～だけに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～だけに","patternReading":"だけに","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~, Q is stronger than usual","slug":"usage","definition":"～だけに most often appears in three types of reasoning: **(1)** P is an objective situation (free entry, the final day, being a professional) → Q becomes understandable or natural; **(2)** P is an expectation or effort → Q is a fitting joy or value; **(3)** P is something the speaker values → a negative Q causes greater regret or shock. In short: **precisely because P, Q cannot help being at that level**.","nuance":"The listener senses **reasonable inference**, not merely a subjective exclamation. In writing, だけに often appears in essays and sports reports; in conversation, use it to emphasize a reason politely rather than forcefully.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"最終日","r":"さいしゅうび","m":"final day"},{"t":"だけに","r":"だけに","m":"precisely because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会場","r":"かいじょう","m":"venue"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"とても"},{"t":"混んで","r":"こんで","m":"crowded"},{"t":"いた"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because it was the final day, the venue was very crowded.","scene":"Returning after the exhibition’s final day."},{"tokens":[{"t":"期待","r":"きたい","m":"expectations"},{"t":"していた","m":"had expected"},{"t":"だけに","r":"だけに","m":"precisely because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"成功","r":"せいこう","m":"success"},{"t":"した","m":"succeeded"},{"t":"時","r":"とき","m":"when"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"嬉しさ","r":"うれしさ","m":"joy"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"ひとしお","m":"especially great"},{"t":"だった"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because I had high hopes, the joy of succeeding was even greater.","scene":"Recalling the joy of reaching a long-held goal."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-025||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing だけあって・からこそ","sub":"～だけに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～だけに","patternReading":"だけに","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~, Q is stronger than usual","slug":"compare","definition":"In this batch, ～だけに is often confused with ～だけあって and ～からこそ. The key distinction: **だけに describes the DEGREE of the result**, **だけあって describes ADMIRATION or deservingness**, while **からこそ emphasizes the UNIQUE cause**.","nuance":"To praise someone as “a true professional—no wonder they are skilled,” Japanese uses だけあって; だけに sounds more like the responsibility is heavier. To stress “the project succeeded only because of him,” use からこそ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"プロ","m":"professional"},{"t":"だけに","r":"だけに","m":"precisely because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ミス","m":"mistakes"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"許されない","r":"ゆるされない","m":"not tolerated"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because they are professionals, mistakes are not tolerated.","scene":"Colleagues discuss the pressure of working with skilled professionals."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～だけあって","point":"だけあって = ADMIRATION: the result matches P’s reputation, and Q is usually GOOD. だけに = Q is STRONGER because of P, and Q may be BAD. Example: 彼はプロだけあって、仕事が上手い。 / プロだけに、責任も重い。"},{"vs":"～からこそ","point":"からこそ = P is the UNIQUE CAUSE of Q. だけに = P makes Q more pronounced than usual; it does not claim that “only P could cause Q.” Example: プロだからこそできる仕事だ。"}]}},"n2-025||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Errors & traps for Vietnamese learners","sub":"～だけに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～だけに","patternReading":"だけに","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~, Q is stronger than usual","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"A classic error for Vietnamese learners is **using だけに instead of だけあって for praise**, and **using だけに in official notices**. Also, do not turn だけに into “the unique cause”; that is the role of からこそ.","nuance":"Remember these three checkpoints. When writing, ask: Am I **praising** → だけあって; **making an official notice** → につき; saying **Q happened only because of P** → からこそ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"プロ","m":"professional"},{"t":"だけに","r":"だけに","m":"precisely because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ミス","m":"mistakes"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"許されない","r":"ゆるされない","m":"not tolerated"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Precisely because they are professionals, mistakes are not tolerated.","scene":"Everyone understands the pressure of being a professional."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼はプロだけに、仕事が上手い。（praise） → ✅ 彼はプロだけあって、仕事が上手い。","❌ 入場無料だけに、閉場は5時です。 → ✅ 入場無料につき、閉場は5時です。（use につき in official notices）","❌ あなたがいただけに、この仕事を任せたい。 → ✅ あなたがいたからこそ、この仕事を任せたい。（unique cause）"],"compare":null}},"n2-025||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～だけに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～だけに","patternReading":"だけに","coreMeaning":"Precisely because ~, Q is stronger than usual","slug":"memory","definition":"Break だけに into **だけ (that exact level) + に (toward)**. Picture two connected vessels: as the level of P rises, the level of Q automatically rises too. The formula is: **“Precisely because P is at that level, Q is at that level too.”**","nuance":"Build this reflex: when you see だけに, silently think “so the degree is also...”. Do not praise and do not identify a unique cause; simply measure the correlation.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-026||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ばかりか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばかりか","patternReading":"ばかりか","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ but also (B is more surprising than A)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ばかりか means **not only A but also B**, where **B is more surprising or notable than A**. It emphasizes that something exceeds expectations. Unlike the neutral だけでなく, ばかりか always carries the nuance of “and even...!”","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, especially in news, speeches, and product reviews. It has a semi-formal tone. Clause B often uses まで, さえ, or も for extra emphasis.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"ばかりか","r":"ばかりか","m":"not only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"中国語","r":"ちゅうごくご","m":"Chinese"},{"t":"まで","r":"まで","m":"even"},{"t":"話せる","r":"はなせる","m":"can speak"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He can speak not only Japanese but even Chinese.","scene":"Describing a talented colleague who surprises everyone."},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"製品","r":"せいひん","m":"product"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"安い","r":"やすい","m":"cheap"},{"t":"ばかりか","r":"ばかりか","m":"not only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"品質","r":"ひんしつ","m":"quality"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"優れている","r":"すぐれている","m":"excellent"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This product is not only cheap but also excellent in quality.","scene":"A product review on a shopping site."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-026||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～ばかりか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばかりか","patternReading":"ばかりか","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ but also (B is more surprising than A)","slug":"forms","definition":"Connection patterns: plain-form V + ばかりか, い-A + ばかりか, な-A + な/である + ばかりか, and N + ばかりか. Clause B often combines with まで, さえ, or も.","nuance":"Do not use the polite form (ます). It is commonly used with qualities and evaluations, and less often with completed actions.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（plain form）","rhs":"ばかりか","note":"Verbs: できる、勉強する"},{"lhs":"い-A","rhs":"ばかりか","note":"安い、高い、美味しい"},{"lhs":"な-A + な/である","rhs":"ばかりか","note":"便利な、親切な、きれいな"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"ばかりか","note":"日本語、彼、国内"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"歌","r":"うた","m":"singing"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手い","r":"うまい","m":"good"},{"t":"ばかりか","r":"ばかりか","m":"not only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"踊り","r":"おどり","m":"dancing"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"プロ並み","r":"プロなみ","m":"professional-level"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She is not only a good singer but also dances at a professional level.","scene":"Praising a talented singer."},{"tokens":[{"t":"新型","r":"しんがた","m":"new type"},{"t":"ウイルス","m":"virus"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"国内","r":"こくない","m":"domestically"},{"t":"ばかりか","r":"ばかりか","m":"not only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"海外","r":"かいがい","m":"overseas"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"広がった","r":"ひろがった","m":"spread"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The new virus spread not only domestically but overseas as well.","scene":"A sentence from a news report."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-026||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～ばかりか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばかりか","patternReading":"ばかりか","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ but also (B is more surprising than A)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this when A is already notable but B appears as an additional, unexpected development. Typical contexts include praise, evaluation, and reports of something spreading.","nuance":"B must be **new and surprising**. If B is not more notable than A, use だけでなく. It often combines with まで, さえ, or も to create a climax.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"レストラン","m":"restaurant"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"安い","r":"やすい","m":"cheap"},{"t":"ばかりか","r":"ばかりか","m":"not only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"雰囲気","r":"ふんいき","m":"atmosphere"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This restaurant is not only cheap but also has a nice atmosphere.","scene":"Recommending a restaurant and highlighting its advantages."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"できる","m":"capable"},{"t":"ばかりか","r":"ばかりか","m":"not only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"人柄","r":"ひとがら","m":"personality"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"素晴らしい","r":"すばらしい","m":"wonderful"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is not only good at his job but also has a wonderful personality.","scene":"A coworker praising a former manager."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"cooking"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"good"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"ばかりか","r":"ばかりか","m":"not only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"掃除","r":"そうじ","m":"cleaning"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"得意","r":"とくい","m":"skilled at"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She is not only good at cooking but also skilled at cleaning.","scene":"Praising a capable friend."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-026||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing nearby patterns","sub":"～ばかりか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばかりか","patternReading":"ばかりか","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ but also (B is more surprising than A)","slug":"compare","definition":"Quick comparison: **ばかりか** = surprise, with B exceeding A. **だけでなく** = neutral listing. **のみならず** = formal, written style. **どころか** = denies A or means “A is nothing compared with B.”","nuance":"Choose ばかりか when you want to surprise the listener. Use だけでなく for simple addition. のみならず is more formal and suits academic contexts. どころか often means “not A but B” or “A is nothing compared with B.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"英語","r":"えいご","m":"English"},{"t":"ばかりか","r":"ばかりか","m":"not only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"フランス語","r":"フランスご","m":"French"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"話せる","r":"はなせる","m":"can speak"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She can speak not only English but also French.","scene":"During a job interview."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜だけでなく","point":"〜だけでなく is neutral: “not only...but also”; 〜ばかりか emphasizes that B is more surprising than A. Example: 彼は日本語だけでなく、中国語も話せる (neutral) vs 彼は日本語ばかりか、中国語まで話せる (surprising)."},{"vs":"〜のみならず","point":"〜のみならず is formal and official written language; 〜ばかりか is less formal and also works in speech."},{"vs":"〜どころか","point":"〜どころか can deny clause A (“not only not A, but B”), while 〜ばかりか accepts A and adds B."}]}},"n2-026||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～ばかりか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばかりか","patternReading":"ばかりか","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ but also (B is more surprising than A)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes: (1) using contradictory A and B; (2) using the pattern when B is not surprising; (3) omitting まで, さえ, or も for emphasis. Check that A and B point in the same direction and that B is more surprising than A.","nuance":"Learners often translate “not only” mechanically and forget to check whether A and B share the same positive or negative quality. Also remember that ばかりか is not used with the polite form, and B often adds まで, さえ, or も for emphasis. For simple listing, choose だけでなく.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"頭","r":"あたま","m":"mind"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いい","m":"smart"},{"t":"ばかりか","r":"ばかりか","m":"not only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"スポーツ","m":"sports"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"できる","m":"good at"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is not only smart but also good at sports.","scene":"Describing an all-around student."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼は背が高いばかりか、低い。 → ✅ 彼は背が高いが、低くない。 (A and B conflict, so do not use ばかりか)","❌ この店は安いばかりか、普通だ。 → ✅ この店は安いだけでなく、普通だ。 (B is not more surprising; use だけでなく)","❌ 彼は頭がいいばかりか、優しい。 → ✅ 彼は頭がいいばかりか、心も美しい。 (adding まで/さえ/も strengthens the emphasis)","❌ 彼女は料理が上手なばかりか、歌も下手だ。 → ✅ 彼女は料理が上手なばかりか、歌も上手だ。 (A and B must point in the same direction; 下手 is opposite)"],"compare":null}},"n2-026||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～ばかりか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばかりか","patternReading":"ばかりか","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ but also (B is more surprising than A)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: ばかり = only, か = rhetorical question. Read it as: “Is A all there is? No, there is B too!” → remember that it goes beyond A.","nuance":"Imagine the listener’s eyes widening when they learn that B goes even further than A.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-026||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～ばかりか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばかりか","patternReading":"ばかりか","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ but also (B is more surprising than A)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases: 〜ばかりか、〜まで / 〜さえ / 〜も. Example: “日本語ばかりか、英語まで” — a frequent JLPT test pattern.","nuance":"These combinations create rhythm and emphasis and are especially suitable for written language.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"ばかりか","r":"ばかりか","m":"not only"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"英語","r":"えいご","m":"English"},{"t":"まで","r":"まで","m":"even"},{"t":"できる","r":"できる","m":"can speak"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He can speak not only Japanese but even English.","scene":"Describing unexpectedly impressive language skills."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-027||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～に際して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に際して","patternReading":"にさいして","coreMeaning":"On the occasion of ~ (formal, important event)","slug":"core","definition":"〜に際して means **on the occasion of / at the time of** an **important, formal event**. It emphasizes that action Q takes place **right at that solemn, official moment**. It is not used for everyday situations.","nuance":"**Very formal**, used almost exclusively in writing, speeches, official notices, and business correspondence. Compared with にあたって, it focuses more on the **moment** of the event than on preparation. It conveys solemnity and respect.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"卒業","r":"そつぎょう","m":"graduation"},{"t":"に際して","r":"にさいして","m":"on the occasion of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"担任","r":"たんにん","m":"homeroom"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"感謝","r":"かんしゃ","m":"thanks"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"言葉","r":"ことば","m":"words"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"伝えた","r":"つたえた","m":"expressed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the occasion of graduation, I expressed my thanks to my homeroom teacher.","scene":"Thanking a teacher on graduation day."},{"tokens":[{"t":"式典","r":"しきてん","m":"ceremony"},{"t":"に際し","r":"にさいし","m":"on the occasion of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"代表者","r":"だいひょうしゃ","m":"representative"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"挨拶","r":"あいさつ","m":"greeting"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"述べた","r":"のべた","m":"gave"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"At the ceremony, the representative delivered a welcome address.","scene":"A formal news report or article."},{"tokens":[{"t":"この度","r":"このたび","m":"this time"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"転勤","r":"てんきん","m":"transfer"},{"t":"に際して","r":"にさいして","m":"on the occasion of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"お世話","r":"おせわ","m":"support"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なった"},{"t":"皆様","r":"みなさま","m":"everyone"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"感謝","r":"かんしゃ","m":"thanks"},{"t":"申し上げます","r":"もうしあげます","m":"offer my thanks"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the occasion of my transfer, I would like to thank everyone who supported me.","scene":"A farewell email to colleagues after a transfer."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-027||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～に際して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に際して","patternReading":"にさいして","coreMeaning":"On the occasion of ~ (formal, important event)","slug":"forms","definition":"〜に際して combines directly with an event noun or a verb in the dictionary or た form. **に際し** is a shortened form with て omitted, used in more formal writing.","nuance":"It is not used with the ない form, ている form, or adjectives. With verbs, it commonly follows verbs such as 卒業する, 出発する, and 退職する. This is **formal written language**, not everyday conversation.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"N + に際して / に際し","note":"Example: 式典に際して、開会の辞を述べる"},{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"V（辞書形） + に際して","note":"Example: 卒業するに際して、..."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"本社","r":"ほんしゃ","m":"head office"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"転勤","r":"てんきん","m":"transfer"},{"t":"に際して","r":"にさいして","m":"on the occasion of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"チーム","m":"team"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"挨拶","r":"あいさつ","m":"greet"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the occasion of my transfer to headquarters, I said goodbye to the whole team.","scene":"Sharing this with colleagues at a farewell party."},{"tokens":[{"t":"新商品","r":"しんしょうひん","m":"new product"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"発売","r":"はつばい","m":"release"},{"t":"に際し","r":"にさいし","m":"on the occasion of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"記者会見","r":"きしゃかいけん","m":"press conference"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開かれた","r":"ひらかれた","m":"was held"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the occasion of the new product's release, a press conference was held.","scene":"An internal company event report."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-027||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～に際して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に際して","patternReading":"にさいして","coreMeaning":"On the occasion of ~ (formal, important event)","slug":"usage","definition":"Typical situations for 〜に際して include **opening or closing remarks**, **book prefaces**, **official notices**, and **event thank-you messages**. All involve a **major or solemn milestone**.","nuance":"It can be used in speech when speaking at a ceremony, but sounds very formal. It may suit an important business email, but not a chat or conversation with friends.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"結婚","r":"けっこん","m":"marriage"},{"t":"に際して","r":"にさいして","m":"on the occasion of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"両親","r":"りょうしん","m":"parents"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"感謝","r":"かんしゃ","m":"thanks"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"手紙","r":"てがみ","m":"letter"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書いた","r":"かいた","m":"wrote"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the occasion of my marriage, I wrote a thank-you letter to my parents.","scene":"Telling a friend about something done on one's wedding day."},{"tokens":[{"t":"商品","r":"しょうひん","m":"product"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"リニューアル","m":"renewal"},{"t":"に際して","r":"にさいして","m":"on the occasion of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"お客様","r":"おきゃくさま","m":"customers"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"説明会","r":"せつめいかい","m":"briefing"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"行った","r":"おこなった","m":"held"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the occasion of the product renewal, we held a briefing for customers.","scene":"Speaking at an internal company meeting."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-027||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with similar patterns","sub":"～に際して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に際して","patternReading":"にさいして","coreMeaning":"On the occasion of ~ (formal, important event)","slug":"compare","definition":"Three easily confused patterns: **にあたって** (on the occasion of, emphasizing preparation), **に先立って** (before the main event), and **を機に** (using an opportunity for later change).","nuance":"For test questions, identify the timing: if the action occurs **before** the event → に先立って; if it is **preparation** → にあたって; if it occurs **at the event** → に際して; if it marks a **lasting turning point** → を機に.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"退職","r":"たいしょく","m":"retirement"},{"t":"に際して","r":"にさいして","m":"on the occasion of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"皆さん","r":"みなさん","m":"everyone"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"ご挨拶","r":"ごあいさつ","m":"greetings"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"申し上げました","r":"もうしあげました","m":"offered"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the occasion of my retirement, I said farewell to everyone.","scene":"Speaking at a farewell party for colleagues."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"にあたって","point":"にあたって = BEFORE/WHEN preparing (e.g., 卒業にあたって準備する — prepare before graduation). に際して = AT the event (e.g., 卒業に際して挨拶する — speak at graduation)."},{"vs":"に先立って","point":"に先立って = BEFORE the main event, with a clear sequence. に際して = AT THE SAME TIME. Example: 式典に先立って会場を確認する vs 式典に際して挨拶する."},{"vs":"を機に","point":"を機に = an OPPORTUNITY/TURNING POINT leading to lasting change. に際して = an action AT that occasion, with no lasting implication. Example: 結婚を機に引っ越した vs 結婚に際して式を挙げた."}]}},"n2-027||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～に際して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に際して","patternReading":"にさいして","coreMeaning":"On the occasion of ~ (formal, important event)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often use に際して as a general way to say “on the occasion of.” But it is **very formal**; using it for trivial tasks sounds comical. Also avoid confusing it with **にあたって** when the action is preparation.","nuance":"In everyday conversation, use **時**, **ながら**, or **こと**; reserve に際して for speeches and formal letters.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"開会","r":"かいかい","m":"opening"},{"t":"に際し","r":"にさいし","m":"on the occasion of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"一言","r":"ひとこと","m":"a few words"},{"t":"ご挨拶","r":"ごあいさつ","m":"greetings"},{"t":"申し上げます","r":"もうしあげます","m":"would like to offer"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the occasion of the opening, I would like to say a few words.","scene":"Opening a speech at a conference."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 朝ごはんに際して、新聞を読む。 → ✅ 朝ごはんを食べながら、新聞を読む。 (Use ながら, not に際して)","❌ 会議に際して、資料をコピーした。（preparation） → ✅ 会議にあたって、資料をコピーした。","❌ 明日の試験に際して、頑張る。 → ✅ 明日の試験に向けて、頑張る。","❌ 買い物に際して、財布を忘れた。 → ✅ 買い物のときに、財布を忘れた。"],"compare":null}},"n2-027||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～に際して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に際して","patternReading":"にさいして","coreMeaning":"On the occasion of ~ (formal, important event)","slug":"memory","definition":"際 in 実際 and 国際 means “border, boundary, or moment.” Imagine yourself **standing at the threshold** of a major event: an opening or graduation. に際して means “at that threshold.”","nuance":"Picture an MC speaking onstage: 式典に際し, followed by an opening speech. The more you say it aloud, the more you remember its formal tone.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-027||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"～に際して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に際して","patternReading":"にさいして","coreMeaning":"On the occasion of ~ (formal, important event)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common expressions in tests and real documents include **式典に際して**, **卒業に際して**, **この度の〜に際して**, and **出版に際して**. Phrases with **に際し** often pair with **一言挨拶を申し上げます**.","nuance":"These phrases appear in speeches, prefaces, and formal emails. When you see them in a reading passage, look at the noun before them: it is always an official event.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この度","r":"このたび","m":"this time"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"出版","r":"しゅっぱん","m":"publication"},{"t":"に際して","r":"にさいして","m":"on the occasion of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"多くの","r":"おおくの","m":"many"},{"t":"方々","r":"かたがた","m":"people"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"支えて","r":"ささえて","m":"supporting"},{"t":"いただきました","r":"いただきました","m":"received"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the occasion of this publication, I received support from many people.","scene":"Acknowledgments at the beginning of a book or report."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-028||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～かたわら · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かたわら","patternReading":"かたわら","coreMeaning":"While doing ~, also doing ~ (two long-term parallel activities)","slug":"core","definition":"～かたわら describes **two activities carried out in parallel**. A is the main activity (such as a career, studies, or primary role) and takes up most of the person's time, while B is a secondary but **notable** activity. Both are **long-term roles** that recur over time, not temporary tasks.","nuance":"The pattern has a **formal, written** (semi-formal) tone. It often highlights the subject's **versatility or admirable abilities** (doing A while also doing B). It is not used in everyday conversation or for two small activities done at once.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"会社員","r":"かいしゃいん","m":"company employee"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"かたわら","m":"alongside"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"小説","r":"しょうせつ","m":"novels"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書いて","r":"かいて","m":"write"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He works for a company while also writing novels.","scene":"Introducing a versatile person in a profile or article"},{"tokens":[{"t":"大学","r":"だいがく","m":"university"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"教える","r":"おしえる","m":"teach"},{"t":"かたわら","m":"alongside"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"自分","r":"じぶん","m":"my own"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"研究","r":"けんきゅう","m":"research"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"続けている","r":"つづけている","m":"continue"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"While teaching at a university, I continue my own research.","scene":"A professor describing parallel work"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-028||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～かたわら · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かたわら","patternReading":"かたわら","coreMeaning":"While doing ~, also doing ~ (two long-term parallel activities)","slug":"forms","definition":"～かたわら attaches to a **dictionary-form verb** or **noun + の**. It does not change for tense or negation; the timing of the action is shown at the end of the sentence.","nuance":"This is a **formal written pattern**. You must use the **plain dictionary form**, not Vて or Vた. With nouns, の must come before かたわら.","structure":[{"lhs":"V","rhs":"辞書形＋かたわら","note":"働くかたわら, 教えるかたわら"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"の＋かたわら","note":"会社員のかたわら, 子育てのかたわら"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"主婦","r":"しゅふ","m":"homemaker"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"かたわら","m":"alongside"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"地域","r":"ちいき","m":"local"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"NPO","m":"nonprofit organization"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"活動","r":"かつどう","m":"activities"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"While working as a homemaker, she also works with a local NPO.","scene":"Interviewing a versatile woman"},{"tokens":[{"t":"大学院","r":"だいがくいん","m":"graduate school"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"研究","r":"けんきゅう","m":"research"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"かたわら","m":"alongside"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"アルバイト","m":"part-time work"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"While conducting graduate research, I also work part-time.","scene":"Describing a hardworking graduate student"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-028||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～かたわら · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かたわら","patternReading":"かたわら","coreMeaning":"While doing ~, also doing ~ (two long-term parallel activities)","slug":"usage","definition":"～かたわら describes **one person who handles a main job or role (A) while also pursuing a notable secondary activity (B)**. Typical contexts include **résumés, character profiles, and professional introduction emails**.","nuance":"Register: **semi-formal**; it is not used in chats or casual conversation. For two small activities done at the same time, use ～ながら or ～しつつ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"子育て","r":"こそだて","m":"child-rearing"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"かたわら","m":"alongside"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"通信講座","r":"つうしんこうざ","m":"distance course"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"資格","r":"しかく","m":"qualification"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"取った","r":"とった","m":"earned"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"While raising children, I earned a qualification through a distance course.","scene":"A mother sharing an achievement on her résumé"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"会社員","r":"かいしゃいん","m":"company employee"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"かたわら","m":"alongside"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"作家","r":"さっか","m":"writer"},{"t":"として","m":"as"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"活躍","r":"かつやく","m":"be active"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He works as a company employee while also pursuing a career as a writer.","scene":"An article introducing someone with two careers"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-028||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"～かたわら · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かたわら","patternReading":"かたわら","coreMeaning":"While doing ~, also doing ~ (two long-term parallel activities)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish ～かたわら from other patterns meaning “while doing.” The key differences are **duration** and **register**.","nuance":"～かたわら emphasizes **two long-term parallel roles**; ～ながら is for **two activities happening simultaneously over a short time**; ～つつ is also written but often carries a sense of **contrast or regret**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"会社員","r":"かいしゃいん","m":"company employee"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"かたわら","m":"alongside"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"小説","r":"しょうせつ","m":"novels"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書いて","r":"かいて","m":"write"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He works for a company while also writing novels.","scene":"Introducing two long-term roles"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～ながら","point":"ながら is for 2 small activities done at once, briefly, in casual speech: 歩きながら電話する。 / かたわら is for 2 long-term roles in written language."},{"vs":"～つつ","point":"つつ also means “while doing” and is written, but often emphasizes contrast or regret (分かりつつもやめられない). / かたわら has no regretful nuance."}]}},"n2-028||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～かたわら · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かたわら","patternReading":"かたわら","coreMeaning":"While doing ~, also doing ~ (two long-term parallel activities)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes learners make with ～かたわら are **using it for two small activities done at once** or **using it in spoken language**.","nuance":"Remember: かたわら is used when **one side is a long-term primary role**, while ながら is used for **brief simultaneous actions**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"大学","r":"だいがく","m":"university"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"教える","r":"おしえる","m":"teach"},{"t":"かたわら","m":"alongside"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"翻訳","r":"ほんやく","m":"translation"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"引き受けている","r":"ひきうけている","m":"take on"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"While teaching at a university, she also takes on translation work.","scene":"A lecturer who also works as a translator"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 音楽を聞くかたわら、宿題をした。 → ✅ 音楽を聞き**ながら**、宿題をした。","❌ テレビを見るかたわら、ご飯を食べた。 → ✅ テレビを見**ながら**、ご飯を食べた。","❌ 彼は会社員のかたわら、昼寝をした。 → ✅ 彼は会社員のかたわら、英語の講師をしている。"],"compare":null}},"n2-028||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～かたわら · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かたわら","patternReading":"かたわら","coreMeaning":"While doing ~, also doing ~ (two long-term parallel activities)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip for ～かたわら: the kanji **傍** (beside) evokes the image of **activity B running alongside main activity A**. Think of a **versatile person** on a résumé: “doing A while also doing B.”","nuance":"Do not confuse it with ながら: かたわら = two long-term roles; ながら = two small actions done at once.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-028||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～かたわら · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かたわら","patternReading":"かたわら","coreMeaning":"While doing ~, also doing ~ (two long-term parallel activities)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common combinations with ～かたわら in tests and written texts include nouns for **careers and roles** (会社員, 主婦, 学生, 教師) and verbs for **long-term activities** (働く, 教える, 研究する).","nuance":"Often found in interview articles and professional profiles. B is commonly a creative, academic, or volunteer activity.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"作家","r":"さっか","m":"writer"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"執筆","r":"しっぴつ","m":"writing"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"かたわら","m":"alongside"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"講演","r":"こうえん","m":"lectures"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"している"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The writer writes books while also giving lectures.","scene":"Introducing a famous writer's activities"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-029||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～をよそに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をよそに","patternReading":"をよそに","coreMeaning":"Ignoring or disregarding others' feelings and expectations","slug":"core","definition":"〜をよそに describes someone disregarding or ignoring other people's concerns, expectations, or opposition and acting as they wish. The key point: the noun before the pattern must be a person's feeling or attitude, such as 心配, 反対, or 批判.","nuance":"The tone is mildly critical or neutral, never openly praising. Register: semi-formal written Japanese, common in journalism, commentary, and narratives. In daily conversation, ～かまわず is more natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"親","r":"おや","m":"parents"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"心配","r":"しんぱい","m":"concern"},{"t":"をよそに","r":"をよそに","m":"ignoring"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"海外","r":"かいがい","m":"overseas"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"旅立った","r":"たびだった","m":"set off"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Ignoring his parents' concerns, he set off overseas.","scene":"A young man studies abroad despite his parents' worries."},{"tokens":[{"t":"周囲","r":"しゅうい","m":"those around her"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"反対","r":"はんたい","m":"opposition"},{"t":"をよそに","r":"をよそに","m":"ignoring"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"起業","r":"きぎょう","m":"starting a business"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"決意した","r":"けついした","m":"resolved to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Ignoring the opposition around her, she resolved to start a business.","scene":"A female entrepreneur ignores public opposition."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-029||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～をよそに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をよそに","patternReading":"をよそに","coreMeaning":"Ignoring or disregarding others' feelings and expectations","slug":"forms","definition":"Structure: **N + をよそに + [action]**. It does not attach to verbs or adjectives. Common nouns include 心配, 反対, 批判, 不安, and 周囲の目.","nuance":"The pattern itself does not change for tense or politeness; all conjugation occurs in the second clause. It is mainly used in written Japanese.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"をよそに","note":"N denotes a person's feeling or attitude."},{"lhs":"Nをよそに","rhs":"、＋ [action]","note":"The second clause describes an active action."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"世間","r":"せけん","m":"public opinion"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"批判","r":"ひはん","m":"criticism"},{"t":"をよそに","r":"をよそに","m":"ignoring"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"方針","r":"ほうしん","m":"policy"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"続けた","r":"つづけた","m":"continued"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Ignoring public criticism, the company continued that policy.","scene":"A news report covers a controversial company."},{"tokens":[{"t":"親","r":"おや","m":"parents"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"不安","r":"ふあん","m":"anxiety"},{"t":"をよそに","r":"をよそに","m":"ignoring"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"フリーター","r":"フリーター","m":"freeter"},{"t":"生活","r":"せいかつ","m":"lifestyle"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"続けている","r":"つづけている","m":"continues"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Despite his parents' anxiety, he continues his freeter lifestyle.","scene":"Someone refuses to take a regular salaried job."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-029||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～をよそに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をよそに","patternReading":"をよそに","coreMeaning":"Ignoring or disregarding others' feelings and expectations","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when someone ignores other people's **worries, opposition, or expectations** and acts anyway. Depending on context, it can suggest courage or stubbornness.","nuance":"It suits **news reports, social commentary, and semi-formal narratives**. It is acceptable in serious conversation, but かまわず is more natural in everyday speech. Do not use it for weather or objective circumstances.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"周囲","r":"しゅうい","m":"those around him"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"目","r":"め","m":"looks"},{"t":"をよそに","r":"をよそに","m":"ignoring"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"大きな","r":"おおきな","m":"loud"},{"t":"声","r":"こえ","m":"voice"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"歌い出した","r":"うたいだした","m":"started singing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Ignoring the looks of those around him, he began singing loudly.","scene":"A young man sings among the crowd at a party."},{"tokens":[{"t":"親","r":"おや","m":"parents"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"反対","r":"はんたい","m":"opposition"},{"t":"をよそに","r":"をよそに","m":"ignoring"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"夢","r":"ゆめ","m":"dream"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"追いかけた","r":"おいかけた","m":"pursued"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Despite his parents' opposition, he pursued his dream.","scene":"A young person follows their passion."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-029||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare similar patterns","sub":"～をよそに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をよそに","patternReading":"をよそに","coreMeaning":"Ignoring or disregarding others' feelings and expectations","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish it from three commonly confused patterns: **にかかわらず** (regardless of conditions or objective factors), **にもかかわらず** (despite a contrary fact), and **に反して** (contrary to an expectation or rule).","nuance":"～をよそに emphasizes **ignoring other people's feelings**, often with mild criticism. ～にかかわらず is neutral about conditions. ～にもかかわらず expresses strong concession.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"親","r":"おや","m":"parents"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"心配","r":"しんぱい","m":"concern"},{"t":"をよそに","r":"をよそに","m":"ignoring"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"離れた","r":"はなれた","m":"left"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Ignoring his parents' concerns, he left Japan.","scene":"A young man goes abroad despite his parents' worries."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～にかかわらず","point":"にかかわらず is used for conditions or circumstances such as weather or gender, not feelings. Example: 天気にかかわらず実施する。"},{"vs":"～にもかかわらず","point":"にもかかわらず describes a result contrary to a known fact and is more formal. Example: 雨にもかかわらず試合が行われた。"},{"vs":"～に反して","point":"に反して means contrary to an expectation, rule, or order. Example: 期待に反して失敗した。"}]}},"n2-029||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common pitfalls","sub":"～をよそに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をよそに","patternReading":"をよそに","coreMeaning":"Ignoring or disregarding others' feelings and expectations","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Three typical traps: using it for weather or conditions, attaching it to a verb or adjective, and putting a state rather than an action in the second clause.","nuance":"This pattern takes only nouns denoting human feelings or expectations. For weather, use にもかかわらず instead. The second clause must describe an active action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"周囲","r":"しゅうい","m":"those around her"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"反対","r":"はんたい","m":"opposition"},{"t":"をよそに","r":"をよそに","m":"ignoring"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"起業","r":"きぎょう","m":"starting a business"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Despite the opposition around her, she started a business.","scene":"A corrected example sentence."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 雨をよそに、試合が行われた。 → ✅ 雨にもかかわらず、試合が行われた。","❌ 彼が反対するをよそに、計画を進めた。 → ✅ 彼の反対をよそに、計画を進めた。","❌ 親の心配をよそに、彼は元気だ。 → ✅ 親の心配をよそに、彼は留学した。"],"compare":null}},"n2-029||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～をよそに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をよそに","patternReading":"をよそに","coreMeaning":"Ignoring or disregarding others' feelings and expectations","slug":"memory","definition":"よそ means **somewhere else or another place**. With を～に, imagine pushing someone else's worries somewhere else and leaving them there.","nuance":"～をよそに suggests that the subject **deliberately ignores** the concern. Remember: 'leave their worries somewhere else.'","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-029||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common collocations","sub":"～をよそに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をよそに","patternReading":"をよそに","coreMeaning":"Ignoring or disregarding others' feelings and expectations","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common noun phrases include **親の心配をよそに**, **周囲の反対をよそに**, and **世間の批判をよそに**. These collocations frequently appear in news articles and exam questions.","nuance":"All of these phrases come before an action performed by the subject. Memorize them to recognize the pattern quickly.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"親","r":"おや","m":"parents"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"心配","r":"しんぱい","m":"concerns"},{"t":"をよそに","r":"をよそに","m":"ignoring"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"留学","r":"りゅうがく","m":"study abroad"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"決断","r":"けつだん","m":"decision"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"made"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Despite his parents' concerns, he decided to study abroad.","scene":"Talking about a friend's decision to study abroad."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-030||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～をはじめ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をはじめ","patternReading":"をはじめ","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a representative example, implying 'and many more'.","slug":"core","definition":"〜をはじめ expresses selecting **A as the most representative example** from a large group. After A, several other items may be listed, or **'and many more'** may be implied. A must clearly represent the whole group. This pattern is used in written language, reports, and announcements, not ordinary conversation.","nuance":"It is **formal and objective**. Unlike など, this pattern emphasizes that A is representative, not just a random example. In casual speech, Japanese speakers usually use や / とか / など.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"東京","r":"とうきょう","m":"Tokyo"},{"t":"をはじめ","r":"をはじめ","m":"representing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大阪","r":"おおさか","m":"Osaka"},{"t":"や"},{"t":"名古屋","r":"なごや","m":"Nagoya"},{"t":"でも","m":"also in"},{"t":"展示会","r":"てんじかい","m":"exhibition"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開かれた","r":"ひらかれた","m":"was held"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The exhibition was held in Tokyo and other places such as Osaka and Nagoya.","scene":"In a cultural news report."},{"tokens":[{"t":"社長","r":"しゃちょう","m":"company president"},{"t":"をはじめとする","r":"をはじめとする","m":"including"},{"t":"役員","r":"やくいん","m":"executives"},{"t":"全員","r":"ぜんいん","m":"all"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"出席した","r":"しゅっせきした","m":"attended"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"All the executives, including the company president, attended the meeting.","scene":"In a company meeting report."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-030||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"～をはじめ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をはじめ","patternReading":"をはじめ","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a representative example, implying 'and many more'.","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern attaches to a **noun** and has four main forms: N + をはじめ (before a list), N + をはじめとして, N + をはじめとする + N, and N + をはじめとした + N. It is not used with verbs.","nuance":"The として / とする / とした forms are more formal and more common in writing. をはじめ used alone is often followed by a comma and an open-ended list.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"をはじめ、other examples / group","note":"Lists a representative item plus others; the list may remain open."},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"をはじめとして、...","note":"Formal; connects to a clause or list."},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"をはじめとする + N","note":"Modifies a group noun."},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"をはじめとした + N","note":"Attributive form (equivalent to をはじめとする); emphasizes a defined group."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"をはじめ","r":"をはじめ","m":"representing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"中国語","r":"ちゅうごくご","m":"Chinese"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"韓国語","r":"かんこくご","m":"Korean"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"多言語","r":"たげんご","m":"multiple languages"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"対応している","r":"たいおうしている","m":"supports"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It supports multiple languages, including Japanese, Chinese, and Korean.","scene":"Language support information on a website."},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"をはじめとした","r":"をはじめとした","m":"including"},{"t":"先進国","r":"せんしんこく","m":"developed countries"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"この"},{"t":"技術","r":"ぎじゅつ","m":"technology"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"使われている","r":"つかわれている","m":"is used"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This technology is used in developed countries, including Japan.","scene":"A sentence in a scientific article."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-030||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～をはじめ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をはじめ","patternReading":"をはじめ","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a representative example, implying 'and many more'.","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to name **one representative item** and signal that there are other similar items as well. It is common in company announcements, news reports, and speeches.","nuance":"It is not used among friends. For quick lists in everyday conversation, use や / など / とか.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"来月","r":"らいげつ","m":"next month"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"東京","r":"とうきょう","m":"Tokyo"},{"t":"をはじめ","r":"をはじめ","m":"including"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"主要都市","r":"しゅようとし","m":"major cities"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"新商品","r":"しんしょうひん","m":"new product"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"販売","r":"はんばい","m":"sell"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"予定","r":"よてい","m":"plan"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Starting next month, the new product will be sold in Tokyo and other major cities.","scene":"An internal company announcement."},{"tokens":[{"t":"環境問題","r":"かんきょうもんだい","m":"environmental issues"},{"t":"をはじめ","r":"をはじめ","m":"starting with"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"さまざまな","r":"さまざまな","m":"various"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"issues"},{"t":"について"},{"t":"話し合った","r":"はなしあった","m":"discussed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We discussed various issues, starting with environmental problems.","scene":"In a meeting."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-030||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare similar patterns","sub":"～をはじめ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をはじめ","patternReading":"をはじめ","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a representative example, implying 'and many more'.","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish をはじめ from easily confused patterns: など, や, and the として/とする forms. The key differences are **representativeness** and **formality**.","nuance":"など/や do not rank the items; をはじめ puts A first and makes it feel like the defining representative of the whole group.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"制度","r":"せいど","m":"system"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"東京","r":"とうきょう","m":"Tokyo"},{"t":"をはじめ","r":"をはじめ","m":"starting with"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"全国","r":"ぜんこく","m":"nationwide"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"広がった","r":"ひろがった","m":"spread"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This system spread nationwide, starting in Tokyo.","scene":"Discussing a new policy."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜など","point":"など gives an 'example' without emphasizing representativeness. をはじめ puts A first as the most representative item."},{"vs":"〜や","point":"や is casual and lists items equally; をはじめ is formal and gives A priority at the head of the list."},{"vs":"〜をはじめとして/とする","point":"として + clause; とする + N. 社長をはじめとして… / 社長をはじめとする役員."}]}},"n2-030||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～をはじめ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をはじめ","patternReading":"をはじめ","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a representative example, implying 'and many more'.","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner errors include using it for personal preferences, using it in casual conversation, adding the particle は, and confusing として with とする.","nuance":"This pattern requires A to be a **recognized representative**. For subjective, everyday lists, choose a simpler pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"には"},{"t":"部長","r":"ぶちょう","m":"department manager"},{"t":"をはじめ","r":"をはじめ","m":"starting with"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"全員","r":"ぜんいん","m":"everyone"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"出席した","r":"しゅっせきした","m":"attended"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Everyone attended the meeting, starting with the department manager.","scene":"Reporting on a company meeting."}],"pitfalls":["❌ りんごをはじめ、私はフルーツが好きだ。 → ✅ 私はりんごやバナナなど、フルーツが好きだ。 (Do not use をはじめ for personal preferences)","❌ 休みに映画をはじめ、ショッピングをした。 → ✅ 休みに映画を見たりショッピングをしたりした。 (Use たり/など in casual speech)","❌ 東京をはじめは、人が多い。 → ✅ 東京をはじめ、各地に人が多い。 (Do not add は after をはじめ)"],"compare":null}},"n2-030||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～をはじめ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をはじめ","patternReading":"をはじめ","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a representative example, implying 'and many more'.","slug":"memory","definition":"はじめ means **'beginning.'** をはじめ evokes a list with A at the front, followed by the remaining items. Remember: 'A is most representative — をはじめ'.","nuance":"When writing a report and you want to suggest a complete list without naming every item, use this pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-030||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common fixed phrases","sub":"～をはじめ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をはじめ","patternReading":"をはじめ","coreMeaning":"Use ~ as a representative example, implying 'and many more'.","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases include: 東京をはじめ、各地で...; 社長をはじめとする役員; 日本をはじめとした先進国; 環境問題をはじめ、さまざまな問題.","nuance":"Place names and job titles appear especially often; learning these as chunks makes them easier to recognize.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"製品","r":"せいひん","m":"product"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"東京","r":"とうきょう","m":"Tokyo"},{"t":"をはじめ","r":"をはじめ","m":"including"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"全国","r":"ぜんこく","m":"nationwide"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"販売","r":"はんばい","m":"sold"},{"t":"されている","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This product is sold nationwide, starting in Tokyo.","scene":"In an advertisement or announcement."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-031||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～からには · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～からには","patternReading":"からには","coreMeaning":"Once P, Q must follow / Duty from an established fact","slug":"core","definition":"～からには expresses the logic “once P, you must Q.” P is an established fact or firm decision, and Q is the unavoidable duty or resolve that follows. With 参加したからには, the speaker treats having participated as something they cannot back out of. This pattern is often used for self-commitment: once you accept or promise something, you cannot do it half-heartedly.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, with a strong, decisive tone. Unlike 〜から / 〜ので, it is not merely a reason; it links doing Q to the responsibility of living up to P. Its force is close to “once P, you definitely have to Q,” not a guess.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"引き受けた","r":"ひきうけた","m":"accepted"},{"t":"からには","r":"からには","m":"once... must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"最後まで","r":"さいごまで","m":"to the end"},{"t":"やり遂げる","r":"やりとげる","m":"finish"},{"t":"つもり","m":"intention"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once I accept it, I intend to see it through to the end.","scene":"You accept a task and commit to taking responsibility."},{"tokens":[{"t":"約束した","r":"やくそくした","m":"promised"},{"t":"からには","r":"からには","m":"once... must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"守らなければならない","r":"まもらなければならない","m":"must keep"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once you make a promise, you must keep it.","scene":"A classic statement about keeping one's word."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-031||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～からには · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～からには","patternReading":"からには","coreMeaning":"Once P, Q must follow / Duty from an established fact","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is P + からには + Q. P before からには is always in the plain form (普通形). Use V-た when P is a committed or completed action, the dictionary form when P is about to happen, and N + である when P describes a status or identity.","nuance":"Q often combines with expressions of intention such as 〜つもりだ, 〜たい, 〜べきだ, and 〜なければならない. Do not use からには after the polite form (〜ます).","structure":[{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"＋ からには、Q","note":"P is an established or completed fact: 参加したからには"},{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"＋ からには、Q","note":"P is about to happen and shows resolve: やるからには"},{"lhs":"Nである","rhs":"＋ からには、Q","note":"P is a status or identity: 学生であるからには"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"である"},{"t":"からには","r":"からには","m":"once... must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"studying"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"第一に","r":"だいいちに","m":"as a top priority"},{"t":"考える","r":"かんがえる","m":"consider"},{"t":"べきだ","m":"should"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once you are a student, you should make studying your top priority.","scene":"A parent reminds a child of a student's role."},{"tokens":[{"t":"やる","m":"do"},{"t":"からには","r":"からには","m":"once... must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"絶対に","r":"ぜったいに","m":"definitely"},{"t":"成功させたい","r":"せいこうさせたい","m":"want to succeed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If I am going to do it, I want to succeed no matter what.","scene":"Showing resolve before starting a project."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-031||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"～からには · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～からには","patternReading":"からには","coreMeaning":"Once P, Q must follow / Duty from an established fact","slug":"usage","definition":"～からには commonly appears in three situations: (1) making a personal resolution before taking on a major task; (2) reminding someone of a duty after a commitment has been established; and (3) stating a firm position in a speech or written argument.","nuance":"The speaker uses it to stress that there is “no turning back.” Q is therefore usually a positive, determined action. Avoid using it with an uncontrollable event that already happened in the past.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来た","r":"きた","m":"came"},{"t":"からには","r":"からには","m":"once... must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"マスターしたい","m":"want to master"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Now that I have come to Japan, I want to master Japanese.","scene":"A student shares a goal after arriving in Japan."},{"tokens":[{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"match"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"出る","r":"でる","m":"enter"},{"t":"からには","r":"からには","m":"once... must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"全力で","r":"ぜんりょくで","m":"with all one's might"},{"t":"戦う","r":"たたかう","m":"fight"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once I enter the match, I have to give it my all.","scene":"An athlete speaks before a match."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-031||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"～からには · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～からには","patternReading":"からには","coreMeaning":"Once P, Q must follow / Duty from an established fact","slug":"compare","definition":"～以上は and ～上は are close in meaning to ～からには, but they differ in formality and nuance. ～からには is the most natural choice in everyday conversation, while ～以上は and ～上は are more formal.","nuance":"～からには is natural in daily speech; ～以上は is more formal and common in notices or speeches; ～上は has a written, somewhat old-fashioned tone and is very formal.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"引き受けた","r":"ひきうけた","m":"accepted"},{"t":"からには","r":"からには","m":"once... must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"全力で","r":"ぜんりょくで","m":"with all one's might"},{"t":"やる","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once I accept it, I will give it my all.","scene":"A sentence compared with 以上は / 上は."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～以上は","point":"More formal; emphasizes that P is an undeniable established fact."},{"vs":"～上は","point":"Highly formal and written, almost a fixed expression."}]}},"n2-031||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～からには · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～からには","patternReading":"からには","coreMeaning":"Once P, Q must follow / Duty from an established fact","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The three most common errors are using からには with an uncertain hypothetical, making Q something other than a duty or resolve, and using the wrong noun form.","nuance":"Check two things before using it: Is P an established fact or settled decision? Is Q an action the speaker is committed or obligated to do? If P is uncertain, use 〜なら / 〜たら instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"参加した","r":"さんかした","m":"participated"},{"t":"からには","r":"からには","m":"once... must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"全力","r":"ぜんりょく","m":"full effort"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"尽くす","r":"つくす","m":"give one's all"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once I have entered, I will give it my all.","scene":"Reminding yourself to try hard after signing up."}],"pitfalls":["❌ もし参加するからには、頑張る。 → ✅ もし参加するなら、頑張る。 / ✅ 参加したからには、頑張る。","❌ 来たからには、昨日は雨が降った。 → ✅ 来たからには、しっかり観光する。","❌ 学生だからには、勉強する。 → ✅ 学生であるからには、勉強する。"],"compare":null}},"n2-031||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"～からには · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～からには","patternReading":"からには","coreMeaning":"Once P, Q must follow / Duty from an established fact","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tip: から = because, には = then. Together: “once P, you must Q.” Imagine stepping into a situation: once you are committed, you must keep going until the end.","nuance":"からには conveys an “all-in commitment.” Use it to create positive pressure for yourself or remind someone else of their responsibility.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-031||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common fixed phrases","sub":"～からには · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～からには","patternReading":"からには","coreMeaning":"Once P, Q must follow / Duty from an established fact","slug":"phrases","definition":"The phrases N/V + からには appear often in exams and conversation: 引き受けたからには, 約束したからには, 日本に来たからには, and 学生であるからには.","nuance":"Each phrase connects to a strong Q such as やり遂げる, 守る, マスターする, or 頑張る. Learning them as chunks sounds more natural than assembling each verb separately.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"約束した","r":"やくそくした","m":"promised"},{"t":"からには","r":"からには","m":"once... must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"守る","r":"まもる","m":"keep"},{"t":"べきだ","m":"should"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once you make a promise, you should keep it.","scene":"Advising someone to honor a commitment."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-032||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ことなく · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことなく","patternReading":"ことなく","coreMeaning":"Without (ever) ~; while continuing Q without ~ (written style)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ことなく means **not doing P at all while Q still occurs**. Unlike ordinary negation, this pattern emphasizes **no exceptions** and **persistence/continuity**: P does not occur throughout the process of Q. It is a formal written form equivalent to 〜ずに / 〜ないで, but more emotionally expressive.","nuance":"Formal and mainly written. Used in literature, speeches, and reports. In conversation, it often sounds dramatic or bookish, so use ずに/ないで instead. It emphasizes “never” or “not at all,” often expressing admiration for persistence or regret over an outcome.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"諦める","r":"あきらめる","m":"give up"},{"t":"ことなく","r":"ことなく","m":"without ever"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"10年間","r":"じゅうねんかん","m":"for 10 years"},{"t":"研究","r":"けんきゅう","m":"research"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"続けた","r":"つづけた","m":"continued"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He continued his research for ten years without ever giving up.","scene":"A written passage about a persistent scientist pursuing a topic"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"笑顔","r":"えがお","m":"smile"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"絶やす","r":"たやす","m":"lose"},{"t":"ことなく","r":"ことなく","m":"without ever"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"患者","r":"かんじゃ","m":"patients"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"接した","r":"せっした","m":"interacted with"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She interacted with patients without ever losing her smile.","scene":"Describing a nurse in a story"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-032||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～ことなく · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことなく","patternReading":"ことなく","coreMeaning":"Without (ever) ~; while continuing Q without ~ (written style)","slug":"forms","definition":"Form the pattern by adding **ことなく** directly after **V dictionary form**. The entire phrase **V dictionary form + ことなく** modifies clause Q. All tense, negation, and politeness information belongs in the final verb of Q; ことなく does not change.","nuance":"There is no special polite form of ことなく; if politeness is needed, put Q in polite form. Do not use the ない, た, or ます forms before ことなく.","structure":[{"lhs":"V dictionary form","rhs":"ことなく + Q","note":"Q is the main clause. Example: 休む + ことなく + 働く。"},{"lhs":"V ない-form","rhs":"✗ Do not use","note":"Never say 休まないことなく."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"休む","r":"やすむ","m":"rest"},{"t":"ことなく","r":"ことなく","m":"without ever"},{"t":"働いた","r":"はたらいた","m":"worked"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He worked without ever resting.","scene":"Describing an employee working continuous overtime"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"文句","r":"もんく","m":"complaints"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"言う","r":"いう","m":"say"},{"t":"ことなく","r":"ことなく","m":"without ever"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"引き受けた","r":"ひきうけた","m":"accepted"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She accepted the work without ever complaining.","scene":"A boss assigns extra work at closing time, and she accepts it gladly"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-032||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & contexts","sub":"～ことなく · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことなく","patternReading":"ことなく","coreMeaning":"Without (ever) ~; while continuing Q without ~ (written style)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to emphasize that **Q occurs while P does not occur at all**, especially when P is something people normally do, such as resting, giving up, or complaining. It suits literature, speeches, reports, and formal emails; in ordinary conversation, use ずに/ないで instead.","nuance":"It is not emotionally neutral: it sounds **formal and vivid**. It describes a long, steady, uninterrupted process. For a single action yesterday, ことなく may sound exaggerated.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"休憩","r":"きゅうけい","m":"break"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"挟む","r":"はさむ","m":"insert"},{"t":"ことなく","r":"ことなく","m":"without ever"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"3時間","r":"さんじかん","m":"3 hours"},{"t":"続いた","r":"つづいた","m":"continued"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The meeting lasted three hours without a break.","scene":"Hearing about a marathon meeting at a company"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"誰","r":"だれ","m":"anyone"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"知られる","r":"しられる","m":"be known"},{"t":"ことなく","r":"ことなく","m":"without ever"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"静かに","r":"しずかに","m":"quietly"},{"t":"去った","r":"さった","m":"left"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He left quietly without anyone knowing.","scene":"A chapter opens with a character disappearing into the night"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-032||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing ずに / ないで","sub":"～ことなく · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことなく","patternReading":"ことなく","coreMeaning":"Without (ever) ~; while continuing Q without ~ (written style)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish ことなく from ずに and ないで. All three mean “do Q without doing P,” but they differ in formality and emphasis.","nuance":"On exams, the distinction often depends on context: formal writing → ことなく; ordinary speech → ないで; neutral prose → ずに.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何も","r":"なにも","m":"anything"},{"t":"言う","r":"いう","m":"say"},{"t":"ことなく","r":"ことなく","m":"without ever"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"出て行った","r":"でていった","m":"walked out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He walked out of the room without saying a word.","scene":"After an argument, he stood up and left"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～ずに","point":"Semi-formal and neutral, without strong emphasis. 諦めずに続けた — a normal action; ことなく = never giving up, with emotional force."},{"vs":"～ないで","point":"Colloquial, often + ください. 諦めないで続けて — a request; ことなく sounds bookish in conversation."},{"vs":"～ことなしに","point":"Means “without P,” where P is often a necessary condition; ことなく = Q continues without P."}]}},"n2-032||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～ことなく · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことなく","patternReading":"ことなく","coreMeaning":"Without (ever) ~; while continuing Q without ~ (written style)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"This section covers common errors, especially **using the wrong verb form before ことなく** (it must be V dictionary form) and **using an overly formal pattern in conversation**.","nuance":"The error V-ない + ことなく is common because learners associate the pattern with negation. In fact, ことなく already expresses negation, so the preceding verb must be affirmative and in dictionary form (辞書形).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"無駄に","r":"むだに","m":"wastefully"},{"t":"する","m":"use"},{"t":"ことなく","r":"ことなく","m":"without ever"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"計画的に","r":"けいかくてきに","m":"systematically"},{"t":"貯めた","r":"ためた","m":"saved"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She saved money systematically without wasting it.","scene":"Talking about a friend’s financial habits"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 休まないことなく働いた。 → ✅ 休むことなく働いた。 (Must use V dictionary form + ことなく.)","❌ Using ことなく in casual conversation sounds dramatic/bookish: 今日は忙しくて、休むことなく働いた。 → ✅ 今日は忙しくて、休まずに/休まないで働いた。","❌ Do not confuse ことなく with ことなしに: ことなく = Q continues normally; ことなしに = “without P (a necessary condition).”"],"compare":null}},"n2-032||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"～ことなく · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことなく","patternReading":"ことなく","coreMeaning":"Without (ever) ~; while continuing Q without ~ (written style)","slug":"memory","definition":"Break it into こと (event) + なく (not existing) = “there is no event of P.” When reading, ことなく signals persistence and uninterrupted continuity. Remember: “without ever doing P, Q still happens.”","nuance":"This tip helps prevent V-ない + ことなく: since なく is already negative, the verb before it must be affirmative and in dictionary form.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-032||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common collocations","sub":"～ことなく · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことなく","patternReading":"ことなく","coreMeaning":"Without (ever) ~; while continuing Q without ~ (written style)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases with ことなく in exams and real texts. Remember each as a whole: V dictionary form + ことなく + the main action.","nuance":"These phrases often appear in literature, journalism, and speeches. 笑顔を絶やすことなく is a particularly familiar literary and expressive combination.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"笑顔","r":"えがお","m":"smile"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"絶やす","r":"たやす","m":"lose"},{"t":"ことなく","r":"ことなく","m":"without ever"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"最後まで","r":"さいごまで","m":"to the end"},{"t":"歌い切った","r":"うたいきった","m":"sang through"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She sang to the end without ever losing her smile.","scene":"A singer stays composed during the concert final"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-033||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～を通じて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を通じて","patternReading":"をつうじて","coreMeaning":"Throughout ~ / Through ~ (channel/means) — formal","slug":"core","definition":"～を通じて has two main meanings: **(1) throughout ~** — covering an entire period or scope (一年を通じて, 生涯を通じて); **(2) through ~** — using ~ as a channel or means (友人を通じて, インターネットを通じて). Both meanings are used in formal writing or polite conversation. The speaker emphasizes something **covering the whole range** or **passing through a specific channel**.","nuance":"Avoid it in casual conversation; use ずっと (throughout) or から (from/through). It sounds objective and formal, making it suitable for journalism, essays, and presentations. Compared with を通して, を通じて leans more toward a **transmission channel** than an experiential process.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"北海道","r":"ほっかいどう","m":"Hokkaido"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"一年","r":"いちねん","m":"all year"},{"t":"を通じて","r":"をつうじて","m":"throughout"},{"t":"観光客","r":"かんこうきゃく","m":"tourists"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"多い","r":"おおい","m":"many"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Hokkaido has many tourists throughout the year.","scene":"Describing Hokkaido tourism in a report."},{"tokens":[{"t":"友人","r":"ゆうじん","m":"friend"},{"t":"を通じて","r":"をつうじて","m":"through"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"ビジネスパートナー","m":"business partner"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"紹介","r":"しょうかい","m":"introduce"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"もらった","m":"was able to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Through a friend, I was introduced to a new business partner.","scene":"Explaining how you met a business partner."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-033||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & usage","sub":"～を通じて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を通じて","patternReading":"をつうじて","coreMeaning":"Throughout ~ / Through ~ (channel/means) — formal","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern **combines only with nouns**; it is not used with verbs or adjectives. There are two structures with two meanings: N for time/scope ＋ を通じて = throughout ~; N for channel/means ＋ を通じて = through ~.","nuance":"In formal writing, it can sometimes be replaced by を通して, but を通じて sounds more objective and emphasizes the **channel or scope**, while を通して emphasizes the **process** more.","structure":[{"lhs":"N (time/scope)","rhs":"＋ を通じて","note":"一年を通じて, 生涯を通じて → throughout ~"},{"lhs":"N (channel/means)","rhs":"＋ を通じて","note":"友人を通じて, インターネットを通じて → through ~"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"地域","r":"ちいき","m":"region"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"一年","r":"いちねん","m":"all year"},{"t":"を通じて","r":"をつうじて","m":"throughout"},{"t":"温暖","r":"おんだん","m":"mild"},{"t":"だ"}],"vi":"This region is warm throughout the year.","scene":"Describing the local climate in an introduction."},{"tokens":[{"t":"ご応募","r":"ごおうぼ","m":"application"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"メール","m":"email"},{"t":"を通じて","r":"をつうじて","m":"through"},{"t":"お願いします","r":"おねがいします","m":"please do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please apply by email.","scene":"A job posting on a company website."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-033||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & contexts","sub":"～を通じて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を通じて","patternReading":"をつうじて","coreMeaning":"Throughout ~ / Through ~ (channel/means) — formal","slug":"usage","definition":"There are two main contexts. **(1) Throughout**: something continues across an entire period or scope — often with 一年, 生涯, 期間. **(2) Through**: using a person, medium, or organization as an intermediary channel — 友人, SNS, インターネット.","nuance":"Very formal; used in journalism, documents, presentations, CVs, and business emails. In casual conversation with friends, Japanese speakers use ずっと or から instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"生涯","r":"しょうがい","m":"whole life"},{"t":"を通じて","r":"をつうじて","m":"throughout"},{"t":"平和","r":"へいわ","m":"peace"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"訴え","r":"うったえ","m":"advocate"},{"t":"続けた","r":"つづけた","m":"continued"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Throughout his life, he continued to advocate for peace.","scene":"Writing about a social activist in an essay."},{"tokens":[{"t":"SNS","m":"social media"},{"t":"を通じて","r":"をつうじて","m":"through"},{"t":"情報","r":"じょうほう","m":"information"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"広めた","r":"ひろめた","m":"spread"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The information was spread through social media.","scene":"Discussing a company communications campaign."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-033||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast: 通じて vs 通して","sub":"～を通じて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を通じて","patternReading":"をつうじて","coreMeaning":"Throughout ~ / Through ~ (channel/means) — formal","slug":"compare","definition":"This section helps distinguish similar patterns. Remember: を通じて = **a transmission channel** or **the entire span**; を通して = **a process or experience**; にかけて = **from point A to point B**; にわたって = **a length with a specific number**.","nuance":"In many cases を通じて and を通して are interchangeable, but を通して is more natural for “learn through experience.” にかけて and にわたって are not used for communication channels.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"him"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"友人","r":"ゆうじん","m":"friend"},{"t":"を通じて","r":"をつうじて","m":"through"},{"t":"知り合った","r":"しりあった","m":"got acquainted"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I got to know him through a friend.","scene":"Explaining how you met someone."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜を通して","point":"Emphasizes PROCESS/experience (経験を通して学ぶ). 〜を通じて emphasizes a CHANNEL or the ENTIRE span."},{"vs":"〜にかけて","point":"Indicates a range FROM A TO B (3月から4月にかけて), without asserting the entire span like 一年を通じて."},{"vs":"〜にわたって","point":"Emphasizes LENGTH with a specific number/scope (3時間にわたって); not used for communication channels."}]}},"n2-033||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～を通じて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を通じて","patternReading":"をつうじて","coreMeaning":"Throughout ~ / Through ~ (channel/means) — formal","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make three mistakes: using を通じて in casual conversation, using it when something happens only at an isolated point in time, and confusing it with にかけて. Remember: を通じて = **the whole span/continuous**; for one isolated day, use の間/にかけて.","nuance":"Although it means “throughout,” 一月を通じて works only when something occurs regularly or across the month. If it rains on just one day in the month, use 一月の間(に) or 一月のうちに.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"一年","r":"いちねん","m":"all year"},{"t":"を通じて","r":"をつうじて","m":"throughout"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"多くの","r":"おおくの","m":"many"},{"t":"イベント","m":"events"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開催される","r":"かいさいされる","m":"are held"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Many events are held throughout the year.","scene":"Describing a year of cultural events."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 一月を通じて、その日だけ雨が降った。 → ✅ 一月の間(に)、その日だけ雨が降った。 (It rained on only one day of the month — not 'throughout')","❌ 昨日、友達を通じて話した。(casual conversation) → ✅ 昨日、友達経由で話した。 / ✅ 昨日、友達を通して話した。","❌ 忙しいを通じて… → ✅ 忙しい中(で)… (を通じて does not follow an adjective; it only follows a noun)","❌ 3月から4月を通じて → ✅ 3月から4月にかけて (for a range from...to, use にかけて)"],"compare":null}},"n2-033||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～を通じて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を通じて","patternReading":"をつうじて","coreMeaning":"Throughout ~ / Through ~ (channel/means) — formal","slug":"memory","definition":"Anchor it with an image: **通じる** = pass through. を通じて = “through ~” from beginning to end. For time → **throughout** (a tunnel through the whole year); for an intermediary → a **channel** (information flowing through a pipe).","nuance":"Do not confuse 通じて (つうじて) with 通して (とおして): 通じて leans toward a channel or scope, while 通して leans toward a process.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-033||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～を通じて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を通じて","patternReading":"をつうじて","coreMeaning":"Throughout ~ / Through ~ (channel/means) — formal","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases: 生涯を通じて (throughout life), 一年を通じて (throughout the year), 友人を通じて (through friends), SNS/インターネットを通じて (through social media/the Internet).","nuance":"The N + を通じて phrase usually comes before a verb: を通じて知る, を通じて交流する, を通じて発表する.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"生涯","r":"しょうがい","m":"a lifetime"},{"t":"を通じて","r":"をつうじて","m":"throughout"},{"t":"学び","r":"まなび","m":"learn"},{"t":"続けたい","r":"つづけたい","m":"want to continue"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to continue learning throughout my life.","scene":"Sharing a personal goal in an interview."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-034||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～を通して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を通して","patternReading":"をとおして","coreMeaning":"Through ~ (a process/experience)","slug":"core","definition":"〜を通して describes achieving the result in the following clause **through a process, activity, or experience**. The key idea is **PROCESS**—it emphasizes what is learned or felt through that process itself.","nuance":"Semi-formal, and natural in both speech and writing. It works especially well in CVs and essays. Compared with を通じて, を通して leans more toward **experience** than an intermediary channel or a period of time. The speaker views P as a lesson or journey.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"経験","r":"けいけん","m":"experience"},{"t":"を通して","r":"をとおして","m":"through"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"多くの","r":"おおくの","m":"many"},{"t":"こと","m":"things"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"学んだ","r":"まなんだ","m":"learned"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Through this experience, I learned many things.","scene":"Reflecting on lessons after a meaningful event"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ボランティア","m":"volunteer"},{"t":"活動","r":"かつどう","m":"activity"},{"t":"を通して","r":"をとおして","m":"through"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"地域","r":"ちいき","m":"community"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"つながり","m":"connections"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"深まった","r":"ふかまった","m":"deepened"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Through volunteer work, community connections grew stronger.","scene":"Summarizing a volunteer campaign"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-034||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & formation","sub":"～を通して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を通して","patternReading":"をとおして","coreMeaning":"Through ~ (a process/experience)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is simply **noun + を通して**. The noun before it can denote a process or experience, or a channel or means. The following clause usually describes a result or realization.","nuance":"There is no verb conjugation because を通して modifies a noun. It is moderately formal. With the meaning “through a channel,” it can sometimes be replaced by を通じて, but を通して is also acceptable.","structure":[{"lhs":"N（process/experience）","rhs":"+ を通して","note":"emphasizes experience: 経験を通して"},{"lhs":"N（medium/means）","rhs":"+ を通して","note":"through a medium: インターネットを通して"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"経験","r":"けいけん","m":"experience"},{"t":"を通して","r":"をとおして","m":"through"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"多くの","r":"おおくの","m":"many"},{"t":"こと","m":"things"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"学びました","r":"まなびました","m":"learned"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Through this experience, I learned many things.","scene":"A formal sentence in an essay"},{"tokens":[{"t":"インターネット","m":"internet"},{"t":"を通して","r":"をとおして","m":"through"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"情報","r":"じょうほう","m":"information"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"集める","r":"あつめる","m":"collect"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Collect information through the internet.","scene":"Talking about how to find information"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-034||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～を通して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を通して","patternReading":"をとおして","coreMeaning":"Through ~ (a process/experience)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when you want to say that **process P led to result Q**. It often appears when sharing experiences, in essays, and in reports. The second clause presents a lesson, personal growth, or a change in awareness.","nuance":"It can be used in conversation when describing life lessons. Both を通して and を通じて work for long periods such as 一年を通して / 一年を通じて. It can also describe a static tool, but then the emphasis should remain on the process of using it.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"留学","r":"りゅうがく","m":"studying abroad"},{"t":"を通して","r":"をとおして","m":"through"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"視野","r":"しや","m":"perspective"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"広がった","r":"ひろがった","m":"broadened"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Studying abroad broadened my perspective.","scene":"Describing the value of studying abroad"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"活動","r":"かつどう","m":"activity"},{"t":"を通して","r":"をとおして","m":"through"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"チームワーク","m":"teamwork"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"大切さ","r":"たいせつさ","m":"importance"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"学びました","r":"まなびました","m":"learned"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Through this activity, I learned the importance of teamwork.","scene":"Summarizing a group project"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-034||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions","sub":"～を通して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を通して","patternReading":"をとおして","coreMeaning":"Through ~ (a process/experience)","slug":"compare","definition":"The closest comparison is **～を通じて**, the pattern most easily confused with it. ～を通じて has two meanings: “throughout a period” and “through a channel.” ～を通して emphasizes a process or experience. **～によって** is formal and indicates a cause or means.","nuance":"For the meaning “through a means,” both を通して and を通じて can be used, but を通して emphasizes the process, while を通じて emphasizes the channel. With a long period such as 一年, を通じて is the better choice.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"経験","r":"けいけん","m":"experience"},{"t":"を通して","r":"をとおして","m":"through"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"成長","r":"せいちょう","m":"growth"},{"t":"できた","m":"was able to achieve"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Through this experience, I was able to grow.","scene":"Emphasizing the result of a process"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜を通じて","point":"を通じて = “throughout” (time) or “via a channel.” を通して = “through a process/experience.” Compare: 一年を通じて (all year) / 経験を通して (through experience)."},{"vs":"〜によって","point":"によって = a means/direct cause, with a formal tone and no sense of experience. Compare: 努力によって成功した (succeeded through effort) / 経験を通して成長した (grew through experience)."}]}},"n2-034||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～を通して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を通して","patternReading":"をとおして","coreMeaning":"Through ~ (a process/experience)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The most common mistake is confusing ～を通して with patterns for a direct cause or means, such as ～によって. Also, when saying “introduced through friends,” を通じて is preferable to を通して when the focus is the channel.","nuance":"Remember: を通して is for experiences; を通じて is for intermediary channels and periods of time. Confusing them can make a sentence sound unnatural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ボランティア","m":"volunteer"},{"t":"を通して","r":"をとおして","m":"through"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大切な","r":"たいせつな","m":"important"},{"t":"こと","m":"things"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"学びました","r":"まなびました","m":"learned"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Through volunteer work, I learned important things.","scene":"Sharing lessons from volunteer work"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 努力を通して試験に合格した。 → ✅ 努力によって試験に合格した。","❌ 友達を通して、事故に遭った。 → ✅ 友達のせいで、事故に遭った。","❌ 努力を通して成功した。 → ✅ 努力によって成功した。（use によって for a direct cause）"],"compare":null}},"n2-034||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～を通して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を通して","patternReading":"をとおして","coreMeaning":"Through ~ (a process/experience)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture 通す as **letting something pass through**. を通して uses P as a **tunnel** leading to Q. P is the process; Q is the result.","nuance":"Memory line: “through an EXPERIENCE” — whenever you want to say “through an experience,” think of を通して. Do not use it for “throughout”; that is を通じて.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-034||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～を通して · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を通して","patternReading":"をとおして","coreMeaning":"Through ~ (a process/experience)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common nouns used with を通して include 経験, 活動, 作品, 日本語, 留学, and ボランティア.","nuance":"Learning these collocations helps you make natural sentences. In exam questions, nouns such as 経験 and 活動 are often provided to connect with the pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"作品","r":"さくひん","m":"work"},{"t":"を通して","r":"をとおして","m":"through"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"作者","r":"さくしゃ","m":"author"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"メッセージ","m":"message"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"伝わる","r":"つたわる","m":"gets across"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Through this work, the author’s message comes across.","scene":"Discussing the meaning of a work of art"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-035||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ともなく · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ともなく","patternReading":"ともなく／ともなしに","coreMeaning":"Unconscious, unintentional action; unclear source or destination","slug":"core","definition":"〜ともなく／ともなしに describes an action done with no clear purpose—doing P unconsciously, absent-mindedly, or without trying. The result (Q) often involves realizing something. The speaker does not consciously direct attention to P, but P still happens like a reflex.","nuance":"Has a narrative, literary tone and suits writing better. With どこ/誰/何, it emphasizes an unclear source or lack of a specific reason. Common verbs involve perception: 見る, 聞く, 眺める, 考える.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"中","r":"なか","m":"inside"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"見る","r":"みる","m":"look"},{"t":"ともなく","m":"absent-mindedly"},{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"外","r":"そと","m":"outside"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"眺めて","r":"ながめて","m":"gaze at"},{"t":"いた","m":"was"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the train, I gazed absent-mindedly out the window.","scene":"On a train, gazing blankly through the window"},{"tokens":[{"t":"隣","r":"となり","m":"next to"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"席","r":"せき","m":"seat"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"会話","r":"かいわ","m":"conversation"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"聞く","r":"きく","m":"hear"},{"t":"ともなしに","m":"without meaning to"},{"t":"聞いて","r":"きいて","m":"hear"},{"t":"しまった","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I ended up overhearing the conversation of the person next to me.","scene":"A nearby conversation reaches your ears"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-035||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"～ともなく · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ともなく","patternReading":"ともなく／ともなしに","coreMeaning":"Unconscious, unintentional action; unclear source or destination","slug":"forms","definition":"The compact structure is **dictionary-form V + ともなく/ともなしに**. Question words (どこ・誰・何) also attach directly to ともなく to mean “unclear where/who/why.” An emphatic form repeats the same verb: 見るともなく見る.","nuance":"～ともなしに sounds slightly more formal and literary than ～ともなく. In everyday speech, people often use 何となく or ぼんやり instead.","structure":[{"lhs":"V (辞書形)","rhs":"＋ ともなく／ともなしに","note":"Q often uses 気づく・聞こえる・見える…"},{"lhs":"どこ・誰・何","rhs":"＋ ともなく","note":"どこともなく (nowhere), どこからともなく (somewhere), 誰ともなく (someone), 何ともなく (no reason)"},{"lhs":"V (辞書形)","rhs":"＋ ともなく ＋ V (same verb)","note":"見るともなく見る: doing P without intending to"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ベランダ"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"遠く","r":"とおく","m":"far away"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見る","r":"みる","m":"look"},{"t":"ともなく","m":"without meaning to"},{"t":"眺めて","r":"ながめて","m":"gaze at"},{"t":"いた","m":"was"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the balcony, I gazed absent-mindedly into the distance.","scene":"Gazing blankly into the distance at dusk"},{"tokens":[{"t":"どこから","m":"from somewhere"},{"t":"ともなく","m":"unclear where"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"笛","r":"ふえ","m":"flute"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"音","r":"おと","m":"sound"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"聞こえて","r":"きこえて","m":"could hear"},{"t":"きた","m":"came"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A flute sound suddenly came from somewhere.","scene":"A flute suddenly sounds on the street"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-035||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～ともなく · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ともなく","patternReading":"ともなく／ともなしに","coreMeaning":"Unconscious, unintentional action; unclear source or destination","slug":"usage","definition":"Three typical situations: ① being absent-minded while waiting or traveling; ② accidentally realizing something after doing P; ③ describing a sound or image appearing from an unknown place.","nuance":"Avoid it in reports, official notices, and commands. To emphasize an unconscious state, use it with ぼんやり or なんとなく.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どこ"},{"t":"ともなく","m":"to nowhere in particular"},{"t":"歩き始めた","r":"あるきはじめた","m":"started walking"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I started walking, without knowing where I was going.","scene":"Wandering aimlessly one afternoon"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ぼんやり"},{"t":"空","r":"そら","m":"sky"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"眺めて","r":"ながめて","m":"gaze at"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"どこから","m":"from somewhere"},{"t":"ともなく","m":"unclear where"},{"t":"歌声","r":"うたごえ","m":"singing voice"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"聞こえて","r":"きこえて","m":"could hear"},{"t":"きた","m":"came"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As I gazed dreamily at the sky, singing suddenly drifted over from somewhere.","scene":"Relaxing on a porch when distant singing appears"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-035||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with つい・ながら","sub":"～ともなく · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ともなく","patternReading":"ともなく／ともなしに","coreMeaning":"Unconscious, unintentional action; unclear source or destination","slug":"compare","definition":"Quick contrast with two easily confused patterns: ～つい and ～ながら. ～ともなく describes an unconscious action, ～つい an inability to resist, and ～ながら two actions performed in parallel.","nuance":"～ともなく emphasizes **lack of intention and unconsciousness**; ～つい emphasizes **not being able to resist, often with regret**; ～ながら emphasizes **parallel actions, possibly intentional**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"聞く","r":"きく","m":"listen"},{"t":"ともなしに","m":"without meaning to"},{"t":"聞いて","r":"きいて","m":"listen"},{"t":"いたら"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"秘密","r":"ひみつ","m":"secret"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"知って","r":"しって","m":"learn"},{"t":"しまった","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"While listening absent-mindedly, I ended up learning a secret.","scene":"Sitting nearby as the conversation reaches your ears"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～つい","point":"つい: accidental because you **COULDN'T RESIST**, often with regret (ついコーヒーを頼んでしまった). ともなく: unconscious and **ABSENT-MINDED**, without guilt."},{"vs":"～ながら","point":"ながら: **TWO actions at once**, with the speaker **INTENTIONALLY** doing both (テレビを見ながら食べる). ともなく: only **ONE action**, done **UNINTENTIONALLY**."}]}},"n2-035||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～ともなく · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ともなく","patternReading":"ともなく／ともなしに","coreMeaning":"Unconscious, unintentional action; unclear source or destination","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The most common learner error is pairing ともなく with **intentional actions** (意識的に, 注意して...). This creates a logical contradiction. The pattern is only for a naturally absent-minded state.","nuance":"To express deliberate action, use another pattern such as つい/うっかり. ～ともなく does not work with commands or requests.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"見る","r":"みる","m":"look"},{"t":"ともなく","m":"without meaning to"},{"t":"壁","r":"かべ","m":"wall"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ポスター"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見て","r":"みて","m":"look at"},{"t":"しまった","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I unconsciously looked at the poster on the wall.","scene":"Waiting and staring at a poster without thinking"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 意識的に見るともなく調査した。 → ✅ 見るともなく窓の外を眺めていた。","❌ 聞くともなく聞いてください。 → ✅ 聞くともなしに聞いてしまった。"],"compare":null}},"n2-035||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"～ともなく · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ともなく","patternReading":"ともなく／ともなしに","coreMeaning":"Unconscious, unintentional action; unclear source or destination","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: とも (also) + なく (not) → “not quite P, and not quite not-P” = absent-mindedness. Picture yourself on a train, gazing through the window with unfocused eyes.","nuance":"Imagine: “The eyes are looking, but the mind is in the clouds”—you are still looking, but not concentrating.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-035||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common expressions","sub":"～ともなく · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ともなく","patternReading":"ともなく／ともなしに","coreMeaning":"Unconscious, unintentional action; unclear source or destination","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common expressions in exams and written Japanese: 見るともなく見る, 聞くともなしに聞く, どこともなく, どこからともなく, 誰ともなく, 何ともなく.","nuance":"Memorize these as fixed expressions rather than analyzing every part.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"誰","r":"だれ","m":"who"},{"t":"ともなく","m":"unclear who"},{"t":"言い出した","r":"いいだした","m":"spoke up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Someone—though it was unclear who—spoke up.","scene":"Someone in the group suddenly speaks"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-036||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ともなしに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ともなしに","patternReading":"ともなしに","coreMeaning":"Doing ~ without intending to; absent-mindedly","slug":"core","definition":"〜ともなしに describes an action done without a clear purpose or intention, unconsciously or absent-mindedly. P happens like a natural reflex, and the speaker then suddenly realizes or happens to learn Q. This pattern is common in writing and often observes inner thoughts.","nuance":"The tone is dreamy and distracted, without deliberate evaluation. It does not express regret (unlike つい) or two simultaneous actions (unlike ながら). It suits narratives, essays, emotional blogs, and is uncommon in casual conversation. Register: semi-formal to literary.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"中","r":"なか","m":"inside"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"見る","r":"みる","m":"look"},{"t":"ともなしに","r":"ともなしに","m":"absent-mindedly"},{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"外","r":"そと","m":"outside"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"眺めていた","r":"ながめていた","m":"was gazing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the train, I absent-mindedly gazed out the window.","scene":"On a train, dreamy and unfocused"},{"tokens":[{"t":"隣","r":"となり","m":"next to me"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"席","r":"せき","m":"seat"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"会話","r":"かいわ","m":"conversation"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"聞く","r":"きく","m":"hear"},{"t":"ともなしに"},{"t":"聞いて","r":"きいて","m":"listen"},{"t":"しまった","r":"しまった","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I unintentionally overheard the conversation of the person next to me.","scene":"In a café, hearing it without trying"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-036||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"～ともなしに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ともなしに","patternReading":"ともなしに","coreMeaning":"Doing ~ without intending to; absent-mindedly","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **V (dictionary form) + ともなしに**. ～ともなく is an equivalent variant, often used in writing. Also, **どこ・誰・何 + ともなく** indicates an unspecified source, destination, or target. The same verb may be repeated to emphasize unconsciousness: VるともなくV.","nuance":"The following clause often contains a perception verb (気づく, 聞こえてくる, 目に入る) or an ongoing action (眺める, 歩く). Do not use it with Vます, Vた, or Vない. The following clause is usually a result or perception.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"+ ともなしに + Q","note":"Unconscious action P"},{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"+ ともなく + Q","note":"Equivalent variant"},{"lhs":"どこ／誰／何","rhs":"+ ともなく","note":"Unknown source/destination/person/reason"},{"lhs":"VるともなくV","rhs":"（同一動詞繰り返し）","note":"Emphasizes unconsciousness"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"外","r":"そと","m":"outside"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見る","r":"みる","m":"look"},{"t":"ともなしに"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"こと","m":"matter"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"考えていた","r":"かんがえていた","m":"was thinking"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Absent-mindedly looking out the window, I was thinking about work.","scene":"Working, but my mind drifts away"},{"tokens":[{"t":"どこから","r":"どこから","m":"from somewhere"},{"t":"ともなしに"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ピアノ","r":"ぴあの","m":"piano"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"音","r":"おと","m":"sound"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"聞こえて","r":"きこえて","m":"can be heard"},{"t":"きた","r":"きた","m":"came"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The sound of a piano drifted in from somewhere.","scene":"Walking outside, suddenly hearing music"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-036||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～ともなしに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ともなしに","patternReading":"ともなしに","coreMeaning":"Doing ~ without intending to; absent-mindedly","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern for unintentional actions in everyday situations such as riding trains, waiting, relaxing, or strolling. The speaker performs P without focusing on it and thereby happens to perceive or experience Q. It suits descriptive writing, memories, and inner thoughts.","nuance":"Do not use it for commands, requests, or deliberate actions. Avoid it in administrative documents and reports. It feels close to なんとなく. The following clause often uses ていた or てしまった.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ベランダ","r":"べらんだ","m":"balcony"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"anything"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"する","r":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ともなしに"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"空","r":"そら","m":"sky"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"眺めていた","r":"ながめていた","m":"was gazing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"With nothing in particular to do, I stood on the balcony gazing at the sky.","scene":"Relaxing alone with nothing to do"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"誰","r":"だれ","m":"anyone"},{"t":"ともなしに"},{"t":"つぶやいた","r":"つぶやいた","m":"muttered"},{"t":"言葉","r":"ことば","m":"words"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"妙に","r":"みょうに","m":"strangely"},{"t":"心","r":"こころ","m":"heart"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"残った","r":"のこった","m":"remained"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Her words, muttered to no one in particular, strangely stayed with me.","scene":"She talks to herself; I happen to hear"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-036||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing similar patterns","sub":"～ともなしに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ともなしに","patternReading":"ともなしに","coreMeaning":"Doing ~ without intending to; absent-mindedly","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish ～ともなしに from three easily confused patterns. The key point: ～ともなしに describes an unconscious, unintentional action, without emphasizing simultaneity or regret.","nuance":"～ながら marks two simultaneous actions, intentional or not; ～つい emphasizes being unable to resist, often with ～てしまう and regret; ～ことなく means not doing P at all.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"テレビ","r":"てれび","m":"TV"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見る","r":"みる","m":"watch"},{"t":"ともなしに"},{"t":"見ていた","r":"みていた","m":"was watching"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I watched TV absent-mindedly, almost without watching it.","scene":"The TV is on, but my mind is elsewhere"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～ながら","point":"ながら = two clearly parallel actions (doing X while doing Y); ともなしに = one unconscious action, with no second action. Example: テレビを見ながらご飯を食べた (watched TV while eating) ≠ テレビを見るともなしに見ていた (watched TV absent-mindedly)."},{"vs":"～つい","point":"つい = unable to resist doing something one should not, often regretting it and used with てしまう; ともなしに = absent-minded, without guilt. Example: ついお酒を飲みすぎた ≠ 窓の外を見るともなしに見ていた."},{"vs":"～ことなく","point":"ことなく = does not do P at all; ともなしに = does do P, but unconsciously. Example: 朝ごはんを食べることなく家を出た (left without breakfast) ≠ 食べるともなしに食べた (ate absent-mindedly)."}]}},"n2-036||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～ともなしに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ともなしに","patternReading":"ともなしに","coreMeaning":"Doing ~ without intending to; absent-mindedly","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners may translate it as “without intending to” but attach it to an intentional action, misunderstand it as “while,” or confuse it with a negative construction.","nuance":"Remember: ともなしに means unconscious and unintentional. For “while doing” use ながら. For “without doing P” use ことなく / ずに.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"テレビ","r":"てれび","m":"TV"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見る","r":"みる","m":"watch"},{"t":"ともなしに","r":"ともなしに","m":"absent-mindedly"},{"t":"見ていたら","r":"みていたら","m":"while watching"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"過ぎていた","r":"すぎていた","m":"had passed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As I gazed absent-mindedly at the TV, time passed without my noticing.","scene":"The TV is on while I sit dazed"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 意識的に見るともなしに調査した。 → ✅ 意識的に調査した。 (意識的に + ともなしに = a contradiction)","❌ ご飯を食べるともなしにテレビを見た。 → ✅ ご飯を食べながらテレビを見た。 (two parallel actions)","❌ 彼は何も食べるともなしに寝た。 → ✅ 彼は何も食べずに寝た。 (negation + ずに)"],"compare":null}},"n2-036||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～ともなしに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ともなしに","patternReading":"ともなしに","coreMeaning":"Doing ~ without intending to; absent-mindedly","slug":"memory","definition":"Word analysis: **とも** (also) + **なし** (none) + **に** → “not really P, but not quite not-P either,” describing a dreamy, dazed state.","nuance":"Picture sitting on a train, looking out the window without really seeing it, with your mind far away. You are '見るともなしに見ている'.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-036||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～ともなしに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ともなしに","patternReading":"ともなしに","coreMeaning":"Doing ~ without intending to; absent-mindedly","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common collocations in exams and literary writing: **どこからともなしに** (from somewhere unknown), **誰ともなく** (no one in particular), **見るともなく見る** (look absent-mindedly), and **聞くともなしに聞く** (hear absent-mindedly).","nuance":"With these fixed phrases, remember that vagueness and lack of a specific source or target are central. Example: どこからともなしに音がする – a sound comes from somewhere unknown.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どこからともなく","r":"どこからともなく","m":"from somewhere"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"歌声","r":"うたごえ","m":"singing voice"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"聞こえてきた","r":"きこえてきた","m":"drifted over"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A singing voice drifted over from somewhere.","scene":"Hearing singing in a deserted alley"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-037||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ではあるまいし · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ではあるまいし","patternReading":"ではあるまいし","coreMeaning":"How could it be ~? / You're not ~, so (don't act irrationally)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ではあるまいし means **“it's not as if you are P”** and mildly criticizes behavior Q as inappropriate, illogical, or excessive for P. The speaker uses P as grounds for criticism. For example, **子どもではあるまいし** = “You're not a child,” implying “yet you're acting like one.” This pattern always expresses the speaker's subjective evaluation and is not used for objective facts.","nuance":"This is an **informal** expression, often used among friends, acquaintances, or in affectionate scolding. The criticism is **mild and somewhat sarcastic**, not as harsh as 〜くせに. Do not use it with superiors or in formal contexts, as it sounds **impolite**. Note that P may be an inferior category (子ども, 素人) or a highly capable one (神様, プロ), used to reject excessively high expectations.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"ではあるまいし","r":"ではあるまいし","m":"not as if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"そんな"},{"t":"こと"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"泣く","r":"なく","m":"cry"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"よ"}],"vi":"You're not a child, so don't cry over something like that.","scene":"Teasing a close friend who cries after mild criticism"},{"tokens":[{"t":"初めて","r":"はじめて","m":"first time"},{"t":"ではあるまいし","r":"ではあるまいし","m":"not as if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"今更","r":"いまさら","m":"only now"},{"t":"緊張","r":"きんちょう"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"It's not your first time, so why are you nervous now?","scene":"A coworker gets nervous before a familiar presentation"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-037||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and forms","sub":"～ではあるまいし · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ではあるまいし","patternReading":"ではあるまいし","coreMeaning":"How could it be ~? / You're not ~, so (don't act irrationally)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure of 〜ではあるまいし is fairly simple: attach it to a **noun, verb in plain form, or な-adjective**. It uses the negative form of である, with the formal/literary form **であるまい**. **〜わけではあるまいし** is used to reject a reason or logic. The clause after the pattern is **Q—the behavior or judgment being criticized**.","nuance":"The following clause is often a **command, prohibition, or sarcastic question**, such as 〜な or 〜じゃないか. P should be brief, usually a **noun referring to a person** (子ども, プロ, 素人...). Do not use past tense or the polite forms 〜です/〜ます.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"＋ ではあるまいし ＋ (criticism)","note":"Example: 子どもではあるまいし"},{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"＋ ではあるまいし ＋ (criticism)","note":"Example: 分からないではあるまいし"},{"lhs":"なA","rhs":"＋ ではあるまいし ＋ (criticism)","note":"Example: 得意ではあるまいし"},{"lhs":"〜わけ","rhs":"＋ ではあるまいし ＋ (criticism)","note":"Example: できないわけではあるまいし"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"プロ","r":"プロ","m":"professional"},{"t":"ではあるまいし","r":"ではあるまいし","m":"not as if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"完璧","r":"かんぺき","m":"perfect"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"求め","r":"もとめ","m":"demand"},{"t":"すぎ","r":"すぎ","m":"too much"},{"t":"じゃないか"}],"vi":"You're not a professional, so why demand perfection?","scene":"Reproaching a team leader for being too demanding"},{"tokens":[{"t":"分から","r":"わから","m":"understand"},{"t":"ない"},{"t":"わけ"},{"t":"ではあるまいし","r":"ではあるまいし","m":"not as if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"自分","r":"じぶん","m":"yourself"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"調べ","r":"しらべ","m":"look up"},{"t":"なさい"}],"vi":"It's not as if you don't understand, so look it up yourself.","scene":"A new employee asks without trying to find out first"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-037||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～ではあるまいし · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ではあるまいし","patternReading":"ではあるまいし","coreMeaning":"How could it be ~? / You're not ~, so (don't act irrationally)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use 〜ではあるまいし in these situations: **①** mildly scolding someone for acting unlike their age or status (e.g. an adult throwing a tantrum), **②** criticizing an excessive or unnecessary action (e.g. demanding perfection despite not being a professional), and **③** making a sarcastic comment online or in everyday conversation.","nuance":"The pattern conveys **mild annoyance, sarcasm, or affectionate reproach**. It is somewhat stronger than 〜わけでもないのに because it implies “you should know better.” But it is milder than 〜くせに, which focuses less on deceit and more on unreasonable behavior. It is often used to **defuse tension**, not start a fight.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"プロ","r":"プロ","m":"professional"},{"t":"ではあるまいし","r":"ではあるまいし","m":"not as if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"最初","r":"さいしょ","m":"from the start"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"うまく","r":"うまく","m":"skillfully"},{"t":"できる","r":"できる","m":"can do"},{"t":"わけ"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ない"},{"t":"よ"}],"vi":"You're not a professional, so you can't expect to do it well from the start.","scene":"A new cook complains about doing poorly"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"girlfriend"},{"t":"ではあるまいし","r":"ではあるまいし","m":"not as if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"LINE","r":"ライン","m":"LINE"},{"t":"し"},{"t":"なく","r":"なく"},{"t":"ても"},{"t":"いい"},{"t":"でしょう"}],"vi":"She's not your girlfriend, so you don't have to message her every day.","scene":"A friend sends too many messages and check-ins"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-037||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing vs 〜わけでもないのに / 〜くせに","sub":"～ではあるまいし · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ではあるまいし","patternReading":"ではあるまいし","coreMeaning":"How could it be ~? / You're not ~, so (don't act irrationally)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes 〜ではあるまいし from two commonly confused patterns: **〜わけでもないのに** and **〜くせに**. All three can criticize or point out something unreasonable, but they differ in **sarcasm, criticism strength, and the type of P presented**.","nuance":"〜ではあるまいし presents P as a “status or role” (you're not a child), which can sound like talking down to the listener. 〜わけでもないのに rejects a specific reason and is gentler, without harsh reproach. 〜くせに is much stronger, emphasizing “you know better but do it anyway,” and often sounds irritating.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"ではあるまいし","r":"ではあるまいし","m":"not as if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"自分","r":"じぶん","m":"yourself"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"決め","r":"きめ","m":"decide"},{"t":"なさい"}],"vi":"You're not a child; decide for yourself.","scene":"A friend asks for advice about a trivial matter"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜わけでもないのに","point":"～ではあるまいし criticizes status or age, while ～わけでもないのに only rejects a reason: “忙しいわけでもないのに連絡しない” (It's not as if they're busy, yet they don't contact me) – it sounds gentle and does not mock someone's maturity."},{"vs":"〜くせに","point":"～くせに is stronger and emphasizes hypocrisy: “知っているくせに教えてくれない” (They know, yet won't tell me) – ～ではあるまいし only calls the behavior unreasonable, without attacking character."}]}},"n2-037||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes and traps","sub":"～ではあるまいし · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ではあるまいし","patternReading":"ではあるまいし","coreMeaning":"How could it be ~? / You're not ~, so (don't act irrationally)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make three mistakes: **①** using P as something admirable or high-status (such as “professor” or “skilled doctor”), although this pattern is normally used when P is **inappropriate or inferior**, creating an odd tone; **②** using it with someone who deserves respect, even though the pattern is **casual and critical**; and **③** confusing it with 〜に違いない (“must be”), because あるまい may look similar, although the meanings are completely opposite.","nuance":"Pay special attention to mistake ①: P is always in a **lowered or rejected position**. For example, “先生ではあるまいし” can sound like “I'm not some great teacher who can explain everything,” which is rude. Use P such as 子ども, プロ, 素人, or 若者 carefully according to context. This is a pragmatic issue, not just grammar.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"ではあるまいし","r":"ではあるまいし","m":"not as if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"頼む","r":"たのむ","m":"ask a favor"},{"t":"よ"}],"vi":"You're not Tanaka, so I wouldn't ask you for that favor. (Sarcastic; don't use it with a boss.)","scene":"Asking a boss to do a menial task in an overly casual way"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 子どもだではあるまいし、自分で決めなさい。 → ✅ 子どもではあるまいし、自分で決めなさい。 (A noun attaches directly to ではあるまいし; do not add だ)","❌ 部長ではあるまいし、自分で決めてください。 → ✅ 子どもではあるまいし、自分で決めてください。 (Do not use it with a superior; it is impolite)","❌ 彼は来るではあるまいし。 → ✅ 彼は来るに違いない。 (～ではあるまいし is not used for predictions; it means “not as if…,” not “must be…”)"],"compare":null}},"n2-037||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"～ではあるまいし · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ではあるまいし","patternReading":"ではあるまいし","coreMeaning":"How could it be ~? / You're not ~, so (don't act irrationally)","slug":"memory","definition":"The quickest way to remember 〜ではあるまいし: **あるまい** is the old/literary form of “surely not,” while **し** suggests “therefore.” Together: **“You're not P, so Q is unnecessary!”** Imagine an adult wailing because they lost a piece of candy—you would naturally say “子どもではあるまいし!”","nuance":"This memory aid highlights two key features: P must be **a wrongly assigned category** (child, professional, young person, etc.), and Q must be **behavior that does not fit P** (throwing a tantrum, demanding perfection, trembling). When you see あるまい, think strong negation with a sarcastic tone.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-037||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～ではあるまいし · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ではあるまいし","patternReading":"ではあるまいし","coreMeaning":"How could it be ~? / You're not ~, so (don't act irrationally)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Several **fixed phrases** frequently appear in exams and everyday life: **子どもではあるまいし** (“You're not a child”), **プロではあるまいし** (“You're not a professional”), **初めてではあるまいし** (“It's not your first time”), and **素人ではあるまいし** (“You're not an amateur”).","nuance":"These phrases often accompany a brief criticism. Memorizing them will help you respond quickly in reading comprehension and listening questions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"プロ","r":"プロ","m":"professional"},{"t":"ではあるまいし","r":"ではあるまいし","m":"not as if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"そこ","r":"そこ","m":"that"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"完璧","r":"かんぺき","m":"perfect"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"しなくて","r":"しなくて","m":"need not make"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"いい"},{"t":"よ"}],"vi":"You're not a professional, so it doesn't have to be that perfect.","scene":"A friend over-edits a presentation"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-038||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～にしたら · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にしたら","patternReading":"にしたら","coreMeaning":"From the viewpoint of ~; from ~'s standpoint","slug":"core","definition":"The patterns ～にしたら / にすれば / にしてみれば express **judging or sensing something from the viewpoint or standpoint of a specific person**. The speaker **puts themselves in N's position** to understand that person's psychology or reaction. N is usually **a person** or an organization with opinions; it is not used for inanimate objects.","nuance":"The nuance of **empathy** is fairly strong. Unlike に違いない, which makes a firm assertion, にしたら only asks **what would make sense from that viewpoint**, without asserting that it is objectively true. It can be used in analysis, debate, and writing. The three forms にしたら (casual), にすれば (slightly formal), and にしてみれば (emphasizing trying to put yourself in that position) are nearly interchangeable.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"にしたら","r":"にしたら","m":"from his viewpoint"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"突然","r":"とつぜん","m":"sudden"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"決定","r":"けってい","m":"decision"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"受け入れがたい","r":"うけいれがたい","m":"hard to accept"},{"t":"だろう","r":"だろう","m":"probably"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"From his viewpoint, this sudden decision would probably be hard to accept.","scene":"A boss changes a colleague's plan at the last minute"},{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"にすれば","r":"にすれば","m":"from a child's view"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"長時間","r":"ちょうじかん","m":"long hours"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"移動","r":"いどう","m":"travel"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"つらい"},{"t":"はずだ","r":"はずだ","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"From a child's viewpoint, traveling for so long must be very tiring.","scene":"A child gets fussy during a long family trip"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-038||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"～にしたら · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にしたら","patternReading":"にしたら","coreMeaning":"From the viewpoint of ~; from ~'s standpoint","slug":"forms","definition":"The pattern **N + にしたら / にすれば / にしてみれば** always comes at the start of a clause, followed by the speaker's judgment from N's viewpoint. N must be **a person or organization noun** (or a personal pronoun). The three forms are interchangeable, differing only in formality and emphasis.","nuance":"にしたら is common in conversation and psychological analysis; にすれば is slightly more formal and frequent in writing; にしてみれば emphasizes **trying to put yourself in that position**, often to encourage the listener to empathize.","structure":[{"lhs":"N (person/organization)","rhs":"にしたら","note":"Casual, common"},{"lhs":"N (person/organization)","rhs":"にすれば","note":"Slightly formal"},{"lhs":"N (person/organization)","rhs":"にしてみれば","note":"Emphasizes trying to put yourself there"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"上司","r":"じょうし","m":"boss"},{"t":"にしたら","r":"にしたら","m":"from the boss's view"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"部下","r":"ぶか","m":"subordinate"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ミス","r":"ミス","m":"mistake"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"許せない","r":"ゆるせない","m":"unforgivable"},{"t":"だろう","r":"だろう","m":"probably"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"From the boss's viewpoint, a subordinate's mistake would probably be unforgivable.","scene":"Discussing a missed deadline with a coworker"},{"tokens":[{"t":"消費者","r":"しょうひしゃ","m":"consumer"},{"t":"にすれば","r":"にすれば","m":"from a consumer's view"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"値上げ","r":"ねあげ","m":"price increase"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"痛い","r":"いたい","m":"painful"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"From a consumer's viewpoint, a price increase hurts.","scene":"Discussing rising fuel prices over a meal"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-038||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"～にしたら · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にしたら","patternReading":"にしたら","coreMeaning":"From the viewpoint of ~; from ~'s standpoint","slug":"usage","definition":"～にしたら often appears when the speaker **explains or defends** someone, or **analyzes psychology** in literature, film, or real life. It adds **depth** by showing a character's viewpoint. In debate, it makes an argument **more persuasive**.","nuance":"Register: it works in both speech and writing, but conversation often uses にしたら, while writing favors にすれば/にしてみれば. This pattern conveys **strong empathy**, so use からすると when you want to remain neutral.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"にしたら","r":"にしたら","m":"from his viewpoint"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"急に","r":"きゅうに","m":"suddenly"},{"t":"転校させられて","r":"てんこうさせられて","m":"be forced to transfer"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"不安","r":"ふあん","m":"anxiety"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"当然","r":"とうぜん","m":"natural"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"From his viewpoint, suddenly being forced to transfer schools, it is natural to feel anxious.","scene":"A mother discusses her child's school transfer with a teacher"},{"tokens":[{"t":"親","r":"おや","m":"parents"},{"t":"にしたら","r":"にしたら","m":"from their viewpoint"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"夜","r":"よる","m":"night"},{"t":"遅く","r":"おそく","m":"late"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"遊んで","r":"あそんで","m":"play"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"心配","r":"しんぱい","m":"worry"},{"t":"だろう","r":"だろう","m":"probably"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"From a parent's viewpoint, they would probably worry if their child played until late at night.","scene":"An outsider comments on parents worrying about a child out late"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-038||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"～にしたら · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にしたら","patternReading":"にしたら","coreMeaning":"From the viewpoint of ~; from ~'s standpoint","slug":"compare","definition":"Many learners confuse にしたら with にとって and からすると. **にとって** emphasizes **importance or value** for someone (私にとって大切な人). **からすると** starts from **evidence or an aspect** to make a judgment (この結果からすると). にしたら takes the **psychological, empathetic viewpoint** of a person.","nuance":"To say 'this is important to me' → にとって. To say 'judging from this sign...' → からすると. To say 'if I were him / from his position...' → にしたら.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"にしたら","r":"にしたら","m":"from his viewpoint"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"提案","r":"ていあん","m":"proposal"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"納得","r":"なっとく","m":"agreement"},{"t":"できない","r":"できない","m":"cannot accept"},{"t":"だろう","r":"だろう","m":"probably"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"From his viewpoint, he probably could not agree to that proposal.","scene":"Considering a coworker's reaction during a meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜にとって","point":"にとって expresses importance or value; にしたら expresses a psychological viewpoint. Examples: 私にとって大切な人 / 彼にしたら不満だろう"},{"vs":"〜からすると","point":"からすると infers from evidence, data, or appearances; にしたら puts you in that person's position. Examples: この結果からすると、失敗だ / 彼にしたら、それは失敗ではない"}]}},"n2-038||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～にしたら · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にしたら","patternReading":"にしたら","coreMeaning":"From the viewpoint of ~; from ~'s standpoint","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The most common error is using にしたら for an **object or abstract situation**. For example, この問題にしたら is wrong; use この問題からすると or この問題は. Also, にしたら cannot replace にとって when discussing importance.","nuance":"When N is **a person**, にしたら is natural. When N is **an object**, switch to からすると (judging from evidence) or にとって (evaluating value).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"にしたら","r":"にしたら","m":"from his viewpoint"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"残業","r":"ざんぎょう","m":"overtime"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"つらい"},{"t":"だろう","r":"だろう","m":"probably"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"From his viewpoint, working overtime must be exhausting.","scene":"Talking about a coworker who often works overtime"}],"pitfalls":["❌ この問題にしたら、難しい。 → ✅ この問題からすると、難しい。 or この問題は難しい。","❌ 私にしたら、家族が一番大切だ。 → ✅ 私にとって、家族が一番大切だ。","❌ 先生にしたら、学生に人気がある。 → ✅ 先生にとって、学生に人気がある。 (Use にとって for evaluation/value.)"],"compare":null}},"n2-038||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～にしたら · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にしたら","patternReading":"にしたら","coreMeaning":"From the viewpoint of ~; from ~'s standpoint","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember **にしたら = に + したら**—roughly **if doing as N** or **if putting yourself in N's place**. When a situation calls for **empathy**, ask yourself: *If I were them, how would I feel?* → use にしたら.","nuance":"This tip helps prevent using it for inanimate objects: only when there are **shoes to step into**—that is, a **person** to embody—should you use にしたら.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-039||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～にすれば · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にすれば","patternReading":"にすれば","coreMeaning":"From N's point of view; considering N's perspective","slug":"core","definition":"The core idea of **～にすれば** is “from N’s point of view,” not simply “for N.” The speaker puts themselves in the position of the person or thing mentioned and gives a feeling or judgment based on N’s perspective.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, with a moderately formal tone. It often conveys **empathy or analysis**: the speaker is not stating an objective fact, but saying “from N’s side, it would seem so.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"にすれば","r":"にすれば","m":"from his view"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"突然","r":"とつぜん","m":"sudden"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"決定","r":"けってい","m":"decision"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"受け入れがたい","r":"うけいれがたい","m":"hard to accept"},{"t":"だろう","r":"だろう","m":"probably"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"From his point of view, this sudden decision must be hard to accept.","scene":"Analyzing a coworker’s feelings after a sudden transfer."},{"tokens":[{"t":"親","r":"おや","m":"parents"},{"t":"にすれば","r":"にすれば","m":"from their view"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"安全","r":"あんぜん","m":"safety"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"一番","r":"いちばん","m":"most"},{"t":"大切","r":"たいせつ","m":"important"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"From the parents’ point of view, their children’s safety matters most.","scene":"Explaining why parents worry when children travel far."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-039||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"～にすれば · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にすれば","patternReading":"にすれば","coreMeaning":"From N's point of view; considering N's perspective","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is simple: **N + にしたら / にすれば / にしてみれば**. All three are nearly interchangeable; **にすれば** is slightly more formal, while **にしてみれば** emphasizes “trying to put yourself in that position.”","nuance":"In casual conversation, **にしたら** sounds most natural. **にしてみれば** is somewhat formal and is often used when asking listeners to imagine the other viewpoint.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"にしたら + [judgment from N’s view]","note":"Casual; common in conversation."},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"にすれば + [judgment]","note":"Slightly more formal."},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"にしてみれば + [judgment]","note":"Emphasizes “trying to put yourself in N’s position.”"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"にしたら","r":"にしたら","m":"from the teacher’s view"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"生徒","r":"せいと","m":"student"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"質問","r":"しつもん","m":"question"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"うれしい","m":"happy"},{"t":"はずだ","r":"はずだ","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"From a teacher’s perspective, having a student ask questions must be wonderful.","scene":"Comforting a young teacher whose class rarely speaks up."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"にしてみれば","r":"にしてみれば","m":"putting yourself in her view"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"その"},{"t":"言葉","r":"ことば","m":"words"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ショック","m":"shock"},{"t":"だった","m":"was"},{"t":"だろう","r":"だろう","m":"probably"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Putting yourself in her shoes, those words must have been a shock.","scene":"Retelling how a friend was criticized publicly."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-039||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"～にすれば · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にすれば","patternReading":"にすれば","coreMeaning":"From N's point of view; considering N's perspective","slug":"usage","definition":"This branch shows practical usage: **～にすれば/にしたら** often explains **why** someone thinks or acts a certain way, especially in psychological analysis, arguments, and defending another person.","nuance":"It works in everyday conversation but sounds slightly analytical. To say only “in my opinion,” use **私としては** instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"上司","r":"じょうし","m":"boss"},{"t":"にすれば","r":"にすれば","m":"from the boss’s view"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"部下","r":"ぶか","m":"subordinate"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ミス","m":"mistake"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"自分","r":"じぶん","m":"one’s own"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ミス","m":"mistake"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"同じ","r":"おなじ","m":"same"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"From the boss’s perspective, a subordinate’s mistake is the same as their own.","scene":"A manager takes responsibility for an employee in a meeting."},{"tokens":[{"t":"店側","r":"みせがわ","m":"the store"},{"t":"にすれば","r":"にすれば","m":"from the store’s view"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"あの"},{"t":"値段","r":"ねだん","m":"price"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"仕方ない","r":"しかたない","m":"unavoidable"},{"t":"だろう","r":"だろう","m":"probably"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"From the store’s perspective, that price is probably unavoidable.","scene":"Explaining a restaurant’s high prices to a complaining customer."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-039||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with similar patterns","sub":"～にすれば · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にすれば","patternReading":"にすれば","coreMeaning":"From N's point of view; considering N's perspective","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch helps distinguish **にすれば** from similar patterns such as **にとって** and **からすると**.","nuance":"にすれば focuses on **the feelings/viewpoint of someone involved**; にとって focuses on **importance**; からすると focuses on **observable evidence**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"にすれば","r":"にすれば","m":"from his view"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"最善","r":"さいぜん","m":"best"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"方法","r":"ほうほう","m":"method"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"From his perspective, this is the best solution.","scene":"Discussing a solution from one person’s point of view."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～にとって","point":"にとって = “what is important or meaningful to someone” → 私にとって大切な人. にすれば = “from someone’s viewpoint” → 彼にすれば不満だろう."},{"vs":"～からすると","point":"からすると = a judgment based on **evidence/observation** → この結果からすると、失敗だ. にすれば = entering someone’s **subjective viewpoint** → 彼にすれば納得できない."}]}},"n2-039||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～にすれば · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にすれば","patternReading":"にすれば","coreMeaning":"From N's point of view; considering N's perspective","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"This branch highlights common mistakes. The key point is that **N in にすれば should be someone capable of feelings**, and is rarely an inanimate object or abstract situation.","nuance":"For an inanimate N, switch to **からすると**. To say “as for me,” use **としては**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"にすれば","r":"にすれば","m":"from his view"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"その"},{"t":"対応","r":"たいおう","m":"response"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"無理もない","r":"むりもない","m":"understandable"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"From his perspective, that response is understandable.","scene":"Seeing a rejected person’s reaction as reasonable."}],"pitfalls":["❌ この問題にしたら、難しい。 → ✅ この問題からすると、難しい。 / この問題は難しい。","❌ 彼にすれば、きっと来る。(if you mean a guess) → ✅ 彼にすれば、来たがっているはずだ。","❌ 私にすれば、それは正しい。 → ✅ 私の考えでは、それは正しい。"],"compare":null}},"n2-039||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～にすれば · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にすれば","patternReading":"にすれば","coreMeaning":"From N's point of view; considering N's perspective","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember the parts: **に (to) + すれば (if doing) = if you put yourself in N’s position**. This leads to the meaning “if I were them, then…”.","nuance":"Imagine **trying on someone else’s shoes**: they are not yours, but you put them on to understand how they feel.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-039||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common collocations","sub":"～にすれば · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にすれば","patternReading":"にすれば","coreMeaning":"From N's point of view; considering N's perspective","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common exam collocations with **にすれば** include **気持ち、無理もない、納得、不満**. When you see these words, にすれば/にしたら is often the answer.","nuance":"These phrases often appear in reading passages and argumentative conversations. They help you identify the grammar pattern quickly.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"親","r":"おや","m":"parents"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"立場","r":"たちば","m":"standpoint"},{"t":"からすれば","r":"からすれば","m":"from the standpoint"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"心配","r":"しんぱい","m":"worry"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"当然","r":"とうぜん","m":"natural"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"From the parents’ standpoint, worrying is perfectly natural.","scene":"Explaining why parents worry when a child is late home."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-040||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～てならない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てならない","patternReading":"てならない","coreMeaning":"I can't help but feel ~; an uncontrollable emotion keeps welling up","slug":"core","definition":"～てならない describes a **strong emotion or feeling that naturally wells up** inside the speaker and becomes **impossible to control**. The speaker does not choose or create the feeling; it rises on its own. It can be translated as **“I can't help but ~”**, **“I keep feeling ~”**, or **“I can't control my ~.”**","nuance":"This is a **formal, primarily written expression** used in diaries, memoirs, letters, and literature. In everyday conversation, Japanese speakers usually use **～てしょうがない** or **～てたまらない** instead. The emotion must be a **natural feeling** such as happiness, sadness, worry, regret, or longing; it is **not** used for deliberate actions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"行方","r":"ゆくえ","m":"whereabouts"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"心配で","r":"しんぱいで","m":"worried"},{"t":"ならない","m":"can't help but feel"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can't help worrying about his whereabouts.","scene":"Writing in a diary while a family member is away."},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"時","r":"とき","m":"time"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"悔やまれて","r":"くやまれて","m":"regret"},{"t":"ならない","m":"can't help but feel"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can't stop regretting that failure from back then.","scene":"Remembering a mistake from the past."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-040||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～てならない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てならない","patternReading":"てならない","coreMeaning":"I can't help but feel ~; an uncontrollable emotion keeps welling up","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is simple: add **～てならない** to the verb or adjective's **て-form**. For an **い-adjective**, change い to くて; for a **な-adjective**, use で. The verb must express an **emotion or mental state** such as 気になる, 悔やむ, 心配する, or 悲しむ.","nuance":"This pattern is rarely further conjugated; it normally appears at the end of an affirmative sentence. Time is understood from context. With action verbs, do not use てならない; use **～ずにはいられない** or **～てしまう** instead.","structure":[{"lhs":"V-て","rhs":"ならない","note":"Emotion verb: 気になってならない"},{"lhs":"いA-くて","rhs":"ならない","note":"嬉しくてならない, 悲しくてならない"},{"lhs":"なA-で","rhs":"ならない","note":"心配でならない, 残念でならない"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"息子","r":"むすこ","m":"son"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"合格","r":"ごうかく","m":"passing"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"嬉しくて","r":"うれしくて","m":"happy"},{"t":"ならない","m":"can't help but feel"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can't contain my joy that my son passed.","scene":"A mother writes in her diary on results day."},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"match"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"負けて","r":"まけて","m":"lose"},{"t":"残念で","r":"ざんねんで","m":"disappointed"},{"t":"ならない","m":"can't help but feel"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can't get over how disappointed I am that we lost that match.","scene":"A former player recalls it in a memoir."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-040||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～てならない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てならない","patternReading":"てならない","coreMeaning":"I can't help but feel ~; an uncontrollable emotion keeps welling up","slug":"usage","definition":"～てならない appears most often in **narrative, emotional writing** such as diaries, memoirs, novels, and personal letters. It emphasizes feelings that **well up and linger painfully**. It can also occur in conversation when the speaker wants a somewhat formal or literary tone.","nuance":"Register: **semi-formal to formal written language**. It suits deep feelings such as **missing one's hometown, worrying about family, regretting a mistake, or being overwhelmingly happy**. For a mild feeling in a chat or text message, it sounds overly dramatic—use **～てしょうがない** instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"故郷","r":"こきょう","m":"hometown"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"こと","m":"matter"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"気になってならない","r":"きになってならない","m":"can't stop worrying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can't stop worrying about things back home.","scene":"Someone away from home writes a letter."},{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"childhood"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"頃","r":"ころ","m":"days"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"懐かしくて","r":"なつかしくて","m":"nostalgic"},{"t":"ならない","m":"can't help but feel"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can't stop longing for my childhood days.","scene":"Writing memoirs at an old desk."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-040||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with てたまらない / てしょうがない","sub":"～てならない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てならない","patternReading":"てならない","coreMeaning":"I can't help but feel ~; an uncontrollable emotion keeps welling up","slug":"compare","definition":"All three patterns—**～てならない / ～てたまらない / ～てしょうがない**—express intense feelings, but they differ in **formality** and emotional focus. **～てならない** is literary and formal; **～てたまらない** is semi-casual and emphasizes unbearable physical feelings or urgent desire; **～てしょうがない** is the most casual and common in conversation.","nuance":"JLPT questions often test this distinction. Remember the formality scale: **てならない (formal) > てたまらない (mid-level) > てしょうがない (casual)**. To describe an **action you cannot stop yourself from doing**, use **～ずにはいられない**, not ～てならない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"こと","m":"matter"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"気になってならない","r":"きになってならない","m":"can't stop worrying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can't help worrying about her.","scene":"A personal letter expressing deep concern."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～てたまらない","point":"Semi-casual; emphasizes an unbearable physical feeling (眠くてたまらない). ～てならない is formal and focuses on inner emotion (悔やまれてならない)."},{"vs":"～てしょうがない","point":"Most casual and common in conversation (気になってしょうがない). ～てならない is written/literary and rarely used in casual speech."},{"vs":"～ずにはいられない","point":"Used for deliberate ACTIONS (食べずにはいられない). ～てならない is only for spontaneous feelings, not actions."}]}},"n2-040||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～てならない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てならない","patternReading":"てならない","coreMeaning":"I can't help but feel ~; an uncontrollable emotion keeps welling up","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"A basic learner error is attaching **～てならない** to every emotion or desire, forgetting that this pattern is **only for spontaneous feelings** and has a **written style**.","nuance":"On the test, if an action verb appears with てならない, it is almost certainly wrong. To say “cannot do without doing,” use **～ずにはいられない**; to say “want something intensely,” use **～てたまらない**; in casual conversation, use **～てしょうがない**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"知らせ","r":"しらせ","m":"news"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞いて","r":"きいて","m":"hear"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"こと","m":"matter"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"心配で","r":"しんぱいで","m":"worried"},{"t":"ならない","m":"can't help but feel"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After hearing the news, I can't help worrying about him.","scene":"In a letter to a close friend."}],"pitfalls":["❌ ラーメンを食べてならない。 → ✅ ラーメンが食べたくてたまらない。 (てならない is NOT used with deliberate actions)","❌ Saying to a close friend: 今日は嬉しくてならない。 → ✅ 今日は嬉しくてしょうがない。 (more casual)","❌ 勉強してならない。 → ✅ 勉強せずにはいられない。 (can't help but study)","❌ 少し疲れてならない。 → ✅ 少し疲れた。 (てならない is not for mild feelings)"],"compare":null}},"n2-040||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～てならない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てならない","patternReading":"てならない","coreMeaning":"I can't help but feel ~; an uncontrollable emotion keeps welling up","slug":"memory","definition":"A machine translation of ならない as “not allowed” can be confusing. Instead, understand it as: **the feeling keeps coming, and you cannot stop it from arriving**. For example, 悔やまれてならない means the regret comes over you by itself.","nuance":"Also remember that ～てならない has a **literary tone**, while ～てしょうがない is its more colloquial counterpart.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-040||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common expressions","sub":"～てならない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てならない","patternReading":"てならない","coreMeaning":"I can't help but feel ~; an uncontrollable emotion keeps welling up","slug":"phrases","definition":"These expressions appear frequently in exams and written Japanese. You can memorize them as fixed chunks for quick recognition and use.","nuance":"They all express **lingering emotions** such as worry, regret, or sadness. You may also encounter 嬉しくてならない for overwhelming happiness.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"心配で","r":"しんぱいで","m":"worried"},{"t":"ならない","m":"can't help but feel"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can't stop worrying about his health.","scene":"In a letter checking on a sick relative."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-041||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ずにはいられない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ずにはいられない","patternReading":"ずにはいられない","coreMeaning":"Cannot help doing ~ (an irresistible impulse)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ずにはいられない expresses an **irresistible impulse**: the speaker cannot help doing action P, even if they know they should not or do not want to. The focus is on an **action** that goes beyond one's control. Unlike 〜てならない, which describes an emotion welling up on its own.","nuance":"Common in **written language** for expressing emotion and in **semi-formal conversation**. It sounds **stronger and more spontaneous** than 〜てしまう. Certainty is unrelated to objective facts—it describes an inner emotional impulse.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","r":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"コメディ","r":"コメディ","m":"comedy film"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見て","r":"みて","m":"watch"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"笑わ","r":"わらわ","m":"laugh"},{"t":"ずにはいられなかった","r":"ずにはいられなかった","m":"couldn't help"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I couldn't help laughing while watching that comedy film.","scene":"Telling a friend about a film you just watched"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"不幸な","r":"ふこうな","m":"unfortunate"},{"t":"話","r":"はなし","m":"story"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞いて","r":"きいて","m":"hear"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"涙","r":"なみだ","m":"tears"},{"t":"せずにはいられなかった","r":"せずにはいられなかった","m":"couldn't help"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Hearing her tragic story, I couldn't hold back my tears.","scene":"Hearing a friend share a sad story and feeling overwhelmed"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-041||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～ずにはいられない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ずにはいられない","patternReading":"ずにはいられない","coreMeaning":"Cannot help doing ~ (an irresistible impulse)","slug":"forms","definition":"Formation: take the **Vない form**, remove **ない**, and add **ずにはいられない**. **する** is exceptional and becomes **せず**, not しず. The past form is **〜ずにはいられなかった**.","nuance":"In polite speech, it can be changed to 〜ずにはいられません. This pattern is formal/written and is less common in casual conversation.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（ない形）","rhs":"〜ずにはいられない","note":"Example: 笑う → 笑わずにはいられない"},{"lhs":"する","rhs":"せずにはいられない","note":"Exception; not しず"},{"lhs":"Vず + にはいられない","rhs":"〜ずにはいられなかった","note":"Past"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"話","r":"はなし","m":"story"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞いたら","r":"きいたら","m":"after hearing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"泣か","r":"なか","m":"cry"},{"t":"ずにはいられない","r":"ずにはいられない","m":"can't help"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After hearing this story, I can't help crying.","scene":"Sharing a moving story"},{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"甘い","r":"あまい","m":"sweet"},{"t":"もの","r":"もの","m":"things"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べ","r":"たべ","m":"eat"},{"t":"ずにはいられない","r":"ずにはいられない","m":"can't help"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can't help eating sweets every day.","scene":"Admitting an addiction to sweets"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-041||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～ずにはいられない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ずにはいられない","patternReading":"ずにはいられない","coreMeaning":"Cannot help doing ~ (an irresistible impulse)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this when an **inner impulse** is so strong that an action bursts out beyond one's control. Typical situations include laughing, crying, getting angry, and commenting on social media.","nuance":"It can express both **positive and negative emotions**. Do not use it for deliberate actions or obligations. Compared with 〜ざるを得ない, this pattern is **emotional**, not **rational**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"思わず","r":"おもわず","m":"without thinking"},{"t":"コメント","r":"コメント","m":"comment"},{"t":"せずにはいられない","r":"せずにはいられない","m":"can't help"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can't help commenting without thinking.","scene":"Seeing an interesting post on social media"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あんな","r":"あんな","m":"such"},{"t":"ひどい","r":"ひどい","m":"terrible"},{"t":"こと","r":"こと","m":"thing"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"されて","r":"されて","m":"be treated"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"怒ら","r":"おこら","m":"get angry"},{"t":"ずにはいられない","r":"ずにはいられない","m":"can't help"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After being treated so badly, I can't help getting angry.","scene":"Being treated unfairly by someone"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"his"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"下手","r":"へた","m":"poor"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"歌","r":"うた","m":"singing"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞いて","r":"きいて","m":"hear"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"笑わ","r":"わらわ","m":"laugh"},{"t":"ずにはいられなかった","r":"ずにはいられなかった","m":"couldn't help"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Hearing him sing so badly, I couldn't help laughing.","scene":"Watching someone sing terribly at karaoke"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-041||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Patterns often confused","sub":"～ずにはいられない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ずにはいられない","patternReading":"ずにはいられない","coreMeaning":"Cannot help doing ~ (an irresistible impulse)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish these three commonly confused patterns: **〜てならない** (an emotion wells up), **〜わけにはいかない** (social duty), and **〜ざるを得ない** (rational necessity). All can suggest 'cannot avoid,' but their underlying motivations differ.","nuance":"〜ずにはいられない emphasizes an **irresistible impulse**, rather than a rational choice or social pressure. It describes what emotion makes you do, not what circumstances require.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"his"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"姿","r":"すがた","m":"appearance"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見たら","r":"みたら","m":"see"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"笑わ","r":"わらわ","m":"laugh"},{"t":"ずにはいられなかった","r":"ずにはいられなかった","m":"couldn't help"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When I saw his expression, I couldn't help laughing.","scene":"Seeing a friend make a funny face"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜てならない","point":"てならない = an emotion wells up (悲しくてならない). ずにはいられない = an uncontrollable action (泣かずにはいられない). Example: 悲しくてならない (unbearably sad) vs 泣かずにはいられない (can't help crying)."},{"vs":"〜わけにはいかない","point":"わけにはいかない = must act because of social duty (謝らないわけにはいかない). ずにはいられない = act because of an inner impulse (笑わずにはいられない)."},{"vs":"〜ざるを得ない","point":"ざるを得ない = forced to act by circumstances or reason (承諾せざるを得ない). ずにはいられない = act because of an irresistible emotion."}]}},"n2-041||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～ずにはいられない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ずにはいられない","patternReading":"ずにはいられない","coreMeaning":"Cannot help doing ~ (an irresistible impulse)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner errors with 〜ずにはいられない include: conjugating **する** as しず instead of せず; using it with an い-adjective such as 悲しい; and confusing it with 〜てならない. Remember the exception する→せず and distinguish actions from emotions to avoid these traps.","nuance":"In JLPT questions, the incorrect **しず** form is a frequent trap. Also check whether the pattern describes an action or merely an emotion, since 〜ずにはいられない cannot attach directly to an い-adjective.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"his"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"うそ","r":"うそ","m":"lie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞いて","r":"きいて","m":"hear"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"怒ら","r":"おこら","m":"get angry"},{"t":"ずにはいられなかった","r":"ずにはいられなかった","m":"couldn't help"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Hearing his lie, I couldn't help getting angry.","scene":"Discovering that a friend lied"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 心配しずにはいられない。 → ✅ 心配せずにはいられない。 (する → せず)","❌ 悲しくてずにはいられない。 → ✅ 悲しくてならない。 / ✅ 泣かずにはいられない。 (いA cannot take ずにはいられない; use てならない for emotions and ずにはいられない for actions)"],"compare":null}},"n2-041||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～ずにはいられない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ずにはいられない","patternReading":"ずにはいられない","coreMeaning":"Cannot help doing ~ (an irresistible impulse)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember: **ずに** means 'without doing,' and **はいられない** means 'cannot remain.' Together: 'cannot remain in a state of not doing P' → **MUST do P**.","nuance":"Imagine watching a comedy, trying to hold back your laughter, but failing—the laughter bursts out. That is the feeling of 〜ずにはいられない.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-041||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～ずにはいられない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ずにはいられない","patternReading":"ずにはいられない","coreMeaning":"Cannot help doing ~ (an irresistible impulse)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases include **笑わずにはいられない** (can't help laughing), **泣かずにはいられない** (can't hold back tears), and **せずにはいられない** (can't help doing). Learn them as collocations for natural usage.","nuance":"Often appears in written reviews and emotional writing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"film"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何度","r":"なんど","m":"how many times"},{"t":"見て","r":"みて","m":"watch"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"感動","r":"かんどう","m":"be moved"},{"t":"せずにはいられない","r":"せずにはいられない","m":"can't help"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No matter how many times I watch this film, I can't help being moved.","scene":"Reviewing a favorite film"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-042||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～わけにはいかない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～わけにはいかない","patternReading":"わけにはいかない","coreMeaning":"Cannot ~ due to norms/circumstances; V-ない + ... = MUST do","slug":"core","definition":"〜わけにはいかない means **cannot do P** because social norms, morality, duty, or circumstances prevent it. With a negative verb (V-ない + わけにはいかない), it means **cannot not do** = **must do**. It is not used for lack of ability.","nuance":"The tone is **firm**, showing **inner pressure** from conscience or social responsibility. It works in both speech and writing, from casual conversation to formal email.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"大事","r":"だいじ","m":"important"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"there is"},{"t":"から","r":"から","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"休む","r":"やすむ","m":"take time off"},{"t":"わけにはいかない","r":"わけにはいかない","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is an important meeting, so I cannot take the day off.","scene":"An employee explains why they cannot go out"},{"tokens":[{"t":"迷惑","r":"めいわく","m":"trouble"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"かけた","r":"かけた","m":"caused"},{"t":"以上","r":"いじょう","m":"since"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"謝らない","r":"あやまらない","m":"not apologize"},{"t":"わけにはいかない","r":"わけにはいかない","m":"cannot not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Since I caused trouble for others, I cannot fail to apologize.","scene":"Reflecting after making a mistake at work"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-042||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"～わけにはいかない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～わけにはいかない","patternReading":"わけにはいかない","coreMeaning":"Cannot ~ due to norms/circumstances; V-ない + ... = MUST do","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern **always combines with a verb**: the dictionary form (V辞書形) or negative form (Vない形). The わけにはいかない part can be put in the past (いかなかった) or polite form (いきません).","nuance":"V辞書形 → **cannot do P**; Vない形 → **cannot not do P** (double negative = must do P).","structure":[{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"わけにはいかない","note":"Cannot do P (due to norms/circumstances)"},{"lhs":"Vない形","rhs":"わけにはいかない","note":"Cannot not do P = MUST do P"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"約束","r":"やくそく","m":"promise"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"破る","r":"やぶる","m":"break"},{"t":"わけにはいかない","r":"わけにはいかない","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I cannot break my promise.","scene":"Telling oneself not to give in to temptation"},{"tokens":[{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"for"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"たばこ"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"やめない","r":"やめない","m":"not quit"},{"t":"わけにはいかない","r":"わけにはいかない","m":"cannot not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For my health, I cannot fail to quit smoking.","scene":"A smoker resolves to quit for their health"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-042||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～わけにはいかない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～わけにはいかない","patternReading":"わけにはいかない","coreMeaning":"Cannot ~ due to norms/circumstances; V-ない + ... = MUST do","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to **explain to someone** why you cannot, or cannot fail to, do something because of **duty, morality, responsibility, or unavoidable circumstances**.","nuance":"It sounds **serious and justified**, rather than merely reluctant. It is useful in emails or messages when making a tactful refusal.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"大事","r":"だいじ","m":"important"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"日","r":"ひ","m":"day"},{"t":"だから","r":"だから","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"遅れる","r":"おくれる","m":"be late"},{"t":"わけにはいかない","r":"わけにはいかない","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Today is an important day, so I cannot be late.","scene":"Declining plans because they cannot be late"},{"tokens":[{"t":"上司","r":"じょうし","m":"boss"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"頼まれた","r":"たのまれた","m":"was asked"},{"t":"から","r":"から","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"断る","r":"ことわる","m":"refuse"},{"t":"わけにはいかない","r":"わけにはいかない","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My boss asked me, so I cannot refuse.","scene":"Telling a colleague they must work overtime"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-042||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"～わけにはいかない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～わけにはいかない","patternReading":"わけにはいかない","coreMeaning":"Cannot ~ due to norms/circumstances; V-ない + ... = MUST do","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish three commonly confused patterns: **〜てはいけない** (objective prohibition), **〜ずにはいられない** (cannot help doing), and **〜わけにはいかない** (cannot due to norms or responsibility).","nuance":"〜わけにはいかない: pressure **from inner social or moral awareness**. 〜てはいけない: an **external rule**. 〜ずにはいられない: an **uncontrollable emotional impulse**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"嘘","r":"うそ","m":"lie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"つく","r":"つく","m":"tell a lie"},{"t":"わけにはいかない","r":"わけにはいかない","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I cannot tell a lie.","scene":"Reflecting when tempted to hide the truth"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜てはいけない","point":"てはいけない = an EXTERNAL RULE forbids it; わけにはいかない = I FEEL I cannot due to norms. Ex: ここに入ってはいけない (rule) vs 嘘をつくわけにはいかない (morality)."},{"vs":"〜ずにはいられない","point":"ずにはいられない = an UNCONTROLLABLE emotional impulse; わけにはいかない = reason or norms forbid it. Ex: 笑わずにはいられない (cannot help laughing) vs 遅れるわけにはいかない (responsibility)."}]}},"n2-042||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors","sub":"～わけにはいかない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～わけにはいかない","patternReading":"わけにはいかない","coreMeaning":"Cannot ~ due to norms/circumstances; V-ない + ... = MUST do","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The most common error is using わけにはいかない for **physical inability**, such as being unable to run because of a leg injury. It is also easily confused with external prohibition.","nuance":"Ask whether the reason for not doing P is **internal (morality/responsibility)** or **external (a rule)**. Use 〜てはいけない for external rules and 〜できない for inability.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"前","r":"まえ","m":"before"},{"t":"だから","r":"だから","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"遊んでいる","r":"あそんでいる","m":"be fooling around"},{"t":"わけにはいかない","r":"わけにはいかない","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"With an exam coming up, I cannot fool around.","scene":"A student turns down an invitation to go out"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 足が痛いから、走るわけにはいかない。 → ✅ 足が痛いから、走れない。 (leg pain means inability; do not use わけにはいかない)","❌ ここに入るわけにはいかない。 → ✅ ここに入ってはいけない。 (external prohibition; use てはいけない)","❌ 約束したから、行くわけにはいかない。 → ✅ 約束したから、行かないわけにはいかない。 (the double negative means you must go)"],"compare":null}},"n2-042||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～わけにはいかない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～わけにはいかない","patternReading":"わけにはいかない","coreMeaning":"Cannot ~ due to norms/circumstances; V-ない + ... = MUST do","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture this: you want to do P, but a **“social reason” wall** blocks you → わけにはいかない. If P is negative, the wall pushes you to do it.","nuance":"Quick test: if replacing it with “cannot” leaves the meaning of **lack of ability** unchanged, わけにはいかない is not the right pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-042||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common expressions","sub":"～わけにはいかない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～わけにはいかない","patternReading":"わけにはいかない","coreMeaning":"Cannot ~ due to norms/circumstances; V-ない + ... = MUST do","slug":"phrases","definition":"In exams and daily life, this pattern often appears with expressions such as **迷惑をかける**, **約束を破る**, **引き受ける**, and **言い出す**. It especially combines with **〜からには / 〜以上** to stress that once you have done P, you cannot stop.","nuance":"With 〜からには/〜以上, the sentence expresses **strong determination** and often uses the double negative (V-ない + わけにはいかない).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"約束","r":"やくそく","m":"promise"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"made"},{"t":"からには","r":"からには","m":"once"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"守らない","r":"まもらない","m":"not keep"},{"t":"わけにはいかない","r":"わけにはいかない","m":"cannot not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once I have made a promise, I cannot fail to keep it.","scene":"The speaker reminds themselves to keep a promise"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-043||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～たびに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～たびに","patternReading":"たびに","coreMeaning":"Whenever ~, ~ (repeated pattern)","slug":"core","definition":"～たびに describes a **repeated pattern**: whenever P occurs, Q reliably follows. The key point is the **consistent, exception-free** relationship between P and Q. Unlike 〜ながら (at the same time) or 〜と (immediately), たびに emphasizes **repetition over multiple occasions**.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing. It often follows verbs for repeated activities or events such as 会う, 行く, 見る, 聴く, and 失敗する. Q is often an emotion, reaction, or comment that occurs **every time**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"会う","r":"あう","m":"meet"},{"t":"たびに","r":"たびに","m":"whenever"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"元気","r":"げんき","m":"energy"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もらう","r":"もらう","m":"receive"},{"t":"気","r":"き","m":"feeling"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"する","r":"する","m":"have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Whenever I meet her, I feel energized.","scene":"A friend always brings positive energy"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"曲","r":"きょく","m":"song"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聴く","r":"きく","m":"listen to"},{"t":"たびに","r":"たびに","m":"whenever"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"学生時代","r":"がくせいじだい","m":"student days"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"思い出す","r":"おもいだす","m":"remember"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Whenever I hear this song, I remember my student days.","scene":"An old song brings back memories"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-043||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & connections","sub":"～たびに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～たびに","patternReading":"たびに","coreMeaning":"Whenever ~, ~ (repeated pattern)","slug":"forms","definition":"～たびに attaches directly to a **verb in dictionary form** or to **noun + の**. This fixed pattern does not change according to tense or negation in the preceding clause. The two forms are V（辞書形） + たびに and N + の + たびに. Examples: 会うたびに (whenever I meet someone), 旅行のたびに (every trip). Do not use the た or ない form before たびに. To express past or negative meaning, place it in the Q clause.","nuance":"Do NOT use the た/ない form before たびに. Past or negative meaning belongs only in Q. A noun must be followed by の.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"+ たびに","note":"Examples: 会うたびに、行くたびに、見るたびに"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"+ の + たびに","note":"Examples: 旅行のたびに、試験のたびに、休みのたびに"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"fail"},{"t":"する","r":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"たびに","r":"たびに","m":"whenever"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"少しずつ","r":"すこしずつ","m":"little by little"},{"t":"成長","r":"せいちょう","m":"grow"},{"t":"している","r":"している","m":"am growing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Every time I fail, I grow a little.","scene":"Learning through setbacks"},{"tokens":[{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"trip"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"たびに","r":"たびに","m":"every time"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"発見","r":"はっけん","m":"discovery"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Every trip brings new discoveries.","scene":"Sharing the fun of traveling"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-043||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～たびに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～たびに","patternReading":"たびに","coreMeaning":"Whenever ~, ~ (repeated pattern)","slug":"usage","definition":"～たびに describes a **repeated action or event** P, with **Q always occurring** whenever P happens. It is very natural in conversation when describing habits, emotions, and experiences.","nuance":"It can also appear in moderately formal writing. In reports, ごとに or 際に is often preferred. Do not use it for an event that occurs only once; use とき instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"たびに","r":"たびに","m":"whenever"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"dish"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"試す","r":"ためす","m":"try"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Whenever I come to this shop, I try a new dish.","scene":"Chatting about a favorite restaurant"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見る","r":"みる","m":"watch"},{"t":"たびに","r":"たびに","m":"whenever"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"泣いて","r":"ないて","m":"crying"},{"t":"しまう","r":"しまう","m":"cannot help"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Every time I watch this movie, I cry.","scene":"Admitting the movie always moves you"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-043||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with と・ごとに・時","sub":"～たびに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～たびに","patternReading":"たびに","coreMeaning":"Whenever ~, ~ (repeated pattern)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish ～たびに from easily confused patterns: ～と, ～ごとに, and ～時. ～と describes a natural, immediate condition and does not emphasize repetition. ～ごとに means “each” interval or unit and does not necessarily express a pattern. ～時 refers to a specific time and may describe one event. ～たびに always involves repeated events and a consistent Q. Examples: 会うたびに恋をする (fall in love every time we meet), 春になると花が咲く (when spring comes, flowers bloom), 3時間ごとに薬を飲む (take medicine every three hours), 昨日テレビを見た時疲れた (I felt tired when I watched TV yesterday).","nuance":"All three patterns can be translated as “when,” but たびに always involves repetition and a patterned reaction.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"曲","r":"きょく","m":"song"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聴く","r":"きく","m":"listen to"},{"t":"たびに","r":"たびに","m":"whenever"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"元気","r":"げんき","m":"energy"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"出る","r":"でる","m":"come out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Whenever I hear this song, I feel energized.","scene":"Compared with と: the song lifts you every time"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～と","point":"と expresses a natural or immediate condition: once P occurs, Q follows as expected. It does not emphasize repeated occasions. Example: 春になると花が咲く. たびに focuses on each repeated occasion: 会うたびに感動する."},{"vs":"～ごとに","point":"ごとに means “each” interval or unit, such as 3時間ごとに = every three hours, or 一人ごとに = each person. It does not necessarily express a repeated P→Q pattern. たびに means each time event P occurs, Q follows."},{"vs":"～時","point":"時 refers to a specific time and may describe one event. たびに requires repetition. Example: 昨日テレビを見たとき疲れた (once) vs 見るたびに泣ける (every time)."}]}},"n2-043||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～たびに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～たびに","patternReading":"たびに","coreMeaning":"Whenever ~, ~ (repeated pattern)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors when using ～たびに. Most importantly, **do not use たびに for an event that occurs only once**, because this pattern requires repetition. The connection must be **V dictionary form or N + の**; never use the た or ない form.","nuance":"When a sentence means “when I did something” but describes only one occasion, use 時. Use ～たびに when you want to emphasize that the result is the same every time.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"曲","r":"きょく","m":"song"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聴く","r":"きく","m":"listen to"},{"t":"たびに","r":"たびに","m":"whenever"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"故郷","r":"こきょう","m":"hometown"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"思い出す","r":"おもいだす","m":"remember"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Whenever I hear this song, I remember my hometown.","scene":"A correct sentence for comparison"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 昨日テレビを見たたびに、疲れた。 → ✅ 昨日テレビを見た**とき**、疲れた。 (たびに expresses repetition; do not use it for a one-time event.)","❌ 会ったたびに、緊張する。 → ✅ 会うたびに、緊張する。 (The verb before たびに must be in dictionary form, not た-form.)","❌ 旅行たびに、新しい発見がある。 → ✅ 旅行**の**たびに、新しい発見がある。 (A noun must be followed by の before たびに.)"],"compare":null}},"n2-043||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～たびに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～たびに","patternReading":"たびに","coreMeaning":"Whenever ~, ~ (repeated pattern)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: **旅 (たび) = trip**. Imagine a trip that repeats: each trip (each time P occurs), Q follows. Example: 会うたびに → each meeting is like another “trip.”","nuance":"Remember: “P happens, Q follows; it keeps repeating, unchanged.” Link たび to the image of an endless loop.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-044||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～一方だ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～一方だ","patternReading":"いっぽうだ","coreMeaning":"Keep ~ing / increasingly ~ (one-way trend)","slug":"core","definition":"～一方だ describes a **continuous change in one direction** with no sign of stopping. The speaker observes reality and states that something keeps increasing, decreasing, or worsening. 一方 means “one direction” or “one side,” so the sentence conveys that there is “only this one direction.”","nuance":"This pattern is **not mainly for complaints**; it primarily records a trend. It is more neutral than ばかりだ and works in formal writing and ordinary conversation. 一方だ cannot describe a completed event—it must refer to an ongoing process.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"物価","r":"ぶっか","m":"prices"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"上がる","r":"あがる","m":"rise"},{"t":"一方だ","r":"いっぽうだ","m":"keep rising"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Prices just keep rising.","scene":"Grocery bills keep getting more expensive."},{"tokens":[{"t":"最近","r":"さいきん","m":"lately"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"残業","r":"ざんぎょう","m":"overtime"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"増える","r":"ふえる","m":"increase"},{"t":"一方だ","r":"いっぽうだ","m":"keep increasing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Lately, my overtime hours just keep increasing.","scene":"Complaining to a coworker after work."},{"tokens":[{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"problem"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"複雑","r":"ふくざつ","m":"complex"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なる"},{"t":"一方だ","r":"いっぽうだ","m":"keep becoming"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The problem is becoming increasingly complex.","scene":"Commenting after a project meeting."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-044||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"～一方だ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～一方だ","patternReading":"いっぽうだ","coreMeaning":"Keep ~ing / increasingly ~ (one-way trend)","slug":"forms","definition":"The only structure is **V（辞書形） + 一方だ**. The verb must express a clear direction of change: 増える (increase), 減る (decrease), 上がる (rise), 下がる (fall), 悪化する (worsen), 進む (progress), or 高くなる (become higher). Do not attach it directly to い-adjectives, な-adjectives, or static states.","nuance":"This pattern **does not conjugate for tense**. To say “has kept …ing from the past until now,” keep the verb in dictionary form and use context to show the time: この十年、人口は減る一方だ. The past form 一方だった is also possible for a trend that occurred during a past period.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"一方だ","note":"V must be a trend-change verb (増える・減る・上がる・悪化する)"},{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"一方だ","note":"Do not use Vた / Vて / past forms; 一方だ follows the dictionary form"},{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"一方だ","note":"Do not attach directly to adjectives (high → 高くなる, not 高い一方だ)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"状況","r":"じょうきょう","m":"situation"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"悪化する","r":"あっかする","m":"worsen"},{"t":"一方だ","r":"いっぽうだ","m":"keep worsening"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The situation just keeps getting worse.","scene":"Discussing project progress before the deadline."},{"tokens":[{"t":"家賃","r":"やちん","m":"rent"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"高くなる","r":"たかくなる","m":"become higher"},{"t":"一方だ","r":"いっぽうだ","m":"keep rising"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Rent just keeps getting higher.","scene":"Looking for a room as rents soar."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-044||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～一方だ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～一方だ","patternReading":"いっぽうだ","coreMeaning":"Keep ~ing / increasingly ~ (one-way trend)","slug":"usage","definition":"～一方だ often appears in news reports about the economy, society, and the environment. When you see it, focus on the idea of a “continuously rising or falling trend.” In everyday speech, Japanese speakers use it to complain about overtime, expenses, and work pressure.","nuance":"Do not use it for a one-time action, such as “sleeping straight through.” It also does not describe an unchanged state such as 高い or 静か.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"少子化","r":"しょうしか","m":"declining birthrate"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"進む","r":"すすむ","m":"progress"},{"t":"一方だ","r":"いっぽうだ","m":"keep progressing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The declining birthrate is becoming increasingly serious.","scene":"Watching a population report on TV."},{"tokens":[{"t":"事故","r":"じこ","m":"accidents"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"増える","r":"ふえる","m":"increase"},{"t":"一方だ","r":"いっぽうだ","m":"keep increasing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Accidents just keep increasing.","scene":"Hearing the morning traffic news."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-044||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast: ばかりだ・続ける・一方で","sub":"～一方だ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～一方だ","patternReading":"いっぽうだ","coreMeaning":"Keep ~ing / increasingly ~ (one-way trend)","slug":"compare","definition":"The three easiest patterns to confuse are **～ばかりだ**, **～続ける**, and **～一方で**. ～ばかりだ tends to describe a situation getting worse; ～続ける describes a subject intentionally continuing an action; ～一方で is a contrastive connector unrelated to trends.","nuance":"Quick tip: 一方だ focuses on V = a change verb moving in one direction; ばかりだ focuses on a bad or unfavorable result; 続ける focuses on a subject intentionally continuing; 一方で focuses on two contrasting clauses.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"物価","r":"ぶっか","m":"prices"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上がる","r":"あがる","m":"rise"},{"t":"一方だ","r":"いっぽうだ","m":"keep rising"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Prices just keep rising.","scene":"A model example: 一方だ = one-way trend."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～ばかりだ","point":"ばかりだ emphasizes that things are getting WORSE or more unfavorable; 一方だ only states a one-way trend, which may be good or bad."},{"vs":"～続ける","point":"続ける means the subject intentionally continues an action (働き続ける); 一方だ describes an objective trend beyond the subject's control."},{"vs":"～一方で","point":"一方で expresses CONTRAST, “on the other hand” (親切な一方で、厳しい); 一方だ expresses a CONTINUOUS TREND (悪化する一方だ)."}]}},"n2-044||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common learner traps","sub":"～一方だ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～一方だ","patternReading":"いっぽうだ","coreMeaning":"Keep ~ing / increasingly ~ (one-way trend)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often use 一方だ with **adjectives describing states** or **repeated actions without a direction**. Both are wrong because 一方だ requires a change verb moving in one direction. Another common error is confusing 一方だ with 一方で.","nuance":"To use 一方だ with an adjective, turn it into a change verb: 高い → 高くなる; 静か → 静かになる. To say “on the other hand,” use 一方で, not 一方だ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"患者","r":"かんじゃ","m":"patients"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"数","r":"かず","m":"number"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"増える","r":"ふえる","m":"increase"},{"t":"一方だ","r":"いっぽうだ","m":"keep increasing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The number of patients just keeps increasing.","scene":"A doctor talks with a nurse."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼は親切な一方だ。 → ✅ 彼は親切な一方で、厳しい面もある。","❌ 昨日は楽しい一方だ。 → ✅ 物価は上がる一方だ。 (一方だ is not used for one-time events or static states)","❌ 私は食べる一方だ。 → ✅ 最近、外食が増える一方だ。 (V must show a directional change)","❌ 家賃が高い一方だ。 → ✅ 家賃が高くなる一方だ。"],"compare":null}},"n2-044||memory":{"title":"🎯 Visual memory tip","sub":"～一方だ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～一方だ","patternReading":"いっぽうだ","coreMeaning":"Keep ~ing / increasingly ~ (one-way trend)","slug":"memory","definition":"The kanji 一方 means **one direction or one side**. Adding だ asserts that “there is only this one direction.” Imagine **a train moving straight ahead** that never reverses, or water **flowing in one direction** forever.","nuance":"This tip helps you remember two things: (1) the trend cannot turn back or reverse, and (2) the verb must have a clear “direction”—increase, decrease, worsen, or improve. Without a direction, use 一方で or another pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-044||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common JLPT phrases","sub":"～一方だ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～一方だ","patternReading":"いっぽうだ","coreMeaning":"Keep ~ing / increasingly ~ (one-way trend)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common exam phrases with 一方だ include **上がる一方だ, 増える一方だ, 悪化する一方だ, 減る一方だ, 進む一方だ, 高くなる一方だ**. Common vocabulary includes 物価, 少子化, 残業, 患者, 事故, and 状況.","nuance":"In exam questions, Vる often appears before the blank, so you only need to fill in 一方だ. In reading passages, summarize the idea as a “continuous one-way trend” to answer questions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"景気","r":"けいき","m":"economy"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"悪化する","r":"あっかする","m":"worsen"},{"t":"一方だ","r":"いっぽうだ","m":"keep worsening"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The economy just keeps getting worse.","scene":"Watching the evening news."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-045||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～一方で · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～一方で","patternReading":"いっぽうで","coreMeaning":"Meanwhile / On the other hand — contrasting two aspects","slug":"core","definition":"〜一方で expresses **two contrasting or complementary aspects** of the same event or topic. The speaker remains neutral and **acknowledges that both sides are true**, without judging either as good or bad. This is a formal structure, especially common in analytical writing, reports, and essays.","nuance":"Often used in **written or formal speech**, and less common in everyday conversation. It sounds **objective and analytical**, without strong emotion. Important: **〜一方で ≠ 〜一方だ**; 一方で means 'on one hand... on the other hand,' while 一方だ means 'continues to... (one-way trend).'","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"インターネット","m":"internet"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"便利","r":"べんり","m":"convenient"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"一方で","r":"いっぽうで","m":"on the other hand"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"プライバシー","m":"privacy"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"problems"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"多い","r":"おおい","m":"many"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The Internet is convenient, but on the other hand, it also raises many privacy issues.","scene":"An essay on the impact of the Internet"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"優秀","r":"ゆうしゅう","m":"excellent"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"研究者","r":"けんきゅうしゃ","m":"researcher"},{"t":"である","m":"is"},{"t":"一方で","r":"いっぽうで","m":"on the other hand"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"優れた","r":"すぐれた","m":"talented"},{"t":"教育者","r":"きょういくしゃ","m":"educator"},{"t":"でも","m":"also"},{"t":"ある","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is both an excellent researcher and a talented educator.","scene":"Introducing someone with two complementary roles"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-045||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"～一方で · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～一方で","patternReading":"いっぽうで","coreMeaning":"Meanwhile / On the other hand — contrasting two aspects","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes the **connection patterns** for 〜一方で. The preceding clause uses the **plain form of a verb / イAい / ナAな / Nである**, followed by a contrasting or complementary clause. 一方（で） can also begin a sentence as a connector between two contrasting sentences.","nuance":"Do not separately make the preceding clause polite or past; for the past, use **Vた一方で**. With nouns, **である一方で** is preferred to **の一方で**. In formal writing, で may become **では** to emphasize 'in this respect.'","structure":[{"lhs":"V plain form (る/た/ない)","rhs":"＋ 一方で（は）、B","note":"Examples: 働く一方で / 増える一方で"},{"lhs":"イA (い)","rhs":"＋ 一方で（は）、B","note":"Examples: 忙しい一方で / 新しい一方で"},{"lhs":"ナA (な)","rhs":"＋ 一方で（は）、B","note":"Examples: 便利な一方で / 簡単な一方で"},{"lhs":"N（である）","rhs":"＋ 一方で（は）、B","note":"Examples: 医者である一方で / 社長である一方で"},{"lhs":"Clause A。","rhs":"一方（で）、Clause B。","note":"Sentence connector — emphasizes the contrast in the second clause"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"スマホ","m":"smartphone"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"便利","r":"べんり","m":"convenient"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"一方で","r":"いっぽうで","m":"on the other hand"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"バッテリー","m":"battery"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"すぐ","m":"quickly"},{"t":"減る","r":"へる","m":"decreases"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This smartphone is convenient, but on the other hand, its battery drains very quickly.","scene":"Commenting on a smartphone"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"医者","r":"いしゃ","m":"doctor"},{"t":"である","m":"is"},{"t":"一方で","r":"いっぽうで","m":"on the other hand"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"小説家","r":"しょうせつか","m":"novelist"},{"t":"でも","m":"also"},{"t":"ある","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is both a doctor and a novelist.","scene":"Introducing someone's dual professions"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-045||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and contexts","sub":"～一方で · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～一方で","patternReading":"いっぽうで","coreMeaning":"Meanwhile / On the other hand — contrasting two aspects","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern is commonly used to **place two aspects side by side** for analysis—for example, to state both benefits and drawbacks, or two simultaneous roles or characteristics of a person or thing. It can appear within a sentence (A一方でB) or at the start of a sentence (A。一方、B。).","nuance":"It is not used in casual conversation and sounds formal and objective. At the beginning of a sentence, **一方** usually means 'in contrast / meanwhile,' clearly contrasting the two clauses.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"都市部","r":"としぶ","m":"urban areas"},{"t":"では"},{"t":"人口","r":"じんこう","m":"population"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"増えて","r":"ふえて","m":"increases"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"},{"t":"一方","r":"いっぽう","m":"meanwhile"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"地方","r":"ちほう","m":"rural areas"},{"t":"では"},{"t":"過疎化","r":"かそか","m":"depopulation"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"進んで","r":"すすんで","m":"progresses"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The urban population is increasing. Meanwhile, depopulation is worsening in rural areas.","scene":"A newspaper article analyzing population trends"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"アプリ","m":"app"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"便利","r":"べんり","m":"convenient"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"一方で","r":"いっぽうで","m":"on the other hand"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"広告","r":"こうこく","m":"advertisements"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"多い","r":"おおい","m":"many"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This app is convenient, but on the other hand, it has a lot of advertisements.","scene":"Discussing an app's pros and cons"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-045||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions from similar patterns","sub":"～一方で · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～一方で","patternReading":"いっぽうで","coreMeaning":"Meanwhile / On the other hand — contrasting two aspects","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes 〜一方で from commonly confused patterns. Most importantly, do not confuse it with **〜一方だ**: 一方で means 'one side... the other side,' while 一方だ means 'continues to...'—similar pronunciation, completely different meaning.","nuance":"Among contrast patterns, **一方で** is more formal and objective than **が/けれど**. Compared with **反面**, 一方で can add a complementary point without requiring a contradiction, while 反面 emphasizes conflict.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"job"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"やりがい","m":"fulfilling"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"has"},{"t":"一方で","r":"いっぽうで","m":"on the other hand"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"プレッシャー","m":"pressure"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"大きい","r":"おおきい","m":"great"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This job is fulfilling, but on the other hand, the pressure is also great.","scene":"Talking about one's job"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～反面","point":"Both present two contrasting sides, but 反面 emphasizes conflict; 一方で can present two parallel aspects. Examples: 便利な一方で、問題も多い / 便利な反面、問題も多い."},{"vs":"～が/けれど","point":"が/けれど is a neutral contrast used in conversation; 一方で is formal and analytical. Examples: 便利だが問題もある (casual) / 便利な一方で問題もある (analytical)."},{"vs":"～一方だ","point":"一方だ = a one-way trend ('increasing more and more'); 一方で = comparing two sides. Examples: 物価が上がる一方だ (prices keep rising) / 物価が上がる一方で、給料は変わらない."}]}},"n2-045||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～一方で · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～一方で","patternReading":"いっぽうで","coreMeaning":"Meanwhile / On the other hand — contrasting two aspects","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner errors: 1) using **一方で** for a one-way trend (use **一方だ**); 2) making clause B a direct result instead of a contrasting or complementary aspect; 3) using it in an overly casual context.","nuance":"This pattern does **not imply a complaint** like 一方だ. The clause after 一方で must be a parallel observation, not a consequence. To say 'increasing more and more,' use 一方だ!","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"job"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"大変","r":"たいへん","m":"demanding"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"一方で","r":"いっぽうで","m":"on the other hand"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"やりがい","m":"fulfilling"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"ある","m":"has"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This job is demanding, but it is also fulfilling.","scene":"Giving a balanced opinion about a job"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 物価が上がる一方で、困っている。\n→ ✅ 物価が上がる一方だ。困っている。\n→ または ✅ 物価が上がる一方で、給料は変わらない。","❌ 彼は毎日勉強している一方で、試験に落ちた。\n→ ✅ 彼は試験が得意な一方で、スポーツは苦手だ。","❌ この辺りは静か一方で、住みやすい。\n→ ✅ この辺りは静かな一方で、住みやすい。 (Missing な)"],"compare":null}},"n2-046||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～に応じて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に応じて","patternReading":"におうじて","coreMeaning":"Depending on ~; adapting to ~","slug":"core","definition":"〜に応じて describes an action or decision **Q changing and adapting flexibly to N** (a condition, situation, or need). N is usually a variable factor such as season, ability, demand, or opinion. It generally means **“depending on” / “in accordance with.”**","nuance":"This pattern is **formal**, common in writing and work-related settings such as contracts, reports, and notices. It emphasizes **deliberate adjustment**, unlike the objective dependence expressed by 〜によって.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"能力","r":"のうりょく","m":"ability"},{"t":"に応じて","r":"におうじて","m":"depending on"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"給料","r":"きゅうりょう","m":"salary"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"決まる","r":"きまる","m":"is decided"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The salary is decided according to ability.","scene":"A new employee asks about the company’s pay policy"},{"tokens":[{"t":"季節","r":"きせつ","m":"season"},{"t":"に応じて","r":"におうじて","m":"depending on"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"メニュー","m":"menu"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"変えて","r":"かえて","m":"change"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The menu is changed according to the season.","scene":"A restaurant owner explains why the menu often changes"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-046||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & conjugation","sub":"～に応じて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に応じて","patternReading":"におうじて","coreMeaning":"Depending on ~; adapting to ~","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern connects only to **nouns**. に応じて works adverbially to modify the following clause; **に応じた + N** works adjectivally to modify a noun. The に応じた form is especially common in administrative writing.","nuance":"Writing に応じる before a verb or noun is incorrect here. Use に応じて before a verb and に応じた before a noun.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"に応じて + V","note":"Action/state Q changes according to N"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"に応じた + N","note":"Adjectival: N2 suited to N1"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お客様","r":"おきゃくさま","m":"customer"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"要望","r":"ようぼう","m":"request"},{"t":"に応じて","r":"におうじて","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"サービス","m":"service"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"変更","r":"へんこう","m":"change"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We change the service according to customer requests.","scene":"A customer service representative replies to an email"},{"tokens":[{"t":"状況","r":"じょうきょう","m":"situation"},{"t":"に応じた","r":"におうじた","m":"suited to"},{"t":"対応","r":"たいおう","m":"response"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"必要","r":"ひつよう","m":"necessary"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A response suited to each situation is necessary.","scene":"A meeting discusses incident response"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-046||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～に応じて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に応じて","patternReading":"におうじて","coreMeaning":"Depending on ~; adapting to ~","slug":"usage","definition":"〜に応じて often appears in **administrative writing, contracts, reports, and workplace conversation**. It expresses **flexible adjustment** according to a changing condition.","nuance":"It is less common in casual speech and sounds somewhat formal. It is natural when speaking to a boss, business partner, or customer. The flexibility is **intentional**, not random.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"需要","r":"じゅよう","m":"demand"},{"t":"に応じて","r":"におうじて","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"生産量","r":"せいさんりょう","m":"production volume"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"調整","r":"ちょうせい","m":"adjust"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Adjust production volume according to demand.","scene":"A company production report"},{"tokens":[{"t":"状況","r":"じょうきょう","m":"situation"},{"t":"に応じて","r":"におうじて","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"対応","r":"たいおう","m":"response"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"変えて","r":"かえて","m":"change"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please change your response according to the situation.","scene":"A boss tells staff how to handle complaints"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-046||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with によって・に従って","sub":"～に応じて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に応じて","patternReading":"におうじて","coreMeaning":"Depending on ~; adapting to ~","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes 〜に応じて from two common patterns: **〜によって** (dependence/cause) and **〜に従って** (following). All three may loosely translate as “according to,” but their nuances differ greatly.","nuance":"に応じて is **deliberate and flexible**. によって focuses on **objective differences, means, or causes**. に従って focuses on **following** an existing rule or procedure.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お客様","r":"おきゃくさま","m":"customer"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ニーズ","m":"needs"},{"t":"に応じて","r":"におうじて","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"商品","r":"しょうひん","m":"product"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"開発","r":"かいはつ","m":"develop"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Develop products according to customer needs.","scene":"The sales team presents a new product plan"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜によって","point":"によって = the result varies or a cause is given, without deliberate adjustment. 例: 人によって意見が違う (not 人に応じて). 台風によって電車が止まる (not 台風に応じて)."},{"vs":"〜に従って","point":"に従って = follow an existing rule, guideline, or standard. 例: 規則に従って行動する (rather than 応じて, because the rule is mandatory)."}]}},"n2-046||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～に応じて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に応じて","patternReading":"におうじて","coreMeaning":"Depending on ~; adapting to ~","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner errors include using に応じて for causes, forgetting to change it to に応じた before a noun, or connecting it directly to a clause.","nuance":"When you mean “because of” or “due to,” use によって. When you mean “suitable for N2,” remember に応じた + N2.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"体力","r":"たいりょく","m":"physical strength"},{"t":"に応じた","r":"におうじた","m":"suited to"},{"t":"運動","r":"うんどう","m":"exercise"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"こと","m":"act of"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"大切","r":"たいせつ","m":"important"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Exercise suited to your physical strength is important.","scene":"A doctor advises a patient"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 台風に応じて、電車が止まった。 → ✅ 台風**によって**、電車が止まった。","❌ 人に応じて、意見が違う。 → ✅ 人**によって**、意見が違う。","❌ 状況に応じる対応が必要だ。 → ✅ 状況に**応じた**対応が必要だ。"],"compare":null}},"n2-046||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～に応じて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に応じて","patternReading":"におうじて","coreMeaning":"Depending on ~; adapting to ~","slug":"memory","definition":"The kanji 応 suggests **responding or meeting a need**. Picture a flexible staff member who **responds to** customer requests: whatever the customer needs, they do. This image helps you remember に応じて.","nuance":"This tip helps distinguish に応じて from によって. によって simply observes objective variation, such as “depending on the person or time,” while に応じて involves **people deliberately adjusting** something to fit. The flexible staff member is the key image.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-046||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～に応じて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に応じて","patternReading":"におうじて","coreMeaning":"Depending on ~; adapting to ~","slug":"phrases","definition":"The phrases **Nに応じたN** are very common in JLPT reading passages, company reports, and administrative documents: 需要に応じた生産調整, 能力に応じた給料, 状況に応じた対応.","nuance":"These phrases are somewhat **formulaic**—just substitute an appropriate noun for N. Memorizing a few common combinations makes reading faster.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"能力","r":"のうりょく","m":"ability"},{"t":"に応じて","r":"におうじて","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"任せる","r":"まかせる","m":"assign"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Assign work according to ability.","scene":"A manager assigns tasks to the team"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-047||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～さえ～ば · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～さえ～ば","patternReading":"さえば","coreMeaning":"As long as ~ / Provided that ~ (the sole sufficient condition)","slug":"core","definition":"さえ～ば expresses P as the **minimum and sole condition** needed for Q to occur. In “PさえVば Q,” the listener understands that **nothing besides P is needed**; once P exists, Q will become reality. This pattern is often used to encourage someone, make an argument, or state a very clear logic.","nuance":"It works in both speech and writing and is slightly formal. The speaker wants to **simplify the issue**, creating a strong, sometimes mechanical impression. It can be used with superiors or customers when encouraging them, but be careful because it sounds very certain.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"さえ","m":"only need"},{"t":"あれば","r":"あれば","m":"have"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何でも","r":"なんでも","m":"anything"},{"t":"買える","r":"かえる","m":"can buy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as you have money, you can buy anything.","scene":"A friend who spends freely says this catchphrase"},{"tokens":[{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"さえ","m":"only need"},{"t":"あれば","r":"あれば","m":"have"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"それ"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"十分","r":"じゅうぶん","m":"enough"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as you are healthy, that is enough.","scene":"A family member comforts you about financial stress"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-047||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～さえ～ば · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～さえ～ば","patternReading":"さえば","coreMeaning":"As long as ~ / Provided that ~ (the sole sufficient condition)","slug":"forms","definition":"さえ attaches **immediately after the element to be emphasized**, while ば is the conditional form of the following verb or adjective. With nouns, use さえあれば; with verbs, use the ます-stem followed by さえすれば.","nuance":"There is no major difference in politeness; this is a neutral pattern. With な-adjectives, **で is mandatory** before さえ (静かでさえあれば); do not omit で.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"さえ + Vば / あれば","note":"It is enough for N to exist."},{"lhs":"Vます-stem","rhs":"さえ + すれば","note":"You only need to perform action V."},{"lhs":"いA stem","rhs":"く + さえ + あれば","note":"It is enough to have that quality."},{"lhs":"なA stem","rhs":"で + さえ + あれば","note":"It is enough to have that quality."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"し","r":"し","m":"do"},{"t":"さえ","m":"only need"},{"t":"すれば","r":"すれば","m":"if you do"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"必ず","r":"かならず","m":"surely"},{"t":"結果","r":"けっか","m":"results"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ついてくる","r":"ついてくる","m":"will follow"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as you make an effort, results will surely follow.","scene":"A manager encourages a new employee"},{"tokens":[{"t":"安く","r":"やすく","m":"cheap"},{"t":"さえ","m":"only need"},{"t":"あれば","r":"あれば","m":"be"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"買う","r":"かう","m":"buy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If it is cheap, I will buy it.","scene":"Answering whether to buy a coat"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-047||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～さえ～ば · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～さえ～ば","patternReading":"さえば","coreMeaning":"As long as ~ / Provided that ~ (the sole sufficient condition)","slug":"usage","definition":"さえ～ば is used when the speaker wants to reduce everything to **one sole condition**. In conversation, it often appears in encouragement such as “Just try your best.” In argumentative writing, it creates a rigorous “sufficient condition” argument.","nuance":"Its tone is somewhat strong because it asserts that Q will certainly occur if P is present. Use it only when **your logic is genuinely sufficient and sound**. If P is just one of several conditions, the sentence may sound mechanical or invite disagreement.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"辞書","r":"じしょ","m":"dictionary"},{"t":"さえ","m":"only need"},{"t":"あれば","r":"あれば","m":"have"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"どんな","r":"どんな","m":"any"},{"t":"単語","r":"たんご","m":"word"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"調べられる","r":"しらべられる","m":"can look up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as you have a dictionary, you can look up any word.","scene":"Talking to a classmate while preparing for a kanji test"},{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"さえ","m":"only need"},{"t":"あれば","r":"あれば","m":"have"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"手伝える","r":"てつだえる","m":"can help"},{"t":"のに","m":"though"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If only I had the time, I could help you.","scene":"Declining a request because you are busy"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-047||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"～さえ～ば · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～さえ～ば","patternReading":"さえば","coreMeaning":"As long as ~ / Provided that ~ (the sole sufficient condition)","slug":"compare","definition":"さえ～ば and ～なら both express conditions, but さえ～ば is much stronger: it explicitly says P is the sole sufficient condition. ～てさえいれば is a variant of さえ～ば used only with て-form verbs, emphasizing maintaining a state continuously.","nuance":"With さえ～ば, the speaker is fully confident; with ～なら, the speaker may still be uncertain. ～てさえいれば often emphasizes “as long as you keep doing P.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"さえ","m":"only need"},{"t":"覚えれば","r":"おぼえれば","m":"if you remember"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"文章","r":"ぶんしょう","m":"text"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"読める","r":"よめる","m":"can read"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as you remember the kanji, you can read the text.","scene":"A Japanese learner reassures themself"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～なら","point":"～さえ～ば emphasizes that P is the SOLE sufficient condition for Q; ～なら merely assumes “if P, then Q,” without saying P alone is enough. Example: お金さえあれば何でも買える (money alone is enough) vs お金があるなら買える (if you have money, you may be able to buy it, but other factors may matter)."},{"vs":"～てさえいれば","point":"～てさえいれば is used with Vて and emphasizes CONTINUOUSLY MAINTAINING a state; ～さえ～ば works with nouns, adjectives, and verbs and focuses on existence or a single occurrence."}]}},"n2-047||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～さえ～ば · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～さえ～ば","patternReading":"さえば","coreMeaning":"As long as ~ / Provided that ~ (the sole sufficient condition)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make mistakes by placing さえ incorrectly or using the wrong verb form. Another problem is logical: using さえ～ば when the condition is not truly the sole requirement.","nuance":"In JLPT questions, the test often checks **the position of さえ** and **the conditional ば form**. Always check: is the form before ば actually conditional, and does さえ follow the correct focused word?","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"さえ","m":"only need"},{"t":"あれば","r":"あれば","m":"have"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大丈夫","r":"だいじょうぶ","m":"fine"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as we have time, everything will be fine.","scene":"Reassuring a friend worried about running late"}],"pitfalls":["❌ お金があればさえ、... → ✅ お金さえあれば、... (さえ must come before the conditional ば form, not after ば.)","❌ 食べるさえすれば、... → ✅ 食べさえすれば、... (Use the ます-stem: 食べます → 食べ + さえすれば.)","❌ 静かさえあれば、... → ✅ 静かでさえあれば、... (With な-A, で must come before さえ.)","❌ 東京にさえ行くば、... → ✅ 東京にさえ行けば、... (The following clause must use the conditional ば form, not the dictionary form.)"],"compare":null}},"n2-047||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～さえ～ば · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～さえ～ば","patternReading":"さえば","coreMeaning":"As long as ~ / Provided that ~ (the sole sufficient condition)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine さえ as a key and ば as a lock. Insert the right key (P), and the Q door opens immediately; nothing else is needed. Easy reminder: “さえ” = even/only, “ば” = if.","nuance":"This is a visual memory aid, not a grammatical nuance. Picture a bunch of keys, but only ONE opens the door.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-047||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases & collocations","sub":"～さえ～ば · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～さえ～ば","patternReading":"さえば","coreMeaning":"As long as ~ / Provided that ~ (the sole sufficient condition)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed phrases appear frequently in JLPT questions and everyday conversation. Memorize them to respond quickly in tests and while listening.","nuance":"Note that 元気でさえあれば is な-A + で + さえ + あれば, and is easily mistaken for 元気さえあれば.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"さえ","m":"only need"},{"t":"あれば","r":"あれば","m":"have"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"どこへでも","r":"どこへでも","m":"anywhere"},{"t":"行ける","r":"いける","m":"can go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as I have time, I can go anywhere.","scene":"Talking about travel plans"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-048||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～てこそ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てこそ","patternReading":"てこそ","coreMeaning":"Only through/when ~ does Q have meaning (an essential condition for Q)","slug":"core","definition":"～てこそ expresses that **P is essential** for Q to truly have meaning and value. It is not an ordinary conditional, but asserts that only after experiencing P does Q become worthwhile and precious. Speakers often use it to **praise effort and hardship** or inspire others.","nuance":"It has a **formal, philosophical tone**, common in speeches, memoirs, and profound advice. Unlike ば/たら (neutral conditions), てこそ emphasizes **P's special value**, as though Q would be meaningless without P. It conveys strong admiration and encouragement.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"苦労","r":"くろう","m":"hardship"},{"t":"してこそ","r":"してこそ","m":"only through"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"成功","r":"せいこう","m":"success"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"喜び","r":"よろこび","m":"joy"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかる","m":"understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only through hardship can one understand the joy of success.","scene":"A father teaches his child the value of hard work."},{"tokens":[{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"してこそ","r":"してこそ","m":"only through"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"本当","r":"ほんとう","m":"true"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"実力","r":"じつりょく","m":"ability"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかる","m":"understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only after failure can you discover your true ability.","scene":"A coach comforts an athlete after a loss."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-048||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"～てこそ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てこそ","patternReading":"てこそ","coreMeaning":"Only through/when ~ does Q have meaning (an essential condition for Q)","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **V-て + こそ + Q**. V is a verb in the て-form, already suggesting 'do P and then' or 'first do P.' こそ attaches directly afterward to emphasize P. With ている, it becomes **V-ていてこそ**, emphasizing a maintained state. The pattern has no separate negative or past form; tense and negation appear in Q.","nuance":"This is a **formal, written pattern**. In everyday conversation, Japanese speakers often use ば/たら instead. It is emphatic and inspirational, and is not used for trivial physical conditions.","structure":[{"lhs":"V-て","rhs":"こそ + Q","note":"P is essential for Q to have meaning"},{"lhs":"V-ていて","rhs":"こそ + Q","note":"emphasizes maintaining state P"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"してこそ","r":"してこそ","m":"only through"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"夢","r":"ゆめ","m":"dream"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"叶う","r":"かなう","m":"come true"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only through effort can dreams come true.","scene":"A speaker inspires graduates at a ceremony."},{"tokens":[{"t":"健康で","r":"けんこうで","m":"healthy"},{"t":"いてこそ","r":"いてこそ","m":"only by staying"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何事","r":"なにごと","m":"everything"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"楽しめる","r":"たのしめる","m":"enjoy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only by staying healthy can you enjoy everything.","scene":"A doctor advises a patient to value their health."},{"tokens":[{"t":"自分","r":"じぶん","m":"oneself"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"経験","r":"けいけん","m":"experience"},{"t":"してこそ","r":"してこそ","m":"only through"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"本当","r":"ほんとう","m":"true"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"理解","r":"りかい","m":"understanding"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"できる","m":"can gain"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only through personal experience can one truly understand.","scene":"A teacher says reading alone is not enough."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-048||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and contexts","sub":"～てこそ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てこそ","patternReading":"てこそ","coreMeaning":"Only through/when ~ does Q have meaning (an essential condition for Q)","slug":"usage","definition":"～てこそ is used when the speaker wants to **emphasize the importance of a process or experience** before attaining the value of Q. It commonly appears in philosophical or encouraging statements, or when praising sacrifice and effort. It is not used for ordinary conditions such as 'if you have money, you can buy a ticket.'","nuance":"Typical contexts include **speeches, essays, memoirs, and advice**, where the speaker wants emotional depth. In everyday conversation it can sound theatrical, unless used jokingly or for strong emphasis.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"してこそ","r":"してこそ","m":"only through"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"成長","r":"せいちょう","m":"growth"},{"t":"できる","m":"can achieve"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only through failure can one grow.","scene":"A boss speaks to a young employee after a mistake."},{"tokens":[{"t":"若い","r":"わかい","m":"young"},{"t":"うちに","r":"うちに","m":"while"},{"t":"苦労","r":"くろう","m":"hardship"},{"t":"してこそ","r":"してこそ","m":"only through"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大人","r":"おとな","m":"adult"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なれる","r":"なれる","m":"become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only through hardship when young can one become an adult.","scene":"A principal speaks at an opening ceremony."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-048||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with てはじめて・さえ〜ば","sub":"～てこそ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てこそ","patternReading":"てこそ","coreMeaning":"Only through/when ~ does Q have meaning (an essential condition for Q)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare ～てこそ with nearby conditional and causal patterns to avoid logical mistakes. Each has a different focus: てこそ emphasizes value, てはじめて timing, さえ～ば the minimum condition, and ばこそ the cause.","nuance":"Place each pattern in a realistic context when comparing them. てこそ is usually more formal and emotional, while the others can be used in speech or neutral writing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"経験","r":"けいけん","m":"experience"},{"t":"してこそ","r":"してこそ","m":"only through"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"その","r":"その","m":"its"},{"t":"大切さ","r":"たいせつさ","m":"importance"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかる","m":"understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only through experience can one understand its importance.","scene":"Working away from home makes someone value their parents' support."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜てはじめて","point":"〜てはじめて emphasizes **time order**: after P happens, Q is recognized for the first time. 〜てこそ emphasizes **P's value**. Compare: 経験してはじめてわかる (only realize it after experience) vs 経験してこそわかる (experience is what brings deep understanding)."},{"vs":"〜さえ〜ば","point":"〜さえ〜ば gives a **minimum sufficient condition** (P alone is enough for Q)—practical and less emotional. 〜てこそ describes an **essential condition that gives Q meaning**—emotional and philosophical. Example: お金さえあれば幸せだ (money alone brings happiness) vs 苦労してこそ幸せがわかる (only hardship reveals happiness)."},{"vs":"〜ばこそ","point":"〜ばこそ states the **sole cause** of Q (because P, Q), often with a positive Q and a deeper reason. 〜てこそ states the **necessary condition** for Q to have value. ばこそ is closer to から/ので; てこそ is closer to てはじめて."}]}},"n2-048||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～てこそ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てこそ","patternReading":"てこそ","coreMeaning":"Only through/when ~ does Q have meaning (an essential condition for Q)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often use ～てこそ as an ordinary 'if...then...' pattern, causing logical or stylistic errors. Remember that てこそ is used only when P is **a process or experience with intrinsic value**, not a material condition or simple concrete situation.","nuance":"In everyday conversation, てこそ can sound theatrical and unnatural. Use ば/たら for practical conditions. If you want emphasis, てはじめて may fit the context better.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あれば","r":"あれば","m":"if there is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"乗れる","r":"のれる","m":"can ride"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you have money, you can ride the train.","scene":"Explaining the correct pattern for a specific situation."}],"pitfalls":["❌ お金があってこそ、電車に乗れる。 → ✅ お金があれば、電車に乗れる。 (Use ば for a simple material condition)","❌ 時間があってこそ、映画が見られる。 → ✅ 時間があれば、映画が見られる。 (Same principle)","❌ 彼は学生でこそ、勉強する。 → ✅ 彼は学生だから、勉強する。 (てこそ is not used for a direct reason)","⚠️ 毎日練習してこそ、上手になる。 — This sounds bookish in daily life; 練習すれば上手になる is more natural."],"compare":null}},"n2-048||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～てこそ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てこそ","patternReading":"てこそ","coreMeaning":"Only through/when ~ does Q have meaning (an essential condition for Q)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember ～てこそ by breaking it into parts: て (the te-form, showing an action that occurs first) + こそ (an emphatic particle meaning 'precisely' or 'only'). Thus, ～てこそ = **only through action P** can one attain the value of Q.","nuance":"Imagine that you must **climb a mountain** (て) before the **view** (こそ) feels truly worthwhile. Without the climb, the view lacks meaning. Remember its formal, philosophical tone.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-048||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～てこそ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てこそ","patternReading":"てこそ","coreMeaning":"Only through/when ~ does Q have meaning (an essential condition for Q)","slug":"phrases","definition":"A collection of phrases commonly combined with ～てこそ in speeches and writing. These phrases are often philosophical and can serve as models for writing.","nuance":"They appear frequently in reading-comprehension questions. Knowing them helps you recognize the pattern quickly. Preserve their formal tone when using them.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"苦労","r":"くろう","m":"hardship"},{"t":"してこそ","r":"してこそ","m":"only through"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"成功","r":"せいこう","m":"success"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"喜び","r":"よろこび","m":"joy"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかる","m":"understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only through hardship can one understand the joy of success.","scene":"A saying often quoted in self-help books."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-049||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～てはじめて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てはじめて","patternReading":"てはじめて","coreMeaning":"Only after P does one first realize or become able to do Q","slug":"core","definition":"〜てはじめて means that only after P occurs can Q be realized or become possible for the first time. The focus is on **time order**: P opens the door to Q. Q is usually new awareness (わかる・気づく) or a new ability (できる).","nuance":"This pattern often has a **reflective emotional nuance**, used when recounting a lesson, regret, or turning point in one’s understanding. It is not used for regularly repeated events. It works in both spoken and written Japanese and is common in personal reflections.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"親","r":"おや","m":"parents"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なって","m":"become"},{"t":"はじめて","m":"only then, first"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"親","r":"おや","m":"parents"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"苦労","r":"くろう","m":"hardships"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかった","m":"understood"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only after becoming a parent did I understand my parents’ hardships.","scene":"A young mother talks with a friend after having a child"},{"tokens":[{"t":"失って","r":"うしなって","m":"lose"},{"t":"はじめて","m":"only then, first"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"大切さ","r":"たいせつさ","m":"importance"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"気づいた","r":"きづいた","m":"realized"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only after losing it did I realize how important it was.","scene":"Regret over a broken relationship"},{"tokens":[{"t":"海外","r":"かいがい","m":"overseas"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"出て","r":"でて","m":"go out"},{"t":"はじめて","m":"only then, first"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"良さ","r":"よさ","m":"good qualities"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかった","m":"understood"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only after going abroad did I understand what is good about Japan.","scene":"A student studying abroad shares this after returning"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-049||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～てはじめて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てはじめて","patternReading":"てはじめて","coreMeaning":"Only after P does one first realize or become able to do Q","slug":"forms","definition":"The pattern consists of **Vて + はじめて** or **Nになって + はじめて**. はじめて does not conjugate; tense and negation appear in Q. Q is often an awareness verb such as わかる, 気づく, or できる.","nuance":"It is moderately formal and common in writing, memoirs, and personal reflections. It is not used for commands or invitations.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"はじめて + Q","note":"P happens first; Q is a new awareness or ability"},{"lhs":"Nになって","rhs":"はじめて + Q","note":"P is becoming a role or state (親、大人、社会人...)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"社会","r":"しゃかい","m":"society"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"出て","r":"でて","m":"enter"},{"t":"はじめて","m":"only then, first"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"大切さ","r":"たいせつさ","m":"importance"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかった","m":"understood"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only after entering the working world did I understand the value of money.","scene":"A young worker learns the value of earning money"},{"tokens":[{"t":"病気","r":"びょうき","m":"illness"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なって","m":"become"},{"t":"はじめて","m":"only then, first"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ありがたさ","m":"value"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかった","m":"understood"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only after becoming ill did I understand how precious good health is.","scene":"Someone recovering from illness reflects with a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-049||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～てはじめて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てはじめて","patternReading":"てはじめて","coreMeaning":"Only after P does one first realize or become able to do Q","slug":"usage","definition":"～てはじめて expresses a **time milestone** after which awareness or ability appears for the first time. A typical situation is a person’s perspective changing after experiencing something.","nuance":"It is used in both speech and writing. It often appears in personal reflections, memoirs, and essays, with a slightly wistful or contemplative tone. It is not used for daily repetition.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"一人暮らし","r":"ひとりぐらし","m":"live alone"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"はじめて","m":"only then, first"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"大切さ","r":"たいせつさ","m":"importance"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかった","m":"understood"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only after living alone did I understand how important my family is.","scene":"A student away from home cooks and misses family"},{"tokens":[{"t":"離れて","r":"はなれて","m":"move away"},{"t":"はじめて","m":"only then, first"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ありがたさ","m":"value"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかった","m":"understood"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only after being apart did I realize how precious that person was.","scene":"Realizing someone’s importance after separation"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-049||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Patterns often confused","sub":"～てはじめて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てはじめて","patternReading":"てはじめて","coreMeaning":"Only after P does one first realize or become able to do Q","slug":"compare","definition":"Learners often confuse ～てはじめて with ～てから, ～てこそ, and ～たびに. The key point is that ～てはじめて emphasizes **Q occurring for the first time after P**, not repetition or a valued condition.","nuance":"～てから is neutral about sequence; ～てこそ praises the value of P; ～たびに describes regular repetition. ～てはじめて suggests a delayed realization or important threshold.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"事故","r":"じこ","m":"accident"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"遭って","r":"あって","m":"experience"},{"t":"はじめて","m":"only then, first"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"シートベルト","m":"seat belt"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"大切さ","r":"たいせつさ","m":"importance"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかった","m":"understood"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only after experiencing an accident did I realize the importance of seat belts.","scene":"An accident survivor advises others to wear seat belts"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜てこそ","point":"～てこそ: P is essential for Q to have meaning and emphasizes P’s value. ～てはじめて: P is the time milestone after which Q first appears."},{"vs":"〜てから","point":"～てから: simply means ‘after P, then Q’ with no first-time emphasis. ～てはじめて: Q occurs only for the first time then."},{"vs":"〜たびに","point":"～たびに: describes repetition, ‘whenever.’ ～てはじめて: marks a one-time threshold."}]}},"n2-049||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes & traps","sub":"～てはじめて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てはじめて","patternReading":"てはじめて","coreMeaning":"Only after P does one first realize or become able to do Q","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"A typical mistake is using ～てはじめて for repeated habits. Remember: はじめて requires Q to be a **one-time turning point**, not an event that happens repeatedly.","nuance":"For ‘whenever’ or gradual change over time, do not use はじめて. Choose たびに, てから, or うちに instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"海外","r":"かいがい","m":"overseas"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"働いて","r":"はたらいて","m":"work"},{"t":"はじめて","m":"only then, first"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"言葉","r":"ことば","m":"language"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"壁","r":"かべ","m":"barrier"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"気づいた","r":"きづいた","m":"realized"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only after working overseas did I realize the language barrier.","scene":"A worker abroad describes the experience"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 毎日食べてはじめて、好きになった。 → ✅ 毎日食べているうちに、好きになった。 (Liking developed gradually over time)","❌ 毎朝起きてはじめて、コーヒーを飲む。 → ✅ 毎朝起きてから、コーヒーを飲む。 (A repeated habit, not a first-time realization)","❌ 子供が生まれてはじめて、毎日寝不足だ。 → ✅ 子供が生まれてから、毎日寝不足だ。 (An ongoing state, not a first-time event)"],"compare":null}},"n2-049||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"～てはじめて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てはじめて","patternReading":"てはじめて","coreMeaning":"Only after P does one first realize or become able to do Q","slug":"memory","definition":"Think of はじめて as **‘the first time.’** This pattern says that P is the key that opens the door to Q. Without P, Q has never existed.","nuance":"The sense of a ‘threshold’ is essential: P is not a daily action but a milestone that changes the person.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-049||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～てはじめて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てはじめて","patternReading":"てはじめて","coreMeaning":"Only after P does one first realize or become able to do Q","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common in exams and daily life: 親になってはじめて, 失ってはじめて, 社会に出てはじめて, and 一人暮らしをしてはじめて. Other examples include 留学生活をしてはじめて and 年を取ってはじめて.","nuance":"These phrases often combine with わかる・気づく・実感する. They emphasize a life lesson.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"年","r":"とし","m":"age"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"とって","m":"grow older"},{"t":"はじめて","m":"only then, first"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"親","r":"おや","m":"parents"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"気持ち","r":"きもち","m":"feelings"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかった","m":"understood"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only after growing older did I understand my parents’ feelings.","scene":"A middle-aged person reflects on their parents"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-050||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"～ものだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものだ","patternReading":"ものだ","coreMeaning":"General truth / Exclamation / Nostalgia (3 meanings)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ものだ has **3 main meanings**, depending on the form before it and the context. Meaning ① expresses a **general truth or universal rule** with the dictionary form. Meaning ② expresses **exclamation** (how...) with V-た or an i-adjective. Meaning ③ expresses **nostalgia** (used to...) with V-た, often alongside 昔 or 子どもの頃.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, from casual conversation to literary language. Meaning ① sounds philosophical or reassuring. Meaning ② shows admiration or surprise. Meaning ③ has a nostalgic tone. Do not use ものだ for a specific event today.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"people"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"誰でも","r":"だれでも","m":"anyone"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"する","m":"make"},{"t":"ものだ","r":"ものだ","m":"that's normal"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Everyone makes mistakes.","scene":"Comforting a coworker who made a mistake"},{"tokens":[{"t":"よく","r":"よく","m":"how impressive"},{"t":"ここまで","r":"ここまで","m":"this far"},{"t":"続けられた","r":"つづけられた","m":"kept going"},{"t":"ものだ","r":"ものだ","m":"how admirable"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"How admirable—you kept going this far!","scene":"Admiring results after years of effort"},{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"頃","r":"ころ","m":"time"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"よく","r":"よく","m":"often"},{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"川","r":"かわ","m":"river"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"泳いだ","r":"およいだ","m":"swam"},{"t":"ものだ","r":"ものだ","m":"used to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When I was little, I used to swim in this river all the time.","scene":"Remembering childhood beside the river"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-050||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & conjugation","sub":"～ものだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものだ","patternReading":"ものだ","coreMeaning":"General truth / Exclamation / Nostalgia (3 meanings)","slug":"forms","definition":"There are **3 main structures**, corresponding to the 3 meanings. Meaning ① uses **V辞書形 + ものだ** for general truths. Meaning ② uses **V-た / いA + ものだ** for exclamations. Meaning ③ uses **V-た + ものだ**, often with adverbs such as 昔 or 子どもの頃 to emphasize the distant past.","nuance":"The polite form is ものです, used in polite conversation or mildly formal writing. Negative forms can also be used with ものだ, but the meaning depends on context.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"ものだ","note":"meaning ①: general truth or rule"},{"lhs":"V-た / いA","rhs":"ものだ","note":"meaning ②: exclamation, often with よく"},{"lhs":"V-た","rhs":"ものだ","note":"meaning ③: nostalgia, often with 昔/子どもの頃"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"過ぎる","r":"すぎる","m":"pass"},{"t":"ものだ","r":"ものだ","m":"that's normal"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Time will pass.","scene":"Reassuring someone who is sad"},{"tokens":[{"t":"昔","r":"むかし","m":"long ago"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"よく","r":"よく","m":"often"},{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"公園","r":"こうえん","m":"park"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"遊んだ","r":"あそんだ","m":"played"},{"t":"ものだ","r":"ものだ","m":"used to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Long ago, I used to play in this park all the time.","scene":"Telling a friend about childhood"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-050||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～ものだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものだ","patternReading":"ものだ","coreMeaning":"General truth / Exclamation / Nostalgia (3 meanings)","slug":"usage","definition":"Choose the meaning of ものだ from **grammar signals and context**. V辞書形 + ものだ → a general truth, used to **advise or explain** a principle. V-た + ものだ with emotion → **exclamation**. V-た + distant-past context → **nostalgia**, often with よく and 昔.","nuance":"Meaning ① sounds experienced and philosophical. Meaning ② often appears with よく (quite/really) and shows admiration or surprise. Meaning ③ conveys longing and fond memories.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"人間","r":"にんげん","m":"people"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"間違い","r":"まちがい","m":"mistakes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"犯す","r":"おかす","m":"make"},{"t":"ものだ","r":"ものだ","m":"that's normal"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Everyone makes mistakes sometimes.","scene":"Explaining this to a junior colleague"},{"tokens":[{"t":"よく","r":"よく","m":"how impressive"},{"t":"一人","r":"ひとり","m":"alone"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"ここまで","r":"ここまで","m":"this far"},{"t":"来た","r":"きた","m":"came"},{"t":"ものだ","r":"ものだ","m":"how admirable"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"How impressive—you made it this far all by yourself.","scene":"Praising an independent international student"},{"tokens":[{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"頃","r":"ころ","m":"days"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"よく","r":"よく","m":"often"},{"t":"夜","r":"よる","m":"night"},{"t":"遅く","r":"おそく","m":"late"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"ものだ","r":"ものだ","m":"used to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When I was a student, I used to study late into the night.","scene":"Remembering student days while viewing an old album"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-050||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"～ものだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものだ","patternReading":"ものだ","coreMeaning":"General truth / Exclamation / Nostalgia (3 meanings)","slug":"compare","definition":"Learners often confuse ものだ (general truth) with similar patterns such as ものではない and はずだ. **ものだ** describes a truth or general rule. **ものではない** denies that norm and means 'should not'. **はずだ** is a logical inference about a specific situation.","nuance":"ものだ sounds philosophical, while はずだ is personal and evidence-based. ものではない sounds like advice or mild criticism.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"年","r":"とし","m":"age"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"取ると","r":"とると","m":"as one ages"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"物忘れ","r":"ものわすれ","m":"forgetfulness"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ひどく","r":"ひどく","m":"become severe"},{"t":"なる","m":"become"},{"t":"ものだ","r":"ものだ","m":"that's normal"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As people age, forgetfulness naturally gets worse.","scene":"Discussing forgetfulness with grandparents"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ものではない","point":"ものだ = people do it / one should do it; ものではない = should not do it. Example: 人は失敗するものだ (mistakes are normal) vs 嘘をつくものではない (one should not lie)."},{"vs":"〜はずだ","point":"はずだ = a logical inference based on evidence, often about a specific event; ものだ = a general truth. Example: 電車はもう着くはずだ (the train should arrive soon) vs 時間は過ぎるものだ (time naturally passes)."}]}},"n2-050||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～ものだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものだ","patternReading":"ものだ","coreMeaning":"General truth / Exclamation / Nostalgia (3 meanings)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The most common error is using ものだ (general truth) for a specific event, such as 'it will rain today.' Remember that **ものだ (general truth) only describes universal rules**, not individual situations. Also, the nostalgic meaning **requires V-た**, not the dictionary form.","nuance":"When correcting a sentence, identify whether it expresses a 'general rule' or a 'specific event' before choosing the pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"誰でも","r":"だれでも","m":"anyone"},{"t":"年","r":"とし","m":"age"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"取る","r":"とる","m":"grow old"},{"t":"ものだ","r":"ものだ","m":"that's normal"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Everyone grows old eventually; that's just how life is.","scene":"Comforting someone worried about gray hair"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 今日、雨が降るものだ。 → ✅ 今日は雨が降るはずだ。 (ものだ is not used for today's specific event)","❌ 子どもの頃、よく遊ぶものだ。 → ✅ 子どもの頃、よく遊んだものだ。 (nostalgia requires V-た)","❌ 彼は今ベトナムにいるものだ。 → ✅ 彼は今ベトナムにいるはずだ。 (a specific inference is not a general truth)","❌ 昨日、映画を見るものだ。 → ✅ 昔はよく映画を見たものだ。 (nostalgia does not use the dictionary form)"],"compare":null}},"n2-050||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～ものだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものだ","patternReading":"ものだ","coreMeaning":"General truth / Exclamation / Nostalgia (3 meanings)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember もの as 'a thing'—ものだ means 'that's the way such a thing is.' Each meaning has its own **recognition signal**. V辞書形 + ものだ → think 'general rule.' V-た + ものだ + emotion → think 'how...!' V-た + 昔/子どもの頃 → think 'used to...'.","nuance":"This is a quick mental shortcut for reading or listening: once you catch the form before ものだ, you can infer the meaning immediately.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-050||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common expressions","sub":"～ものだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものだ","patternReading":"ものだ","coreMeaning":"General truth / Exclamation / Nostalgia (3 meanings)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are common expressions in exams and daily life. They help you become familiar with the natural rhythm of ものだ and provide **ready-made patterns** you can use immediately.","nuance":"These expressions often appear in emotional writing or personal reflections. Memorizing them helps you respond faster in sentence-transformation exercises.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"年","r":"とし","m":"age"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"取ると","r":"とると","m":"as one ages"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"早起き","r":"はやおき","m":"early rising"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なる","r":"なる","m":"become"},{"t":"ものだ","r":"ものだ","m":"that's normal"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As people age, they naturally start waking up early.","scene":"A grandfather talking to his grandchild about early rising"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-051||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～に違いない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に違いない","patternReading":"にちがいない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (well-founded inference)","slug":"core","definition":"〜に違いない expresses the speaker’s **near-absolute confidence** (~95%) in a judgment based on observed signs or logical inference. It is not a verified fact, but a firm conclusion meaning “it cannot be otherwise.”","nuance":"Used comfortably in both speech and writing. It is **stronger and more emphatic** than はずだ or だろう. In polite communication, it may sound slightly rude toward a superior because it sounds overly certain.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"犯人","r":"はんにん","m":"culprit"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"知っている","r":"しっている","m":"know"},{"t":"に違いない","r":"にちがいない","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He must know who the culprit is.","scene":"A coworker avoids eye contact when questioned by police."},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"車","r":"くるま","m":"car"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"スピード","m":"speed"},{"t":"からすると","m":"judging from"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"急いでいる","r":"いそいでいる","m":"hurrying"},{"t":"に違いない","r":"にちがいない","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Judging from that car’s speed, they must be in a hurry.","scene":"Watching a car speed past from the sidewalk."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-051||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～に違いない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に違いない","patternReading":"にちがいない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (well-founded inference)","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern attaches directly to the plain form of verbs and to i-adjectives; na-adjectives and nouns must drop だ. It does not change for tense or politeness because に違いない itself remains fixed.","nuance":"Formal, slightly **stiff**, and highly assertive. Do not negate に違いない itself (“に違いない + ない” does not exist); use Vない形 + に違いない instead, e.g. 来ないに違いない.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"に違いない","note":"来るに違いない / 知っているに違いない"},{"lhs":"いA","rhs":"に違いない","note":"高いに違いない"},{"lhs":"なA（だ→∅）","rhs":"に違いない","note":"有名に違いない"},{"lhs":"N（だ→∅）","rhs":"に違いない","note":"彼に違いない"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"レストラン"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"いつも"},{"t":"混んでいる","r":"こんでいる","m":"crowded"},{"t":"。"},{"t":"味","r":"あじ","m":"taste"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"に違いない","r":"にちがいない","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This restaurant is always crowded. The food must be good.","scene":"Waiting outside a popular restaurant."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"練習","r":"れんしゅう","m":"practice"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"skilled"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なる","m":"become"},{"t":"に違いない","r":"にちがいない","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She practices every day, so she must become good at it.","scene":"A classmate talks about a diligent Japanese learner."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-051||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～に違いない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に違いない","patternReading":"にちがいない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (well-founded inference)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use 〜に違いない when you have **concrete evidence**—something seen, heard, smelled, or otherwise reliably known—from which you draw a conclusion. It often appears with grounds-giving structures such as からすると and から見ると.","nuance":"It shows the **highest confidence** among common speculation patterns: に違いない > はずだ > だろう > かもしれない. Because it is so strong, use it only when your confidence is genuinely high; use something else when you are doubtful.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"こんな"},{"t":"高品質","r":"こうひんしつ","m":"high-quality"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"製品","r":"せいひん","m":"product"},{"t":"なら"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"値段","r":"ねだん","m":"price"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"高い","r":"たかい","m":"expensive"},{"t":"に違いない","r":"にちがいない","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A product this high-quality must be expensive too.","scene":"Commenting on a premium item in a store."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あんな"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"泣いて","r":"ないて","m":"crying"},{"t":"いた"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"よほど","m":"really"},{"t":"悔しかった","r":"くやしかった","m":"upset"},{"t":"に違いない","r":"にちがいない","m":"must have been"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She cried that much, so she must have been terribly upset.","scene":"Hearing that a classmate cried after a test."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-051||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"～に違いない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に違いない","patternReading":"にちがいない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (well-founded inference)","slug":"compare","definition":"This comparison branch helps you **avoid confusing** に違いない with other inference patterns. Each pattern has its own confidence level and function.","nuance":"The key point: に違いない **makes a strong claim** based on observable grounds, while だろう is only a **guess**. はずだ leans on reason and logic. かもしれない expresses uncertainty and a lower possibility.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あの"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"に違いない","r":"にちがいない","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That person must be a student.","scene":"They wear a student ID and enter the library."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜はずだ","point":"はずだ: ~80% confidence, with a specific logical reason (電車だから5時には着くはずだ). に違いない: ~95%, based on observable evidence. Examples: 彼は毎日練習していたから、上手なはずだ (logical); こんなに上手に話すから、毎日練習していたに違いない (inferred from what is observed)."},{"vs":"〜だろう","point":"だろう: ~60% guess, no strong basis required, used when uncertain. に違いない: requires clear grounds. Compare: 明日は雨だろう (experience-based guess) vs 黒い雲が出ているから、雨に違いない (black clouds are visible)."},{"vs":"〜かもしれない","point":"かもしれない: low possibility, “may.” に違いない: very high possibility. Example: 彼は来るかもしれない (he may come) vs 彼はもう連絡したから、来るに違いない (he must come because he already contacted us)."},{"vs":"〜に相違ない","point":"Almost synonymous, but に相違ない sounds stiffer and more written. に違いない is more natural in everyday life."}]}},"n2-051||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～に違いない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に違いない","patternReading":"にちがいない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (well-founded inference)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Typical mistakes with 〜に違いない include using it without evidence (just a feeling), confusing it with はずだ, failing to remove だ after なA/N, or creating an incorrect double negative. Remember: it follows the plain form and requires clear grounds for inference.","nuance":"The most serious mistake is using に違いない without evidence, making the sentence **blunt or funny** because its certainty is disproportionate.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"合格","r":"ごうかく","m":"pass"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"に違いない","r":"にちがいない","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He studies every day, so he must pass the exam.","scene":"Telling a friend about a diligent student."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 明日は雨に違いない。（just a feeling） → ✅ 明日は雨だろう／雨かもしれない。","❌ 日本はアジアに違いない。（obvious fact） → ✅ 日本はアジアにある。","❌ 彼女は有名だに違いない。 → ✅ 彼女は有名に違いない。","❌ 彼は来るに違いないない。 → ✅ 彼は来ないに違いない。"],"compare":null}},"n2-051||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～に違いない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に違いない","patternReading":"にちがいない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (well-founded inference)","slug":"memory","definition":"To remember it: imagine a **balance scale**. One side holds the evidence you observe and the other holds your conclusion. When the scale tips strongly toward “the conclusion is correct,” use に違いない.","nuance":"No example; remember the mental image.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-051||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common combinations","sub":"～に違いない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に違いない","patternReading":"にちがいない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (well-founded inference)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These combinations commonly appear with に違いない in conversation and on tests. Combining it with きっと, よほど, and どう見ても makes sentences natural and emphasizes the speaker’s certainty.","nuance":"Words such as きっと, よほど, and どう見ても often appear to reinforce certainty.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どう見ても","r":"どうみても","m":"no matter how you look"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"犯人","r":"はんにん","m":"culprit"},{"t":"に違いない","r":"にちがいない","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No matter how you look at it, he must be the culprit.","scene":"Police discuss the camera footage."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-052||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～を余儀なくされる · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を余儀なくされる","patternReading":"をよぎなくされる","coreMeaning":"Be forced by circumstances to do P, with no choice (extremely formal)","slug":"core","definition":"～を余儀なくされる describes a person or organization being **forced by circumstances or an objective event** to carry out P, with **no other choice**. This is a passive form: the subject takes no initiative. It is extremely formal and used in news, reports, and official documents; **never** in everyday conversation.","nuance":"Very formal, appearing almost exclusively in writing such as news, notices, and minutes. It conveys **unavoidable circumstances** overpowering the subject, with no personal choice. It is much heavier and more formal than ざるを得ない or しかない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"台風","r":"たいふう","m":"typhoon"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"影響","r":"えいきょう","m":"impact"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"イベント"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし","m":"cancellation"},{"t":"を余儀なくされた","r":"をよぎなくされた","m":"was forced to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Due to the typhoon, the event was forced to be canceled.","scene":"Event organizers' website notice."},{"tokens":[{"t":"経営","r":"けいえい","m":"business"},{"t":"悪化","r":"あっか","m":"deterioration"},{"t":"により","m":"due to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"工場","r":"こうじょう","m":"factory"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"閉鎖","r":"へいさ","m":"closure"},{"t":"を余儀なくされた","r":"をよぎなくされた","m":"was forced to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Due to worsening business conditions, the factory was forced to close.","scene":"Economic news about a major company."},{"tokens":[{"t":"震災","r":"しんさい","m":"earthquake disaster"},{"t":"後","r":"ご","m":"after"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"多く","r":"おおく","m":"many"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"住民","r":"じゅうみん","m":"residents"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"避難","r":"ひなん","m":"evacuation"},{"t":"生活","r":"せいかつ","m":"life"},{"t":"を余儀なくされた","r":"をよぎなくされた","m":"were forced to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After the earthquake, many residents were forced to live in shelters.","scene":"Reporter’s disaster-area report."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-052||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～を余儀なくされる · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を余儀なくされる","patternReading":"をよぎなくされる","coreMeaning":"Be forced by circumstances to do P, with no choice (extremely formal)","slug":"forms","definition":"The core structure is **N + を余儀なくされる**. N must be a noun indicating an event or activity, such as 中止, 閉鎖, 撤退, or 避難. The passive form with される is most common; the active form is ～を余儀なくさせる. Do **not** attach a plain verb directly.","nuance":"『される』 gives the sentence the meaning of “being forced”; the affected person does not want it. The past form ～を余儀なくされた is very common in reports. The させる form has a causer as the subject.","structure":[{"lhs":"N（event）","rhs":"を余儀なくされる","note":"Someone is forced by circumstances to undergo N"},{"lhs":"N（event）","rhs":"を余儀なくさせた","note":"A cause or organization forced it (active)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"大雪","r":"おおゆき","m":"heavy snow"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"列車","r":"れっしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"運転","r":"うんてん","m":"operation"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし","m":"suspension"},{"t":"を余儀なくされた","r":"をよぎなくされた","m":"was forced to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Due to heavy snow, train services were forced to stop.","scene":"Emergency radio announcement."},{"tokens":[{"t":"戦争","r":"せんそう","m":"war"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"離れる","r":"はなれる","m":"leave"},{"t":"こと"},{"t":"を余儀なくされた","r":"をよぎなくされた","m":"was forced to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of the war, they were forced to leave their family.","scene":"Character speaking in a war documentary."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-052||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～を余儀なくされる · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を余儀なくされる","patternReading":"をよぎなくされる","coreMeaning":"Be forced by circumstances to do P, with no choice (extremely formal)","slug":"usage","definition":"～を余儀なくされる appears when major events are forced by external circumstances: disasters, war, epidemics, and economic crises. It is common in news reports, company notices, and government reports.","nuance":"The tone is extremely formal, conveying helplessness before the situation. It is objective rather than personal. Cause phrases such as 〜により and 〜で explain the circumstances.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"新型","r":"しんがた","m":"novel"},{"t":"コロナ"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"影響","r":"えいきょう","m":"impact"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"多く","r":"おおく","m":"many"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"stores"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"閉店","r":"へいてん","m":"close"},{"t":"を余儀なくされた","r":"をよぎなくされた","m":"were forced to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Due to the impact of COVID-19, many stores were forced to close.","scene":"Article on the pandemic’s impact on small businesses."},{"tokens":[{"t":"台風","r":"たいふう","m":"typhoon"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"上陸","r":"じょうりく","m":"landfall"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"屋外","r":"おくがい","m":"outdoor"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"イベント"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし","m":"cancellation"},{"t":"を余儀なくされた","r":"をよぎなくされた","m":"was forced to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Due to the typhoon’s landfall, outdoor events were forced to be canceled.","scene":"Local government notice."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-052||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with ざるを得ない, しかない","sub":"～を余儀なくされる · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を余儀なくされる","patternReading":"をよぎなくされる","coreMeaning":"Be forced by circumstances to do P, with no choice (extremely formal)","slug":"compare","definition":"All these patterns can mean “be forced to,” but they differ in who applies the pressure and in formality. ～を余儀なくされる emphasizes **external circumstances**, leaves no choice, and is used only in formal writing.","nuance":"Compared with ざるを得ない, this pattern is objective and report-like. しかない sounds personal and casual. を余儀なくさせる is active and reverses the roles of the causer and affected party.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"不況","r":"ふきょう","m":"recession"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"リストラ"},{"t":"を余儀なくされた","r":"をよぎなくされた","m":"was forced to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Due to the recession, the company was forced to cut staff.","scene":"Labor and employment news."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～ざるを得ない","point":"ざるを得ない: the speaker admits “cannot avoid doing it,” and works in formal speech too. 余儀なくされる: external circumstances force the subject; formal writing about major events."},{"vs":"～しかない","point":"しかない: colloquial “have no choice,” chosen by the speaker after considering options. 余儀なくされる: completely passive, imposed by overwhelming circumstances, formal writing."},{"vs":"～を余儀なくさせる","point":"させる is active: A forces B to act. される is passive: B is forced. Same root, opposite roles."}]}},"n2-052||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～を余儀なくされる · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を余儀なくされる","patternReading":"をよぎなくされる","coreMeaning":"Be forced by circumstances to do P, with no choice (extremely formal)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often overuse this pattern for every situation meaning “be forced to,” making the level of formality sound wrong. Remember: ～を余儀なくされる is **only for major, objective events in formal writing**.","nuance":"Using it for a small matter sounds comically formal—like writing an official notice to ask for a day off.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"支援","r":"しえん","m":"funding"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"打ち切り","r":"うちきり","m":"cutoff"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"計画","r":"けいかく","m":"plan"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし","m":"cancellation"},{"t":"を余儀なくされた","r":"をよぎなくされた","m":"was forced to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When funding was cut, he was forced to cancel the plan.","scene":"News about a community project losing funding."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 雨が降ったから、傘を使うことを余儀なくされた。 → ✅ 雨が降ったから、傘を使った。/ 傘を使わざるを得なかった。","❌ 残業を余儀なくされた。（a personal, minor situation） → ✅ 残業するしかなかった。/ 残業せざるを得なかった。","❌ 彼は日本に行くを余儀なくされた。 → ✅ 彼は日本に行くことを余儀なくされた。/ 彼は日本行きを余儀なくされた。"],"compare":null}},"n2-052||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip — 余儀=no choice","sub":"～を余儀なくされる · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を余儀なくされる","patternReading":"をよぎなくされる","coreMeaning":"Be forced by circumstances to do P, with no choice (extremely formal)","slug":"memory","definition":"The kanji 余儀 (よぎ) originally means “an extra ritual,” but in 余儀なく it evokes “no other way.” Add される, the passive form, and the meaning becomes **being forced to act with no choice**.","nuance":"An odd but useful mnemonic: pair it with serious events—picture a huge storm or a company going bankrupt.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-052||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common collocations","sub":"～を余儀なくされる · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～を余儀なくされる","patternReading":"をよぎなくされる","coreMeaning":"Be forced by circumstances to do P, with no choice (extremely formal)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Noun + を余儀なくされる collocations frequently appear in JLPT questions and news articles. Memorize them as **fixed collocations** for immediate, accurate use.","nuance":"In tests, the noun is often given and you must choose を余儀なくされる, not を余儀なくさせる, unless an active causer is needed.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"経営難","r":"けいえいなん","m":"business difficulties"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"同社","r":"どうしゃ","m":"the company"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"海外","r":"かいがい","m":"overseas"},{"t":"事業","r":"じぎょう","m":"business"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"撤退","r":"てったい","m":"withdrawal"},{"t":"を余儀なくされた","r":"をよぎなくされた","m":"was forced to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Due to business difficulties, the company was forced to withdraw from overseas operations.","scene":"Trade section of the Nikkei."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-053||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ならいざしらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ならいざしらず","patternReading":"ならいざしらず","coreMeaning":"P is understandable, but Y is unacceptable","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern 〜ならいざしらず means that case P would be understandable or acceptable, but the real situation Y is unacceptable. It contrasts two cases and often conveys criticism, blame, or surprise. A typical example is: 'Children are understandable, but adults are not.'","nuance":"Formal and often used in writing, speeches, or serious conversation. It has a sarcastic or critical tone and is not used for praise.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"ならいざしらず","r":"ならいざしらず","m":"if it were..."},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大人","r":"おとな","m":"adult"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"そんな","m":"such"},{"t":"言い訳","r":"いいわけ","m":"excuse"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"する","m":"make"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"呆れた","r":"あきれた","m":"astonished"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Children might be understandable, but hearing an adult give such an excuse is astonishing.","scene":"Hearing an adult make an absurd excuse"},{"tokens":[{"t":"素人","r":"しろうと","m":"amateur"},{"t":"ならいざしらず","r":"ならいざしらず","m":"if it were..."},{"t":"、"},{"t":"プロ","r":"ぷろ","m":"professional"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"こんな","m":"this kind of"},{"t":"ミス","r":"みす","m":"mistake"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"する","m":"make"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"problem"},{"t":"だ","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"An amateur might be understandable, but a professional making this kind of mistake is a serious problem.","scene":"Discussing a professional mistake at work"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-053||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"～ならいざしらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ならいざしらず","patternReading":"ならいざしらず","coreMeaning":"P is understandable, but Y is unacceptable","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure 〜ならいざしらず attaches directly to a noun or a verb in plain form (普通形). The following clause presents situation Y and a critical comment. This pattern does not take the polite ます form and often appears with a negative expression or an inverted sentence.","nuance":"Formal and somewhat old-fashioned. With verbs, the ない form is common, as in 知らないならいざしらず. Do not place は after P.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"＋ ならいざしらず、～","note":"A noun naming an acceptable subject or case"},{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"＋ ならいざしらず、～","note":"A verb in plain form; ない may be added"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"ならいざしらず","r":"ならいざしらず","m":"if it were..."},{"t":"、"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"遅刻","r":"ちこく","m":"be late"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"信じられない","r":"しんじられない","m":"unbelievable"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A student being late might be understandable, but a teacher being late is unbelievable.","scene":"Seeing a teacher arrive late to class"},{"tokens":[{"t":"急ぐ","r":"いそぐ","m":"be in a hurry"},{"t":"ならいざしらず","r":"ならいざしらず","m":"if it were..."},{"t":"、"},{"t":"暇","r":"ひま","m":"free"},{"t":"なのに","r":"なのに","m":"even though"},{"t":"タクシー","r":"たくしー","m":"taxi"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"乗る","r":"のる","m":"take"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"もったいない","m":"wasteful"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Taking a taxi when you are in a hurry is understandable, but taking one when you have plenty of time is wasteful.","scene":"Friends suggest taking a taxi while free"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-053||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～ならいざしらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ならいざしらず","patternReading":"ならいざしらず","coreMeaning":"P is understandable, but Y is unacceptable","slug":"usage","definition":"The main use of 〜ならいざしらず is to contrast an exceptional case P with real situation Y and emphasize how unreasonable Y is. P is often a subject with an objective reason to be forgiven, such as a child, amateur, or an earlier era. The pattern is not used for praise or comfort.","nuance":"It feels like 'P would be OK, but Y is really...'. It often appears in essays, criticism, or complaints to close colleagues. Avoid using it with superiors because it may offend.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"昔","r":"むかし","m":"the past"},{"t":"ならいざしらず","r":"ならいざしらず","m":"if it were..."},{"t":"、"},{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"時代","r":"じだい","m":"era"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"そんな","m":"such"},{"t":"考え","r":"かんがえ","m":"idea"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"通らない","r":"とおらない","m":"not hold"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That idea might have been understandable in the past, but it is unacceptable in today's world.","scene":"Criticizing an outdated social view"},{"tokens":[{"t":"アマチュア","r":"あまちゅあ","m":"amateur"},{"t":"ならいざしらず","r":"ならいざしらず","m":"if it were..."},{"t":"、"},{"t":"プロ","r":"ぷろ","m":"professional"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"そんな","m":"such"},{"t":"ミス","r":"みす","m":"mistake"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"する","m":"make"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"許せない","r":"ゆるせない","m":"unforgivable"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"An amateur might be understandable, but a professional making such a mistake is unforgivable.","scene":"Discussing a professional athlete's mistake"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-053||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare similar patterns","sub":"～ならいざしらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ならいざしらず","patternReading":"ならいざしらず","coreMeaning":"P is understandable, but Y is unacceptable","slug":"compare","definition":"The key difference is that 〜ならいざしらず expresses contrast and criticism, while はともかく simply sets one issue aside to discuss another. With ならいざしらず, P is acceptable but Y is criticized. With はともかく, the speaker is not criticizing Y and only wants to focus on it. 〜ともかくとして is a more formal variant of はともかく, common in administrative writing, and also lacks a critical tone.","nuance":"Use ならいざしらず to express indignation; use はともかく to shift focus politely.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"ならいざしらず","r":"ならいざしらず","m":"if it were..."},{"t":"、"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"そんな","m":"such"},{"t":"こと","m":"thing"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"言う","r":"いう","m":"say"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A student saying such a thing might be understandable, but a teacher saying it...","scene":"Criticizing an inappropriate teacher remark"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～はともかく","point":"はともかく: set P aside and make Y the focus. ならいざしらず: P is acceptable, but Y is not. Ex: 値段はともかく、品質は良い vs 子どもならいざしらず、大人が…"},{"vs":"～ともかくとして","point":"Same meaning as はともかく but more formal and written; ならいざしらず has a clearly critical tone."}]}},"n2-053||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～ならいざしらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ならいざしらず","patternReading":"ならいざしらず","coreMeaning":"P is understandable, but Y is unacceptable","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with 〜ならいざしらず: (1) making P and Y the same or essentially the same, which removes the contrast; (2) confusing it with 〜はともかく, changing the meaning; (3) using it for praise or with someone of higher status. P and Y must clearly differ in age, experience, time, status, or another criterion.","nuance":"Learners often make P and Y too similar. Also avoid this pattern when giving praise, because it is inherently linked to criticism.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"ならいざしらず","r":"ならいざしらず","m":"if it were..."},{"t":"、"},{"t":"社会人","r":"しゃかいじん","m":"working adult"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"そんな","m":"such"},{"t":"マナー","r":"まなー","m":"manners"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"困る","r":"こまる","m":"be troubling"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A student having such manners might be understandable, but a working adult behaving this way is troubling.","scene":"Complaining about unprofessional workplace behavior"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 子どもならいざしらず、子どもも勉強すべきだ。 → ✅ 子どもならいざしらず、大人はもっと勉強すべきだ。 (P and Y must differ)","❌ 値段ならいざしらず、品質が良い。 → ✅ 値段はともかく、品質が良い。 (To simply set an issue aside, use はともかく)","❌ 彼は頭がいいならいざしらず、本当に優秀だ。 → ✅ 頭がいいのは確かだが、本当に優秀だ。 (Do not use it for praise)"],"compare":null}},"n2-053||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～ならいざしらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ならいざしらず","patternReading":"ならいざしらず","coreMeaning":"P is understandable, but Y is unacceptable","slug":"memory","definition":"For a quick memory aid, split いざしらず into いざ, a filler word, plus 知らず, 'not know.' Literally, it suggests 'if it is P, leave it out of consideration.' P needs no discussion because it is understandable, while Y causes surprise. Picture children versus adults, amateurs versus professionals, and the past versus the present.","nuance":"This mnemonic helps you remember that P is the minor case and Y is the serious case.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-053||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～ならいざしらず · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ならいざしらず","patternReading":"ならいざしらず","coreMeaning":"P is understandable, but Y is unacceptable","slug":"phrases","definition":"Typical phrases with 〜ならいざしらず include 子どもならいざしらず, 素人ならいざしらず, and 昔ならいざしらず. They often begin a critical comment and appear frequently on JLPT questions.","nuance":"When you hear one of these phrases, expect a criticized situation to follow.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"昔","r":"むかし","m":"the past"},{"t":"ならいざしらず","r":"ならいざしらず","m":"if it were..."},{"t":"、"},{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ネット","r":"ねっと","m":"internet"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"調べられる","r":"しらべられる","m":"can look up"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"だから"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"知らない","r":"しらない","m":"not know"},{"t":"では"},{"t":"済まされない","r":"すまされない","m":"not acceptable"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Not knowing might have been understandable in the past, but now that information can be looked up online, ignorance is no excuse.","scene":"Criticizing someone for not looking up available information"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-054||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～とあって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とあって","patternReading":"とあって","coreMeaning":"Precisely because / Due to (special circumstances), ...","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern ～とあって expresses that **precisely because of special circumstances or an event**, a natural result or reaction follows. The preceding clause is often a noun or plain-form verb describing a notable event, such as a holiday, concert, or anniversary. The following clause states the observable consequence.","nuance":"It has a **formal, written** tone and is used in news reports and event coverage. It is not used in everyday conversation for personal reasons.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"連休","r":"れんきゅう","m":"long holiday"},{"t":"とあって","r":"とあって","m":"because it is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"高速道路","r":"こうそくどうろ","m":"expressways"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"どこも","m":"everywhere"},{"t":"渋滞","r":"じゅうたい","m":"traffic jams"},{"t":"して","m":"being"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because it was a long holiday, expressways were jammed everywhere.","scene":"Holiday traffic news"},{"tokens":[{"t":"人気歌手","r":"にんきかしゅ","m":"popular singer"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"コンサート","m":"concert"},{"t":"とあって","r":"とあって","m":"because it was"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"チケット","m":"tickets"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"即日","r":"そくじつ","m":"the same day"},{"t":"完売","r":"かんばい","m":"sold out"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because it was a concert by a popular singer, the tickets sold out that same day.","scene":"Entertainment news on ticket sales"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-054||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～とあって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とあって","patternReading":"とあって","coreMeaning":"Precisely because / Due to (special circumstances), ...","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes how とあって connects. The pattern follows an **event noun** or a **verb/adjective in plain form**. With な-adjectives, omit だ and add とあって. The following clause always describes an observable result or reaction.","nuance":"The preceding expression often conveys something unusual, special, or large-scale: 連休, 初日, 無料, 超人気, 記念日... The following clause often includes どこも, すぐ, もう, or すごく to emphasize the reaction.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"とあって、[result]","note":"連休・初日・無料・記念日..."},{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"とあって、[result]","note":"人気歌手が来るとあって..."},{"lhs":"Aい（普通形）","rhs":"とあって、[result]","note":"安いとあって…"},{"lhs":"Na（語幹）","rhs":"とあって、[result]","note":"Do not add だ"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"初日","r":"しょにち","m":"first day"},{"t":"とあって","r":"とあって","m":"because it was"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"開店前","r":"かいてんまえ","m":"before opening"},{"t":"から","m":"from"},{"t":"長い","r":"ながい","m":"long"},{"t":"列","r":"れつ","m":"line"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"できて","m":"formed"},{"t":"いた"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because it was opening day, a long line had formed before the store opened.","scene":"A newly opened store"},{"tokens":[{"t":"無料体験","r":"むりょうたいけん","m":"free trial"},{"t":"とあって","r":"とあって","m":"because it was"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"多くの","r":"おおくの","m":"many"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"people"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"申し込んで","r":"もうしこんで","m":"signing up"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because it was a free trial, many people are signing up.","scene":"Free class advertisement"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-054||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & contexts","sub":"～とあって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とあって","patternReading":"とあって","coreMeaning":"Precisely because / Due to (special circumstances), ...","slug":"usage","definition":"Typical contexts include **holidays, openings, anniversaries, unusual events, and current news**. This pattern often appears at the start of a news report to explain a phenomenon that is taking place.","nuance":"Do not use it to give a personal reason, such as “I was late because I was stuck in traffic.” If the subject wants to explain why they did something, use から/ので.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"創業100周年","r":"そうぎょうひゃくしゅうねん","m":"100th anniversary"},{"t":"とあって","r":"とあって","m":"because it was"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会社全体","r":"かいしゃぜんたい","m":"the whole company"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"祝賀ムード","r":"しゅくがむーど","m":"celebratory mood"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"包まれて","r":"つつまれて","m":"wrapped in"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because it was the company’s 100th anniversary, the whole company was filled with a celebratory mood.","scene":"Company news report"},{"tokens":[{"t":"台風","r":"たいふう","m":"typhoon"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"近づいて","r":"ちかづいて","m":"approaching"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"とあって","r":"とあって","m":"because it was"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"空港","r":"くうこう","m":"airport"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"欠航","r":"けっこう","m":"cancellations"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"相次いで","r":"あいついで","m":"one after another"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because a typhoon was approaching, flight cancellations were occurring one after another at the airport.","scene":"Online weather news"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-054||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions","sub":"～とあって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とあって","patternReading":"とあって","coreMeaning":"Precisely because / Due to (special circumstances), ...","slug":"compare","definition":"A quick comparison of two easily confused patterns: **～から** and **～だけに**. All can express a cause, but から gives a subjective or ordinary reason; だけに emphasizes “as expected”; とあって simply describes a special circumstance leading to a result.","nuance":"For multiple-choice questions, choose とあって when the sentence sounds like a formal news report and the preceding clause is an event such as 連休, 初日, or 無料. Choose だけに when the nuance is “as befits...” or “naturally because...”.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"人気店","r":"にんきてん","m":"popular restaurant"},{"t":"とあって","r":"とあって","m":"because it is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"予約","r":"よやく","m":"reservations"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"取れない","r":"とれない","m":"cannot get"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because it is a popular restaurant, it is impossible to get a reservation.","scene":"Recommending a popular restaurant"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～から","point":"から gives a subjective reason and does not require a special circumstance. とあって describes an objective, noteworthy circumstance. 例: 暑いから窓を開けた。 / 猛暑とあって冷房がよく売れている。"},{"vs":"～だけに","point":"だけに means “as expected, precisely because of that, the result is even more...” とあって simply links “special circumstance → result” without emphasizing deservedness. 例: 人気店だけに、予約が取れない。 / 人気店とあって、予約が取れない。"}]}},"n2-054||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～とあって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とあって","patternReading":"とあって","coreMeaning":"Precisely because / Due to (special circumstances), ...","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"This section highlights common learner errors. The biggest mistake is using とあって for ordinary daily reasons or personal feelings.","nuance":"Quick test: if replacing the cause with “ordinary and expected” still makes sense, とあって is probably wrong. Use から/ので instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"猛暑","r":"もうしょ","m":"extreme heat"},{"t":"とあって","r":"とあって","m":"because it was"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"水","r":"みず","m":"water"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"消費量","r":"しょうひりょう","m":"consumption"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"急増して","r":"きゅうぞうして","m":"surging"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of the extreme heat, water consumption is surging.","scene":"Report on an unusual heat wave"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 毎日暑いとあって、水をたくさん飲む。 → ✅ 猛暑とあって、水の消費量が急増している。","❌ 彼は親切とあって、みんなに好かれている。 → ✅ 彼は親切なので、みんなに好かれている。","❌ 明日休みとあって、嬉しい。 → ✅ 明日休みなので、嬉しい。"],"compare":null}},"n2-054||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～とあって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とあって","patternReading":"とあって","coreMeaning":"Precisely because / Due to (special circumstances), ...","slug":"memory","definition":"Use a visual memory aid: a **special stage**. とあって acts like a sign saying “because this is a special situation,” followed by a natural public reaction. Always ask: “Is this situation noteworthy?” If yes, use とあって; if not, use another pattern.","nuance":"Helpful phrase: “UNUSUAL context → NATURAL reaction.” If you forget, remember that news reports about 連休/初日/無料 often use とあって.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-055||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～をめぐって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をめぐって","patternReading":"をめぐって","coreMeaning":"Around ~ as an issue (debate/dispute)","slug":"core","definition":"〜をめぐって means 'around an issue or topic.' The key point is that at least two sides have different opinions or positions, often leading to debate, dispute, or conflict. It commonly combines with nouns such as 議論, 争い, 対立, 裁判, and social issues such as 法案, 予算, 環境問題, and 遺産相続.","nuance":"This is formal, writing-oriented language common in journalism, current affairs, and official documents. It is uncommon in everyday conversation; for ordinary 'about something,' use について.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"土地","r":"とち","m":"land"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"所有権","r":"しょゆうけん","m":"ownership"},{"t":"をめぐって","r":"をめぐって","m":"over"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"隣人","r":"りんじん","m":"neighbor"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"裁判","r":"さいばん","m":"lawsuit"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Over the land ownership, I took my neighbor to court.","scene":"A news report about a land dispute"},{"tokens":[{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"法案","r":"ほうあん","m":"bill"},{"t":"をめぐって","r":"をめぐって","m":"over"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"国会","r":"こっかい","m":"Diet"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"活発な","r":"かっぱつな","m":"lively"},{"t":"議論","r":"ぎろん","m":"debate"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"行われた","r":"おこなわれた","m":"took place"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Lively debate took place in the Diet over the new bill.","scene":"News about a Diet session"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-055||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"～をめぐって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をめぐって","patternReading":"をめぐって","coreMeaning":"Around ~ as an issue (debate/dispute)","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern combines directly with a NOUN. Use をめぐって when modifying a following verb; use をめぐる + N when modifying a noun. The pattern itself has no negative or past form—the main verb carries the tense.","nuance":"をめぐって comes before a verb (V), while をめぐる comes before a noun (N'). In either form, the noun refers to a controversial issue or one involving multiple viewpoints.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"をめぐって","note":"Modifies a verb: an action over N"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"をめぐる＋N'","note":"Modifies a noun: N' is often 議論・争い・対立・裁判"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼ら","r":"かれら","m":"they"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"遺産","r":"いさん","m":"inheritance"},{"t":"をめぐって","r":"をめぐって","m":"over"},{"t":"争った","r":"あらそった","m":"fought"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"They fought over the inheritance.","scene":"A family torn apart by an inheritance"},{"tokens":[{"t":"遺産","r":"いさん","m":"inheritance"},{"t":"をめぐる","r":"をめぐる","m":"over"},{"t":"争い","r":"あらそい","m":"dispute"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"長く","r":"ながく","m":"for long"},{"t":"続いた","r":"つづいた","m":"continued"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The dispute over the inheritance lasted a long time.","scene":"Opening sentence of a news article"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-055||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～をめぐって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をめぐって","patternReading":"をめぐって","coreMeaning":"Around ~ as an issue (debate/dispute)","slug":"usage","definition":"～をめぐって is used when there is a 'hot issue'—a controversial matter or important event attracting multiple opinions. Common verbs include 議論する, 話し合う, 争う, 対立する, and 裁判になる. It appears in formal speech and writing, news, commentary, and essays.","nuance":"Do not use it for trivial personal matters. If a sentence simply means 'talk about' someone or something, use について or に関して. をめぐって always suggests opposing sides or viewpoints.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"予算配分","r":"よさんはいぶん","m":"budget allocation"},{"t":"をめぐって","r":"をめぐって","m":"over"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"各部署","r":"かくぶしょ","m":"departments"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"意見","r":"いけん","m":"opinions"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"対立している","r":"たいりつしている","m":"are opposed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The departments are divided over the budget allocation.","scene":"Departments argue over the budget at a company meeting"},{"tokens":[{"t":"環境問題","r":"かんきょうもんだい","m":"environmental issue"},{"t":"をめぐる","r":"をめぐる","m":"over"},{"t":"国際会議","r":"こくさいかいぎ","m":"international conference"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来月","r":"らいげつ","m":"next month"},{"t":"開かれる","r":"ひらかれる","m":"will be held"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"An international conference on the environmental issue will be held next month.","scene":"News about an upcoming event"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-055||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing it from について","sub":"～をめぐって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をめぐって","patternReading":"をめぐって","coreMeaning":"Around ~ as an issue (debate/dispute)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish をめぐって from other ways to say 'about.' をめぐって always involves conflict or opposition; について simply states a topic; に関して is more formal but does not require multiple viewpoints.","nuance":"Learners may overuse をめぐって when translating 'about.' If there is no debate, dispute, or multi-sided discussion, choose another pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"制度","r":"せいど","m":"system, rules"},{"t":"をめぐって","r":"をめぐって","m":"over"},{"t":"議論","r":"ぎろん","m":"discuss"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We are discussing the new system, with opposing views.","scene":"Opposing views at a meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"について","point":"について works for any topic and does not require debate. ❌ 食事をめぐって友達と話した。 → ✅ 食事について友達と話した。"},{"vs":"に関して","point":"に関して is formal but only means 'regarding'; をめぐって suggests opposing views. 彼の意見に関しては賛成だ。 (no conflict)"}]}},"n2-055||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～をめぐって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をめぐって","patternReading":"をめぐって","coreMeaning":"Around ~ as an issue (debate/dispute)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner errors with をめぐって mainly come from translating 'about' too broadly or omitting part of the structure.","nuance":"Remember: をめぐって is not general 'about.' It is like 'over' in a dispute: argue over a matter.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"制度","r":"せいど","m":"system, rules"},{"t":"をめぐって","r":"をめぐって","m":"over"},{"t":"議論","r":"ぎろん","m":"discuss"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We are discussing the new system.","scene":"A meeting with differing opinions"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食事をめぐって友達と話した。 → ✅ 食事について友達と話した。 (Eating is not a disputed issue.)","❌ 環境問題めぐって会議が開かれた。 → ✅ 環境問題をめぐって会議が開かれた。 (The particle を is missing.)","❌ 彼をめぐって話し合った。 → ✅ 彼について話し合った。 (This is simple discussion, not a dispute.)","❌ この本をめぐって感想を述べた。 → ✅ この本について感想を述べた。 (There are no opposing views.)"],"compare":null}},"n2-055||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～をめぐって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をめぐって","patternReading":"をめぐって","coreMeaning":"Around ~ as an issue (debate/dispute)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip for をめぐって: 巡 (めぐ) contains the 辶 movement radical plus 川? Think 'go around.' When an issue has people presenting different views as if standing around a round table, use をめぐって.","nuance":"Association: を is the object particle, and めぐって means going around. Going around a central 'point' suggests an issue causing conflict.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-055||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～をめぐって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をめぐって","patternReading":"をめぐって","coreMeaning":"Around ~ as an issue (debate/dispute)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases with をめぐる/をめぐって in tests and news combine with verbs and nouns expressing debate, opposition, and legal judgment.","nuance":"These phrases are nearly fixed collocations. Memorizing them can help you process exam questions faster.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"issue"},{"t":"をめぐる","r":"をめぐる","m":"over"},{"t":"議論","r":"ぎろん","m":"debate"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"白熱している","r":"はくねつしている","m":"is heated"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The debate over this issue is getting heated.","scene":"A television commentary"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-056||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ざるを得ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ざるを得ない","patternReading":"ざるをえない","coreMeaning":"Must ~; cannot avoid doing it (reluctantly)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ざるを得ない expresses having to do something despite not wanting to, because circumstances leave no other choice. ざる is an old negative form equivalent to ない, while を得ない means “cannot obtain,” giving the sense “cannot not do.” This formal expression emphasizes reluctance and helplessness.","nuance":"Its nuance is much stronger than 〜しなければならない: it clearly shows that the speaker does not want to act. It works in speech and writing, but is especially natural in formal writing. In casual conversation, Japanese speakers often use 〜しかない or 〜ないといけない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"証拠","r":"しょうこ","m":"evidence"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見せられて","r":"みせられて","m":"shown"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"認め","r":"みとめ","m":"admit"},{"t":"ざるを得ない","r":"ざるをえない","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once the evidence is shown, I cannot help but admit it.","scene":"In a meeting, the other side presents irrefutable evidence."},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"状況","r":"じょうきょう","m":"situation"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"計画","r":"けいかく","m":"plan"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"変更","r":"へんこう","m":"change"},{"t":"せざるを得ない","r":"せざるをえない","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In this situation, we have no choice but to change the plan.","scene":"An emergency meeting after a sudden market change."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-056||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"～ざるを得ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ざるを得ない","patternReading":"ざるをえない","coreMeaning":"Must ~; cannot avoid doing it (reluctantly)","slug":"forms","definition":"General formula: **Vない-stem + ざるを得ない**. With する, use **せ** → せざるを得ない; this exception must be memorized. With 来る, use **こ** → 来ざるを得ない. For the past, change ない to なかった, giving ざるを得なかった.","nuance":"This is the easiest point to get wrong. Do not use the usual nai-stem of する (し); change it to せ. 得ない in this pattern is always pronounced えない.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vない-stem","rhs":"+ ざるを得ない","note":"Remove ない from Vない: 行く→行か, 食べる→食べ"},{"lhs":"する","rhs":"→ せざるを得ない","note":"REQUIRED exception: しざる/するざる are both wrong"},{"lhs":"来る","rhs":"→ 来(こ)ざるを得ない","note":"Pronounced こ, not き"},{"lhs":"Quá khứ","rhs":"Vない-stem + ざるを得なかった","note":"The negative meaning remains because ざる already carries it"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"来週","r":"らいしゅう","m":"next week"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"出席","r":"しゅっせき","m":"attend"},{"t":"せざるを得ない","r":"せざるをえない","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have no choice but to attend next week’s meeting.","scene":"Your boss asks, and you cannot refuse."},{"tokens":[{"t":"体調","r":"たいちょう","m":"health"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"悪くて","r":"わるくて","m":"being poor"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"match"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"棄権","r":"きけん","m":"withdraw"},{"t":"せざるを得なかった","r":"せざるをえなかった","m":"had to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because I was feeling unwell, I had no choice but to withdraw from the match.","scene":"An athlete falls ill before an important match."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-056||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～ざるを得ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ざるを得ない","patternReading":"ざるをえない","coreMeaning":"Must ~; cannot avoid doing it (reluctantly)","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern often appears when the speaker is convinced by **evidence or data**, forced by **objective circumstances**, or must make a **difficult decision** at work. Do not use it for something you willingly want to do.","nuance":"In news and office contexts, 〜ざるを得ない presents decisions such as raising prices, changing plans, or canceling events in a polite, objective way. It conveys more helplessness than 〜しかない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"物価","r":"ぶっか","m":"prices"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"上昇","r":"じょうしょう","m":"rise"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"伴い","r":"ともない","m":"as a result"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"値上げ","r":"ねあげ","m":"raise prices"},{"t":"せざるを得ない","r":"せざるをえない","m":"must"},{"t":"状況","r":"じょうきょう","m":"situation"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As prices continue to rise, we have no choice but to raise our prices.","scene":"A price increase notice during inflation."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"実力","r":"じつりょく","m":"ability"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"認め","r":"みとめ","m":"acknowledge"},{"t":"ざるを得ない","r":"ざるをえない","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"His ability cannot be denied.","scene":"After seeing an outstanding presentation."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-056||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Patterns often confused","sub":"～ざるを得ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ざるを得ない","patternReading":"ざるをえない","coreMeaning":"Must ~; cannot avoid doing it (reluctantly)","slug":"compare","definition":"The three patterns most likely to cause confusion. Remember: **ざるを得ない** = circumstances force you to act, and you reluctantly decide to do so. **わけにはいかない** = you feel you must not do something because it would violate standards or your pride. **を余儀なくされる** = an outside force compels you, leaving no personal control. **ずにはいられない** = inner emotion cannot be restrained, so you must act.","nuance":"〜ざるを得ない still involves a reluctant choice under pressure; 〜を余儀なくされる is often used for accidents, disasters, or orders from superiors, where the subject is completely passive.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"提案","r":"ていあん","m":"proposal"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"反対","r":"はんたい","m":"oppose"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"方針","r":"ほうしん","m":"policy"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"従わ","r":"したがわ","m":"follow"},{"t":"ざるを得ない","r":"ざるをえない","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I oppose that proposal, but I have no choice but to follow company policy.","scene":"An employee disagrees but must follow instructions."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜わけにはいかない","point":"〜ざるを得ない = CIRCUMSTANCES force action. 〜わけにはいかない = PERSONAL DUTY or standards prevent action. Ex: 約束があるので、行かざるを得ない (I must go because I have a promise) VS ここで黙っているわけにはいかない (I cannot stay silent here; it would be wrong)."},{"vs":"〜を余儀なくされる","point":"〜ざるを得ない emphasizes YOUR reluctant decision. 〜を余儀なくされる emphasizes being completely forced from outside, often without control. Ex: 大雨で中止せざるを得なかった / 大雨で中止を余儀なくされた."},{"vs":"〜ずにはいられない","point":"〜ざるを得ない comes from REASON and recognizing the situation. 〜ずにはいられない comes from sudden EMOTION that cannot be restrained. Ex: 彼の説明を聞けば、納得せざるを得ない / 面白くて笑わずにはいられなかった."}]}},"n2-056||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"～ざるを得ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ざるを得ない","patternReading":"ざるをえない","coreMeaning":"Must ~; cannot avoid doing it (reluctantly)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"A classic error is conjugating する as しざる. Remember: **する → せざる**. With 来る, many learners misread it as きざる; it must be read **こざる**.","nuance":"On tests, the trap is usually conjugating する or 来る. Also, do not use this pattern for something you want to do; saying “I have to go out because I want to” is contextually wrong.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"上司","r":"じょうし","m":"boss"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"命令","r":"めいれい","m":"order"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"従わ","r":"したがわ","m":"follow"},{"t":"ざるを得ない","r":"ざるをえない","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because it is my boss’s order, I have no choice but to follow it.","scene":"Being assigned unwanted work."}],"pitfalls":["Wrong: 従うざるを得ない → Right: 従わざるを得ない","Wrong: 準備をするざるを得ない → Right: 準備をせざるを得ない","Wrong: 欠席しざるを得ない → Right: 欠席せざるを得ない","Wrong: この本が面白いから、読まざるを得ない → Right: この本が面白いから、読むのをやめられない (the reason is not coercion)"],"compare":null}},"n2-056||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～ざるを得ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ざるを得ない","patternReading":"ざるをえない","coreMeaning":"Must ~; cannot avoid doing it (reluctantly)","slug":"memory","definition":"Think of ざる as a “basket”? Here, ざる is an old form of ない, expressing negation. Combined with を得ない (“cannot obtain”), it means “cannot reach the state of not doing” → “must do.”","nuance":"When you see する, do not think of し; think of せ—like “set to do it.” You can associate せ with “submit and do it.”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-056||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～ざるを得ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ざるを得ない","patternReading":"ざるをえない","coreMeaning":"Must ~; cannot avoid doing it (reluctantly)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases in news and office contexts include **認めざるを得ない** (cannot deny), **変更せざるを得ない** (must change), **値上げせざるを得ない** (must raise prices), and **中止せざるを得ない** (must cancel).","nuance":"On exams, common verbs include 認める, 変更する, 中止する, 削減する, and 断念する—all formal verbs.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"コスト","m":"costs"},{"t":"削減","r":"さくげん","m":"cut"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ため","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"人員","r":"じんいん","m":"staff"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"削減","r":"さくげん","m":"cut"},{"t":"せざるを得ない","r":"せざるをえない","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"To reduce costs, we have no choice but to cut staff.","scene":"A company restructuring announcement."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-057||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ものではない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものではない","patternReading":"ものではない","coreMeaning":"Should not ~ (moral/common-sense criticism)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ものではない expresses **criticism or admonishment**: the action ~ goes against morality, common sense, or social norms. The speaker relies on shared common sense to conclude that it is 'inherently wrong,' rather than merely stating a personal opinion.","nuance":"The tone is **strong and somewhat reproachful**. It is used in both speech and writing; the casual spoken form is もんじゃない. Compared with べきではない, ものではない emphasizes **moral nature** more than reason or rules.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"秘密","r":"ひみつ","m":"secret"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勝手に","r":"かってに","m":"without permission"},{"t":"話す","r":"はなす","m":"tell"},{"t":"ものではない","r":"ものではない","m":"should not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should not reveal someone else's secret without permission.","scene":"You tell a coworker not to reveal someone else's private matters."},{"tokens":[{"t":"食べ物","r":"たべもの","m":"food"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"粗末","r":"そまつ","m":"waste"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"する","m":"make"},{"t":"ものではない","r":"ものではない","m":"should not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should not waste food.","scene":"A mother shakes her head as her child prepares to throw away rice."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-057||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～ものではない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものではない","patternReading":"ものではない","coreMeaning":"Should not ~ (moral/common-sense criticism)","slug":"forms","definition":"Fixed pattern: **V（辞書形） + ものではない**. The verb before it is always in dictionary form; do not use ます, た, or ない forms. Only ものではない may change to ものではありません or もんじゃない.","nuance":"This structure **negates the whole clause**. In everyday speech, もんじゃない sounds more natural and casual; ものではありません is used with elders, customers, or in formal settings.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"ものではない","note":"Plain form/admonition"},{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"ものではありません","note":"Polite"},{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"もんじゃない","note":"Casual, spoken"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"嘘","r":"うそ","m":"lie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"つく","m":"tell/make up"},{"t":"ものではない","r":"ものではない","m":"should not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should not lie.","scene":"An adult sternly teaches a child."},{"tokens":[{"t":"約束","r":"やくそく","m":"promise"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"破る","r":"やぶる","m":"break"},{"t":"ものではありません","r":"ものではありません","m":"should not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should not break a promise.","scene":"A former employee reminds a new manager to keep their word."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-057||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～ものではない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものではない","patternReading":"ものではない","coreMeaning":"Should not ~ (moral/common-sense criticism)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when the speaker wants to **criticize or stop** an action that violates common sense. It often appears in adult admonitions, workplace reminders, or comments based on social norms.","nuance":"The speaker takes the position of a 'shared standard,' so the sentence can sound heavy. Avoid it for personal preferences or harmless decisions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"年上","r":"としうえ","m":"older"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"そんな","m":"such"},{"t":"態度","r":"たいど","m":"attitude"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"とる","m":"take"},{"t":"ものではない","r":"ものではない","m":"should not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should not take that attitude with someone older than you.","scene":"A manager corrects a young employee with an older customer."},{"tokens":[{"t":"困って","r":"こまって","m":"be in trouble"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見て","r":"みて","m":"see"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"見て見ぬふり","r":"みてみぬふり","m":"pretend not to see"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ものではない","r":"ものではない","m":"should not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should not ignore someone who is in trouble.","scene":"A manager criticizes indifference toward a teammate."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-057||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing it from べきではない","sub":"～ものではない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものではない","patternReading":"ものではない","coreMeaning":"Should not ~ (moral/common-sense criticism)","slug":"compare","definition":"Quick comparison: **～べきではない** = should not according to reason or rules; **～ものではない** = should not because of morality or social common sense, with a stronger critical tone.","nuance":"～ものではない almost **denies the nature** of the action: 'it is inherently not something one should do.' べきではない simply says it is inadvisable for a reason. For technical matters such as taking medicine, use べきではない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"飲む","r":"のむ","m":"take"},{"t":"べきではない","r":"べきではない","m":"should not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Children should not take this medicine.","scene":"A doctor gives a safety warning about prescribed medicine."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜べきではない","point":"べきではない is based on reason or rules; ものではない on morality or common sense. Example: この薬は子供が飲むべきではない (unsafe) — ❌ この薬は子供が飲むものではない, which sounds moral."},{"vs":"〜ものだ","point":"ものだ expresses the common-sense idea that one 'should' do something (約束は守るものだ), while ものではない negates it. The two form a direct pair."}]}},"n2-057||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～ものではない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものではない","patternReading":"ものではない","coreMeaning":"Should not ~ (moral/common-sense criticism)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"A common learner error is using **～ものではない** for situations unrelated to morality, such as safety, health, or technology. Use ～べきではない / ～ないほうがいい/ ～向けではない instead. Also remember that V before it must be in **dictionary form**.","nuance":"When Japanese speakers hear ものではない, they sense a **moral judgment**. Using it in the wrong context can sound offensive. Ask yourself: 'Does this action violate a shared social standard?'","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"飲む","r":"のむ","m":"take"},{"t":"べきではない","r":"べきではない","m":"should not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Children should not take this medicine.","scene":"A doctor advises a parent about a safe dosage."}],"pitfalls":["❌ この薬は子供が飲むものではない。 → ✅ この薬は子供が飲むべきではない。 (safety/medicine issue, not morality)","❌ 彼女に言ったものではない。 → ✅ 彼女に言うものではない。 (V before ものではない must be dictionary form 言う)","❌ 約束を破らないものではない。 → ✅ 約束を破るものではない。 (the pattern is already negative; do not use Vない)","❌ 私は納豆を食べるものではない。 → ✅ 私は納豆を食べたくない。 (do not use ものではない for personal preferences)"],"compare":null}},"n2-057||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～ものではない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものではない","patternReading":"ものではない","coreMeaning":"Should not ~ (moral/common-sense criticism)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture an adult crossing their arms and shaking their head: **'People just don't do that!'** → That is ものではない. 'もの' represents **nature or common sense**, while 'ではない' negates it.","nuance":"Unlike べきではない, which focuses on rules, think: 'That is **not something** people should do.'","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-057||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～ものではない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものではない","patternReading":"ものではない","coreMeaning":"Should not ~ (moral/common-sense criticism)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases frequently appear in JLPT questions and everyday life: **嘘をつく、秘密を話す、食べ物を粗末にする、約束を破る、年上の人にそんな態度をとる、見て見ぬふりをする**…","nuance":"All of these describe typical **morally criticized actions**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"食べ物","r":"たべもの","m":"food"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"粗末","r":"そまつ","m":"waste"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"する","m":"make"},{"t":"ものではない","r":"ものではない","m":"should not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should not waste food.","scene":"A mother shakes her head as her child prepares to throw away rice."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-058||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～次第で · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～次第で","patternReading":"しだいで","coreMeaning":"The result changes depending on ~","slug":"core","definition":"～次第で shows that the result or action that follows depends directly on the factor before it. This factor may be the method, weather, price, effort, and so on. The speaker does not state a definite result, but says that it changes according to X.","nuance":" fairly neutral but slightly formal, and common in writing and workplace conversation. With 次第では, the speaker opens up a specific possibility in the following clause, such as “the picnic may be canceled depending on the weather.” Compared with によって, 次第で emphasizes the condition’s deciding power more than its cause.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"やり方","r":"やりかた","m":"method"},{"t":"次第で","r":"しだいで","m":"depending on"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"結果","r":"けっか","m":"result"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"大きく","r":"おおきく","m":"greatly"},{"t":"変わる","r":"かわる","m":"change"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The result changes greatly depending on the method.","scene":"Discussing a project strategy"},{"tokens":[{"t":"天気","r":"てんき","m":"weather"},{"t":"次第では","r":"しだいでは","m":"depending on"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"遠足","r":"えんそく","m":"picnic"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし","m":"cancellation"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なる","m":"become"},{"t":"かもしれない","r":"かもしれない","m":"might"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Depending on the weather, the picnic might be canceled.","scene":"Planning a weekend outing"},{"tokens":[{"t":"値段","r":"ねだん","m":"price"},{"t":"次第で","r":"しだいで","m":"depending on"},{"t":"買う","r":"かう","m":"buy"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"どうか","r":"どうか","m":"or not"},{"t":"決める","r":"きめる","m":"decide"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’ll decide whether to buy it depending on the price.","scene":"Unsure about buying an expensive item"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-058||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & connections","sub":"～次第で · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～次第で","patternReading":"しだいで","coreMeaning":"The result changes depending on ~","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic formula: **N + 次第で(は)** — N is a noun or noun phrase, with no の particle inserted. **N + 次第だ** is used at the end of a sentence to give a conclusion. **Vます + 次第** is a completely different structure meaning “as soon as.”","nuance":"**次第では** is conditional and often introduces a specific possibility. **次第で** neutrally describes general dependence. **次第だ** sounds assertive, like a judgment. **Vます + 次第** is formal and commonly used in polite workplace announcements.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"次第で(は)","note":"Depending on N, the result changes"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"次第だ","note":"Conclusion: everything depends on N"},{"lhs":"Vます","rhs":"次第","note":"As soon as V (different meaning)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"営業時間","r":"えいぎょうじかん","m":"business hours"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"お店","r":"おみせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"次第です","r":"しだいです","m":"depends on"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The opening hours depend on the shop.","scene":"Asking a small shop about its hours"},{"tokens":[{"t":"合否","r":"ごうひ","m":"pass or fail"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"テスト","m":"test"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"結果","r":"けっか","m":"result"},{"t":"次第だ","r":"しだいだ","m":"depends on"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Passing or failing depends on the test results.","scene":"Talking about an upcoming exam"},{"tokens":[{"t":"着き次第","r":"つきしだい","m":"as soon as I arrive"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"連絡","r":"れんらく","m":"contact"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’ll contact you as soon as I arrive.","scene":"Promising a business partner an update after landing"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-058||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～次第で · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～次第で","patternReading":"しだいで","coreMeaning":"The result changes depending on ~","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this when you want to say that a result has not been decided and may change according to a particular factor. It often appears in negotiations, announcements, and planning.","nuance":"It can also be used in casual speech, but sounds somewhat rational or businesslike. Use **次第では** to emphasize a positive or negative possibility; use **次第で** for general dependence.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"状況","r":"じょうきょう","m":"situation"},{"t":"次第で","r":"しだいで","m":"depending on"},{"t":"対応","r":"たいおう","m":"response"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"変える","r":"かえる","m":"change"},{"t":"必要","r":"ひつよう","m":"necessary"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We may need to change our response depending on the situation.","scene":"Discussing response procedures in a meeting"},{"tokens":[{"t":"価格","r":"かかく","m":"price"},{"t":"次第では","r":"しだいでは","m":"depending on price"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"購入","r":"こうにゅう","m":"purchase"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"検討","r":"けんとう","m":"consider"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Depending on the price, I’ll consider buying it.","scene":"Replying to a sales consultant"},{"tokens":[{"t":"天気","r":"てんき","m":"weather"},{"t":"次第で","r":"しだいで","m":"depending on"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"予定","r":"よてい","m":"plans"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"変え","r":"かえ","m":"change"},{"t":"ましょう","r":"ましょう","m":"let’s"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let’s change our plans depending on the weather.","scene":"Texting a friend before an outing"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-058||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions from similar patterns","sub":"～次第で · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～次第で","patternReading":"しだいで","coreMeaning":"The result changes depending on ~","slug":"compare","definition":"Learners often confuse **次第で** with **に応じて** and **によって**, since all can be loosely translated as “depending on.” The key difference is the viewpoint: a result being determined, an action being actively adjusted, or a cause/means being identified.","nuance":"**に応じて** = actively change an action to fit; **によって** = emphasize a cause, means, or difference; **次第で** = the result follows a condition, as if placed on a scale.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"能力","r":"のうりょく","m":"ability"},{"t":"次第で","r":"しだいで","m":"depending on"},{"t":"給料","r":"きゅうりょう","m":"salary"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"変わる","r":"かわる","m":"change"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Salaries change depending on ability.","scene":"Discussing company pay policies"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜に応じて","point":"に応じて = “adjust to fit depending on” (active action); 次第で = “the result is determined by” (dependence). Example: 能力に応じて給料を決める (set pay according to ability) vs 能力次第で給料が変わる (pay changes depending on ability)."},{"vs":"〜によって","point":"によって emphasizes a cause, means, or agent: “due to/by/using.” 次第で emphasizes a result changing according to a condition. Example: 国によって習慣が違う (customs differ by country) vs やり方次第で結果が変わる (the result changes according to the method)."},{"vs":"Vます + 次第","point":"As soon as V: act immediately after an action is completed (着き次第、連絡します). N+次第で: depending on, not a time expression."}]}},"n2-058||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes & traps","sub":"～次第で · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～次第で","patternReading":"しだいで","coreMeaning":"The result changes depending on ~","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes include using た-form + 次第 for “as soon as,” adding の before 次第, or placing a plain-form verb directly before 次第.","nuance":"When you remember “as soon as,” think **Vます + 次第**. **N + 次第で** means “depending on.” Before 次第, use a noun or a phrase such as **かどうか** (“whether or not”), not a plain-form verb.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"次第で","r":"しだいで","m":"depending on"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"結果","r":"けっか","m":"result"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"決まる","r":"きまる","m":"be decided"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The result is determined by your effort.","scene":"Encouraging a colleague studying for an exam"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 着いた次第で、連絡します。 → ✅ 着き次第、連絡します。 (for “as soon as,” use Vます+次第, not N+次第で)","❌ 努力の次第で、結果が決まる。 → ✅ 努力次第で、結果が決まる。 (no の before 次第)","❌ 行く次第で決めよう。 → ✅ 行くかどうか次第で決めよう。 (次第 requires a noun or a phrase such as 'かどうか')"],"compare":null}},"n2-058||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～次第で · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～次第で","patternReading":"しだいで","coreMeaning":"The result changes depending on ~","slug":"memory","definition":"Tip 1: **次第** contains 次 = next/order and 第 = rank/order, suggesting “according to X’s order.” Imagine the result being **lined up according to X**. Tip 2: picture a **scale** — N is the weight, and the result tilts toward N.","nuance":"With **次第では**, add an image of “opening a door to a possibility” — the door opens only if condition X occurs.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-058||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases & collocations","sub":"～次第で · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～次第で","patternReading":"しだいで","coreMeaning":"The result changes depending on ~","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases: **やり方次第** (depending on the method), **天気次第** (depending on the weather), **価格次第** (depending on the price), **努力次第** (depending on effort), and **状況次第** (depending on the situation).","nuance":"These phrases are common on JLPT exams and in workplace conversation. Learn them as vocabulary chunks.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"次第ですが","r":"しだいですが","m":"depending on, but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"参加","r":"さんか","m":"attend"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It depends on the time, but I’ll attend.","scene":"Replying to a meeting invitation email"},{"tokens":[{"t":"結果","r":"けっか","m":"result"},{"t":"次第で","r":"しだいで","m":"depending on"},{"t":"また","m":"again"},{"t":"連絡","r":"れんらく","m":"contact"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’ll contact you again depending on the result.","scene":"Ending a job interview"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-059||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～に伴って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に伴って","patternReading":"にともなって","coreMeaning":"Along with / as ~ progresses (parallel changes; latter follows)","slug":"core","definition":"～に伴って describes two events changing at the same time, with the second naturally resulting from the first. As A changes, B changes correspondingly.","nuance":"Used in speech and writing, but especially common in reports, news, and essays. The speaker observes objectively without expressing emotion. Used for social, natural, and economic phenomena—not personal decisions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"経済","r":"けいざい","m":"economy"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"発展","r":"はってん","m":"development"},{"t":"に伴って","r":"にともなって","m":"along with"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"環境問題","r":"かんきょうもんだい","m":"environmental issues"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"深刻化","r":"しんこくか","m":"become serious"},{"t":"して","m":"become"},{"t":"いる","m":"be -ing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Along with economic development, environmental problems are also becoming more serious.","scene":"News report on growth and the environment"},{"tokens":[{"t":"高齢化","r":"こうれいか","m":"aging population"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"進展","r":"しんてん","m":"progress"},{"t":"に伴って","r":"にともなって","m":"along with"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"医療費","r":"いりょうひ","m":"medical costs"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"増加","r":"ぞうか","m":"increase"},{"t":"して","m":"become"},{"t":"いる","m":"be -ing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As the population ages, medical costs are increasing.","scene":"News about an aging society"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-059||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～に伴って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に伴って","patternReading":"にともなって","coreMeaning":"Along with / as ~ progresses (parallel changes; latter follows)","slug":"forms","definition":"Formula: **V（辞書形）** or **N** + に伴って. The preceding part must express change or development; the following part is a parallel result. The variant に伴い is used in writing; に伴う + N modifies a noun.","nuance":"Not used with い-adjectives or な-adjectives. The preceding part commonly includes 発展・変化・増加・普及・進歩. Do not use the た-form before に伴って.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"に伴って","note":"Two parallel events; the latter is a result"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"に伴って","note":"The preceding noun expresses change"},{"lhs":"～","rhs":"に伴い","note":"Formal written form"},{"lhs":"～","rhs":"に伴う + N","note":"Modifies a noun"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"経済成長","r":"けいざいせいちょう","m":"economic growth"},{"t":"に伴って","r":"にともなって","m":"along with"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"物価","r":"ぶっか","m":"prices"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"上昇","r":"じょうしょう","m":"rise"},{"t":"して","m":"become"},{"t":"いる","m":"be -ing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Along with economic growth, prices are also rising.","scene":"Economic discussion"},{"tokens":[{"t":"人口増加","r":"じんこうぞうか","m":"population growth"},{"t":"に伴う","r":"にともなう","m":"associated with"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"problem"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"深刻","r":"しんこく","m":"serious"},{"t":"だ"}],"vi":"The problems associated with population growth are serious.","scene":"Sentence from a news article"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-059||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & contexts","sub":"～に伴って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に伴って","patternReading":"にともなって","coreMeaning":"Along with / as ~ progresses (parallel changes; latter follows)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this when two social, natural, or economic phenomena **change at the same time**, with one leading to the other. It emphasizes an objective causal relationship.","nuance":"Formal and slightly stiff, especially in writing. For personal observations such as “the older I get, the stronger I become,” につれて or ので sounds more natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"技術","r":"ぎじゅつ","m":"technology"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"進歩","r":"しんぽ","m":"progress"},{"t":"に伴って","r":"にともなって","m":"along with"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"私たち","r":"わたしたち","m":"our"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"生活","r":"せいかつ","m":"lives"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"便利","r":"べんり","m":"convenient"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なった","m":"became"}],"vi":"Along with technological progress, our lives became more convenient.","scene":"Japanese essay"},{"tokens":[{"t":"留学","r":"りゅうがく","m":"studying abroad"},{"t":"に伴って","r":"にともなって","m":"along with"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"語学力","r":"ごがくりょく","m":"language ability"},{"t":"だけでなく","m":"not only"},{"t":"精神的","r":"せいしんてき","m":"mentally"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"成長","r":"せいちょう","m":"mature"},{"t":"した","m":"did"}],"vi":"Along with studying abroad, not only my language skills but also my character matured.","scene":"Personal experience sharing"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-059||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"～に伴って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に伴って","patternReading":"にともなって","coreMeaning":"Along with / as ~ progresses (parallel changes; latter follows)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish ～に伴って from easily confused patterns: **につれて**, **に応じて**, and **たびに**. The key differences involve the relationship between changes and repetition.","nuance":"に伴って = parallel change and consequence, objectively; につれて = gradual change, possibly personal; に応じて = flexible adjustment; たびに = every time an event repeats.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"年","r":"とし","m":"age"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"取る","r":"とる","m":"grow older"},{"t":"に伴って","r":"にともなって","m":"along with"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"記憶力","r":"きおくりょく","m":"memory"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"衰える","r":"おとろえる","m":"decline"}],"vi":"As I grow older, my memory declines.","scene":"Comparison sentence showing objective nuance"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～につれて","point":"に伴って emphasizes a resulting consequence (A → B) objectively; につれて emphasizes gradual change over time and can describe personal feelings (例: 年を取るにつれて、体力が落ちる)."},{"vs":"～に応じて","point":"With に伴って, B is an INEVITABLE result; with に応じて, B is a FLEXIBLE action adjusted to conditions (例: 能力に応じて、給料が決まる)."},{"vs":"～たびに","point":"に伴って describes one continuous period of change; たびに describes repeated events occurring each time (例: 会うたびに、彼は大人になっている)."}]}},"n2-059||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～に伴って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に伴って","patternReading":"にともなって","coreMeaning":"Along with / as ~ progresses (parallel changes; latter follows)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often use に伴って for any “because” relationship, but this pattern requires the preceding part to express a **process of change** and the following part to be a **natural consequence**.","nuance":"“雨に伴って” sounds unnatural because rain is not a change unfolding over time, and bringing an umbrella is a deliberate personal action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降り","r":"ふり","m":"fall"},{"t":"そう","m":"seem likely"},{"t":"なので","m":"so"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"傘","r":"かさ","m":"umbrella"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持って","r":"もって","m":"carry"},{"t":"きた","m":"brought"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It looked like rain, so I brought an umbrella.","scene":"Correct alternative to a common mistake"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 雨に伴って、傘を持ってきた。 → ✅ 雨が降りそうなので、傘を持ってきた。 (に伴って does not fit a personal decision)","❌ 彼は病気に伴って、会社を休んだ。 → ✅ 彼は病気のため、会社を休んだ。 (illness is a reason, not a progressing change)","❌ 運動に伴って、疲れた。 → ✅ 運動したので、疲れた。 (personal fatigue does not use に伴って)"],"compare":null}},"n2-059||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～に伴って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に伴って","patternReading":"にともなって","coreMeaning":"Along with / as ~ progresses (parallel changes; latter follows)","slug":"memory","definition":"Use this image to remember it: 伴 consists of 亻 (person) + 半 (half), suggesting “a person walking half a step alongside.” When A moves, B—the “other half”—has to follow.","nuance":"Quick test: **“If A increases, must B automatically increase?”** If YES, use に伴って; if B does not naturally follow, use に応じて or ので.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-059||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common collocations","sub":"～に伴って · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に伴って","patternReading":"にともなって","coreMeaning":"Along with / as ~ progresses (parallel changes; latter follows)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Typical collocations in exams and everyday materials: 経済発展, 技術進歩, 高齢化, 人口増加, 国際化, 時代の変化.","nuance":"These phrases recur in news and essays, often with に伴って, に伴い, or に伴う.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"国際化","r":"こくさいか","m":"internationalization"},{"t":"に伴って","r":"にともなって","m":"along with"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"英語","r":"えいご","m":"English"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"重要性","r":"じゅうようせい","m":"importance"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"増して","r":"まして","m":"increase"},{"t":"いる","m":"be -ing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Along with internationalization, the importance of English is increasing.","scene":"Essay on modern society"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-060||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～につれて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～につれて","patternReading":"につれて","coreMeaning":"As A gradually changes, B changes along with it (step by step)","slug":"core","definition":"〜につれて describes two things changing together over time: as A gradually changes, B changes along with it at a similar pace. This is a common N2 pattern for describing processes. The key point is gradual, continuous change—not a shock, one-time event, or sudden change.","nuance":"The nuance is natural and objective: the speaker observes the process without expressing intent or giving orders. It works in both speech and writing; 〜につれ is shorter and somewhat more formal or literary. Compared with 〜たびに, 〜につれて emphasizes continuous flow rather than each repeated occurrence.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"住む","r":"すむ","m":"live"},{"t":"につれて","r":"につれて","m":"gradually"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"good"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なって","r":"なって","m":"become"},{"t":"きた","r":"きた","m":"gradually"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The longer I lived in Japan, the better my Japanese became.","scene":"Discussing Japanese progress after a year in Japan"},{"tokens":[{"t":"春","r":"はる","m":"spring"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なる","r":"なる","m":"become"},{"t":"につれて","r":"につれて","m":"gradually"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日","r":"ひ","m":"day"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"長く","r":"ながく","m":"longer"},{"t":"なって","r":"なって","m":"become"},{"t":"いく","r":"いく","m":"gradually"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As spring arrives, the days gradually get longer.","scene":"Talking with a colleague as the weather warms"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-060||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and connections","sub":"～につれて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～につれて","patternReading":"につれて","coreMeaning":"As A gradually changes, B changes along with it (step by step)","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes how to connect 〜につれて. The first clause can be a plain-form verb (dictionary form) or a noun indicating change. The second clause always describes an ongoing change, often combining with いく/くる/なってくる to emphasize the process.","nuance":"The first clause often uses change-related verbs such as なる・経つ・慣れる・登る; typical nouns include 時間 and 年齢. Do not use the た-form in the first clause, and the second clause cannot be an order, suggestion, or volitional statement.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"につれて、Second clause","note":"Example: 慣れるにつれて、仕事がうまくなる"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"につれて、Second clause","note":"Example: 年齢につれて、考え方が変わる"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"慣れる","r":"なれる","m":"get used to"},{"t":"につれて","r":"につれて","m":"gradually"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ミス","m":"mistakes"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"減って","r":"へって","m":"decrease"},{"t":"きた","r":"きた","m":"gradually"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As I got used to the job, my mistakes gradually decreased.","scene":"A new employee talks with a mentor after three months"},{"tokens":[{"t":"年齢","r":"ねんれい","m":"age"},{"t":"につれて","r":"につれて","m":"as age changes"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"趣味","r":"しゅみ","m":"hobbies"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"変わった","r":"かわった","m":"changed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As I got older, my interests changed.","scene":"Reflecting on changing interests at age thirty"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-060||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～につれて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～につれて","patternReading":"につれて","coreMeaning":"As A gradually changes, B changes along with it (step by step)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when two things change in parallel over time or through a process: as A changes, B changes accordingly. Typical situations include aging, time passing, getting used to work, climbing higher, and the seasons changing.","nuance":"The second clause usually describes a natural result that the speaker does not intentionally create, so it does not take commands, requests, or strong volition such as「～てください」or「～たい」. In conversation, it can replace 〜につれ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"年","r":"とし","m":"age"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"取る","r":"とる","m":"grow older"},{"t":"につれて","r":"につれて","m":"as it changes"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"体","r":"からだ","m":"body"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"変化","r":"へんか","m":"changes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"感じる","r":"かんじる","m":"feel"},{"t":"ように"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"began to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As I got older, I began to notice changes in my body.","scene":"A middle-aged person reflects on health"},{"tokens":[{"t":"山","r":"やま","m":"mountain"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"登る","r":"のぼる","m":"climb"},{"t":"につれて","r":"につれて","m":"as I climb"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"景色","r":"けしき","m":"scenery"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"美しく","r":"うつくしく","m":"beautiful"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The higher we climbed, the more beautiful the scenery became.","scene":"Talking with a friend about mountain climbing"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-060||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing に伴って","sub":"～につれて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～につれて","patternReading":"につれて","coreMeaning":"As A gradually changes, B changes along with it (step by step)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes 〜につれて from commonly confused patterns. The key point is that につれて emphasizes gradual change and a natural result.","nuance":"Use 〜に伴って when two things occur in parallel without stressing gradualness; use 〜たびに for a result repeated each time; use 〜に応じて for deliberately changing something according to conditions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"経済","r":"けいざい","m":"economy"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"発展","r":"はってん","m":"develop"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"につれて","r":"につれて","m":"as it develops"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"生活","r":"せいかつ","m":"life"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"豊かに","r":"ゆたかに","m":"prosperous"},{"t":"なって","r":"なって","m":"become"},{"t":"きた","r":"きた","m":"gradually"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As the economy developed, life became more prosperous.","scene":"Discussing national development"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"に伴って","point":"に伴って emphasizes parallel events or an inevitable accompanying result, not necessarily gradual change. につれて emphasizes gradual progress. Example: 経済の発展に伴って、環境問題が深刻になった (accompanying result) vs 成長するにつれて、考え方が変わった (gradually)."},{"vs":"たびに","point":"たびに describes a repeated result each time; につれて describes one directional change over time. Example: 会うたびに成長している (each time we meet) vs 会うにつれて好きになった (the more we met, the more I liked them)."},{"vs":"に応じて","point":"に応じて describes flexibly changing something according to requirements or conditions, intentionally; につれて describes natural change. Example: 予算に応じて計画を立てる (plan according to the budget) vs 時間が経つにつれて暗くなる (it gets darker over time)."}]}},"n2-060||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～につれて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～につれて","patternReading":"につれて","coreMeaning":"As A gradually changes, B changes along with it (step by step)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are common mistakes learners make with 〜につれて. Understanding them helps avoid losing points on the JLPT and making errors in conversation.","nuance":"Remember the golden rule: the second clause is not a deliberate one-off action; it describes continuous, natural change.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"経つ","r":"たつ","m":"pass"},{"t":"につれて","r":"につれて","m":"as it passes"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"悲しみ","r":"かなしみ","m":"sadness"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"薄れて","r":"うすれて","m":"fade"},{"t":"きた","r":"きた","m":"gradually"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As time passed, the sadness gradually faded.","scene":"Comforting a friend after a breakup"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 勉強するにつれて、試験に合格した。 → ✅ 勉強するにつれて、成績が上がっていった。 (the second clause must show gradual change, not a one-time result)","❌ 成長するにつれて、車を買いたい。 → ✅ 成長するにつれて、考え方が大人になった。 (do not use volition such as たい)","❌ 日本の生活に慣れたにつれて、日本語が上手になった。 → ✅ 日本の生活に慣れるにつれて、日本語が上手になった。 (use the dictionary form in the first clause, not the た-form)"],"compare":null}},"n2-060||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～につれて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～につれて","patternReading":"につれて","coreMeaning":"As A gradually changes, B changes along with it (step by step)","slug":"memory","definition":"A way to remember 〜につれて: 連れる means to lead someone along. Picture A holding B’s hand and leading it forward step by step. The second clause often contains ていく or てくる, like following the process.","nuance":"This mnemonic helps you remember that 〜につれて requires gradual change and that the second clause cannot be a disconnected action.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-060||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～につれて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～につれて","patternReading":"につれて","coreMeaning":"As A gradually changes, B changes along with it (step by step)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are common combinations found in tests and everyday life. Memorizing them helps you respond quickly while reading.","nuance":"These phrases often combine with change-related verbs such as 経つ, 慣れる, 取る, and なる. Pay attention to the N+につれて form, as in 年齢につれて.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"長く","r":"ながく","m":"long"},{"t":"付き合う","r":"つきあう","m":"know"},{"t":"につれて","r":"につれて","m":"as it continues"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"お互い","r":"おたがい","m":"each other"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"理解","r":"りかい","m":"understanding"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"深まった","r":"ふかまった","m":"deepened"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The longer we knew each other, the better we understood one another.","scene":"Talking about a long-standing friendship"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-061||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ばかりに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばかりに","patternReading":"ばかりに","coreMeaning":"Just because ~, causing a bad result (regret, remorse)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ばかりに expresses that **one single reason or trait** led to a **negative, unwanted result**. The speaker always feels **regret or remorse**. The result **must** be bad or unfortunate—it cannot be used for a positive outcome.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, but it often carries **strong emotion**, showing mild self-blame or complaint. This is not an objective structure; the speaker stresses, “It all happened just because of that one thing.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"一度","r":"いちど","m":"once"},{"t":"うそ","m":"lie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ついた","r":"ついた","m":"told"},{"t":"ばかりに","r":"ばかりに","m":"just because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"信用","r":"しんよう","m":"trust"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"すべて","m":"all"},{"t":"失って","r":"うしなって","m":"lost"},{"t":"しまった","m":"ended up losing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because I lied once, I lost everyone’s trust.","scene":"Recounting a past mistake with regret"},{"tokens":[{"t":"有名","r":"ゆうめい","m":"famous"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"ばかりに","r":"ばかりに","m":"just because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"どこ","m":"wherever"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行って","r":"いって","m":"go"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"注目","r":"ちゅうもく","m":"attention"},{"t":"され","m":"be subject to"},{"t":"て"},{"t":"疲れる","r":"つかれる","m":"tiring"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because I’m famous, I attract attention everywhere I go. It’s exhausting.","scene":"A celebrity complains to a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-061||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"～ばかりに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばかりに","patternReading":"ばかりに","coreMeaning":"Just because ~, causing a bad result (regret, remorse)","slug":"forms","definition":"〜ばかりに attaches to a **V plain form** (especially Vた), an **Aい**, or a **Naな** (with な). The following clause is a **negative result**. It does not attach directly to N; use Nである instead.","nuance":"With verbs, **Vた + ばかりに** is especially common because it describes a past action that caused consequences. With adjectives, **Aすぎる + ばかりに** (just because it is too...) is also common.","structure":[{"lhs":"V plain form / Vた","rhs":"ばかりに","note":"Vた is the most common"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"ばかりに","note":"Aすぎる is also common"},{"lhs":"Naな","rhs":"ばかりに","note":"Na + な + ばかりに"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"地図","r":"ちず","m":"map"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見なかった","r":"みなかった","m":"did not look at"},{"t":"ばかりに","r":"ばかりに","m":"just because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"道","r":"みち","m":"road"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"迷って","r":"まよって","m":"got lost"},{"t":"2時間","r":"にじかん","m":"2 hours"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"ロスした","m":"wasted"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because I didn’t look at the map, I got lost and wasted two hours.","scene":"Recounting an unfortunate detour"},{"tokens":[{"t":"不注意","r":"ふちゅうい","m":"careless"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"ばかりに","r":"ばかりに","m":"just because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"事故","r":"じこ","m":"accident"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"起こした","r":"おこした","m":"caused"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because I was careless, I caused an accident.","scene":"Explaining the cause of an accident"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-061||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～ばかりに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばかりに","patternReading":"ばかりに","coreMeaning":"Just because ~, causing a bad result (regret, remorse)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜ばかりに is used when the speaker wants to stress that **one small cause** or trait alone led to a **major, unfortunate result**. It often appears in complaints, recollections, and expressions of regret.","nuance":"The emphasis is very strong: this pattern conveys **dissatisfaction or mild blame** toward the situation. The result often uses **〜てしまう** (an unfortunate completion) or **〜ことになる** (end up in a certain result).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"早まった","r":"はやまった","m":"acted rashly"},{"t":"ばかりに","r":"ばかりに","m":"just because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大切","r":"たいせつ","m":"important"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"書類","r":"しょるい","m":"document"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"削除して","r":"さくじょして","m":"deleted"},{"t":"しまった","m":"ended up deleting"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because I acted too quickly, I deleted an important document.","scene":"After accidentally clicking the wrong option"},{"tokens":[{"t":"正直","r":"しょうじき","m":"honestly"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"言った","r":"いった","m":"said"},{"t":"ばかりに","r":"ばかりに","m":"just because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"かえって","m":"instead"},{"t":"怒られた","r":"おこられた","m":"was scolded"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because I told the truth, I was scolded instead.","scene":"Being misunderstood for being too honest"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-061||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with similar patterns","sub":"～ばかりに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばかりに","patternReading":"ばかりに","coreMeaning":"Just because ~, causing a bad result (regret, remorse)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare 〜ばかりに with other cause patterns to avoid mistakes. The key point: **ばかりに always involves a bad result and regret**, emphasizing “just because of one single reason.”","nuance":"Among similar patterns, ばかりに has the clearest negative nuance. Other patterns may be neutral or positive.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"なかった","m":"did not have"},{"t":"ばかりに","r":"ばかりに","m":"just because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大学","r":"だいがく","m":"university"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行けなかった","r":"いけなかった","m":"could not attend"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because he had no money, he couldn’t go to university.","scene":"Discussing an unfair situation in life"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～からこそ","point":"からこそ emphasizes a cause leading to **success or a positive result**; ばかりに is used only for a **bad result**."},{"vs":"～だけに","point":"だけに = “precisely because... the result is even more...” The result may be good or bad; ばかりに always means “just because of one insignificant reason” → unfortunately."},{"vs":"～せいで","point":"せいで simply gives a negative cause; ばかりに adds “just because of one single reason” + regret."}]}},"n2-061||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～ばかりに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばかりに","patternReading":"ばかりに","coreMeaning":"Just because ~, causing a bad result (regret, remorse)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often mistakenly use **ばかりに with positive results** because they interpret it as a neutral “because... so...” pattern. Another common error is using the wrong connection after a noun.","nuance":"This pattern is **never** used to emphasize a positive reason. If the result is good, use ～からこそ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"うそ","m":"lie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ついた","r":"ついた","m":"told"},{"t":"ばかりに","r":"ばかりに","m":"just because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"girlfriend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"嫌われた","r":"きらわれた","m":"was disliked by"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because I lied, my girlfriend came to hate me.","scene":"Describing the consequences of a lie"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 努力したばかりに、合格できた。 → ✅ 努力したからこそ、合格できた。","❌ 学生ばかりに、注意された。 → ✅ 学生であるばかりに、注意された。","❌ 静かなばかりに、落ち着く。 → ✅ 静かだからこそ、落ち着く。 (positive result)"],"compare":null}},"n2-061||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～ばかりに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばかりに","patternReading":"ばかりに","coreMeaning":"Just because ~, causing a bad result (regret, remorse)","slug":"memory","definition":"Associate **ばかり** (“only, nothing but”) + **に** (“due to”) with “due only to that.” Always ask: “Is the result regrettable?” If **yes**, ばかりに may be appropriate.","nuance":"Imagine a **tiny grain of sand** falling into a machine and ruining the entire machine—the cause is small, but the consequence is huge.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-061||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～ばかりに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ばかりに","patternReading":"ばかりに","coreMeaning":"Just because ~, causing a bad result (regret, remorse)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases with ばかりに describe classic regretful situations: misspeaking, lying, acting rashly, being famous, and so on.","nuance":"Remembering these phrases helps you form sentences quickly in conversation and on tests. They all carry the nuance of “just because..., I brought trouble upon myself.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"口","r":"くち","m":"mouth"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"滑らせた","r":"すべらせた","m":"misspoke"},{"t":"ばかりに","r":"ばかりに","m":"just because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"him"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"怒らせて","r":"おこらせて","m":"angered"},{"t":"しまった","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because I let something slip, I ended up angering him.","scene":"After saying something that should have stayed unsaid"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-062||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～かねる · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かねる","patternReading":"かねる","coreMeaning":"Difficult to ~ / Reluctant to ~ politely","slug":"core","definition":"〜かねる means the speaker finds it difficult or inappropriate to do something. It is a polite refusal, often used to avoid offending the other person. The reason is not physical ability, but feelings, ethics, rules, or social position.","nuance":"Formal and common in business, customer service, and writing. The polite form ～かねます sounds softer. Compared with direct できません, かねる is much gentler and more tactful.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"件","r":"けん","m":"matter"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"お答え","r":"おこたえ","m":"answer"},{"t":"し"},{"t":"かねます","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am afraid I cannot answer that matter.","scene":"A coworker asks about the manager's salary."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"行動","r":"こうどう","m":"behavior"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"到底","r":"とうてい","m":"at all"},{"t":"理解","r":"りかい","m":"understand"},{"t":"し"},{"t":"かねる","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"His behavior is difficult to understand.","scene":"Two employees discuss their boss's unusual management style."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-062||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～かねる · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かねる","patternReading":"かねる","coreMeaning":"Difficult to ~ / Reluctant to ~ politely","slug":"forms","definition":"Attach the pattern directly to the **ます-stem** (remove ます). For example: 行く → 行き(ます) → 行きかねる. With する verbs, use し＋かねる: 理解する → 理解しかねる. かねる is a group 2 verb and conjugates normally.","nuance":"For formal speech, かねます is standard in customer service. かねる is common in writing or formal statements.","structure":[{"lhs":"マス形","rhs":"＋かねる","note":"Example: 答えます → 答えかねる"},{"lhs":"する動詞","rhs":"＋し＋かねる","note":"Example: 理解する → 理解しかねる"},{"lhs":"マス形","rhs":"かねます","note":"Common use: お答えしかねます"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"提案","r":"ていあん","m":"proposal"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"賛成","r":"さんせい","m":"agree"},{"t":"し"},{"t":"かねる","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I find it difficult to agree with his proposal.","scene":"A team meeting asks for opinions on a new plan."},{"tokens":[{"t":"個人情報","r":"こじんじょうほう","m":"personal information"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"ので"},{"t":"詳細","r":"しょうさい","m":"details"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"お答え","r":"おこたえ","m":"answer"},{"t":"し"},{"t":"かねます","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Since this is personal information, I cannot answer in detail.","scene":"A customer asks for details about another person's records."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-062||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～かねる · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かねる","patternReading":"かねる","coreMeaning":"Difficult to ~ / Reluctant to ~ politely","slug":"usage","definition":"Use it when you need to **preserve the other person's dignity**: answering calls, declining customer requests, or disagreeing while remaining gentle. It is especially common among flight attendants and bank employees.","nuance":"Register: formal spoken and written language. Do not use it with close friends. A refusal with かねる means 'I am sorry, but I cannot' and avoids sounding offensive.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ご要望","r":"ごようぼう","m":"request"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"お応え","r":"おこたえ","m":"meet"},{"t":"し"},{"t":"かねます","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We are afraid we cannot meet your request.","scene":"A customer demands an invalid compensation."},{"tokens":[{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"ような"},{"t":"無理","r":"むり","m":"unreasonable"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"ご要望","r":"ごようぼう","m":"request"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ご協力","r":"ごきょうりょく","m":"cooperate"},{"t":"し"},{"t":"かねます","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We cannot cooperate with such an unreasonable request.","scene":"Replying to a partner's email with excessive terms."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-062||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions","sub":"～かねる · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かねる","patternReading":"かねる","coreMeaning":"Difficult to ~ / Reluctant to ~ politely","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish ～かねる from two similar patterns: (1) ～かねない — the ない reverses the meaning to 'may lead to' and is used for negative events; (2) ～わけにはいかない — inability caused by principles or duty, often with a specific reason, while ～かねる is a subjective refusal that needs no explanation.","nuance":"Remember ～かねる as an intentional refusal, not general 'cannot.' ～かねない stresses unintended risk, ～わけにはいかない stresses a principled barrier, and ～かねる stresses the speaker's choice.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"条件","r":"じょうけん","m":"condition"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"お受け","r":"おうけ","m":"accept"},{"t":"し"},{"t":"かねます","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Under those conditions, we cannot accept the offer.","scene":"A company replies to a partner about a contract."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～かねない","point":"かねる = intentional refusal; かねない = an unwanted negative event may occur. Example: お答えしかねます (cannot answer) vs 彼は怒りかねない (he may get angry)."},{"vs":"～わけにはいかない","point":"かねる is a polite refusal based on feelings or implicit rules; わけにはいかない stresses a specific principle or duty. Example: その件はお答えしかねます (I cannot discuss it) vs 彼には話すわけにはいかない (I cannot tell him because I promised to keep it secret)."}]}},"n2-062||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～かねる · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かねる","patternReading":"かねる","coreMeaning":"Difficult to ~ / Reluctant to ~ politely","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often use かねる as a general way to say 'cannot,' but it is mainly used in formal contexts and for subjective, ethical, or rule-based reasons.","nuance":"The most common error is using かねる for illness or health. That sounds unnatural and mismatches the context. Remember that かねる relates to personal judgment, rules, or social position.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"急","r":"きゅう","m":"sudden"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"お願い","r":"おねがい","m":"request"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"それ","m":"that"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"引き受け","r":"ひきうけ","m":"accept"},{"t":"かねます","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is a sudden request, but I am afraid I cannot accept it.","scene":"A boss assigns work outside my role without warning."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 風邪を引いたので、行きかねます。 → ✅ 風邪を引いたので、行けません。","❌ 彼の言うことは信じるかねる。 → ✅ 彼の言うことは信じかねる。","❌ 友達に「手伝いかねるよ」 → ✅ 「悪いけど手伝えないよ」( with close friends, かねる is too formal; use casual speech)"],"compare":null}},"n2-062||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～かねる · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かねる","patternReading":"かねる","coreMeaning":"Difficult to ~ / Reluctant to ~ politely","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory image: かねる is written 兼ねる, originally meaning to take on multiple duties or carry a burden. Imagine being asked to carry something too heavy—you push it away and say 'I cannot carry it' → かねる. In customer service, remember お答えしかねます as a gentle way to say 'I cannot answer.'","nuance":"The image of 'not being able to carry it' helps prevent confusion with inability from illness: かねる is for feelings, rules, or ethics, not physical ability.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-062||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～かねる · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かねる","patternReading":"かねる","coreMeaning":"Difficult to ~ / Reluctant to ~ politely","slug":"phrases","definition":"These verbal-noun + しかねます phrases function almost like set formulas in polite Japanese. Memorize them for tactful refusals.","nuance":"Phrases with ご/お + verbal noun + し + かねます show a very high level of politeness.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ご期待","r":"ごきたい","m":"expectations"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"沿い","r":"そい","m":"meet"},{"t":"かねます","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We are afraid we cannot meet your expectations.","scene":"A company replies about a product that fell short."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-063||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～かねない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かねない","patternReading":"かねない","coreMeaning":"A negative or worrying outcome may occur","slug":"core","definition":"～かねない expresses the **possibility that something undesirable may happen**, along with the speaker's **worry or warning**. The speaker uses it based on real signs or intuition, not groundless speculation. The base form かねる means 'cannot do'; adding ない creates 'not impossible' → 'may happen'.","nuance":"The nuance is **negative**. It often appears at the end of a sentence to warn someone or express concern. It works in both speech and writing; it remains natural in formal written language.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"本気","r":"ほんき","m":"serious"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"それ","m":"that"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"やり","m":"do"},{"t":"かねない","r":"かねない","m":"may do"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is the kind of person who might actually do that.","scene":"Worrying that a reckless person may do something foolish."},{"tokens":[{"t":"このまま","m":"as it is"},{"t":"放置","r":"ほうち","m":"leave alone"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大きな","r":"おおきな","m":"major"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"problem"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なり","m":"become"},{"t":"かねない","r":"かねない","m":"may become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If we leave it like this, it could become a major problem.","scene":"A manager warns a colleague about an ignored minor error."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-063||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"～かねない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かねない","patternReading":"かねない","coreMeaning":"A negative or worrying outcome may occur","slug":"forms","definition":"～かねない attaches to the **ます-form of a verb after removing ます**. For example: なる → なり + かねない, 失敗する → 失敗し + かねない. The preceding element is always an **action or development** leading to a bad outcome; do not use it directly with nouns or adjectives.","nuance":"It usually ends a sentence and is rarely used in questions. The polite form is ～かねません, and the past form is ～かねなかった. In conversation, よ may be added to emphasize the warning.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vます (remove ます)","rhs":"V + かねない","note":"Examples: なり → なりかねない, 忘れ → 忘れかねない"},{"lhs":"Vます (remove ます) + polite","rhs":"V + かねません","note":"Use in formal contexts: 問題になりかねません"},{"lhs":"Vます (remove ます) + past","rhs":"V + かねなかった","note":"Expresses 'almost happened' or 'could have happened then'"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"計画","r":"けいかく","m":"plan"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"fail"},{"t":"し","m":"do"},{"t":"かねない","r":"かねない","m":"may happen"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This plan could fail.","scene":"Telling a teammate that the risky plan may fail."},{"tokens":[{"t":"約束","r":"やくそく","m":"promise"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"忘れ","r":"わすれ","m":"forget"},{"t":"かねない","r":"かねない","m":"may forget"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You might forget the promise.","scene":"Gently reminding someone who often forgets appointments."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-063||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～かねない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かねない","patternReading":"かねない","coreMeaning":"A negative or worrying outcome may occur","slug":"usage","definition":"Use ～かねない when the speaker **sees a risk** and wants to **warn someone or express concern**: 事故になりかねない (could cause an accident), 誤解されかねない (could be misunderstood), 健康を損ないかねない (could harm one's health). It is especially common in advice and warnings at work, at home, and in the news.","nuance":"The concern is **stronger than かもしれない** because the speaker wants the listener to act. It often combines with ので/から to give a reason for advising against something. It is moderately formal, not rude.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"副作用","r":"ふくさよう","m":"side effects"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"出","r":"で","m":"appear"},{"t":"かねない","r":"かねない","m":"may occur"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This medicine may cause side effects.","scene":"A doctor warns a patient about a new medicine."},{"tokens":[{"t":"あんな","m":"such"},{"t":"無謀","r":"むぼう","m":"reckless"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"計画","r":"けいかく","m":"plan"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"fail"},{"t":"し","m":"do"},{"t":"かねない","r":"かねない","m":"may happen"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A reckless plan like that could fail.","scene":"Friends discuss an overly risky business plan."},{"tokens":[{"t":"睡眠不足","r":"すいみんぶそく","m":"lack of sleep"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"損ない","r":"そこない","m":"damage"},{"t":"かねない","r":"かねない","m":"may harm"},{"t":"ので"},{"t":"注意","r":"ちゅうい","m":"care"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Lack of sleep can harm your health, so please be careful.","scene":"Advising a younger brother who stays up gaming."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-063||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Easily confused patterns","sub":"～かねない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かねない","patternReading":"かねない","coreMeaning":"A negative or worrying outcome may occur","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish ～かねない from two commonly confused patterns: **～かねる** (from the same root but with the opposite meaning) and **～かもしれない** (a neutral possibility).","nuance":"・かねる: the subject **cannot or refuses to do** something because it is difficult or uncomfortable.・かねない: a bad outcome **may occur**, and the speaker is worried.・かもしれない: makes no judgment about good or bad.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"発言","r":"はつげん","m":"remark"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"誤解","r":"ごかい","m":"misunderstanding"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"招き","r":"まねき","m":"cause"},{"t":"かねない","r":"かねない","m":"may cause"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That remark could cause a misunderstanding.","scene":"Advising a colleague about easily misunderstood wording."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～かねる","point":"かねる = 'cannot / be reluctant to do' (the subject refuses), while かねない = 'a bad thing may happen.' Examples: 言いかねる (be reluctant to say) / 失敗しかねない (may fail)."},{"vs":"～かもしれない","point":"かもしれない works for both good and bad possibilities and has no worried nuance. かねない is only for negative outcomes. Examples: 雨が降るかもしれない / 事故になりかねない."}]}},"n2-063||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"～かねない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かねない","patternReading":"かねない","coreMeaning":"A negative or worrying outcome may occur","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often use かねない for positive events or confuse it with かねる (refusal). Remember: **かねない is only for negative outcomes**, and the preceding form must be the **ます-stem of a verb**.","nuance":"Before かねない, the subject is usually an **event, situation, or person likely to cause harm**. Do not use it to talk about your own positive intentions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"行動","r":"こうどう","m":"action"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"周り","r":"まわり","m":"people around"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"誤解","r":"ごかい","m":"misunderstanding"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"招き","r":"まねき","m":"cause"},{"t":"かねない","r":"かねない","m":"may cause"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That action could cause misunderstandings among the people around you.","scene":"Advising a friend to think before acting."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼は優しいので、手伝いかねない。 → ✅ 彼は優しいので、すぐに手伝ってくれるだろう。 (かねない is only for NEGATIVE outcomes)","❌ 彼は成功しかねない。 → ✅ 彼は成功するかもしれない。 (do not use かねない for good results)","❌ 漢字が難しいから、読めないかねない。 → ✅ 漢字が難しいから、読めないかもしれない。 (かねない does not combine with the negative ない form; use Vます + かねない)","❌ この店はおいしいので、また行きかねない。 → ✅ この店はおいしいので、また行くだろう。 (a good action is not worrying)"],"compare":null}},"n2-063||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～かねない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かねない","patternReading":"かねない","coreMeaning":"A negative or worrying outcome may occur","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: かねない = 'not impossible' → **may happen** (a bad thing). Ask yourself: 'Is this something I fear may happen?' If YES → use かねない.","nuance":"It always carries **worry or warning**. If the sentence has no negative meaning, use another pattern such as かもしれない.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-063||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～かねない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～かねない","patternReading":"かねない","coreMeaning":"A negative or worrying outcome may occur","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases in tests and daily life: **事故になりかねない** (could lead to an accident), **失敗しかねない** (could fail), **誤解を招きかねない** (could cause misunderstanding), **健康を損ないかねない** (could harm health).","nuance":"Often used in **notices, warnings, and news reports**. It sounds especially natural with 放置する/このまま.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"このまま","m":"as it is"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"計画","r":"けいかく","m":"plan"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"fail"},{"t":"し","m":"do"},{"t":"かねない","r":"かねない","m":"may fail"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If things continue like this, the plan could fail.","scene":"Worrying as the project falls behind schedule."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-064||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～どころか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～どころか","patternReading":"どころか","coreMeaning":"Not only not ~, but also... / Far from ~, actually ~","slug":"core","definition":"〜どころか strongly rejects an expectation suggested by the listener or situation. The following clause is always opposite to or far beyond the first: “not only not ~, but also...” or “far from ~, not even ~....”","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing; in dialogue, it often sounds emphatic, surprised, or critical. It is less formal than administrative prose and rejects more strongly than はおろか when the second clause contrasts with the first.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"病気","r":"びょうき","m":"illness"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"治る","r":"なおる","m":"recover"},{"t":"どころか","r":"どころか","m":"far from"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"悪化","r":"あっか","m":"worsen"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The illness is not getting better; it is getting worse.","scene":"A doctor speaks to a patient after reviewing test results"},{"tokens":[{"t":"英語","r":"えいご","m":"English"},{"t":"どころか","r":"どころか","m":"let alone"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"話せない","r":"はなせない","m":"cannot speak"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let alone English, I can’t even speak Japanese.","scene":"A friend claims ten years of English study but cannot speak it"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-064||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"～どころか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～どころか","patternReading":"どころか","coreMeaning":"Not only not ~, but also... / Far from ~, actually ~","slug":"forms","definition":"〜どころか attaches directly to a **noun**, **verb in dictionary form**, **na-adjective stem** (without な), or **i-adjective**. No special change is needed; add どころか directly after the word.","nuance":"With verbs, the plain form is used, including dictionary, past た, and negative ない forms. With na-adjectives, remove な before adding どころか. With nouns, do not use が/を before どころか; は/も may follow for emphasis but are usually unnecessary.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"どころか","note":"Example: 英語どころか"},{"lhs":"V dictionary form","rhs":"どころか","note":"Example: 休むどころか"},{"lhs":"Na-adj stem","rhs":"どころか","note":"Example: 静かどころか"},{"lhs":"I-adj","rhs":"どころか","note":"Example: 高いどころか"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"休む","r":"やすむ","m":"rest"},{"t":"どころか","r":"どころか","m":"far from"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"残業","r":"ざんぎょう","m":"overtime"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"続いて","r":"つづいて","m":"continue"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Far from getting any rest, I have to keep working overtime.","scene":"An employee complains to a friend about work pressure"},{"tokens":[{"t":"静か","r":"しずか","m":"quiet"},{"t":"どころか","r":"どころか","m":"far from"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"うるさくて","r":"うるさくて","m":"so noisy"},{"t":"眠れない","r":"ねむれない","m":"cannot sleep"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Quiet? Not at all—it’s so noisy I can’t sleep.","scene":"A hotel guest complains to the front desk about the room"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-064||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～どころか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～どころか","patternReading":"どころか","coreMeaning":"Not only not ~, but also... / Far from ~, actually ~","slug":"usage","definition":"Usage 1: the first clause is an **expected or predicted outcome**, while the second rejects it and states the opposite reality, usually something worse. Usage 2: the first clause is the **minimum level**, and the second says that even this minimum is not met.","nuance":"Less common in formal writing; frequent in dialogue, interviews, and blogs. It can sound **sarcastic, irritated, or surprised**. It is casual with friends, but in professional settings use it tactfully to avoid offending others.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"謝る","r":"あやまる","m":"apologize"},{"t":"どころか","r":"どころか","m":"far from"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"まだ","r":"まだ","m":"still"},{"t":"言い訳","r":"いいわけ","m":"excuses"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Far from apologizing, he is still making excuses.","scene":"A boss questions an employee who caused a serious problem"},{"tokens":[{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"どころか","r":"どころか","m":"let alone"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"have no"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don’t even have time, let alone money.","scene":"Turning down friends’ plans because you are too busy"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-064||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Quick comparison","sub":"～どころか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～どころか","patternReading":"どころか","coreMeaning":"Not only not ~, but also... / Far from ~, actually ~","slug":"compare","definition":"Quickly distinguish three easily confused patterns: **どころか** requires the second clause to contrast with or reject the first; **ばかりか** simply adds a stronger second point; **はおろか** lists levels from low to high, often with a negative second clause.","nuance":"はおろか is more written and formal, while どころか is common in conversation and emotionally stronger because it reverses expectations. Check the direction: contrast → どころか, addition → ばかりか, descending list → はおろか.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"感謝","r":"かんしゃ","m":"gratitude"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"どころか","r":"どころか","m":"far from"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"文句","r":"もんく","m":"complaints"},{"t":"ばかり","r":"ばかり","m":"nothing but"},{"t":"言って","r":"いって","m":"say"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Far from being grateful, he does nothing but complain.","scene":"Describing an ungrateful person who was helped"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ばかりか","point":"どころか is used when the second clause CONTRASTS with or rejects the expectation; ばかりか ADDS more information, often positively. ❌ 歌うどころか、踊りも上手だ → ✅ 歌うばかりか、踊りも上手だ"},{"vs":"〜はおろか","point":"はおろか also means “let alone ~, even ~,” but tends to LIST levels in descending order; どころか stresses CONTRAST and rejection of expectation. Example: 漢字はおろか、ひらがなも書けない。 vs 漢字どころか、ひらがなも書けない。"}]}},"n2-064||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～どころか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～どころか","patternReading":"どころか","coreMeaning":"Not only not ~, but also... / Far from ~, actually ~","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often use どころか like “not only...but also...” for positive addition, but this pattern **is not used for simple addition**. If the second clause goes in the same direction, use ばかりか.","nuance":"In JLPT questions, the main trap is choosing どころか when ばかりか is needed. どころか requires contrast or rejection of expectation, while ばかりか simply adds information, often positively. For cumulative “not only...but also...,” use ばかりか.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"謝る","r":"あやまる","m":"apologize"},{"t":"どころか","r":"どころか","m":"far from"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"逆に","r":"ぎゃくに","m":"instead"},{"t":"怒り出した","r":"おこりだした","m":"got angry"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Far from apologizing, he got angry instead.","scene":"Retelling an argument where the person doubled down"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼女は歌うどころか、踊りも上手だ。 → ✅ 彼女は歌うばかりか、踊りも上手だ。","❌ 彼は優しいどころか、親切だ。 → ✅ 彼は優しいばかりか、親切だ。 (both are positive)","❌ 雨が降るどころか、風も強い。 → ✅ 雨が降るばかりか、風も強い。 (added information, not contrast)"],"compare":null}},"n2-064||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～どころか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～どころか","patternReading":"どころか","coreMeaning":"Not only not ~, but also... / Far from ~, actually ~","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: think of どころか as asking, “What do you mean, level X?”—rejecting even level X before moving to Y. When you see どころか, ask whether the second clause contrasts with or falls below the first.","nuance":"This is only a memory aid, not a grammar rule. Whenever you see どころか, think of an expectation X being completely rejected and replaced by Y.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-065||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～はもとより · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～はもとより","patternReading":"はもとより","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ (obvious) but also ~ (additional)","slug":"core","definition":"～はもとより expresses that not only the first part (an obvious, commonly accepted point), but also the second part (a more noteworthy point) applies. The second part is often followed by も to emphasize the addition. This is a formal expression commonly found in writing and speeches.","nuance":"More formal and sophisticated than はもちろん. Use it to give a well-rounded evaluation: the first part is the foundation, while the second is an impressive added point. Avoid it in casual everyday conversation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"英語","r":"えいご","m":"English"},{"t":"はもとより","r":"はもとより","m":"not only...but also"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"中国語","r":"ちゅうごくご","m":"Chinese"},{"t":"や"},{"t":"フランス語","r":"ふらんすご","m":"French"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"話せる","r":"はなせる","m":"can speak"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He can speak not only English but also Chinese and French.","scene":"Introducing a friend’s language skills"},{"tokens":[{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ため","m":"for"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"運動","r":"うんどう","m":"exercise"},{"t":"はもとより","r":"はもとより","m":"not only...but also"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"食事","r":"しょくじ","m":"diet"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"大切","r":"たいせつ","m":"important"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For good health, not only exercise but diet is also important.","scene":"Sharing health tips in conversation"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-065||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"～はもとより · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～はもとより","patternReading":"はもとより","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ (obvious) but also ~ (additional)","slug":"forms","definition":"Main structure: [Noun 1] + はもとより + [Noun 2] + も. Noun 1 is obvious, while noun 2 is the more important addition. The variant ももとより can also occur, but は is more common.","nuance":"In formal writing, it often appears at the beginning of a clause to add items to a list. It is not used with commands, invitations, or greetings.","structure":[{"lhs":"N1","rhs":"はもとより","note":"N1 is the obvious, expected point"},{"lhs":"N2","rhs":"も","note":"N2 is the added point, emphasized by も"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"ホテル","m":"hotel"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"設備","r":"せつび","m":"facilities"},{"t":"はもとより","r":"はもとより","m":"not only...but also"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"サービス","m":"service"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"一流","r":"いちりゅう","m":"first-class"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This hotel has not only excellent facilities but also first-class service.","scene":"Writing a hotel review after a stay"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"桜","r":"さくら","m":"cherry blossoms"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"国内","r":"こくない","m":"in Japan"},{"t":"はもとより","r":"はもとより","m":"not only...but also"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"世界中","r":"せかいじゅう","m":"around the world"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"有名","r":"ゆうめい","m":"famous"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Japan’s cherry blossoms are famous not only domestically but around the world as well.","scene":"Introducing Japanese culture to international friends"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-065||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～はもとより · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～はもとより","patternReading":"はもとより","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ (obvious) but also ~ (additional)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to emphasize that, besides the obvious first part, the second part is equally or even more noteworthy. It often appears in introductions, evaluations, and speeches.","nuance":"Polite, objective, and somewhat formal. With friends, はもちろん sounds more natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ダンス","m":"dancing"},{"t":"はもとより","r":"はもとより","m":"not only...but also"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"歌","r":"うた","m":"singing"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"good at"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She is good not only at dancing but also at singing.","scene":"Praising a performing friend’s talents"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"味","r":"あじ","m":"taste"},{"t":"はもとより","r":"はもとより","m":"not only...but also"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"雰囲気","r":"ふんいき","m":"atmosphere"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"いい"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This restaurant has not only good food but also a nice atmosphere.","scene":"Recommending a restaurant to friends"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-065||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with はもちろん・はおろか","sub":"～はもとより · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～はもとより","patternReading":"はもとより","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ (obvious) but also ~ (additional)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes ～はもとより from easily confused patterns. Knowing the key difference helps you use them correctly.","nuance":"はもとより and はもちろん are nearly interchangeable, but はもとより is more formal. はおろか is negative or self-deprecating. ばかりか emphasizes an addition beyond expectations.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"英語","r":"えいご","m":"English"},{"t":"はもとより","r":"はもとより","m":"not only...but also"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"good at"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is good at not only English but Japanese as well.","scene":"Praising someone’s language skills"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"はもちろん","point":"Same meaning, but はもちろん works in everyday speech; はもとより is used in writing and formal contexts."},{"vs":"はおろか","point":"はもとより: A is obvious + B is added. はおろか: not even A, let alone B (negative). Compare: ひらがなはもとより、漢字も書ける (can write not only Hiragana but also Kanji) vs 漢字はおろか、ひらがなも書けない (cannot write even Hiragana, let alone Kanji)."},{"vs":"ばかりか","point":"ばかりか also means “not only A but B,” but B is surprising and exceeds A; はもとより treats A as obvious."}]}},"n2-065||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～はもとより · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～はもとより","patternReading":"はもとより","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ (obvious) but also ~ (additional)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"～はもとより is a formal grammar pattern, but learners often omit the added part. The most common error is giving only one noun without the “also ~” part, making the sentence incomplete. Learners also confuse it with はもちろん or use it for an unsuitable negative meaning. Remember that はもとより normally links at least two nouns: the first is obvious, and the second is added, usually with も. For an addition beyond expectations, use ばかりか; for “not even A,” use はおろか. When correcting an error, add part B with も and make sure the meaning is positive or neutral.","nuance":"Remember that this is a two-part pattern: obvious A + added B. Without B, the pattern is incomplete.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"味","r":"あじ","m":"taste"},{"t":"はもとより","r":"はもとより","m":"not only...but also"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"値段","r":"ねだん","m":"price"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"安い","r":"やすい","m":"cheap"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This restaurant has not only good food but also low prices.","scene":"Recommending an affordable, tasty restaurant"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 宿題はもとより、できなかった。 → ✅ 宿題はもとより、テストの勉強もできなかった。 (The sentence is wrong because B is missing)","❌ 今日はもとより、雨が降っている。 → ✅ 今日は気温はもとより、湿度も高い。 (Two nouns are needed for comparison)","❌ 彼は英語はもとより、フランス語を話せない。 → ✅ 彼は英語はもとより、フランス語も話せない。 (Part B must take も)","❌ この店は味はもとより、値段が安い。 → ✅ この店は味はもとより、値段も安い。 (Part B must take も)"],"compare":null}},"n2-065||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～はもとより · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～はもとより","patternReading":"はもとより","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ (obvious) but also ~ (additional)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tip: もとより is written 元より, meaning “it was that way from the beginning.” Therefore, the item before はもとより is an obvious fact that needs no debate.","nuance":"Memory cue: “ALREADY good, and even more impressive.”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-065||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～はもとより · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～はもとより","patternReading":"はもとより","coreMeaning":"Not only ~ (obvious) but also ~ (additional)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases often appear on exams and in formal contexts. Memorize them to build quick recognition.","nuance":"They commonly combine with nouns such as 設備, サービス, 実力, 国内, 国外, and 品質.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"商品","r":"しょうひん","m":"product"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"品質","r":"ひんしつ","m":"quality"},{"t":"はもとより","r":"はもとより","m":"not only...but also"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"デザイン","m":"design"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"優れている","r":"すぐれている","m":"excellent"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This product has not only high quality but also an excellent design.","scene":"Evaluating a product in a company catalog"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-066||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～にほかならない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にほかならない","patternReading":"にほかならない","coreMeaning":"Nothing other than ~ / Precisely ~ (strong assertion)","slug":"core","definition":"〜にほかならない expresses a **strong assertion**: something is **exactly ~**, and nothing else. The speaker emphasizes the confirmed **true nature** or **underlying cause** of an event, leaving no doubt. It is a decisive, judgment-like statement often found in written and formal language.","nuance":"**Strong, direct, and formal**; suitable for reports, speeches, and essays, but not casual messages to friends. Compared with 〜に違いない (personal inference), 〜にほかならない **asserts an established fact** and strongly calls for agreement.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"成功","r":"せいこう","m":"success"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"結果","r":"けっか","m":"result"},{"t":"にほかならない","r":"にほかならない","m":"is precisely"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Her success is nothing other than the result of her efforts.","scene":"Analyzing the cause of someone's success"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"事故","r":"じこ","m":"accident"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"安全管理","r":"あんぜんかんり","m":"safety management"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"怠慢","r":"たいまん","m":"negligence"},{"t":"にほかならない","r":"にほかならない","m":"is precisely"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This accident is nothing other than negligence in safety management.","scene":"Speaking at a workplace safety meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-066||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and usage","sub":"～にほかならない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にほかならない","patternReading":"にほかならない","coreMeaning":"Nothing other than ~ / Precisely ~ (strong assertion)","slug":"forms","definition":"There are two main forms: **noun + にほかならない** and **V-こと + にほかならない**. にほかならない always appears at the **end of the sentence** as the predicate; it cannot be placed before a noun as a modifier.","nuance":"Use the V-こと form when emphasizing a specific **action or event** as the cause, such as 成功したのは、チームワークがよかったことにほかならない。 It can occur in formal speech, but still has a written-style tone.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"N + にほかならない","note":"Most common use: ～はN＋にほかならない"},{"lhs":"V-こと","rhs":"V-こと + にほかならない","note":"Emphasizes that the cause is ~"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"成功した","r":"せいこうした","m":"succeeded"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"チームワーク","r":"チームワーク","m":"teamwork"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"よかった","r":"よかった","m":"was good"},{"t":"こと"},{"t":"にほかならない","r":"にほかならない","m":"is precisely"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The reason for the success is nothing other than good teamwork.","scene":"Summarizing a successful project"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"job"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"選んだ","r":"えらんだ","m":"chose"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"支える","r":"ささえる","m":"support"},{"t":"こと"},{"t":"にほかならない","r":"にほかならない","m":"is precisely"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"His reason for choosing this job is nothing other than to support his family.","scene":"Discussing a friend's career choice"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-066||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"～にほかならない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にほかならない","patternReading":"にほかならない","coreMeaning":"Nothing other than ~ / Precisely ~ (strong assertion)","slug":"usage","definition":"It is mainly used to explain a **core cause** or conclude the **true nature** of something after analysis. In both cases, the speaker presents a **certain, exclusive explanation**.","nuance":"Very formal and **rhetorical/assertive**. In JLPT reading passages, recognize the meaning \"precisely ~ / due to ~ / nothing other than ~.\" Do not interpret it as \"probably.\"","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"豊か","r":"ゆたか","m":"prosperous"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"生活","r":"せいかつ","m":"life"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"先人たち","r":"せんじんたち","m":"predecessors"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"苦労","r":"くろう","m":"hardship"},{"t":"にほかならない","r":"にほかならない","m":"is precisely"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Our prosperous life today is nothing other than the result of our predecessors' hardships.","scene":"Speech at an anniversary ceremony"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"成功した","r":"せいこうした","m":"succeeded"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"たゆまぬ","r":"たゆまぬ","m":"unrelenting"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"結果","r":"けっか","m":"result"},{"t":"にほかならない","r":"にほかならない","m":"is precisely"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"His success is nothing other than the result of his tireless efforts.","scene":"Commenting on a newly promoted friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-066||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns to distinguish","sub":"～にほかならない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にほかならない","patternReading":"にほかならない","coreMeaning":"Nothing other than ~ / Precisely ~ (strong assertion)","slug":"compare","definition":"The closest patterns often confused with this one are **〜に違いない** and **〜に過ぎない**. All three contain に plus a negative form, but their **meanings are completely different**.","nuance":"Ask what attitude the speaker has: are they **making an inference** (に違いない), **downplaying something** (に過ぎない), or making a **maximum-strength assertion** (にほかならない)?","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"合格","r":"ごうかく","m":"passing"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日々","r":"ひび","m":"daily"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"積み重ね","r":"つみかさね","m":"accumulation"},{"t":"にほかならない","r":"にほかならない","m":"is precisely"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Passing the exam is nothing other than the result of daily effort.","scene":"Sharing study experience in an interview"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～に違いない","point":"に違いない = 'must be' based on inference; にほかならない = asserts a clear underlying nature/cause. Example: 彼はきっと来るに違いない (inference) vs 成功したのは努力にほかならない (assertion)."},{"vs":"～に過ぎない","point":"に過ぎない downplays: 'only ~'; にほかならない strongly emphasizes: 'exactly ~'. Example: それは一つの意見に過ぎない (only an opinion) vs それが原因にほかならない (that is precisely the cause)."}]}},"n2-066||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～にほかならない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にほかならない","patternReading":"にほかならない","coreMeaning":"Nothing other than ~ / Precisely ~ (strong assertion)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make three mistakes: placing にほかならない before a noun, using it in an overly casual context, and confusing it with the inference pattern に違いない.","nuance":"Remember: にほかならない always **closes the sentence** as a predicate, like a decisive final stamp—it comes at the end.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"幸せ","r":"しあわせ","m":"happiness"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"支え","r":"ささえ","m":"support"},{"t":"にほかならない","r":"にほかならない","m":"is precisely"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My happiness today is nothing other than my family's support.","scene":"In a personal reflection"}],"pitfalls":["❌ それはにほかならない問題だ。 → ✅ それは問題にほかならない。 (にほかならない does not modify a noun; it serves as the final predicate.)","❌ 遅刻はタクシーが来なかったにほかならない。 → Use せいだ/ためだ in ordinary conversation; にほかならない suits written analysis.","❌ 明日は雨が降るにほかならない。 → ✅ 明日は雨が降るに違いない。 (にほかならない asserts an established cause/nature, not a future prediction.)"],"compare":null}},"n2-066||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"～にほかならない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にほかならない","patternReading":"にほかならない","coreMeaning":"Nothing other than ~ / Precisely ~ (strong assertion)","slug":"memory","definition":"Break it down as ほか = 他 = **something else**. にほかならない = **not something else** → naturally, 'it is precisely that.' Picture a finger **pointing straight** at the cause or essence of an issue.","nuance":"This tip keeps you from confusing it with に違いない: にほかならない does not mean 'must be'; it means 'is precisely.'","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-066||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～にほかならない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～にほかならない","patternReading":"にほかならない","coreMeaning":"Nothing other than ~ / Precisely ~ (strong assertion)","slug":"phrases","definition":"This pattern often combines with nouns for **results, causes, and actions**: 努力の結果, 安全管理の怠慢, 先人の苦労, 家族の支え. When you see these phrases, にほかならない is likely to follow.","nuance":"In JLPT questions, you will often see 〜は、〜にほかならない with topics such as 'success,' 'accidents,' and 'life.' Use this pattern to recognize the answer quickly.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"発展","r":"はってん","m":"development"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"社員","r":"しゃいん","m":"employees"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"協力","r":"きょうりょく","m":"cooperation"},{"t":"にほかならない","r":"にほかならない","m":"is precisely"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This company's growth is nothing other than the result of its employees' cooperation.","scene":"Speech at a company anniversary"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-067||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～に過ぎない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に過ぎない","patternReading":"にすぎない","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Nothing more than ~ (downplaying, humble)","slug":"core","definition":"〜に過ぎない means the speaker **downplays / minimizes** the degree of something: 'it's only ~, nothing special, no more than ~.' It can show **humility** or a **realistic** assessment. It often combines with ただ／単に to emphasize 'only.'","nuance":"This is a **neutral, formal** expression used in both speech and writing, but it appears often in written language and speeches because of its humble, rational tone. It does **not** express uncertainty or guesswork; the speaker knows the fact and intentionally **downplays** it. Its minimizing effect is much stronger than だけ (only).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"me"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"個人的な","r":"こじんてきな","m":"personal"},{"t":"意見","r":"いけん","m":"opinion"},{"t":"に過ぎない","r":"にすぎない","m":"only..."},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is nothing more than my personal opinion.","scene":"Downplaying your opinion in a meeting"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"まだ","m":"only"},{"t":"新入社員","r":"しんにゅうしゃいん","m":"new employee"},{"t":"に過ぎない","r":"にすぎない","m":"only..."},{"t":"のに"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大きな","r":"おおきな","m":"major"},{"t":"責任","r":"せきにん","m":"responsibility"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"任された","r":"まかされた","m":"was entrusted with"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is only a new employee, yet he was given major responsibilities.","scene":"A young colleague is entrusted with major work"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-067||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～に過ぎない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に過ぎない","patternReading":"にすぎない","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Nothing more than ~ (downplaying, humble)","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch organizes the connection patterns. General structure: **N / V(る・た) + に過ぎない**. To emphasize 'only this much,' add **ただ / 単に / ほんの** before it. に過ぎない is already a **complete negative form** and is not further conjugated into a negative form; it inherently means 'not exceed.'","nuance":"This expression is somewhat **bookish** and formal, so it is less common in casual conversation. In informal contexts, Japanese speakers often use **ただの～だ** or **～だけだ** instead. For the past: **〜だったに過ぎない** / **〜に過ぎなかった**; polite: **〜に過ぎません**.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"＋ に過ぎない","note":"N often combines with ただ／単なる: 単なる噂に過ぎない"},{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"＋ に過ぎない","note":"An action 'only goes this far': 知っているに過ぎない"},{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"＋ に過ぎない","note":"An event happened but is unimportant: 聞いたに過ぎない"},{"lhs":"Nである","rhs":"＋ に過ぎない","note":"Formal written style: 説明であるに過ぎない"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"単なる","r":"たんなる","m":"mere"},{"t":"仮説","r":"かせつ","m":"hypothesis"},{"t":"に過ぎない","r":"にすぎない","m":"only..."},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is merely a hypothesis.","scene":"A researcher modestly calls it a hypothesis"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"聞いた","r":"きいた","m":"heard"},{"t":"に過ぎない","r":"にすぎない","m":"only..."},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He only heard about it.","scene":"He only heard about it; he did not decide"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-067||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～に過ぎない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に過ぎない","patternReading":"にすぎない","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Nothing more than ~ (downplaying, humble)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜に過ぎない **reduces the value or degree** of something. Typical uses include: ① **humility** when commenting on oneself; ② **dismissal / reassurance** when saying a concern is minor; ③ **realistic comparison** when something has not yet reached a significant level.","nuance":"The tone is usually **rational and objective**, though it can carry mild sarcasm when used about a person. In casual speech, speakers rarely use the full pattern; they may use **ただの～だ** or **～にすぎないよ** in an argument for emphasis.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"それ"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ただの","m":"just a"},{"t":"噂","r":"うわさ","m":"rumor"},{"t":"に過ぎない","r":"にすぎない","m":"only..."},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"},{"t":"信じない","r":"しんじない","m":"don't believe"},{"t":"ほうがいい","m":"had better"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It's only a rumor, so you shouldn't believe it.","scene":"Reassuring a friend worried by a rumor."},{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"アプリ","m":"app"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"まだ","m":"still"},{"t":"試験段階","r":"しけんだんかい","m":"testing stage"},{"t":"に過ぎない","r":"にすぎない","m":"only..."},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This app is still only in the testing stage.","scene":"Managing a customer's high expectations"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-067||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast: に過ぎない vs にほかならない","sub":"～に過ぎない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に過ぎない","patternReading":"にすぎない","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Nothing more than ~ (downplaying, humble)","slug":"compare","definition":"These two patterns are easy to confuse: **〜に過ぎない** **downplays** something, while **〜にほかならない** **asserts** it strongly. Learners may translate both as 'only,' creating illogical sentences such as '彼女は天才に過ぎない.' To say 'she is truly a genius,' use にほかならない; use に過ぎない when downplaying.","nuance":"The difference lies in the **speaker's intention**: to praise or emphasize something, use にほかならない. To be humble or deny importance, use に過ぎない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"それ"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"単なる","r":"たんなる","m":"mere"},{"t":"噂","r":"うわさ","m":"rumor"},{"t":"に過ぎない","r":"にすぎない","m":"only..."},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That is merely a rumor.","scene":"Emphasizing that it should not be taken seriously."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜にほかならない","point":"〜に過ぎない = 'only, no more than' (downplaying); 〜にほかならない = 'nothing other than' (absolute assertion). ❌ 彼女は天才に過ぎない → ✅ 彼女は天才にほかならない."},{"vs":"〜だけだ","point":"〜だけだ simply limits something (only); 〜に過ぎない adds an evaluative sense of being ordinary or insignificant."}]}},"n2-067||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～に過ぎない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に過ぎない","patternReading":"にすぎない","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Nothing more than ~ (downplaying, humble)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"This branch lists common errors learners make with 〜に過ぎない. Most importantly, it always downplays and is never used to praise. Also, the structure requires **に** before 過ぎない, and it must not be confused with **〜に違いない**.","nuance":"Many learners learn the meaning 'only' and then use it for praise, overlooking its minimizing tone. Others omit に because they are used to dropping particles.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"それ"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"my"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"考え","r":"かんがえ","m":"thought"},{"t":"に過ぎない","r":"にすぎない","m":"only..."},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That's only my opinion.","scene":"Humbly making a suggestion to the group."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼女は天才に過ぎない。 → ✅ 彼女は天才にほかならない。 (に過ぎない downplays, so it cannot praise her)","❌ これは私の意見過ぎない。 → ✅ これは私の意見に過ぎない。 (に is required)","❌ 彼が犯人に過ぎない。 → ✅ 彼が犯人に違いない。 (to mean 'he must be the culprit,' use に違いない; に過ぎない would mean 'he is only the culprit,' which is illogical)"],"compare":null}},"n2-067||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～に過ぎない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に過ぎない","patternReading":"にすぎない","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Nothing more than ~ (downplaying, humble)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember it by **breaking down the words**: 過ぎる = exceed, ない = not → **not exceed** = only this much, nothing more. Imagine a line: the thing stays **within that line** and does not go beyond it.","nuance":"When unsure, ask yourself: 'Do I want to **downplay** something or show humility?' If yes, use に過ぎない. If you want to assert something strongly, use にほかならない.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-067||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～に過ぎない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に過ぎない","patternReading":"にすぎない","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Nothing more than ~ (downplaying, humble)","slug":"phrases","definition":"〜に過ぎない often appears with abstract nouns such as **opinions, rumors, stages, and people in a low-ranking position**. Learning these chunks helps you respond quickly in JLPT questions and real-world texts.","nuance":"These phrases all carry a **humble tone or a warning not to overestimate** something. In reading passages, watch for ただ／単に／ほんの before the pattern as clues.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"言った","r":"いった","m":"said"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ほんの","m":"just a little"},{"t":"参考意見","r":"さんこういけん","m":"reference opinion"},{"t":"に過ぎない","r":"にすぎない","m":"only..."},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What I said was merely a little reference opinion.","scene":"Humbly speaking in a meeting."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-068||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～というものだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～というものだ","patternReading":"というものだ","coreMeaning":"That is what ~ truly is / That is ~ (defining its nature or principle)","slug":"core","definition":"〜というものだ reflects the **essential nature** of something or a concept. It is not used to describe an event, but to **define** it: “That is what ~ truly is” or “That is indeed ~.” The speaker presents a general truth or life principle, often with strong conviction.","nuance":"It has a **formal, slightly philosophical** tone and often appears in essays, speeches, or advice. Avoid it in casual conversation to describe ordinary daily events.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"から","m":"from"},{"t":"学ぶ","r":"まなぶ","m":"learn"},{"t":"の","m":"the act of"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"成長","r":"せいちょう","m":"growth"},{"t":"というものだ","r":"というものだ","m":"is what truly is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Learning from failure is what true growth is all about.","scene":"An older brother comforts his sibling after failing an exam."},{"tokens":[{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"さえ","m":"just"},{"t":"あれば","m":"if you have"},{"t":"幸せ","r":"しあわせ","m":"happiness"},{"t":"というものではない","r":"というものではない","m":"does not always mean"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Having money does not automatically mean being happy.","scene":"Replying when a close friend says, “Being rich means being happy.”"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-068||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structures & forms","sub":"～というものだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～というものだ","patternReading":"というものだ","coreMeaning":"That is what ~ truly is / That is ~ (defining its nature or principle)","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern follows **nouns, verbs (Vる), and plain-form adjectives**. Do not use it with the て-form or た-form to describe a specific event. Study the negative form **〜というものではない** together with it to reject a mistaken assumption.","nuance":"Both forms express a **generalization**, not a specific case. The pattern normally stays in the present tense when defining something; past usage is rare and requires conjugation before という.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"Vる + というものだ","note":"Example: 助け合うというものだ"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"N + というものだ","note":"Example: 友情というものだ"},{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"Vる + というものではない","note":"Rejecting an assumption"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"N + というものではない","note":"Rejecting a false definition"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"困って","r":"こまって","m":"in trouble"},{"t":"いる","m":"be"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"助ける","r":"たすける","m":"help"},{"t":"の","m":"the act of"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"本当","r":"ほんとう","m":"true"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"友情","r":"ゆうじょう","m":"friendship"},{"t":"というものだ","r":"というものだ","m":"is what truly is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Helping people in trouble is what true friendship is all about.","scene":"In an essay about the meaning of friendship."},{"tokens":[{"t":"高ければ","r":"たかければ","m":"if expensive"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"というものではない","r":"というものではない","m":"does not always mean"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Expensive does not automatically mean good.","scene":"Giving advice when a friend always chooses luxury goods."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-068||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～というものだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～というものだ","patternReading":"というものだ","coreMeaning":"That is what ~ truly is / That is ~ (defining its nature or principle)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this when the speaker **asserts the true nature** of an abstract concept such as friendship, love, or growth. The negative form rejects a mistaken understanding: X does not automatically mean Y.","nuance":"Its **judgmental, advisory** tone makes it common in literature, speeches, and social commentary. In conversation, it sounds like a strong statement from someone speaking with experience.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"安ければ","r":"やすければ","m":"if cheap"},{"t":"安い","r":"やすい","m":"cheap"},{"t":"ほど","m":"the more"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"というものではない","r":"というものではない","m":"does not always mean"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Cheaper does not automatically mean better.","scene":"Responding when a colleague praises cheap products."},{"tokens":[{"t":"親","r":"おや","m":"parents"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"大切","r":"たいせつ","m":"important"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"する","m":"make"},{"t":"の","m":"the act of"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"親孝行","r":"おやこうこう","m":"filial devotion"},{"t":"というものだ","r":"というものだ","m":"is what truly is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Treating your parents well is what filial devotion truly means.","scene":"A mother teaches her child through a proverb."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-068||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"～というものだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～というものだ","patternReading":"というものだ","coreMeaning":"That is what ~ truly is / That is ~ (defining its nature or principle)","slug":"compare","definition":"The three easiest pairs to confuse: **〜ものだ** states an obvious truth; **〜とは限らない** says exceptions are possible; **〜ものではない** advises against doing something. Meanwhile, 〜というものだ/ではない defines an essence or rejects a mistaken belief.","nuance":"Remember: というものだ **defines essence**; ものだ states an **obvious truth**; とは限らない indicates an **exception**; ものではない advises **against doing something**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"多ければ","r":"おおければ","m":"if many"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"というものではない","r":"というものではない","m":"does not always mean"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"More does not automatically mean better.","scene":"Debating quality versus quantity."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～ものだ","point":"〜ものだ: an obvious truth (e.g., 人間はいつか死ぬものだ) / 〜というものだ: defining a concept’s essence (e.g., 困っている人を助けるのが友情というものだ)"},{"vs":"～とは限らない","point":"〜とは限らない: says exceptions are possible (e.g., 金持ちが幸せとは限らない) / 〜というものではない: rejects a false assumption (e.g., 金があれば幸せというものではない)"},{"vs":"～ものではない","point":"〜ものではない: advises “do not do it” (e.g., 人を傷つけるものではない) / 〜というものではない: “X does not automatically mean Y” (e.g., 多ければいいというものではない)"}]}},"n2-068||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～というものだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～というものだ","patternReading":"というものだ","coreMeaning":"That is what ~ truly is / That is ~ (defining its nature or principle)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The most common learner errors are **using というものだ for a single event** or using the wrong negative form. This pattern is only for judgments about essence, general truths, or principles.","nuance":"Not every sentence meaning “that is” can use it. For a simple introduction or factual statement, use だ/です. The negative is というものではない—do not simply add ない to ものだ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"一生懸命","r":"いっしょうけんめい","m":"with all one’s effort"},{"t":"やれば","m":"if you do"},{"t":"必ず","r":"かならず","m":"certainly"},{"t":"成功する","r":"せいこうする","m":"succeed"},{"t":"というものではない","r":"というものではない","m":"does not always mean"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Working as hard as possible does not guarantee success.","scene":"Comforting someone who failed despite trying very hard."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 今日は寒いというものだ。 → ✅ 今日は寒い。 (Not for a simple event; use it only for an essential judgment.)","❌ 彼は先生というものだ。 → ✅ 彼は先生だ。 (Not for introducing a specific person or thing.)","❌ 高ければいいというものだ。 → ✅ 高ければいいというものではない。 (Use the negative form to reject an assumption.)","❌ 彼はお金がないというものだ。 → ✅ 彼はお金がないようだ。 (This is a guess, not a definition of essence.)"],"compare":null}},"n2-068||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～というものだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～というものだ","patternReading":"というものだ","coreMeaning":"That is what ~ truly is / That is ~ (defining its nature or principle)","slug":"memory","definition":"Ask yourself: **“Am I defining the true nature of ~?”** If yes, use 〜というものだ; if you want to reject an assumption, use 〜というものではない.","nuance":"Associate it with the English phrase “That is what ~ is all about,” which closely matches “that is what ~ truly is.” The negative resembles “It is not necessarily the case that ~.”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-068||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases & collocations","sub":"～というものだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～というものだ","patternReading":"というものだ","coreMeaning":"That is what ~ truly is / That is ~ (defining its nature or principle)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are **set phrases** often seen in exams and written texts: 本当の友情・大人・成長 + というものだ; 幸せ・成功・高ければいい + というものではない.","nuance":"Abstract nouns such as 友情, 大人, 成長, 幸せ, and 青春 often occur with this pattern because they invite a renewed definition. When you see these nouns, think of というものだ/ではない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"すれば","m":"if you do"},{"t":"必ず","r":"かならず","m":"certainly"},{"t":"報われる","r":"むくわれる","m":"be rewarded"},{"t":"というものではない","r":"というものではない","m":"does not always mean"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Making an effort does not guarantee that you will be rewarded.","scene":"You say, “I keep trying, but I still fail.”"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-069||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ものがある · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものがある","patternReading":"ものがある","coreMeaning":"There is something noteworthy ~ (subjective impression)","slug":"core","definition":"～ものがある expresses the speaker's **subjective judgment** that something contains a **noteworthy quality or characteristic**. The speaker senses it even if they cannot clearly explain why. It often means **“there is something ~”** or **“there is no denying that ~.”**","nuance":"This pattern is fairly formal but works in both speech and writing. It often appears in emotional, thoughtful comments and evaluations. It emphasizes a **subjective feeling** rather than an objective claim. Do not use it for casual, ordinary observations.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"演奏","r":"えんそう","m":"performance"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"聴く","r":"きく","m":"listen"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"people"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"心","r":"こころ","m":"heart"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"動かす","r":"うごかす","m":"move"},{"t":"ものがある","r":"ものがある","m":"there is something"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is something about his performance that touches listeners' hearts.","scene":"Talking about a performer who moves the audience"},{"tokens":[{"t":"その"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"film"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"考えさせられる","r":"かんがえさせられる","m":"make one think"},{"t":"ものがある","r":"ものがある","m":"there is something"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is something about that film that makes you think.","scene":"Sharing thoughts after watching a profound film"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-069||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & connecting forms","sub":"～ものがある · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものがある","patternReading":"ものがある","coreMeaning":"There is something noteworthy ~ (subjective impression)","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern combines **a clause describing a quality** with ものがある. Common forms are **dictionary-form V + ものがある**, **い-adjective + ものがある**, and **な-adjective + ものがある**. ものがある itself does not change for tense; it highlights the existence of a quality.","nuance":"It often combines with evaluative adjectives and verbs such as 感動的, 哀れ, 物悲しい, 問題がある, 心を動かす, and 惹きつける. The part before ものがある is usually an **emotionally colored evaluation**, not an objective description.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"ものがある","note":"Example: 彼の話には人を引き込むものがある。"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"ものがある","note":"Example: 一人旅には寂しいものがある。"},{"lhs":"Aな","rhs":"ものがある","note":"Example: 彼女の歌声には感動的なものがある。"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"一人","r":"ひとり","m":"alone"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"旅","r":"たび","m":"travel"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"どこか"},{"t":"寂しい","r":"さびしい","m":"lonely"},{"t":"ものがある","r":"ものがある","m":"there is something"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is something lonely about traveling alone.","scene":"Reflecting on the feeling of traveling alone"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの"},{"t":"選手","r":"せんしゅ","m":"athlete"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"走り","r":"はしり","m":"running style"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"鬼気迫る","r":"ききせまる","m":"intense"},{"t":"ものがある","r":"ものがある","m":"there is something"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is something fiercely intense about that athlete's running.","scene":"Commenting on a high-pressure performance"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-069||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～ものがある · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものがある","patternReading":"ものがある","coreMeaning":"There is something noteworthy ~ (subjective impression)","slug":"usage","definition":"～ものがある is used when the speaker wants to say that something contains **a noteworthy emotional quality or value**. It often **praises a quality** or **expresses a deep feeling** that is difficult to name precisely.","nuance":"Typical contexts involve thoughtful experiences, such as evaluating a film, song, writing style, or the loneliness of being away from home. It is not used for ordinary comments about weather or prices. The register is somewhat formal and suits essays, speeches, and sincere reviews.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"その"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"film"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"考えさせられる","r":"かんがえさせられる","m":"make one think"},{"t":"ものがある","r":"ものがある","m":"there is something"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is something about that film that makes you think.","scene":"A film reviewer comments on the film's value"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"文章","r":"ぶんしょう","m":"writing style"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"読む","r":"よむ","m":"read"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"people"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"惹きつける","r":"ひきつける","m":"attract"},{"t":"ものがある","r":"ものがある","m":"there is something"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is something about her writing that attracts readers.","scene":"Commenting on a writer or blogger"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-069||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing ものだ・というものだ","sub":"～ものがある · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものがある","patternReading":"ものがある","coreMeaning":"There is something noteworthy ~ (subjective impression)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes ～ものがある from similar patterns that also contain もの. **～ものだ** expresses a general truth, rule, or exclamation; **～というものだ** defines or asserts the essence of something; **～ものがある** points to a subtle quality sensed by the speaker.","nuance":"When choosing a pattern, ask: “Do I mean a general truth (ものだ), an essential definition (というものだ), or a subjective impression of something (ものがある)?” ものがある is usually more personal and emotional.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"歌声","r":"うたごえ","m":"singing voice"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"耳","r":"みみ","m":"ear"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"奪う","r":"うばう","m":"captivate"},{"t":"ものがある","r":"ものがある","m":"there is something"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is something captivating about her singing voice.","scene":"Expressing an impression of a singer"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～ものだ","point":"ものだ describes a GENERAL TRUTH or rule (e.g. 人間はいつか死ぬものだ). ものがある describes the speaker's SUBJECTIVE IMPRESSION of a quality (e.g. 彼の言葉には胸を打つものがある)."},{"vs":"～というものだ","point":"というものだ DEFINES or asserts an essence (“that is precisely ~”), while ものがある evaluates something as containing A NOTEWORTHY QUALITY. E.g. これこそ運命というものだ vs この物語には不思議なものがある."}]}},"n2-069||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～ものがある · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものがある","patternReading":"ものがある","coreMeaning":"There is something noteworthy ~ (subjective impression)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often misuse ～ものがある for simple comments about weather or preferences, where no noteworthy quality is implied. They may also choose a subject or particle that makes the sentence unnatural.","nuance":"The pattern requires something that is being evaluated. ～には + ものがある is common for saying “there is something in ~.” Do not use を directly with ものがある.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"冬","r":"ふゆ","m":"winter"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"寒さ","r":"さむさ","m":"cold"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"どこか"},{"t":"物悲しい","r":"ものがなしい","m":"melancholy"},{"t":"ものがある","r":"ものがある","m":"there is something"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is something melancholy about this winter's cold.","scene":"A character senses melancholy on a rainy winter day"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 今日は寒いものがある。 → ✅ この冬の寒さには、どこか物悲しいものがある。 (It needs an emotional context, not an ordinary weather description)","❌ 彼は才能があるものがある。 → ✅ 彼の演奏には才能を感じさせるものがある。 (Before ものがある, use a clause or evaluative adjective, not a noun directly)","❌ 彼の話を聞いて感動するものがある。 → ✅ 彼の話には心を打つものがある。 (The subject of ものがある is the thing containing the quality, not the person experiencing it)"],"compare":null}},"n2-069||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"～ものがある · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものがある","patternReading":"ものがある","coreMeaning":"There is something noteworthy ~ (subjective impression)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember ものがある with the English formula **“There is something about ~.”** When you want to say “I feel there is something special or noteworthy in ~,” think of ものがある. もの (物) = thing; ある = exist. Together: **“there is something.”**","nuance":"This mnemonic helps you avoid using it in overly ordinary situations. If “there is something about it” would not sound natural in English, ものがある is probably not appropriate.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-069||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common collocations","sub":"～ものがある · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ものがある","patternReading":"ものがある","coreMeaning":"There is something noteworthy ~ (subjective impression)","slug":"phrases","definition":"In JLPT questions and real-world contexts, ～ものがある often combines with verbs and adjectives expressing a strong emotional impact or thoughtful evaluation. The phrases below are especially common.","nuance":"Learn these as chunks: 心を動かすものがある = “there is something that touches the heart”; 考えさせられるものがある = “there is something that makes you think”; 惹きつけるものがある = “there is something captivating.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"話","r":"はなし","m":"story"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"people"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"引き込む","r":"ひきこむ","m":"draw in"},{"t":"ものがある","r":"ものがある","m":"there is something"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is something about his story that draws listeners in.","scene":"A compelling storyteller"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-070||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～とは限らない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とは限らない","patternReading":"とはかぎらない","coreMeaning":"Not necessarily ~ / Not always ~","slug":"core","definition":"〜とは限らない expresses the **rejection of an absolute claim**. It means 'not necessarily ~' or 'not always ~,' emphasizing that there are always **exceptions**.","nuance":"Use it to gently challenge a claim without asserting the opposite; it only rejects the idea that something is 'always true.' Common in both speech and writing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"高い","r":"たかい","m":"expensive"},{"t":"食べ物","r":"たべもの","m":"food"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"とは限らない","r":"とはかぎらない","m":"not necessarily"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Expensive food is not necessarily delicious.","scene":"At an expensive restaurant, but the food is not good"},{"tokens":[{"t":"多数派","r":"たすうは","m":"majority"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"意見","r":"いけん","m":"opinion"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"正しい","r":"ただしい","m":"correct"},{"t":"とは限らない","r":"とはかぎらない","m":"not necessarily"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The majority opinion is not necessarily correct.","scene":"In a meeting when someone says everyone thinks that way"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-070||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"～とは限らない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とは限らない","patternReading":"とはかぎらない","coreMeaning":"Not necessarily ~ / Not always ~","slug":"forms","definition":"The pattern 〜とは限らない attaches to the **end of a clause** in the plain form to deny that the clause is inevitable. Main connecting forms are **V（普通形）**, **Aい（普通形）**, **N**, and **Aな**. With な-adjectives and nouns, だ is usually omitted before と (e.g. 静かとは限らない, 医者とは限らない).","nuance":"More formal than the casual expression 'とは限らないよ.' In writing, it often appears with **必ずしも** for emphasis. In speech, it can stand alone: **そうとは限らない**.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"＋ とは限らない","note":"例: できるとは限らない"},{"lhs":"いA（普通形）","rhs":"＋ とは限らない","note":"例: 高いとは限らない"},{"lhs":"なA","rhs":"＋ とは限らない","note":"例: 静かとは限らない (omit だ)"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"＋ とは限らない","note":"例: 医者とは限らない"},{"lhs":"〜からといって〜","rhs":"＋ とは限らない","note":"例: 高いからといっておいしいとは限らない"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"できる","m":"can do it"},{"t":"とは限らない","r":"とはかぎらない","m":"not necessarily"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He may not necessarily be able to do it.","scene":"Discussing a beginner's ability"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"辺り","r":"あたり","m":"area"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"静か","r":"しずか","m":"quiet"},{"t":"とは限らない","r":"とはかぎらない","m":"not necessarily"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This area is not necessarily quiet.","scene":"Advising a friend who is looking for a home"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-070||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～とは限らない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とは限らない","patternReading":"とはかぎらない","coreMeaning":"Not necessarily ~ / Not always ~","slug":"usage","definition":"Use 〜とは限らない when the speaker wants to **correct** an overly absolute assumption or modestly say that something is not always true. It is common in discussions, advice, and academic writing.","nuance":"It has a neutral level of politeness and can be used with a boss or customer for a gentle disagreement: **必ずしもそうとは限りません**. The polite form is とは限りません. The tone is mild and considerate.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"必ずしも","r":"かならずしも","m":"not necessarily"},{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"多い","r":"おおい","m":"a lot"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"幸せ","r":"しあわせ","m":"happy"},{"t":"とは限らない","r":"とはかぎらない","m":"not necessarily"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Having a lot of money does not necessarily make someone happy.","scene":"During a counseling session"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"いつも","m":"always"},{"t":"元気","r":"げんき","m":"well"},{"t":"とは限らない","r":"とはかぎらない","m":"not necessarily"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is not always well.","scene":"Everyone thinks he is healthy, but he often gets sick"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-070||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"～とは限らない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とは限らない","patternReading":"とはかぎらない","coreMeaning":"Not necessarily ~ / Not always ~","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes 〜とは限らない from easily confused patterns. The key point: **〜とは限らない** says 'not always true,' while 〜からといって requires a **specific reason before it** and rejects the conclusion drawn from that reason.","nuance":"The patterns can all feel like 'not necessarily,' but 〜とは限らない can stand at the end of a sentence, whereas 〜からといって requires AからといってBとは限らない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"悪い","r":"わるい","m":"bad / at fault"},{"t":"とは限らない","r":"とはかぎらない","m":"not necessarily"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is not necessarily at fault.","scene":"When everyone is criticizing a coworker"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜からといって","point":"〜からといって always has a reason 'because A' before it, while 〜とは限らない simply rejects the claim 'not necessarily' in the clause itself. Example: 高いからといっておいしいとは限らない (just because it is expensive, it is not necessarily delicious) ↔ 高いとは限らない (it is not necessarily expensive)."},{"vs":"〜わけではない","point":"〜わけではない denies the whole claim 'that is not the case,' while 〜とは限らない allows that it may be true but has exceptions. Example: 彼が悪いわけではない (it is not that he is at fault) ↔ 彼が悪いとは限らない (he is not necessarily at fault)."}]}},"n2-070||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～とは限らない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とは限らない","patternReading":"とはかぎらない","coreMeaning":"Not necessarily ~ / Not always ~","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with 〜とは限らない. The most serious are **using it when the result is already certain** and **misreading a negative clause + とは限らない**.","nuance":"Remember: 〜とは限らない does not mean 'definitely not'; it means 'not certain that it is/does.' Thus 彼が来ないとは限らない means 'he may still come.'","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来ない","r":"こない","m":"not come"},{"t":"とは限らない","r":"とはかぎらない","m":"not necessarily"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is not certain that he will not come (he may still come).","scene":"In a meeting when someone says he probably will not come"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼は絶対に来ない。 (when you mean 'he may not come') → ✅ 彼は来るとは限らない。 (rejects absolute certainty that he will not come)","❌ Misreading '彼が来ないとは限らない' as 'he definitely will not come' → ✅ The correct meaning is 'he may still come.'","❌ 高いからおいしいとは限らない。 (missing といって) → ✅ 高いからといっておいしいとは限らない。","❌ 静かなとは限らない。 (using な before とは) → ✅ 静かとは限らない。 / 静かだとは限らない。 (use the plain form or omit だ)"],"compare":null}},"n2-070||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～とは限らない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とは限らない","patternReading":"とはかぎらない","coreMeaning":"Not necessarily ~ / Not always ~","slug":"memory","definition":"A quick memory aid for 〜とは限らない: connect it with the idea of a **limit** (限る). Saying that something is 'not limited to that' means there is more than one possibility — in other words, **not necessarily**.","nuance":"This is a memory aid, not the exact grammatical meaning. The English equivalent 'not necessarily' is also a useful shortcut.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-070||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～とは限らない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～とは限らない","patternReading":"とはかぎらない","coreMeaning":"Not necessarily ~ / Not always ~","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are common fixed combinations with 〜とは限らない. Learning them helps you use the pattern naturally and handle reading-comprehension questions.","nuance":"必ずしも〜とは限らない is formal, while そうとは限らない is common as a conversational response.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お金持ち","r":"おかねもち","m":"rich person"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"幸せ","r":"しあわせ","m":"happy"},{"t":"とは限らない","r":"とはかぎらない","m":"not necessarily"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Rich people are not necessarily happy.","scene":"Talking with a friend about happiness"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-071||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"～上で · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～上で","patternReading":"うえで","coreMeaning":"After ~ / In terms of ~ (formal)","slug":"core","definition":"〜上で has two main meanings: **V-た + 上で** means 'after completing A, do B'—A **must be completed first**; **N + の上で** means 'in terms of N'—it refers to a **viewpoint or aspect**. This is a formal pattern often used at work and in written texts.","nuance":"Common in **written and formal speech**, not casual conversation with friends. For meaning ①, **V-た is required**; for meaning ②, N is often an abstract field such as health, work, or theory.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"内容","r":"ないよう","m":"content"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"よく","r":"よく","m":"carefully"},{"t":"確認","r":"かくにん","m":"confirm"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"上で","r":"うえで","m":"after"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"サイン","m":"sign"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After checking the content carefully, please sign.","scene":"A boss instructs an employee before signing a contract"},{"tokens":[{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"上で","r":"うえで","m":"in terms of"},{"t":"大切","r":"たいせつ","m":"important"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"こと","r":"こと","m":"thing"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"規則正しい","r":"きそくただしい","m":"regular"},{"t":"生活","r":"せいかつ","m":"lifestyle"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In terms of health, a regular lifestyle is important.","scene":"An article about health"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-071||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form & conjugation","sub":"～上で · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～上で","patternReading":"うえで","coreMeaning":"After ~ / In terms of ~ (formal)","slug":"forms","definition":"Learn the **two main connections**: use **V-た** with verbs, and **N + の** with nouns. If N is an **action** (相談, 検討...), 上で means 'after'; if N is a **field** (健康, 仕事...), it means 'in terms of'.","nuance":"When **N + の上で** comes before another noun, it can become **N + の上の + N**. Add the particle **は** for contrast or emphasis: 仕事の上では...","structure":[{"lhs":"V-た","rhs":"上で、B。","note":"After completing A, do B"},{"lhs":"N(動作) + の","rhs":"上で、B。","note":"After completing N (often a decision after discussion or confirmation)"},{"lhs":"N(scope) + の","rhs":"上では / でも / 上の + N","note":"In terms of / from the perspective of N"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"話し合い","r":"はなしあい","m":"discussion"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"上で","r":"うえで","m":"after"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"決定","r":"けってい","m":"decision"},{"t":"します","m":"will do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After discussing it, we will decide.","scene":"An announcement in a meeting"},{"tokens":[{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"上で","r":"うえで","m":"in terms of"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"problem"},{"t":"ない","m":"none"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is no problem in terms of work.","scene":"Evaluating an employee"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-071||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～上で · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～上で","patternReading":"うえで","coreMeaning":"After ~ / In terms of ~ (formal)","slug":"usage","definition":"Usage: ① **V-た上で** shows a sequence: complete A before doing B, often for procedural requests and reports. ② **N + 上で** means 'in terms of' and is used for opinions, comparisons, or conditions within a specific scope.","nuance":"Meaning ① often emphasizes **careful consideration** before taking action. Meaning ② is usually followed by an **objective conclusion or observation**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"内容","r":"ないよう","m":"content"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"よく","r":"よく","m":"carefully"},{"t":"確認","r":"かくにん","m":"confirm"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"上で","r":"うえで","m":"after"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"サイン","m":"sign"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After checking the content carefully, please sign.","scene":"A boss instructs an employee before signing a contract"},{"tokens":[{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"上で","r":"うえで","m":"in terms of"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"problem"},{"t":"ない","m":"none"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"性格的","r":"せいかくてき","m":"personality-wise"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"合わない","r":"あわない","m":"do not suit"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is no problem in terms of work, but we do not get along personality-wise.","scene":"Commenting on a colleague"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-071||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"～上で · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～上で","patternReading":"うえで","coreMeaning":"After ~ / In terms of ~ (formal)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare ~上で with similar patterns: **～てから / ～あとで** are neutral and informal; **～上は** means 'once you have...' with a sense of responsibility; **～にあたって** means 'on the occasion of, when undertaking' an important matter.","nuance":"～上で (meaning ①) is more formal than **てから** and often involves a serious procedure; **～上は** expresses resolve, not mere sequence; **～にあたって** emphasizes the time before an event begins and does not mean 'after'.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"話し合い","r":"はなしあい","m":"discussion"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"上で","r":"うえで","m":"after"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"決定","r":"けってい","m":"decision"},{"t":"します","m":"will do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After discussing it, we will decide.","scene":"An announcement in a meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～てから / ～あとで","point":"上で is formal and stresses that A MUST be completed first; てから/あとで simply connect events in order. Example: 話し合った上で決めます (we will decide after thorough discussion—formal) vs 話し合ってから決めます (we will decide after talking—casual)."},{"vs":"～上は","point":"上で means 'after', while 上は means 'once you have' + responsibility/resolve. Example: 確認した上でサインする (sign after confirming) vs こうなった上は最後までやる (now that things have come to this, I will see it through)."},{"vs":"～にあたって","point":"にあたって emphasizes BEFORE starting (on the occasion of), while 上で means AFTER completion. Example: 試験にあたって注意する (take care on the occasion of the exam) vs 試験の内容を確認した上で申し込む (apply after checking the exam details)."}]}},"n2-071||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～上で · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～上で","patternReading":"うえで","coreMeaning":"After ~ / In terms of ~ (formal)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make these mistakes: using a **dictionary-form verb** before 上で, or using 上で when they mean 'before'. Remember: 上で (meaning ①) **always takes V-た**, while 'before' requires **前に**.","nuance":"This grammar error makes the sentence **completely incorrect**; on tests, it is a typical trap testing form conjugation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"報告","r":"ほうこく","m":"report"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞いた","r":"きいた","m":"heard"},{"t":"上で","r":"うえで","m":"after"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"判断","r":"はんだん","m":"judgment"},{"t":"します","m":"will do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After hearing the report, I will make a decision.","scene":"A supervisor makes a decision"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食べる上で、手を洗ってください。 → ✅ 食べる前に、手を洗ってください。","❌ 報告を聞く上で、判断します。 → ✅ 報告を聞いた上で、判断します。","❌ 会議を始める上で、資料を配ってください。 → ✅ 会議を始めるにあたって、資料を配布いたします。 (on the occasion of starting the meeting, we will distribute the materials)","❌ 確認する上で、サインする。 → ✅ 確認した上で、サインする。"],"compare":null}},"n2-071||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～上で · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～上で","patternReading":"うえで","coreMeaning":"After ~ / In terms of ~ (formal)","slug":"memory","definition":"The image of 'above' helps you remember: **上で** means standing on the foundation of a completed action (after), or standing on a viewpoint (in terms of). **V-た** is the key to recognizing the 'after' meaning.","nuance":"Tip: remember **V-た + 上で** → た suggests 'already finished'; **N + の上で** → when N is a scope, it means 'in terms of'.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-071||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～上で · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～上で","patternReading":"うえで","coreMeaning":"After ~ / In terms of ~ (formal)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common workplace phrases: **確認した上で** (after confirming), **検討の上で** (after considering), **話し合いの上で** (after discussing), **健康の上では** (in terms of health).","nuance":"These phrases appear often in notices and business emails; recognizing them helps you read JLPT passages faster.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"確認","r":"かくにん","m":"confirm"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"上で","r":"うえで","m":"after"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ご連絡","r":"ごれんらく","m":"contact"},{"t":"します","m":"will do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After confirming, I will contact you again.","scene":"Replying to a customer by email"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-072||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～からといって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～からといって","patternReading":"からといって","coreMeaning":"Just because ~ does not mean... (rejecting a reason)","slug":"core","definition":"～からといって rejects a reason: no matter how true P is, P cannot justify Q. It means “Just because P does not mean Q” or “Even if P, Q does not follow.”","nuance":"It often sounds critical, preventive, or cautionary. It works in speech and writing; in writing, it commonly appears in essays and articles. The latter clause often uses negatives such as わけではない, とは限らない, or てはいけない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"有名","r":"ゆうめい","m":"famous"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"からといって","r":"からといって","m":"just because...doesn't mean"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"その","r":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"意見","r":"いけん","m":"opinion"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"正しい","r":"ただしい","m":"correct"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"言えない","r":"いえない","m":"cannot say"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because someone is famous does not mean their opinion is correct.","scene":"Debating a controversial statement by a celebrity."},{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"からといって","r":"からといって","m":"just because...doesn't mean"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何でも","r":"なんでも","m":"anything"},{"t":"許される","r":"ゆるされる","m":"be forgiven"},{"t":"わけ","r":"わけ","m":"meaning"},{"t":"ではない","r":"ではない","m":"is not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because someone is a child does not mean everything is forgiven.","scene":"Parents teaching a child about discipline."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-072||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"～からといって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～からといって","patternReading":"からといって","coreMeaning":"Just because ~ does not mean... (rejecting a reason)","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern attaches directly to a statement in the plain form. Nouns and な-adjectives require だ before からといって. The latter clause is usually negative or prohibitive.","nuance":"There is no special polite form; with polite speech, simply use the polite form in the latter clause. Do not use ます before からといって. The latter clause may contain はずがない, わけではない, とは限らない, てはいけない, or べきではない.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（辞書形／た形／ない形）","rhs":"＋ からといって","note":"Plain verb form"},{"lhs":"いA","rhs":"＋ からといって","note":"Example: 忙しいからといって"},{"lhs":"なA","rhs":"＋ だ ＋ からといって","note":"Drop な and use だ, e.g. 静かだからといって"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"＋ だ ＋ からといって","note":"Drop だ in a negative clause, e.g. 学生じゃないからといって"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"忙しい","r":"いそがしい","m":"busy"},{"t":"からといって","r":"からといって","m":"even if...must not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"食事","r":"しょくじ","m":"meal"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"抜いて","r":"ぬいて","m":"skip"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"いけない","r":"いけない","m":"must not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No matter how busy you are, you must not skip meals.","scene":"A mother reminding her busy year-end child."},{"tokens":[{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"合格","r":"ごうかく","m":"pass"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"からといって","r":"からといって","m":"just because...doesn't mean"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"studying"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"やめて","r":"やめて","m":"stop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"いけない","r":"いけない","m":"must not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because you passed the exam does not mean you can stop studying.","scene":"Speaking to a friend who plans to stop studying after passing."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-072||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～からといって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～からといって","patternReading":"からといって","coreMeaning":"Just because ~ does not mean... (rejecting a reason)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when rejecting a seemingly reasonable but unjustified cause-and-effect relationship. It often accompanies negative conclusions such as てはいけない, わけではない, or とは限らない.","nuance":"In conversation, it can offer gentle advice, but with a subordinate it may sound critical. Use caution with superiors because it can carry a judgmental tone.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"からといって","r":"からといって","m":"just because...not necessarily"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"幸せ","r":"しあわせ","m":"happy"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"限らない","r":"かぎらない","m":"not necessarily"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because you have money does not mean you are happy.","scene":"Advising a friend chasing money."},{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"からといって","r":"からといって","m":"just because...doesn't mean"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何でも","r":"なんでも","m":"anything"},{"t":"許される","r":"ゆるされる","m":"be forgiven"},{"t":"わけ","r":"わけ","m":"meaning"},{"t":"ではない","r":"ではない","m":"is not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because someone is a child does not mean everything is forgiven.","scene":"Parents teaching a child about discipline."},{"tokens":[{"t":"有名","r":"ゆうめい","m":"famous"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"からといって","r":"からといって","m":"just because...doesn't mean"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"その","r":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"意見","r":"いけん","m":"opinion"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"正しい","r":"ただしい","m":"correct"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"言えない","r":"いえない","m":"cannot say"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because someone is famous does not mean their opinion is correct.","scene":"Debating a controversial statement by a celebrity."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-072||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with から・からには","sub":"～からといって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～からといって","patternReading":"からといって","coreMeaning":"Just because ~ does not mean... (rejecting a reason)","slug":"compare","definition":"Contrast ～からといって with easily confused patterns. Unlike から, its latter clause is not a natural result but rejects the result. Unlike からには, it does not create an obligation; it rejects one.","nuance":"Do not confuse it with から (because): although both contain から, からといって has a contrasting latter clause. からには expresses taking responsibility, while からといって prevents or rejects an assumption.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"からといって","r":"からといって","m":"just because...doesn't mean"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"運動会","r":"うんどうかい","m":"sports day"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"やめる","r":"やめる","m":"cancel"},{"t":"わけ","r":"わけ","m":"meaning"},{"t":"には","r":"には","m":"as for"},{"t":"いかない","r":"いかない","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because it is raining does not mean we have to cancel the sports day.","scene":"Discussing cancellation because of light rain."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜から","point":"から: P naturally causes Q; からといって: P is not a valid reason for Q. Example: 雨だから運動会をやめた (We canceled the sports day because of rain) vs 雨だからといって運動会をやめるわけにはいかない (Just because it rains, we cannot cancel it)."},{"vs":"〜からには","point":"からには: once P, one must do Q (commitment); からといって: even with P, one need not or must not do Q. Example: 約束したからには守る (Having promised, I must keep it) vs 約束したからといって無理に守ることはない (A promise does not mean you must keep it at any cost)."},{"vs":"〜といっても","point":"といっても: the latter clause describes reality differing from expectations about P; からといって: it rejects a logical consequence from P. Example: 日本人といっても日本語が上手とは限らない (Being Japanese does not guarantee Japanese skill) vs 日本人だからといって日本語が上手とは限らない (Just being Japanese does not guarantee Japanese skill)."}]}},"n2-072||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common pitfalls","sub":"～からといって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～からといって","patternReading":"からといって","coreMeaning":"Just because ~ does not mean... (rejecting a reason)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"A common mistake is using ～からといって with an ordinary action in the latter clause. The latter clause must be negative, volitionally negative, or cautionary/prohibitive. Do not use it for a natural result caused by P. Also avoid it with superiors because its critical tone may sound impolite.","nuance":"The most common error is translating “because” directly as からといって. Remember that からといって always rejects an assumption; it is not a simple reason marker.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"から","r":"から","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"傘","r":"かさ","m":"umbrella"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持って","r":"もって","m":"carry"},{"t":"きた","r":"きた","m":"brought"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because it was raining, I brought an umbrella.","scene":"Someone brings an umbrella because of rain."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 雨だからといって、傘を持ってきた。 → ✅ 雨だったから、傘を持ってきた。 (Because it was raining, I brought an umbrella; do not use からといって because the latter clause is not negative)","❌ 高いからといって、買わない。 → ✅ 高いからといって、買わないわけではない。 (Add わけではない to mean “it is not necessarily that I will not buy it”)","❌ 先生だからといって、いつも正しい。 → ✅ 先生だからといって、いつも正しいとは限らない。 (Use とは限らない to reject a generalization)","❌ 忙しいからといって、休む。 → ✅ 忙しいからといって、休んではいけない。 (The latter clause needs advice or prohibition)","❌ できるからといって、慢心する。 → ✅ できるからといって、慢心するべきではない。 (Advise against Q even when P is true)"],"compare":null}},"n2-072||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～からといって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～からといって","patternReading":"からといって","coreMeaning":"Just because ~ does not mean... (rejecting a reason)","slug":"memory","definition":"A quick memory aid is to think of this pattern as “just because P doesn't mean Q.” Whenever you see からといって, expect negation or rejection in the latter clause. Picture the speaker shaking their head and rejecting a seemingly reasonable explanation.","nuance":"The English equivalent is an effective memory aid because it matches the meaning closely. Do not remember it as simple “because”; remember “that excuse still does not make Q possible.”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-072||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common collocations","sub":"～からといって · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～からといって","patternReading":"からといって","coreMeaning":"Just because ~ does not mean... (rejecting a reason)","slug":"phrases","definition":"The most common phrases after ～からといって in JLPT questions and conversation are わけではない (does not mean), とは限らない (not necessarily), てはいけない (must not), and べきではない (should not). Memorizing these combinations improves accuracy.","nuance":"These combinations form fixed meaning units. When you see からといって in a reading passage, the latter clause is very likely to contain one of them.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"からといって","r":"からといって","m":"just because...doesn't mean"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"幸せ","r":"しあわせ","m":"happy"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"限らない","r":"かぎらない","m":"not necessarily"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Just because you have money does not mean you are happy.","scene":"Discussing views on life."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-073||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ようがない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ようがない","patternReading":"ようがない","coreMeaning":"There is no way to ~ (completely impossible)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ようがない expresses that there is **absolutely no way or method** to carry out an action. Unlike できない (lacking ability), this pattern stresses **the absence of any possible means**: even if you want to act, there is no way to do so.","nuance":"It conveys **helplessness and resignation**, sometimes as an **accepted conclusion** after considering the options. It works in both speech and writing and is not overly formal. In conversation, どうしようもない is very natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"完全に","r":"かんぜんに","m":"completely"},{"t":"壊れて","r":"こわれて","m":"broken"},{"t":"しまって","m":"completely"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"直し","r":"なおし","m":"repair"},{"t":"ようがない","r":"ようがない","m":"no way"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is completely broken, so there is no way to fix it.","scene":"A broken computer cannot be salvaged"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"連絡先","r":"れんらくさき","m":"contact details"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"住所","r":"じゅうしょ","m":"home address"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"知らない","r":"しらない","m":"do not know"},{"t":"から","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"連絡","r":"れんらく","m":"contact"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"とり","r":"とり","m":"make"},{"t":"ようがない","r":"ようがない","m":"no way"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I know neither his contact details nor his address, so there is no way to contact him.","scene":"You urgently need him but cannot find him"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-073||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～ようがない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ようがない","patternReading":"ようがない","coreMeaning":"There is no way to ~ (completely impossible)","slug":"forms","definition":"The basic structure is **Vます (remove ます) + ようがない**; with suru-verbs, **Nの + Vます + ようがない** is also possible. Do not use the dictionary form or te-form.","nuance":"It reflects the speaker's **subjective judgment** and usually appears at the end of a sentence. The variant 〜ようもない is stronger, close to 'there is not even a way'.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vます (remove ます)","rhs":"ようがない","note":"例: 直し + ようがない"},{"lhs":"Nの","rhs":"Vます (remove ます) + ようがない","note":"例: 連絡の + とり + ようがない"},{"lhs":"Vます","rhs":"ようもない","note":"Stronger emphasis"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"一人","r":"ひとり","m":"alone"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"直し","r":"なおし","m":"repair"},{"t":"ようがない","r":"ようがない","m":"no way"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is no way to fix it by myself.","scene":"Severely damaged item needs a specialist"},{"tokens":[{"t":"一度","r":"いちど","m":"once"},{"t":"壊れたら","r":"こわれたら","m":"if broken"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"元","r":"もと","m":"original state"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"戻し","r":"もどし","m":"restore"},{"t":"ようがない","r":"ようがない","m":"no way"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once it breaks, there is no way to restore it.","scene":"A corrupted file will not reopen"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-073||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～ようがない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ようがない","patternReading":"ようがない","coreMeaning":"There is no way to ~ (completely impossible)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when the speaker wants to say that there is **no way out or option** for changing the situation. It usually expresses a subjective assessment.","nuance":"It has a **resigned** tone and is stronger than できない. Do not use it for a lack of skill. For example, 'I cannot speak English' does not take ようがない because there is a way to learn; use 話せない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"完全に","r":"かんぜんに","m":"completely"},{"t":"道","r":"みち","m":"path"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"消えて","r":"きえて","m":"disappeared"},{"t":"しまって","m":"completely"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"進み","r":"すすみ","m":"move forward"},{"t":"ようがない","r":"ようがない","m":"no way"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The path has completely disappeared, so there is no way to go on.","scene":"Lost in the woods with no GPS signal"},{"tokens":[{"t":"財布","r":"さいふ","m":"wallet"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"落として","r":"おとして","m":"drop"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"帰り","r":"かえり","m":"return home"},{"t":"ようがない","r":"ようがない","m":"no way"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I dropped my wallet, so there is no way to get home.","scene":"Losing all your money on a trip means no ticket"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-073||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions & related forms","sub":"～ようがない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ようがない","patternReading":"ようがない","coreMeaning":"There is no way to ~ (completely impossible)","slug":"compare","definition":"A quick comparison with patterns learners often confuse, focusing on the **key difference**.","nuance":"ようがない concerns **a method**; the other patterns concern ability, rules, or ethics.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"散らかりすぎて","r":"ちらかりすぎて","m":"too messy"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"片付け","r":"かたづけ","m":"tidy"},{"t":"ようがない","r":"ようがない","m":"no way"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The room is so messy that there is no way to clean it up.","scene":"Seeing a room buried under clutter"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜できない","point":"できない = lacking the ability (you could learn). ようがない = no possible method exists. Example: 日本語が話せない (cannot speak Japanese) ↔ 連絡のとりようがない (no way to contact)."},{"vs":"〜わけにはいかない","point":"わけにはいかない = cannot because of moral, social, or rule-based constraints. ようがない = cannot because no method exists. Example: 約束があるから行かないわけにはいかない ↔ 道がなくて行きようがない."}]}},"n2-073||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes & traps","sub":"～ようがない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ようがない","patternReading":"ようがない","coreMeaning":"There is no way to ~ (completely impossible)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The key trap is that **not every verb can be used**. The verb must describe an action with a specific 'method,' not a natural action.","nuance":"Learners often use ようがない for every meaning of 'cannot,' but Japanese uses it only when the idea of a method exists.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"そんなに","m":"that"},{"t":"複雑","r":"ふくざつ","m":"complicated"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"説明","r":"せつめい","m":"explain"},{"t":"しようがない","r":"しようがない","m":"no way"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is so complicated that there is no way to explain it.","scene":"Explaining a confusing grammar rule"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 眠るようがない。 → ✅ 眠れない。 (眠る is a natural action, so there is no 'method of sleeping')","❌ 直すようがない。 → ✅ 直しようがない。 (use the Vます stem)","❌ 説明するようがない。 → ✅ 説明しようがない / 説明のしようがない。 (with suru-verbs, の or し is commonly used)"],"compare":null}},"n2-073||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～ようがない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ようがない","patternReading":"ようがない","coreMeaning":"There is no way to ~ (completely impossible)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember: **よう = 方 (method)** and **がない = there is none**. Together: 'there is no method' → no way out.","nuance":"When you ask, 'Does a way to do ~ exist?' and the answer is **NO**, use ようがない. Associate it with the common phrase しようがない = 'nothing can be done'.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-073||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～ようがない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ようがない","patternReading":"ようがない","coreMeaning":"There is no way to ~ (completely impossible)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Several fixed phrases that frequently appear on tests and in conversation.","nuance":"どうしようもない is the most familiar phrase, meaning 'nothing can be done' or 'there is no way out.'","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"どう","m":"what"},{"t":"しようもない","r":"しようもない","m":"nothing can be done"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is nothing we can do now.","scene":"Trying everything but unable to change the result"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-074||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～まい · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～まい","patternReading":"まい","coreMeaning":"Probably not ~ / Will not ~ (negative volition or conjecture)","slug":"core","definition":"〜まい is an archaic negative auxiliary with two main meanings: ① negative volition—resolving not to do something; ② negative conjecture—judging that something is very unlikely. It is the negative counterpart of 〜よう (volitional), but is literary and common in journalism.","nuance":"It is not used in everyday conversation; it sounds archaic and formal. When used, it sounds forceful and decisive, often appearing in writing, speeches, or inner monologue. In modern speech, Japanese speakers use **～ないようにしよう** (volition) or **～ないだろう** (conjecture).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"二度と","r":"にどと","m":"never again"},{"t":"あんな","m":"that kind of"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"まい","m":"will not"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"心","r":"こころ","m":"heart"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"誓った","r":"ちかった","m":"swore"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I swore in my heart that I would never make such a mistake again.","scene":"After making a serious mistake, a character makes a vow"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あれほど","m":"to that extent"},{"t":"苦労","r":"くろう","m":"hardship"},{"t":"した","m":"went through"},{"t":"のだから","m":"since that was so"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"忘れる","r":"わすれる","m":"forget"},{"t":"まい","m":"surely not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After going through such hardship, he surely cannot forget it either.","scene":"Thinking about a friend who endured hardship"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-074||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"～まい · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～まい","patternReading":"まい","coreMeaning":"Probably not ~ / Will not ~ (negative volition or conjecture)","slug":"forms","definition":"The pattern ～まい attaches directly to the **dictionary form of a verb**. With する, both **するまい** and **しまい** are possible; with 来る, use **くるまい** (sometimes **こまい**). Adding か in ～まいか creates a rhetorical question: 'Could it be not...?'","nuance":"It is not used with adjectives or other auxiliaries. This is an archaic form found in formal speech or writing. For polite expression, use ～ないでしょう / ～ないつもりだ.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vる（辞書形）","rhs":"＋まい","note":"Examples: 行く → 行くまい、食べる → 食べるまい"},{"lhs":"する","rhs":"するまい / しまい","note":"Both are possible; しまい sounds more archaic"},{"lhs":"来る","rhs":"くるまい / こまい","note":"こまい is rare, mainly literary"},{"lhs":"〜まいか","rhs":"～まいか","note":"Asking oneself 'Could it be not...?'"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"もう","m":"anymore"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"まい","m":"surely not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He probably will not come anymore.","scene":"It is late; stating this to a colleague"},{"tokens":[{"t":"そんな","m":"such"},{"t":"こと","m":"thing"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ある","m":"exist"},{"t":"まい","m":"surely not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Something like that surely did not happen.","scene":"Rejecting an unbelievable rumor"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-074||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"～まい · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～まい","patternReading":"まい","coreMeaning":"Probably not ~ / Will not ~ (negative volition or conjecture)","slug":"usage","definition":"～まい is used most often in **written language** (novels and journalism) and **formal speech**. Meaning ① emphasizes **personal resolve**: 'I definitely will not'; meaning ② is **negative conjecture**: 'I believe it surely will not,' with stronger confidence than ～ないだろう. ～まいか is used for rhetorical doubt.","nuance":"Avoid using it with superiors in everyday conversation. In business, use ～ないでしょう for polite negative conjecture. ～まい sounds **classical, literary, serious, and decisive**. In meaning ②, it expresses an objective judgment more than emotion.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どんな","m":"any"},{"t":"苦難","r":"くなん","m":"hardship"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"決意","r":"けつい","m":"resolve"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"揺るがす","r":"ゆるがす","m":"shake"},{"t":"まい","m":"surely not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No matter how great the hardship, it surely cannot shake her resolve.","scene":"An essay analyzing a strong female character"},{"tokens":[{"t":"今更","r":"いまさら","m":"at this point"},{"t":"言って","r":"いって","m":"say"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"どうにも","m":"in any way"},{"t":"なる","m":"change"},{"t":"まい","m":"surely not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even saying it now would not change anything.","scene":"Friends comfort someone after a missed opportunity"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-074||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"～まい · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～まい","patternReading":"まい","coreMeaning":"Probably not ~ / Will not ~ (negative volition or conjecture)","slug":"compare","definition":"Learners often confuse ～まい with ～に違いない because both express conjecture. The difference: **に違いない** affirms that something **will** happen; **まい** denies that it will happen. Compared with ～ないだろう, まい is more formal and archaic. Compared with ～ないようにしよう, volitional まい is written, while ないようにしよう is used in everyday speech.","nuance":"Compared with ないだろう, まい sounds literary, forceful, and decisive, while ないだろう is neutral and natural. In speech, using まい for conjecture may sound theatrical or old-fashioned.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"まい","m":"surely not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He probably will not come.","scene":"Guessing that he will miss the meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"に違いない","point":"に違いない = affirms 'surely yes'; まい = denies 'surely not.' Example: 彼は来るに違いない (he will surely come) vs 彼は来るまい (he probably will not come)."},{"vs":"ないだろう","point":"ないだろう = neutral negative conjecture in speech; まい = formal, classical, and common in writing."},{"vs":"ないようにしよう","point":"ないようにしよう = common way to express resolve; まい = literary and rarely spoken."}]}},"n2-074||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"～まい · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～まい","patternReading":"まい","coreMeaning":"Probably not ~ / Will not ~ (negative volition or conjecture)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner errors with ～まい include **adding ない before まい** (a double negative), **using まい in everyday conversation**, and **not knowing the two forms of する**.","nuance":"On the JLPT, identify the meaning of まい from context. Do not mechanically translate it as 'not'; first decide whether it expresses volition or conjecture.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"まい","m":"surely not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He probably will not come.","scene":"Answering 'Is he coming?'"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼は来ないまい。 → ✅ 彼は来るまい。 (まい already expresses negation, so ない is unnecessary)","❌ 今度はうまくいかないまい。 → ✅ 今度はうまくいくまい。 (to say 'this time probably will not go well')","❌ あまり食べないまい。 → ✅ あまり食べまい。 (まい already carries negative meaning)","❌ そんなことはないまい。 → ✅ そんなことはあるまい。"],"compare":null}},"n2-074||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～まい · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～まい","patternReading":"まい","coreMeaning":"Probably not ~ / Will not ~ (negative volition or conjecture)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember **まい** as the negative twin of **よう**. よう says 'let's do it!', while まい shakes its head: 'I will not / surely not.' You can also associate its sound with a firm future vow.","nuance":"When reading a sentence with まい, pause and ask: is this the speaker's **resolve** or the speaker's **conjecture**? The context will tell you.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-074||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases and collocations","sub":"～まい · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～まい","patternReading":"まい","coreMeaning":"Probably not ~ / Will not ~ (negative volition or conjecture)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases on the JLPT include **二度と～まい** (never...again), **～まいか** (Could it be not...?), and **～あるまい** (there surely is no...).","nuance":"These phrases appear often in writing, newspapers, and novels. 二度と + まい is especially natural because both carry a strong negative force.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"二度と","r":"にどと","m":"never again"},{"t":"嘘","r":"うそ","m":"lie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"つく","m":"tell"},{"t":"まい","m":"will not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will never lie again.","scene":"A vow after lying to a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-075||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～に相違ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に相違ない","patternReading":"にそういない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (strong, formal, evidence-based certainty)","slug":"core","definition":"〜に相違ない expresses the speaker’s **near-absolute certainty** (~95–99%) about a judgment, based on specific signs or evidence. Unlike a verified truth, this is **strong subjective belief**. The pattern is formal and often appears in reports, narratives, legal documents, and academic writing.","nuance":"Compared with に違いない, に相違ない is much **stiffer and more formal**. In daily conversation, it sounds like reading a verdict or government document. Because it conveys high certainty, it feels **decisive and uncompromising**. In essays or reports, it creates a reliable tone; in casual speech, switch to に違いない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"筆跡","r":"ひっせき","m":"handwriting"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"もの","m":"thing"},{"t":"に相違ない","r":"にそういない","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This handwriting must be his; there can be no doubt.","scene":"An investigator examines handwriting in a file"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"犯人","r":"はんにん","m":"culprit"},{"t":"に相違ない","r":"にそういない","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She must be the culprit.","scene":"A decisive judgment in an investigation report"},{"tokens":[{"t":"山田さん","r":"やまださん","m":"Mr. Yamada"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"成功する","r":"せいこうする","m":"succeed"},{"t":"に相違ない","r":"にそういない","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Mr. Yamada is certain to succeed.","scene":"A formal assessment in a recommendation letter"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-075||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～に相違ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に相違ない","patternReading":"にそういない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (strong, formal, evidence-based certainty)","slug":"forms","definition":"Attach the pattern directly after a **noun**, **な-adjective stem**, or the **plain form (普通形)** of a verb or い-adjective. Do not add だ before に相違ない; に相違ない itself **does not change** for tense or politeness—the time reference is carried by the preceding clause.","nuance":"As a fixed formal phrase, it is not used with ます/です. For a past inference, use **Vた + に相違ない**. For a negative inference, use **Vない + に相違ない** (must not be...). In written Japanese, it appears in short, decisive judgments.","structure":[{"lhs":"N / ナA stem","rhs":"に相違ない","note":"彼が犯人に相違ない。"},{"lhs":"V plain form / イA plain form","rhs":"に相違ない","note":"彼は成功するに相違ない。"},{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"に相違ない","note":"彼はもう帰ったに相違ない。"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"古代","r":"こだい","m":"ancient"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"遺跡","r":"いせき","m":"ruins"},{"t":"に相違ない","r":"にそういない","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This must be an ancient ruin.","scene":"An archaeologist concludes after examining the site"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"帰った","r":"かえった","m":"returned"},{"t":"に相違ない","r":"にそういない","m":"must have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He must have gone home already.","scene":"A staff member makes an end-of-day assessment"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-075||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～に相違ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に相違ない","patternReading":"にそういない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (strong, formal, evidence-based certainty)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜に相違ない is used when the speaker makes a **decisive conclusion** in a formal context. It often appears in **investigative reports, inspection findings, news reports, essays**, or expert judgments. Because it means “there can be no discrepancy,” it often occurs with nouns such as 事実, 真実, 証言, and 遺跡.","nuance":"Unlike かもしれない (about a 50% possibility), に相違ない is almost **absolute certainty** while still being a **subjective judgment** that has not been verified. In ordinary conversation, it creates a cold, formal distance and may sound stiff or humorous with friends.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"証言","r":"しょうげん","m":"testimony"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"真実","r":"しんじつ","m":"truth"},{"t":"に相違ない","r":"にそういない","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That testimony must be true.","scene":"A judge records it in the trial minutes"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"計画","r":"けいかく","m":"plan"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"失敗する","r":"しっぱいする","m":"fail"},{"t":"に相違ない","r":"にそういない","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This plan is certain to fail.","scene":"A risk expert concludes in a meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-075||compare":{"title":"⚖️ に違いない / はずだ compared","sub":"～に相違ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に相違ない","patternReading":"にそういない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (strong, formal, evidence-based certainty)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch helps prevent confusion between に相違ない and nearby inference patterns. The key points are **certainty** and **formality**. In N1 exam passages with a formal or academic tone, prefer に相違ない; in ordinary speech, use に違いない.","nuance":"に相違ない and に違いない are nearly equivalent and mainly differ in register. はずだ emphasizes an inference from objective grounds; に相違ない emphasizes the speaker’s nearly certain subjective belief.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"犯人","r":"はんにん","m":"culprit"},{"t":"に相違ない","r":"にそういない","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He must be the culprit.","scene":"A written investigation conclusion"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜に違いない","point":"Both mean “must be,” but に相違ない is formal and written; に違いない is natural in conversation. Example: 彼が犯人に相違ない。 ↔ 彼が犯人に違いない。"},{"vs":"〜はずだ","point":"はずだ = a logical inference from information and may be wrong; に相違ない = near-absolute certainty based on evidence. Example: 彼は来るはずだ。 (reasoned expectation) / 彼は来るに相違ない。 (I am certain)."},{"vs":"〜かもしれない","point":"かもしれない = about a 50% possibility; に相違ない = about 95–99% certainty. Example: 明日は雨かもしれない。 / 明日は雨に相違ない。"}]}},"n2-075||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～に相違ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に相違ない","patternReading":"にそういない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (strong, formal, evidence-based certainty)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse に相違ない with に違いない and use it freely in conversation. In reality, **に相違ない is formal written language** and sounds like a courtroom verdict when spoken. Since it is a fixed phrase, do not insert だ or ます/です before or after it.","nuance":"Register matters more than meaning. In an exam, に相違ない fits letters, reports, and essays; in casual dialogue, choose に違いない. The structure is always [N/V/A plain form] + に相違ない, with no だ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"報告","r":"ほうこく","m":"report"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"真実","r":"しんじつ","m":"truth"},{"t":"に相違ない","r":"にそういない","m":"must be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That report must be true.","scene":"A department head reads the report and concludes"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼は学生だに相違ない。 → ✅ 彼は学生に相違ない。","❌ 彼は犯人に相違ないです。 → ✅ 彼は犯人に相違ない。","❌ 明日は雨に違いない相違ない。 → ✅ 明日は雨に相違ない。"],"compare":null}},"n2-075||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～に相違ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～に相違ない","patternReading":"にそういない","coreMeaning":"Must be ~ (strong, formal, evidence-based certainty)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember it visually: 相違 (そうい) = “difference,” ない = “none” → “no difference” → “completely certain.” Picture an investigator stamping “CONCLUSION: CERTAIN” on a case file.","nuance":"When you see 相違, do not panic; it is a Sino-Japanese word meaning “difference.” Link it to 違いない in speech: imagine identical twins, one in a suit (formal) and one in casual clothes (spoken).","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-076||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～はおろか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～はおろか","patternReading":"はおろか","coreMeaning":"Not to mention ~; even ~ is not possible (emphasizing lack)","slug":"core","definition":"〜はおろか emphasizes an extreme comparison: if a larger, more difficult, or higher-level thing (A) is unavailable or cannot be done, then an easier, smaller, or minimal thing (B) is even less possible. It describes a serious lack or failure to meet even the minimum.","nuance":"This pattern is formal and somewhat literary, often used in essays, reports, complaints, or evaluations. Unlike 〜はもとより, which can be followed by a positive result, 〜はおろか almost always goes with a negative or unfavorable result. It conveys surprise or criticism: “Even the smallest thing is unavailable or undone.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"海外","r":"かいがい","m":"overseas"},{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"travel"},{"t":"はおろか","r":"はおろか","m":"let alone"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"国内","r":"こくない","m":"domestic"},{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"travel"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"できない","r":"できない","m":"cannot do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m so busy that I can’t even travel domestically, let alone overseas.","scene":"A friend says work is too busy to go anywhere."},{"tokens":[{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"はおろか","r":"はおろか","m":"let alone"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ひらがな","r":"ひらがな","m":"hiragana"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"書けない","r":"かけない","m":"cannot write"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can’t write hiragana, let alone kanji.","scene":"Discussing a student’s Japanese ability."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-076||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"～はおろか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～はおろか","patternReading":"はおろか","coreMeaning":"Not to mention ~; even ~ is not possible (emphasizing lack)","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **N1 + はおろか + N2 + も/さえ/すら + ない**. N1 is larger, more difficult, or higher-level; N2 is smaller, easier, or more basic. The following clause must carry a negative sense.","nuance":"Do not put a verb or adjective directly before はおろか; it follows a **particle + noun**. To connect a verb, nominalize it with Vるの or use a noun.","structure":[{"lhs":"N1 + はおろか","rhs":"N2 + も + ない","note":"N1 is the larger or harder matter; N2 is smaller or easier."},{"lhs":"N1 + はおろか","rhs":"N2 + さえ/すら + ない","note":"さえ/すら adds stronger emphasis."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"食事","r":"しょくじ","m":"eating"},{"t":"はおろか","r":"はおろか","m":"let alone"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"水","r":"みず","m":"water"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲む","r":"のむ","m":"drink"},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"do not have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don’t even have time to drink water, let alone eat.","scene":"An employee complains about an overloaded schedule."},{"tokens":[{"t":"謝罪","r":"しゃざい","m":"apology"},{"t":"はおろか","r":"はおろか","m":"let alone"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"連絡","r":"れんらく","m":"contact"},{"t":"すら","r":"すら","m":"even"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"none"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There has been no contact, let alone an apology.","scene":"A partner disappears after making a mistake."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-076||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～はおろか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～はおろか","patternReading":"はおろか","coreMeaning":"Not to mention ~; even ~ is not possible (emphasizing lack)","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern emphasizes degree: “Not to mention the large or expected thing (A), even the small or basic thing (B) is unavailable or unattainable.”","nuance":"常に The following clause is usually **negative**: ない、できない、ない.... Register: written, formal notices, and serious complaints. It may also appear in job interviews when discussing extreme busyness or hardship.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"英語","r":"えいご","m":"English"},{"t":"はおろか","r":"はおろか","m":"let alone"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"ろくに","r":"ろくに","m":"properly"},{"t":"話せない","r":"はなせない","m":"cannot speak"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can’t speak Japanese properly, let alone English.","scene":"Assessing someone’s language ability."},{"tokens":[{"t":"貯金","r":"ちょきん","m":"savings"},{"t":"はおろか","r":"はおろか","m":"let alone"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"生活費","r":"せいかつひ","m":"living expenses"},{"t":"すら","r":"すら","m":"even"},{"t":"稼げない","r":"かせげない","m":"cannot earn"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can’t even earn enough for living expenses, let alone save money.","scene":"Talking about financial hardship."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-076||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare","sub":"～はおろか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～はおろか","patternReading":"はおろか","coreMeaning":"Not to mention ~; even ~ is not possible (emphasizing lack)","slug":"compare","definition":"〜はおろか emphasizes lack: A, the expected or larger thing, is unavailable, and B, the minimal or smaller thing, is also unavailable. Unlike 〜はもとより, which can be followed by a positive clause, 〜はおろか requires a negative or unfavorable result. 〜どころか emphasizes an opposite result, not merely an increasing degree. 〜さえ/すら only means “even B,” while 〜はおろか compares two levels, A and B.","nuance":"Choose はおろか when emphasizing a downward scale: A is already impossible, so B is even less possible, with a negative or unfavorable result. Choose はもとより when the following clause can be affirmative or positive; choose どころか when it is sharply opposite rather than simply “even impossible.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"マンション","r":"マンション","m":"condominium"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"購入","r":"こうにゅう","m":"purchase"},{"t":"はおろか","r":"はおろか","m":"let alone"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"家賃","r":"やちん","m":"rent"},{"t":"すら","r":"すら","m":"even"},{"t":"払えない","r":"はらえない","m":"cannot pay"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can’t even pay the rent, let alone buy a condominium.","scene":"Talking about limited finances."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜はもとより","point":"はもとより = “not only A, but also B”; the following clause need not be negative. はおろか = “A is already impossible, and B is too”; the following clause is almost always negative or unfavorable. Example: 彼女は英語はもとより、中国語も話せる／彼女は英語はおろか、日本語も話せない。"},{"vs":"〜どころか","point":"どころか highlights an **opposite fact**: “not only not A, but instead something entirely different.” はおろか only raises the degree from A (easy/expected) to B (harder/rarer), with neither possible. Example: 褒めるどころか、叱られた／買い物はおろか、食事すら忘れていた。"},{"vs":"〜さえ / 〜すら","point":"さえ/すら simply means “even B is not possible,” without an N1 that serves as a comparison base. はおろか requires **two levels**, A and B: A is impossible, so B is even more so."}]}},"n2-076||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～はおろか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～はおろか","patternReading":"はおろか","coreMeaning":"Not to mention ~; even ~ is not possible (emphasizing lack)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"A common mistake for learners is treating 〜はおろか as a neutral translation of “not to mention,” then combining it with a positive following clause.","nuance":"Most importantly, **do not use a positive following clause**. はおろか means that even the minimum is unavailable, so a positive result makes the sentence semantically wrong.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"朝","r":"あさ","m":"morning"},{"t":"ご飯","r":"ごはん","m":"meal"},{"t":"はおろか","r":"はおろか","m":"let alone"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"水","r":"みず","m":"water"},{"t":"すら","r":"すら","m":"even"},{"t":"飲んで","r":"のんで","m":"drink"},{"t":"いない","r":"いない","m":"have not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I haven’t even had water, let alone breakfast.","scene":"A doctor asks a patient about eating and drinking."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 英語はおろか、中国語も話せる。 → ✅ 英語はもとより、中国語も話せる。","❌ 漢字はおろか、ひらがなも書ける。 → ✅ 漢字はもとより、ひらがなも書ける。","❌ 時間はおろか、お金がない。 → ✅ 時間はおろか、お金もない。"],"compare":null}},"n2-076||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"～はおろか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～はおろか","patternReading":"はおろか","coreMeaning":"Not to mention ~; even ~ is not possible (emphasizing lack)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: おろか (疎か) means “to take lightly” or “to neglect.” Imagine that if someone cannot even attend to a small matter like drinking water or making contact, the larger matter is out of the question. Remember the English equivalents “let alone” and “not to mention”: the first item is expected, while the second is minimal.","nuance":"Remember おろか (疎か) = “neglect” or “take lightly.” The feeling is: “If this matter is already being neglected, forget about that one.” Associating it with “let alone” helps recall はおろか.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-076||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～はおろか · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～はおろか","patternReading":"はおろか","coreMeaning":"Not to mention ~; even ~ is not possible (emphasizing lack)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases and collocations used with 〜はおろか in JLPT questions and everyday contexts.","nuance":"A very common set is: 〜はおろか ... もない / さえない / すらない. Other frequent following clauses include ろくに〜ない, 連絡すらない, and 時間もない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"はおろか","r":"はおろか","m":"let alone"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"do not have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don’t even have money, let alone time.","scene":"Talking about the conditions for taking a trip."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-077||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"～向き · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～向き","patternReading":"むき / むけ","coreMeaning":"Naturally suitable (向き) / Designed for (向け)","slug":"core","definition":"The patterns **～向き** and **～向け** both follow nouns, but differ in viewpoint: **向き** emphasizes that a person or thing is **naturally suitable** for someone or something; **向け** emphasizes that a product or content was **intentionally made** for a target audience. They are not opposite rules, but two perspectives: inherent suitability (向き) versus production purpose (向け).","nuance":"Both are common in speech and writing. **向き** also means a geographic direction, as in 南向き (south-facing), and cannot be replaced with 向け. **向け** often appears in advertising and product descriptions, such as ～向けの商品 and ～向けに開発する. When unsure, ask: “Did someone intentionally create it for this audience?” If so, choose 向け.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"几帳面","r":"きちょうめん","m":"meticulous"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"向き","r":"むき","m":"suitable"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This work is naturally well suited to meticulous people.","scene":"A boss assigns work and comments on its nature"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"教材","r":"きょうざい","m":"textbook"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"初心者","r":"しょしんしゃ","m":"beginners"},{"t":"向けに","r":"むけに","m":"for"},{"t":"作られて","r":"つくられて","m":"made"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This textbook was designed for beginners.","scene":"Introducing a foreign-language textbook"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-077||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structures & connections","sub":"～向き · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～向き","patternReading":"むき / むけ","coreMeaning":"Naturally suitable (向き) / Designed for (向け)","slug":"forms","definition":"These forms attach directly after a **noun** without の: N + 向き/向け. Common endings are **N向きだ**, **N向きの+N**, **N向けだ**, **N向けの+N**, and **N向けに+V**. With 向け, N向けに + verb (作る, 開発する, 設計する) is very common because it expresses an action performed for a target audience.","nuance":"In speech, the short form N向き/N向け + だ is natural enough. **N向きの+N** usually describes suitability, while **N向けの+N** usually describes a mass-produced product. Direction nouns such as 南 and 北 take 向き, not 向け.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"向きだ / 向きの + N","note":"naturally suitable"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"向けだ / 向けの + N / 向けに + V","note":"intentionally designed for"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"南向き","r":"みなみむき","m":"south-facing"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日当たり","r":"ひあたり","m":"sunlight"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This room faces south, so it gets plenty of sunlight.","scene":"An agent introduces an apartment"},{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"向け","r":"むけ","m":"for"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"アニメ","m":"anime"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"大人","r":"おとな","m":"adults"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"見て","r":"みて","m":"watching"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Adults are watching anime made for children.","scene":"Discussing a children’s anime"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-077||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～向き · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～向き","patternReading":"むき / むけ","coreMeaning":"Naturally suitable (向き) / Designed for (向け)","slug":"usage","definition":"There are three clear situations: **(1)** 向き comments on natural suitability, such as for a job, personality, or ability. **(2)** 向き indicates geographic direction, such as 南向き and 東向き. **(3)** 向け describes products, services, and content designed for a target audience, such as books, apps, films, and medicine. For production actions, use N向けに.","nuance":"The nuance is this: 向き is used when a person or thing has inherent traits that make it suitable, while 向け is used when something is intentionally designed or produced for a particular group, such as a book, app, or product.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"スポーツ","m":"sports"},{"t":"向き","r":"むき","m":"suitable"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"体","r":"からだ","m":"body"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"して","m":"has"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He has a naturally athletic build.","scene":"Commenting on a classmate’s physique"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"向けに","r":"むけに","m":"for"},{"t":"開発","r":"かいはつ","m":"develop"},{"t":"された","r":"された","m":"was"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This medicine was developed specifically for children.","scene":"Listening to advice at a pharmacy"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-077||compare":{"title":"⚖️ 向け vs 向き & に限り","sub":"～向き · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～向き","patternReading":"むき / むけ","coreMeaning":"Naturally suitable (向き) / Designed for (向け)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch clears up two common confusions: **向き vs 向け** and **向け vs に限り**. The short rule is: 向き = **naturally suitable**, 向け = **made for**, and に限り = **only permitted for**.","nuance":"Unlike に限り, 向け does not impose a rule or restriction. A product described as 初心者向け can be used by anyone; it is simply designed to suit beginners. 会員に限り, however, means that nonmembers are not allowed to participate.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"向け","r":"むけ","m":"for"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book is for children.","scene":"Introducing a picture book in a bookstore"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～向け","point":"向き = NATURALLY suitable; 向け = INTENTIONALLY designed. With the same noun: 子供向き (children naturally suit it) vs 子供向け (a product for children)."},{"vs":"～に限り","point":"向け = made FOR a target group; に限り = restricted ONLY to that group. 初心者向け ≠ 初心者に限り."}]}},"n2-077||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"～向き · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～向き","patternReading":"むき / むけ","coreMeaning":"Naturally suitable (向き) / Designed for (向け)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"This section summarizes errors learners often make when translating “for” into Japanese. The most common is using 向き for things intentionally manufactured by people.","nuance":"The 向き/向け mistake is not usually fatal grammar, but it sounds unnatural. Japanese listeners understand it yet may find it noticeably awkward. Remember: manufactured items (medicine, apps, books, films) → 向け; inherent traits (personality, physique, house direction) → 向き.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"アプリ","m":"app"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"初心者","r":"しょしんしゃ","m":"beginners"},{"t":"向け","r":"むけ","m":"for"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This app is for beginners.","scene":"Answering a question about a language-learning app"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 初心者の向けの商品だ。 → ✅ 初心者向けの商品だ。 (No の between the noun and 向き/向け)","❌ 几帳面な人の向きの仕事だ。 → ✅ 几帳面な人向きの仕事だ。 (No の between the noun and 向き/向け)","❌ 南向けの部屋は日当たりがいい。 → ✅ 南向きの部屋は日当たりがいい。 (Directions use only 向き)"],"compare":null}},"n2-077||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～向き · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～向き","patternReading":"むき / むけ","coreMeaning":"Naturally suitable (向き) / Designed for (向け)","slug":"memory","definition":"Visual mnemonic: the character **向** resembles an arrow pointing in a direction. **向き** is an arrow “naturally pointing toward what suits it.” **向け** is an arrow “pulled by a human hand” toward a target. When you see “manufactured,” “designed,” or “developed,” choose 向け.","nuance":"The mantra: *A human hand made it → 向け; no human hand, naturally suitable → 向き.*","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-077||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～向き · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～向き","patternReading":"むき / むけ","coreMeaning":"Naturally suitable (向き) / Designed for (向け)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear frequently in JLPT questions and shopping contexts: **初心者向け**, **子供向け**, **女性向け**, **南向き**, and **几帳面な人向き**. Learning the whole phrase helps you quickly choose the correct 向き or 向け.","nuance":"In these phrases, 向け + の + N is most common: 子供向けの映画 and 女性向けの雑誌. 向き + の is less common and usually occurs with nouns describing a person or thing’s inherent traits.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"女性","r":"じょせい","m":"women"},{"t":"向け","r":"むけ","m":"for"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This shop is for women.","scene":"Introducing a clothing shop"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-078||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～をおいて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をおいて","patternReading":"をおいて","coreMeaning":"No one/nothing but ~; asserting uniqueness","slug":"core","definition":"〜をおいて（ほかに） expresses that the subject or object mentioned is **unique**, with no one or nothing else able to replace it. Its literal origin is 置く = “to place, leave”: once N is set aside, no other option remains—so N is the number-one choice. The following clause is almost **always negative** (ない / いない / 考えられない).","nuance":"This is a formal pattern common in writing, speeches, and commentary. It carries a strong **absolute praise** nuance, firmly stating that only N is worthy. Compared with ordinary ways to say “only,” ～をおいて is much more decisive and emphatic.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"をおいて","m":"besides ~"},{"t":"ほかに","m":"else"},{"t":"適任者","r":"てきにんしゃ","m":"qualified person"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"いない","m":"there is none"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Besides him, there is no other suitable person.","scene":"A director needs a project leader"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"film"},{"t":"をおいて","m":"besides ~"},{"t":"名作","r":"めいさく","m":"masterpiece"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ない","m":"there is none"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Other than this film, no work deserves to be called a masterpiece.","scene":"A film review"},{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"将来","r":"しょうらい","m":"future"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"考える","r":"かんがえる","m":"think about"},{"t":"なら","m":"if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"教育","r":"きょういく","m":"education"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"投資","r":"とうし","m":"investment"},{"t":"をおいて","m":"besides ~"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"ない","m":"there is nothing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If we think about our children's future, nothing is more important than investing in education.","scene":"Parents discuss spending on their children"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-078||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structures & forms","sub":"～をおいて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をおいて","patternReading":"をおいて","coreMeaning":"No one/nothing but ~; asserting uniqueness","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **N + をおいて（ほかに） + 否定句**. N is usually a person, object, or abstract noun. There is no special conjugation; simply keep をおいて after the noun. ほか / ほかに / ほかには may be added to emphasize that there is “no other option.”","nuance":"In formal writing, the following clause often uses ない / いない / 考えられない. For greater politeness, use ありません / いません. ほか may be omitted when the meaning is clear, but adding it makes the uniqueness more explicit.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"をおいてほかに + ない / いない","note":"No one/nothing besides N"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"をおいて + V-られない（考えられない）","note":"Cannot imagine an option besides N"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"をおいてほかには + 否定","note":"Adding は increases contrast and emphasis"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"責任者","r":"せきにんしゃ","m":"person in charge"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"をおいて","m":"besides ~"},{"t":"ほかに","m":"else"},{"t":"いません","m":"there is none"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As for the person in charge, there is no one but him.","scene":"A polite staff introduction"},{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"代表する","r":"だいひょうする","m":"represent"},{"t":"作家","r":"さっか","m":"writer"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"をおいて","m":"besides ~"},{"t":"考えられない","r":"かんがえられない","m":"cannot imagine"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No other writer but her can be imagined to represent Japan today.","scene":"A literary magazine article"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-078||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"～をおいて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をおいて","patternReading":"をおいて","coreMeaning":"No one/nothing but ~; asserting uniqueness","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to **evaluate, select, or assert priority** in three main cases: (1) the best person for a task or position, (2) the most deserving object or work, and (3) the most important action to take. The preceding noun is the sole choice; the following negative clause excludes all others.","nuance":"This pattern is common in writing, speeches, advertising, and commentary. It can sound slightly **exaggerated**, so avoid it in everyday conversation when merely stating a personal preference.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"job"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"をおいて","m":"besides ~"},{"t":"ほかに","m":"else"},{"t":"頼める","r":"たのめる","m":"can ask"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いない","m":"there is none"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For this job, there is no one else we can ask besides him.","scene":"Managers assign an important task"},{"tokens":[{"t":"次","r":"つぎ","m":"next"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"開催地","r":"かいさいち","m":"host city"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"都市","r":"とし","m":"city"},{"t":"をおいて","m":"besides ~"},{"t":"ほかに","m":"else"},{"t":"ない","m":"there is none"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For the next host city, there is no suitable place besides this city.","scene":"A committee discusses the host city"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-078||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with ～に限って, だけ, ならでは","sub":"～をおいて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をおいて","patternReading":"をおいて","coreMeaning":"No one/nothing but ~; asserting uniqueness","slug":"compare","definition":"Contrast ～をおいて with three easily confused patterns: **～に限って**, **～だけ/のみ**, and **～ならでは**. The key point is that ～をおいて always **asserts N is unique and is followed by a negative clause**, often with a praising nuance.","nuance":"～に限って limits the meaning to “N alone” without judging N as the best. ～だけ/のみ simply means “only N.” ～ならでは means “something only N can offer,” highlighting N’s distinctive quality without excluding all other options or requiring a negative clause.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"任務","r":"にんむ","m":"mission"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"をおいて","m":"besides ~"},{"t":"ほかに","m":"else"},{"t":"考えられない","r":"かんがえられない","m":"cannot imagine"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For this mission, I cannot imagine anyone else but him.","scene":"Assigning an important company task"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～に限って","point":"に限って = “N alone” (limits scope without comparison). をおいて = “there is no one/nothing besides N” (asserts uniqueness). Example: 彼に限って失敗しない → “He alone never fails”; 彼をおいてほかにいない → “There is no one but him.”"},{"vs":"～だけ / ～のみ","point":"だけ/のみ = “only N” (lists a limited scope; the following clause may be affirmative). をおいて = “there is no better option besides N,” and the following clause must be negative."},{"vs":"～ならでは","point":"ならでは = “something only N can offer; distinctive to N” (often praising, with no required negative). をおいて = “N is unique and irreplaceable,” and the following clause is always negative. Example: 日本ならではの文化 → “culture unique to Japan”; 日本をおいてほかに考えられない → “No place but Japan can be imagined.”"}]}},"n2-078||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～をおいて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をおいて","patternReading":"をおいて","coreMeaning":"No one/nothing but ~; asserting uniqueness","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The most common mistake is using an **affirmative** following clause. Since ～をおいて means that nothing remains when N is excluded, the following clause must be negative. An affirmative ending creates a logical contradiction.","nuance":"Also remember that ～をおいて follows a **noun** and does not attach directly to a verb. For example, 外食をおいて is correct, while 食べるをおいて is incorrect.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"をおいて","m":"besides ~"},{"t":"ほかに","m":"else"},{"t":"大切","r":"たいせつ","m":"important"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"もの","m":"thing"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ない","m":"there is none"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Nothing is more important than your health.","scene":"Encouraging a loved one to value health"}],"pitfalls":["❌ このカフェをおいてほかにいい店がある。 → ✅ このカフェをおいてほかにいい店はない。","❌ 彼をおいて、他の人も適任だ。 → ✅ 彼をおいてほかに適任者はいない。","❌ 映画を見るをおいて一番好きなことはない。 → ✅ 映画鑑賞をおいて一番好きなことはない。"],"compare":null}},"n2-078||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～をおいて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をおいて","patternReading":"をおいて","coreMeaning":"No one/nothing but ~; asserting uniqueness","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture this: you place (置いて) N aside, but when you look around, **no one and nothing is left**. Therefore, N is unique. Memory formula: **N + をおいて（ほかに） + negative**.","nuance":"おいて is the te-form of 置く. The stronger your association with “placing something aside,” the easier it is to remember why the following clause is always negative.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-078||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～をおいて · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～をおいて","patternReading":"をおいて","coreMeaning":"No one/nothing but ~; asserting uniqueness","slug":"phrases","definition":"In exam questions and formal texts, ～をおいて often appears with nouns such as **適任者 / 代表者 / 名作 / 投資 / 方法**. Learn these as fixed phrases for faster recall.","nuance":"These phrases commonly appear as '～をおいてほかに...ない' or '～をおいて考えられない', with a formal tone and strong praise.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"をおいて","m":"besides ~"},{"t":"適任者","r":"てきにんしゃ","m":"qualified person"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"いない","m":"there is none"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is no suitable person besides him.","scene":"A comment in the workplace"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-079||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"～きりだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～きりだ","patternReading":"きりだ","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Ever since ~, nothing more has happened","slug":"core","definition":"～きり(だ) evokes the image of **cutting off**. Meaning 1: **N + きり** = “only N, with nothing else.” Meaning 2: **V-た + きり** = “ever since doing that, ... has not happened again” – the state stops at that point.","nuance":"Common in casual speech. Meaning 2 almost always takes a negative following clause: ない・こない・していない, often conveying waiting, worry, or regret.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"二人","r":"ふたり","m":"two people"},{"t":"きり","r":"きり","m":"only"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"話したい","r":"はなしたい","m":"want to talk"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to talk with just the two of us.","scene":"Wanting a private talk with a close friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"去年","r":"きょねん","m":"last year"},{"t":"別れた","r":"わかれた","m":"broke up"},{"t":"きり","r":"きり","m":"ever since"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会って","r":"あって","m":"meet"},{"t":"いない","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have not seen him since we broke up last year.","scene":"Answering whether you still contact an ex"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-079||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structures & conjugation","sub":"～きりだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～きりだ","patternReading":"きりだ","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Ever since ~, nothing more has happened","slug":"forms","definition":"There are **three main patterns**: (1) **N + きり(だ)** – noun limitation; (2) **V-た + きり + [negative clause]** – “ever since”; (3) **V-た + きりだ** – sentence-final, stressing “that was the end.”","nuance":"When connecting to a following action verb, きり often takes で: 二人きりで話す. To connect two clauses, use きり、 or きりで、.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"きり(だ)","note":"Only N, with nothing else. Examples: 二人きり、一人きり"},{"lhs":"V-た","rhs":"きり + [negative]","note":"Ever since V was completed, the following action has not happened"},{"lhs":"V-た","rhs":"きりだ","note":"Sentence-final: did it and stopped there"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"財布","r":"さいふ","m":"wallet"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"千円","r":"せんえん","m":"1,000 yen"},{"t":"きり","r":"きり","m":"only"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is only 1,000 yen left in my wallet.","scene":"Checking a wallet with only one 1,000-yen bill left"},{"tokens":[{"t":"一度","r":"いちど","m":"once"},{"t":"行った","r":"いった","m":"went"},{"t":"きり","r":"きり","m":"and no more"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"もう"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"気","r":"き","m":"desire"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"しない","m":"do not have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I went once and have no desire to go again.","scene":"Describing a crowded vacation spot you disliked"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-079||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～きりだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～きりだ","patternReading":"きりだ","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Ever since ~, nothing more has happened","slug":"usage","definition":"In conversation, ～きり limits an item or amount: “only, just.” Or, with V-た, it marks a point in time and shows that the event did not continue afterward.","nuance":"In many contexts it sounds stronger than だけ, emphasizing scarcity or a cutoff. Meaning 2 usually takes a negative clause, creating a feeling of waiting, worry, or regret. Example: 出かけたきり帰らない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今朝","r":"けさ","m":"this morning"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"息子","r":"むすこ","m":"son"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"家","r":"いえ","m":"home"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"出た","r":"でた","m":"left"},{"t":"きり","r":"きり","m":"ever since"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"まだ"},{"t":"帰らない","r":"かえらない","m":"has not returned"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My son left home this morning and still has not returned.","scene":"A mother worries as her son stays out all day"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの日","r":"あのひ","m":"that day"},{"t":"出かけた","r":"でかけた","m":"left"},{"t":"きり","r":"きり","m":"ever since"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"帰って","r":"かえって","m":"return"},{"t":"こなかった","r":"こなかった","m":"did not come"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She left that day and never came back.","scene":"Telling how a friend suddenly disappeared"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-079||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with だけ / たまま","sub":"～きりだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～きりだ","patternReading":"きりだ","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Ever since ~, nothing more has happened","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare ～きり with two easily confused patterns. **～だけ** limits quantity neutrally, without a sense of “cutting off.” **～たまま** stresses maintaining a state, while **～たきり** stresses a time point and no continuation.","nuance":"「だけ」 works with many verb forms; 「きり」 in meaning 2 requires V-た and a negative following clause. 「まま」 often occurs with ～ている / ～ておく, unlike「きり」.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"開けた","r":"あけた","m":"opened"},{"t":"まま","m":"left as is"},{"t":"寝た","r":"ねた","m":"slept"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I slept with the window open.","scene":"Falling asleep with the window open in summer"},{"tokens":[{"t":"出かけた","r":"でかけた","m":"left"},{"t":"きり","r":"きり","m":"since then"},{"t":"帰らない","r":"かえらない","m":"does not return"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"They left and never came back.","scene":"Describing someone who disappeared"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～だけ","point":"だけ: neutral “only,” widely used. きり: “only” with a cutoff/scarcity nuance, often for small amounts. Examples: りんごだけ食べた (only ate apples, amount unstated). 二人きりで話す (just two people, no one else)."},{"vs":"～たまま","point":"たまま: keeps the state after an action (sitting, standing, open...). たきり: nothing happens after that point, with a negative clause. Examples: 窓を開けたまま寝た (slept with the window open). 出かけたきり帰らない (left and did not return)."}]}},"n2-079||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～きりだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～きりだ","patternReading":"きりだ","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Ever since ~, nothing more has happened","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are common mistakes learners make with ～きりだ. Learn to avoid them so your sentences sound natural.","nuance":"With the “ever since” meaning, **never** use V-る. A missing negative clause after it sounds unnatural in Japanese.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"会った","r":"あった","m":"met"},{"t":"きり","r":"きり","m":"ever since"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"連絡","r":"れんらく","m":"contact"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"いない","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have not contacted him since I met him.","scene":"Talking about an old acquaintance"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼に会うきり会っていない。 → ✅ 彼に会ったきり会っていない。","❌ 一人きりに暮らしている。 → ✅ 一人きりで暮らしている。（Use で before the verb）","❌ 財布に千円きりしかない。 → ✅ 財布に千円きりだ。（Do not combine きり and しか; N + きり does not require a negative）"],"compare":null}},"n2-079||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"～きりだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～きりだ","patternReading":"きりだ","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Ever since ~, nothing more has happened","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture a **cutting stroke**. With a noun, cut everything away and keep only N. With a verb, cut at the V-た moment so nothing continues—hence the negative following clause.","nuance":"This helps distinguish きり from だけ (simple limitation) and まま (maintaining a state).","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-079||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～きりだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～きりだ","patternReading":"きりだ","coreMeaning":"Only ~ / Ever since ~, nothing more has happened","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed phrases appear often in exams and everyday Japanese. Memorizing them helps you recognize the grammar immediately.","nuance":"They often appear at the beginning or middle of a sentence, followed by a negative clause.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"二人","r":"ふたり","m":"two people"},{"t":"きり","r":"きり","m":"only"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"世界","r":"せかい","m":"world"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A world with just the two of us.","scene":"A romantic movie line or confession"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-080||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～てでも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てでも","patternReading":"てでも","coreMeaning":"Even if I have to ~, I will do it (strong resolve)","slug":"core","definition":"〜てでも expresses very strong resolve: even if the speaker must take a difficult, costly, or unusual action in the first clause, they will definitely do what follows. It emphasizes drastic means used to achieve a goal. The first-clause action is often an unwanted sacrifice that the speaker accepts.","nuance":"Subjective and strongly personal. It works in speech and writing, often with a first-person subject. It is much stronger and more emotional than ordinary 〜ても.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"借金","r":"しゃっきん","m":"debt"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"でも","m":"even if I must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"夢","r":"ゆめ","m":"dream"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"実現","r":"じつげん","m":"realize"},{"t":"させたい","m":"want to make happen"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if I have to go into debt, I want to make my dream come true.","scene":"Determined to start a business without capital."},{"tokens":[{"t":"徹夜","r":"てつや","m":"stay up all night"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"でも","m":"even if I must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"今日中","r":"きょうじゅう","m":"by today"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"終わらせる","r":"おわらせる","m":"finish"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if I have to stay up all night, I will finish this work today.","scene":"Making a promise to the boss before a deadline."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-080||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"～てでも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てでも","patternReading":"てでも","coreMeaning":"Even if I have to ~, I will do it (strong resolve)","slug":"forms","definition":"Structure: [verb te-form] + でも + [a following clause showing intention or resolve]. The first clause describes a sacrifice, and the second gives the goal. Do not use the た or ない form before でも.","nuance":"Polite but still forceful. It can appear in formal writing. Avoid third-person subjects unless quoting someone's thoughts.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"でも","note":"Before clause: difficult or costly action"},{"lhs":"Vてでも","rhs":"〜たい / 〜つもりだ / 〜する","note":"After clause: strong intention"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"無理","r":"むり","m":"overexertion"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"でも","m":"even if I must"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if I have to push myself too hard, I will go.","scene":"Answering whether you will attend despite being busy."},{"tokens":[{"t":"節約","r":"せつやく","m":"save money"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"でも","m":"even if I must"},{"t":"留学","r":"りゅうがく","m":"study abroad"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"つもり","m":"plan"},{"t":"だ","m":"be"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if I have to cut costs, I plan to study abroad.","scene":"Telling a friend about study-abroad plans."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-080||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～てでも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てでも","patternReading":"てでも","coreMeaning":"Even if I have to ~, I will do it (strong resolve)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when the second-clause goal matters so much that the speaker is willing to accept anything in the first clause. The first-clause action must be difficult, costly, or sacrificial.","nuance":"Do not use it for ordinary daily actions. It carries strong, sometimes slightly dramatic emotion.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どんな","m":"any"},{"t":"苦労","r":"くろう","m":"hardship"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"でも","m":"even if I must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"教育","r":"きょういく","m":"education"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"受けさせたい","r":"うけさせたい","m":"want to have receive"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No matter what hardships I face, I want my child to receive an education.","scene":"A father planning to send his child abroad."},{"tokens":[{"t":"土下座","r":"どげざ","m":"prostrate oneself"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"でも","m":"even if I must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"her"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"謝りたい","r":"あやまりたい","m":"want to apologize"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if I have to prostrate myself, I want to apologize to her.","scene":"Wanting forgiveness after making a mistake."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-080||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish ても / てまで","sub":"～てでも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てでも","patternReading":"てでも","coreMeaning":"Even if I have to ~, I will do it (strong resolve)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish 〜てでも from 〜ても and 〜てまで: 〜てでも shows strong resolve and willingness to use difficult or costly means to reach a goal. 〜ても simply expresses a concessive condition without emphasizing sacrifice. 〜てまで often suggests excess or criticism. For example, 'タクシーを使ってでも来てください' stresses determination, while '歩いて来てください' is too ordinary for てでも.","nuance":"In writing, 〜てでも expresses will; 〜てまで often appears with negation or criticism.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"タクシー","m":"taxi"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"使って","r":"つかって","m":"use"},{"t":"でも","m":"even if I must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"来て","r":"きて","m":"come"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if you have to take a taxi, please come.","scene":"Inviting an important business partner."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ても","point":"〜ても only states a condition: 'even if ...'; it does not express resolve or sacrifice. 〜てでも adds the idea of using drastic means if necessary."},{"vs":"〜てまで","point":"〜てまで often appears in negative or critical statements: 'there is no need to go that far.' 〜てでも affirms the resolve to act."}]}},"n2-080||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～てでも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てでも","patternReading":"てでも","coreMeaning":"Even if I have to ~, I will do it (strong resolve)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often use てでも with ordinary actions, which removes the sense of sacrifice. Check whether the first action is genuinely costly or difficult.","nuance":"In formal writing, use it in the right context and avoid overusing it.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"タクシー","m":"taxi"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"使って","r":"つかって","m":"use"},{"t":"でも","m":"even if I must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"来て","r":"きて","m":"come"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if you have to take a taxi, please come.","scene":"Inviting an important partner to an urgent meeting."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 電車に乗ってでも来てください。 → ✅ タクシーを使ってでも来てください。 (Taking the train is too ordinary; there is no sacrifice)","❌ 雨が降ってでも行く。 → ✅ 雨を押してでも行く。 (降る is unintentional, so it cannot connect to てでも)","❌ 疲れてでも、働くつもりだ。 → ✅ 無理をしてでも、働くつもりだ。 (疲れる describes a state, not a deliberate action)","❌ お金を借りてでも、買わない。 → ✅ お金を借りてでも、買いたい。 (The after clause must show intention or affirmation, not negation)"],"compare":null}},"n2-080||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"～てでも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てでも","patternReading":"てでも","coreMeaning":"Even if I have to ~, I will do it (strong resolve)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture yourself facing an important goal and ready to 'pay the price' with a drastic action. てでも is like saying 'even if I have to...'.","nuance":"Remember: て = do, でも = even so. Together: 'even if I have to do something, I will not back down.'","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-080||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"～てでも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てでも","patternReading":"てでも","coreMeaning":"Even if I have to ~, I will do it (strong resolve)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases with 〜てでも emphasize willingness to accept a sacrifice in order to achieve an important goal.","nuance":"In the JLPT, look for forms such as N + してでも and 無理してでも.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"無理","r":"むり","m":"overexertion"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"でも","m":"even if I must"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"出席","r":"しゅっせき","m":"attend"},{"t":"したい","m":"want to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if I have to push myself too hard, I want to attend.","scene":"Talking about an important event."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-081||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～てたまらない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てたまらない","patternReading":"てたまらない","coreMeaning":"Feel ~ to an unbearable degree; be unable to contain it","slug":"core","definition":"～てたまらない expresses an emotion or sensation so intense that the speaker cannot control or endure it any longer. It emphasizes a state that has exceeded the speaker's limit of tolerance. Unlike objective expressions, this pattern is subjective and direct.","nuance":"This is a highly emotional expression, often used in everyday conversation with friends and family. With older people or in formal contexts, ～て仕方がない or ～てならない is softer. With close people, ～てたまらない is completely natural. The speaker must directly experience the emotion, so when describing someone else, add ようだ / らしい.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"会いたくて","r":"あいたくて","m":"want to meet"},{"t":"たまらない","r":"たまらない","m":"unbearable"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to see her so badly I can't stand it.","scene":"Texting a partner after a week apart"},{"tokens":[{"t":"歯","r":"は","m":"tooth"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"痛くて","r":"いたくて","m":"hurt"},{"t":"たまらない","r":"たまらない","m":"unbearable"},{"t":"ので"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"歯医者","r":"はいしゃ","m":"dentist"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行った","r":"いった","m":"went"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My tooth hurt so badly that I went to the dentist.","scene":"Unable to sleep because of toothache at midnight"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-081||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～てたまらない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てたまらない","patternReading":"てたまらない","coreMeaning":"Feel ~ to an unbearable degree; be unable to contain it","slug":"forms","definition":"Attach たまらない to the て-form of an adjective or verb. For い-adjectives, drop い and add くて. For な-adjectives, add で. For verbs, use the て-form to connect to たまらない, especially V-たくて when expressing desire.","nuance":"All these forms have the same strong nuance. With ナadj, never use くて (e.g. ✕ 心配くてたまらない, ○ 心配でたまらない). With verbs, use emotion or desire verbs, not ordinary action verbs such as 食べる unless you mean “want to eat.”","structure":[{"lhs":"イadj","rhs":"～くてたまらない","note":"嬉しくてたまらない / 眠くてたまらない"},{"lhs":"ナadj","rhs":"～でたまらない","note":"心配でたまらない / 退屈でたまらない"},{"lhs":"V-て（emotion）","rhs":"～てたまらない","note":"気になってたまらない / 腹が立ってたまらない"},{"lhs":"V-たくて","rhs":"～たくてたまらない","note":"会いたくてたまらない / 食べたくてたまらない"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"眠くて","r":"ねむくて","m":"sleepy"},{"t":"たまらない","r":"たまらない","m":"unbearable"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am unbearably sleepy.","scene":"Studying until 2 a.m."},{"tokens":[{"t":"面接","r":"めんせつ","m":"interview"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"結果","r":"けっか","m":"result"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"心配","r":"しんぱい","m":"worried"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"たまらない","r":"たまらない","m":"unbearable"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am unbearably worried about the interview results.","scene":"Just after leaving the interview room"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-081||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～てたまらない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てたまらない","patternReading":"てたまらない","coreMeaning":"Feel ~ to an unbearable degree; be unable to contain it","slug":"usage","definition":"～てたまらない is used when the speaker wants to express an uncontrollable state that is surging: a parched throat, intense hunger, throbbing pain, overwhelming worry, deep longing, or joy so strong it brings tears. It is an emotional outpouring, not an objective description.","nuance":"This pattern is very natural for physical sensations such as thirst, hunger, and pain. With a third-person subject, add ようだ (seems) or らしい (apparently) to mark an inference. Example: 彼は嬉しくてたまらない**ようだ**. (Do not use: 彼は嬉しくてたまらない。)","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"外","r":"そと","m":"outside"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"暑くて","r":"あつくて","m":"hot"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"のど","r":"のど","m":"throat"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"渇いて","r":"かわいて","m":"thirsty"},{"t":"たまらない","r":"たまらない","m":"unbearable"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is so hot outside that I am unbearably thirsty.","scene":"Walking in the blazing summer midday"},{"tokens":[{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"match"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"前","r":"まえ","m":"before"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"足","r":"あし","m":"legs"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"震えて","r":"ふるえて","m":"tremble"},{"t":"たまらない","r":"たまらない","m":"unbearable"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before the match, my legs are shaking uncontrollably.","scene":"Nervous before entering the field"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-081||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare similar patterns","sub":"～てたまらない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てたまらない","patternReading":"てたまらない","coreMeaning":"Feel ~ to an unbearable degree; be unable to contain it","slug":"compare","definition":"〜てたまらない is closest in meaning to ～てならない and ～ずにはいられない. However, ～てたまらない describes an emotion or sensation that is unbearable; ～てならない focuses on a feeling that arises naturally, while ～ずにはいられない describes an action one cannot help doing.","nuance":"～てたまらない often emphasizes discomfort, distress, or strong desire. ～てならない is more formal and can be used with 思われる, as in その考えが正しいと思われてならない. ～ずにはいられない always takes a verb and describes an irresistible action, such as 笑わずにはいられない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"生まれて","r":"うまれて","m":"be born"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"嬉しくて","r":"うれしくて","m":"happy"},{"t":"たまらない","r":"たまらない","m":"unbearable"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Our child was born, and I am happier than I can express.","scene":"A father telling his close friend about the newborn"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～てならない","point":"～てたまらない emphasizes an intense, uncontrollable emotion; ～てならない describes a feeling or thought arising naturally, often with 思われて・感じられて, and is more formal."},{"vs":"～ずにはいられない","point":"～てたまらない describes an emotional state; ～ずにはいられない describes an action one cannot help doing (e.g. 笑わずにはいられない = cannot help laughing)."}]}},"n2-081||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～てたまらない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てたまらない","patternReading":"てたまらない","coreMeaning":"Feel ~ to an unbearable degree; be unable to contain it","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners may translate “unbearable” too directly and write 彼がたまらない, which is incorrect because the emotion is missing. An emotion adjective or verb must come first. Also watch the connection with ナadj and the subject restrictions.","nuance":"English can say “he can't stand it” without naming the feeling, but Japanese requires the specific emotion. For a third person, simply adding ている is not enough; use ようだ/らしい to show inference.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"こと","r":"こと","m":"about him"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"たまらない","r":"たまらない","m":"unbearable"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I like him so much I can't stand it.","scene":"Confessing to a close friend"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼がたまらない。 → ✅ 彼のことが好きでたまらない。 (add a specific emotion or state)","❌ 心配くてたまらない。 → ✅ 心配でたまらない。 (ナadj takes で, not くて)","❌ 彼は嬉しくてたまらない。 → ✅ 彼は嬉しくてたまらないようだ。 (third person needs ようだ/らしい)"],"compare":null}},"n2-081||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～てたまらない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てたまらない","patternReading":"てたまらない","coreMeaning":"Feel ~ to an unbearable degree; be unable to contain it","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember that たまらない is itself a strong word: 堪(た)まらない → “unable to endure.” It often follows the て-form to show the cause or state. Example: 暑くてたまらない = unbearably hot.","nuance":"This word intensifies the speaker's own emotion and sounds very natural in conversation. Picture the “unbearable” feeling as a balloon stretched to bursting—an easy image to remember.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-081||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～てたまらない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～てたまらない","patternReading":"てたまらない","coreMeaning":"Feel ~ to an unbearable degree; be unable to contain it","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear frequently on JLPT tests and in everyday life. Memorize them for expressing intense emotions when needed.","nuance":"気になってたまらない = unable to stop worrying or wondering, often used when something is on your mind. 会いたくてたまらない = desperately miss someone and want to see them. 心配でたまらない = unbearably worried; very common.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"気","r":"き","m":"mind"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なって","r":"なって","m":"become"},{"t":"たまらない","r":"たまらない","m":"unbearable"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can't stop worrying about it.","scene":"Waiting for an important message"},{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"会いたくて","r":"あいたくて","m":"want to meet"},{"t":"たまらない","r":"たまらない","m":"unbearable"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I miss my mother so much I can't stand it.","scene":"An exchange student missing home"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-082||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ほど～ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ほど～ない","patternReading":"ほどない","coreMeaning":"Nothing compares to ~ (X is the most extreme) / X is not as ~ as Y","slug":"core","definition":"〜ほど〜ない is a degree-based comparison pattern. It has two uses: (1) [X] ほど [noun] はない — states that X is the highest or most extreme; (2) [X] は [Y] ほど [negative adjective/verb] — compares X as inferior to Y. Both end in 'ない'.","nuance":"This pattern emphasizes the speaker's emotional evaluation (importance, preference, difficulty, etc.). It is neutral and works in both speech and writing. Compared with 一番, it makes a figurative comparison: there is no need to list a group; simply say that nothing compares to X.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"ほど","r":"ほど","m":"as"},{"t":"大切な","r":"たいせつな","m":"valuable"},{"t":"もの","r":"もの","m":"thing"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"there is no"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Nothing is as valuable as health.","scene":"Talking with a friend about life's most important things."},{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"ほど","r":"ほど","m":"as"},{"t":"寒く","r":"さむく","m":"cold"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Today is not as cold as yesterday.","scene":"Chatting with a coworker about the weather."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-082||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～ほど～ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ほど～ない","patternReading":"ほどない","coreMeaning":"Nothing compares to ~ (X is the most extreme) / X is not as ~ as Y","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic connection formulas. Before ほど comes a noun or a verb in dictionary or た-form. The following clause is negative: Aい→Aくない, Aな→Aではない, V→Vない, N→Nはない.","nuance":"With い-adjectives, conjugate to くない; with nouns, は must come before ない. In the past, use なかった.","structure":[{"lhs":"[N1] ほど [N2] は","rhs":"ない","note":"N1 is the extreme; N2 is the general noun. E.g.: 健康ほど大切なものはない"},{"lhs":"[N1] は [N2] ほど [A]","rhs":"ない","note":"Comparison N1 < N2. E.g.: 今日は昨日ほど寒くない"},{"lhs":"[Vる/Vた] ほど","rhs":"[negative]","note":"Compared with the level the subject imagined or experienced. E.g.: 思ったほど難しくない"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"東京","r":"とうきょう","m":"Tokyo"},{"t":"ほど","r":"ほど","m":"as"},{"t":"大きい","r":"おおきい","m":"large"},{"t":"都市","r":"とし","m":"city"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"there is no"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No city is as large as Tokyo.","scene":"Commenting on Tokyo's development."},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"film"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"評判","r":"ひょうばん","m":"reputation"},{"t":"ほど","r":"ほど","m":"as"},{"t":"よく","r":"よく","m":"good"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This film is not as good as its reputation.","scene":"Replying after a friend asks about a film you watched."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-082||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～ほど～ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ほど～ない","patternReading":"ほどない","coreMeaning":"Nothing compares to ~ (X is the most extreme) / X is not as ~ as Y","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to say: (1) nothing surpasses X — 'Nほど...ものはない'; (2) X does not reach Y's level — 'XはYほど...ない'; (3) reality is below expectations — '思ったほど...ない'.","nuance":"In speech, 思ったほど and 予想したほど are common. Formal writing often uses the full Nほど...はない pattern. Form ① makes a strong assertion.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"思った","r":"おもった","m":"thought"},{"t":"ほど","r":"ほど","m":"as"},{"t":"面白く","r":"おもしろく","m":"interesting"},{"t":"なかった","r":"なかった","m":"was not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book was not as interesting as I thought.","scene":"Talking with a friend about a book you just finished."},{"tokens":[{"t":"北海道","r":"ほっかいどう","m":"Hokkaido"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"東京","r":"とうきょう","m":"Tokyo"},{"t":"ほど","r":"ほど","m":"as"},{"t":"暑く","r":"あつく","m":"hot"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Hokkaido is not as hot as Tokyo.","scene":"Talking about summer weather while traveling."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-082||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compared with ～をおいて","sub":"～ほど～ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ほど～ない","patternReading":"ほどない","coreMeaning":"Nothing compares to ~ (X is the most extreme) / X is not as ~ as Y","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish ～ほど～ない from similar patterns. The key difference is meaning: ～ほど～ない concerns DEGREE (nothing reaches X's level) or inferiority. ～をおいて emphasizes UNIQUENESS (apart from X, there is no other person or thing). ～より makes a more direct comparison, while ～に限る means 'X is the best choice'. Choose based on context.","nuance":"All three can suggest 'the best,' but ～ほど～ない emphasizes degree; ～をおいて emphasizes uniqueness; ～に限る emphasizes a preferred choice, implying that nothing else is needed.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"ほど","r":"ほど","m":"as"},{"t":"できる","r":"できる","m":"capable"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"いない","r":"いない","m":"there is no"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No one is as capable as he is.","scene":"Praising a coworker."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～をおいて","point":"～をおいて: 'There is no one or nothing besides X' — emphasizes UNIQUENESS; ～ほど～ない: 'nothing matches X in degree' — emphasizes DEGREE. E.g.: 彼をおいて... vs 彼ほど...ない"},{"vs":"～より","point":"～より is used for direct comparisons (A is more than B). 'AはBより高くない' means A is not higher than B and may be equal or lower; 'AはBほど高くない' means A is definitely lower than B. The nuance differs clearly."},{"vs":"～に限る","point":"～に限る: 'X is the best choice' — a sole, fairly casual preference; ～ほど～ない: confirms X's supreme degree through an overall comparison. E.g.: 夏はビールに限る vs ビールほどおいしいものはない."}]}},"n2-082||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～ほど～ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ほど～ない","patternReading":"ほどない","coreMeaning":"Nothing compares to ~ (X is the most extreme) / X is not as ~ as Y","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors with ～ほど～ない include replacing は with が in the fixed phrase 'ほど...はない', omitting ない and turning the sentence affirmative, and using より instead of ほど in a negative comparison. These errors change the meaning or make the grammar incorrect. Remember: ほど + negative = not as; は is required.","nuance":"The most common error is using が instead of は in the fixed phrase 'ほど...はない'. Also, after using ほど in a negative comparison, learners sometimes accidentally leave the predicate affirmative.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あの","r":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"ほど","r":"ほど","m":"as"},{"t":"安く","r":"やすく","m":"cheap"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This shop is not as cheap as that one.","scene":"Comparing prices outside two restaurants."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 健康ほど大切なものがない。 → ✅ 健康ほど大切なものはない。 (The fixed phrase 'ほど...はない' requires は, not が.)","❌ 今日は昨日ほど寒い。 → ✅ 今日は昨日ほど寒くない。 (To mean 'not as,' the sentence must end in ない/なかった.)","❌ 駅は私の家ほど遠くない。 → ✅ 私の家は駅ほど遠くない。 (The comparison order is reversed: AはBほど...ない means A is inferior to B.)"],"compare":null}},"n2-082||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～ほど～ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ほど～ない","patternReading":"ほどない","coreMeaning":"Nothing compares to ~ (X is the most extreme) / X is not as ~ as Y","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture ほど as a level mark. Formula ① says X is at the highest mark; formula ② says X is below Y's mark. When you hear ほど with a negative, think of an inferior comparison.","nuance":"Quick cue: 'Xほど...ものはない' = X is unbeatable; 'XはYほど...ない' = X ranks below Y. When ほど appears with a negative, think 'not as high'.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-082||phrases":{"title":"💬 Phrases & idioms","sub":"～ほど～ない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ほど～ない","patternReading":"ほどない","coreMeaning":"Nothing compares to ~ (X is the most extreme) / X is not as ~ as Y","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases that help you speak naturally and frequently appear on exams.","nuance":"Phrases such as '健康ほど大切なものはない', '思ったほど…ない', and '評判ほど…ない' are very common. Memorize them for quick use.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"命","r":"いのち","m":"life"},{"t":"ほど","r":"ほど","m":"as"},{"t":"大切な","r":"たいせつな","m":"valuable"},{"t":"もの","r":"もの","m":"thing"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"there is no"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Nothing is as valuable as life.","scene":"Emphasizing the value of life."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-083||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～上に · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～上に","patternReading":"うえに","coreMeaning":"Moreover ~; adding in the same direction (good + good / bad + bad)","slug":"core","definition":"上に connects two facts, with the latter clause added to the former, usually with the same evaluative direction. Concise translations: “Moreover” and “not only… but also…”. This pattern makes a list more cohesive and emphasizes accumulation.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, with a slightly formal tone. The speaker adds another reason, advantage, or disadvantage to make the overall claim more convincing. Compared with それに, 上に is more written and links the two clauses more tightly.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"restaurant"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"food"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"安い","r":"やすい","m":"cheap"},{"t":"上に","r":"うえに","m":"moreover"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"おいしい","r":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The food at this restaurant is not only cheap but also delicious.","scene":"Praising a restaurant after eating"},{"tokens":[{"t":"道","r":"みち","m":"road"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"迷った","r":"まよった","m":"got lost"},{"t":"上に","r":"うえに","m":"moreover"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"降ってきた","r":"ふってきた","m":"started falling"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I not only got lost but also got caught in the rain.","scene":"Recounting an unlucky day"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-083||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～上に · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～上に","patternReading":"うえに","coreMeaning":"Moreover ~; adding in the same direction (good + good / bad + bad)","slug":"forms","definition":"上に follows a predicate in the short (普通形) form. With な-adjectives and nouns, な and の must be retained. This pattern is more formal than それに.","nuance":"The first clause is often a known observation or event; the second adds information. With verbs, the た-form is very common because the pattern often lists events that have already occurred.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"上に","note":"Vた is common: 落ちた上に、迷った上に"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"上に","note":"安い上に、広い上に"},{"lhs":"Naな","rhs":"上に","note":"親切な上に、きれいな上に"},{"lhs":"Nの","rhs":"上に","note":"病気の上に、学生の上に"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"広い","r":"ひろい","m":"spacious"},{"t":"上に","r":"うえに","m":"moreover"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日当たり","r":"ひあたり","m":"sunlight"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"いい","r":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This room is spacious and also gets plenty of sunlight.","scene":"An agent showing an apartment"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"親切","r":"しんせつ","m":"kind"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"上に","r":"うえに","m":"moreover"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"有能","r":"ゆうのう","m":"capable"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She is not only kind but also highly capable.","scene":"Commenting on a former boss"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-083||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～上に · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～上に","patternReading":"うえに","coreMeaning":"Moreover ~; adding in the same direction (good + good / bad + bad)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use it in conversation and writing to add information in the same direction as an earlier fact, reason, or feature. The listener understands it as “on top of all that, there is also…”.","nuance":"Do not connect contrasting clauses. To say “although... but...”, use が or けれど. 上に often appears in formal writing, news articles, and JLPT N2 questions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"病気","r":"びょうき","m":"illness"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"上に","r":"うえに","m":"moreover"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"job"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"失った","r":"うしなった","m":"lost"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Not only did they fall ill, but they also lost their job.","scene":"Comforting a friend facing mounting hardships"},{"tokens":[{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"近い","r":"ちかい","m":"close"},{"t":"上に","r":"うえに","m":"moreover"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"家賃","r":"やちん","m":"rent"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"安い","r":"やすい","m":"cheap"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is close to the station, and the rent is cheap too.","scene":"Texting a friend about a new home"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-083||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions from similar patterns","sub":"～上に · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～上に","patternReading":"うえに","coreMeaning":"Moreover ~; adding in the same direction (good + good / bad + bad)","slug":"compare","definition":"Learners often confuse these patterns because all can be translated as “not only… but also”. Distinguish the main point emphasized by each pattern.","nuance":"上に simply adds information neutrally; ばかりか adds surprise; だけでなく is commonly used for parallel listing; 上で has a completely different time-related meaning.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"restaurant"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"安い","r":"やすい","m":"cheap"},{"t":"上に","r":"うえに","m":"moreover"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"味","r":"あじ","m":"taste"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"いい","r":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This restaurant is both cheap and delicious.","scene":"A model sentence contrasted with ばかりか"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～ばかりか","point":"上に: adds a same-direction point neutrally; ばかりか: the latter is surprising and beyond expectations. Ex.: 安い上に味もいい / 安いばかりか、味もいい."},{"vs":"～上で","point":"上に = ADDING INFORMATION; 上で = doing one thing and then another. Ex.: 調べた上で返事する (reply after checking) — 調べた上に、… (not only checked, but also...)."},{"vs":"～だけでなく","point":"上に emphasizes natural ACCUMULATION; だけでなく emphasizes LISTING. Both can mean “not only… but also,” but 上に links the clauses more tightly."}]}},"n2-083||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors / traps","sub":"～上に · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～上に","patternReading":"うえに","coreMeaning":"Moreover ~; adding in the same direction (good + good / bad + bad)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"A classic error is using 上に to connect contrasting clauses because it can mean “moreover”. In reality, 上に requires both clauses to have the same evaluative direction.","nuance":"You may see violations in casual speech, but in writing and on the JLPT, use it correctly. For contrast, use が・けれど・のに depending on the degree of contrast.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"restaurant"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"安い","r":"やすい","m":"cheap"},{"t":"上に","r":"うえに","m":"moreover"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"味","r":"あじ","m":"taste"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"いい","r":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This restaurant is both cheap and delicious.","scene":"A correct model sentence for the same-direction rule"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 値段が安い上に、品質が悪い。 → ✅ 値段は安いが、品質が悪い。","❌ 頭がいい上に、スポーツが下手だ。 → ✅ 頭がいいが、スポーツが下手だ。","❌ 食事をした上に、出かけた。 → ✅ 食事をした上で、出かけた。"],"compare":null}},"n2-083||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～上に · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～上に","patternReading":"うえに","coreMeaning":"Moreover ~; adding in the same direction (good + good / bad + bad)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture 上に as a cake with an extra layer of frosting: everything is stacked on top. If the next layer has a different flavor—good versus bad—the cake fails.","nuance":"Quick check: write both clauses, decide whether the first is positive or negative, and check whether the second has the same polarity. Same polarity → 上に works.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-083||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases & spoken use","sub":"～上に · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～上に","patternReading":"うえに","coreMeaning":"Moreover ~; adding in the same direction (good + good / bad + bad)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These common combinations appear in reading passages and conversation. Memorizing them in good-good or bad-bad pairs helps you choose answers quickly.","nuance":"When you see 上に on a test, underline the keywords in the first and second clauses. If both are positive or both negative, choose the meaning “moreover”.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"広い","r":"ひろい","m":"spacious"},{"t":"上に","r":"うえに","m":"moreover"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日当たり","r":"ひあたり","m":"sunlight"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"いい","r":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This room is both spacious and sunny.","scene":"An apartment advertisement"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-084||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～といっても · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～といっても","patternReading":"といっても","coreMeaning":"Although ~, in reality (narrowing the scope)","slug":"core","definition":"～といっても follows a claim to narrow or qualify what was just said. It means “although I say..., in reality...”. The speaker prevents the listener from assuming they are more skilled or have more than they really do.","nuance":"This pattern is neutral and works in speech and writing, but is especially natural in conversation. It sounds gentle and modest, not forceful. Compared with 〜とはいえ, といっても sounds more casual and familiar.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"cooking"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"できる","r":"できる","m":"can do"},{"t":"といっても","r":"といっても","m":"although I say"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"簡単","r":"かんたん","m":"simple"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"もの","r":"もの","m":"dishes"},{"t":"しか"},{"t":"作れない","r":"つくれない","m":"can only make"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although I can cook, in reality I can only make simple dishes.","scene":"You praise my cooking; I modestly clarify the truth."},{"tokens":[{"t":"貯金","r":"ちょきん","m":"savings"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"といっても","r":"といっても","m":"although I say"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"数万円","r":"すうまんえん","m":"tens of thousands of yen"},{"t":"程度","r":"ていど","m":"about"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although I have savings, in reality it is only a few tens of thousands of yen.","scene":"Someone says you have savings; you correct the impression."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-084||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"～といっても · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～といっても","patternReading":"といっても","coreMeaning":"Although ~, in reality (narrowing the scope)","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern follows a clause or quotation as if reporting what was said: [clause] + といっても + [limitation]. Its origin is と言って + も, meaning “even if one says...”.","nuance":"With nouns and な-adjectives, だ may be added for clarity: 学生だといっても, 有名だといっても. In casual speech, だ is often omitted, but including it is more standard. The second clause often uses だけ・しか～ない・まだ to express a limitation.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vる / Vた / Vている","rhs":"といっても、〜"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"といっても、〜"},{"lhs":"Aな + だ","rhs":"といっても、〜"},{"lhs":"N + だ","rhs":"といっても、〜"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"話せる","r":"はなせる","m":"can speak"},{"t":"といっても","r":"といっても","m":"although I say"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"まだ","r":"まだ","m":"still only"},{"t":"N4","m":"N4"},{"t":"レベル","m":"level"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although I can speak Japanese, I am only at N4 level.","scene":"You are asked if you speak Japanese and clarify your level."},{"tokens":[{"t":"読書","r":"どくしょ","m":"reading"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"といっても","r":"といっても","m":"although I say"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"漫画","r":"まんが","m":"manga"},{"t":"ばかり","m":"only"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although I like reading, in reality I only read manga.","scene":"You clarify that your reading mainly means manga."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-084||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～といっても · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～といっても","patternReading":"といっても","coreMeaning":"Although ~, in reality (narrowing the scope)","slug":"usage","definition":"～といっても often appears in conversation when speakers adjust their own words. Use it when someone praises you or when you want to clarify the actual degree of something.","nuance":"The second clause usually gives a limitation rather than a total negation. If it strongly contradicts the first clause, use 〜とはいえ instead. といっても is more conversational.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ダイエット","m":"diet"},{"t":"する","r":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"といっても","r":"といっても","m":"although I say"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"激しい","r":"はげしい","m":"intense"},{"t":"運動","r":"うんどう","m":"exercise"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"無理","r":"むり","m":"impossible"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although I plan to lose weight, intense exercise is impossible for me.","scene":"You plan to lose weight but cannot exercise intensely."},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"restaurant"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"高い","r":"たかい","m":"expensive"},{"t":"といっても","r":"といっても","m":"although I say"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ランチ","m":"lunch"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"1000円","r":"いっせんえん","m":"1,000 yen"},{"t":"だけ","m":"only"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although this restaurant is expensive, lunch is only 1,000 yen.","scene":"The restaurant seems expensive, but lunch costs only 1,000 yen."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-084||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing it from とはいえ / ものの","sub":"～といっても · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～といっても","patternReading":"といっても","coreMeaning":"Although ~, in reality (narrowing the scope)","slug":"compare","definition":"～といっても is easily confused with ～とはいえ and ～ものの. They all express concession, but differ in strength and usage.","nuance":"Of the three, といっても is the lightest and most casual; とはいえ is formal and written; ものの emphasizes an opposite result after an action has already occurred.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お金持ち","r":"おかねもち","m":"wealthy person"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"といっても","r":"といっても","m":"although I say"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"生活","r":"せいかつ","m":"lifestyle"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"普通","r":"ふつう","m":"ordinary"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although I am wealthy, my lifestyle is ordinary.","scene":"Someone thinks you are rich; you clarify your ordinary lifestyle."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～とはいえ","point":"とはいえ accepts the first clause and then gives a STRONGLY OPPOSITE idea in a formal tone; といっても only LIMITS the degree gently."},{"vs":"～ものの","point":"ものの follows something that HAS HAPPENED and gives a clearly opposite result; といっても modifies a general claim without requiring total opposition."}]}},"n2-084||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"～といっても · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～といっても","patternReading":"といっても","coreMeaning":"Although ~, in reality (narrowing the scope)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The most common error is using といっても when there is nothing to limit—that is, when the second clause repeats the first. This pattern requires a contrast between the claim and reality.","nuance":"Avoid it when you want to stress a strong opposition; use 〜とはいえ. Also, do not replace と with から because と is the quotation particle.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"アパート","m":"apartment"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"広い","r":"ひろい","m":"spacious"},{"t":"といっても","r":"といっても","m":"although I say"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"6畳","r":"ろくじょう","m":"six tatami mats"},{"t":"しか"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"only has"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although this apartment is spacious, it only has six tatami mats.","scene":"Introducing your apartment while clarifying its actual size."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼は金持ちだといっても、本当に金持ちだ。 → ✅ 彼は金持ちだといっても、年収は500万円くらいだ。","❌ 料理ができるといっても、上手だ。 → ✅ 料理ができるといっても、簡単なものだけだ。","❌ 日本語が話せるといっても、日常会話ができる。 → ✅ 日本語が話せるといっても、挨拶しかできない。"],"compare":null}},"n2-084||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～といっても · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～といっても","patternReading":"といっても","coreMeaning":"Although ~, in reality (narrowing the scope)","slug":"memory","definition":"Breakdown: と marks a quotation, いって is 言って, and も means “also/even” → “even if you say...”. Picture a map being enlarged and then shrunk.","nuance":"This is only a memory aid, not a specific context.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-084||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～といっても · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～といっても","patternReading":"といっても","coreMeaning":"Although ~, in reality (narrowing the scope)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These common combinations with といっても appear in tests and conversation, forming limitation patterns.","nuance":"Combining it with limiting adverbs makes the meaning “that is all” clearer.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"good"},{"t":"といっても","r":"といっても","m":"although I say"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"まだまだ","m":"still far from good"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although my Japanese is good, I still have a long way to go.","scene":"Modestly replying when praised for your Japanese."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-086||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ないでもない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないでもない","patternReading":"ないでもない","coreMeaning":"Not exactly not ~ / could also ~ (half-hearted admission)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ないでもない / 〜なくもない is a **double negative**: “it’s not that...”. It expresses a **half-hearted admission** when the speaker is hesitant and avoids a strong commitment. Translate it as “not exactly not ~” or “could also ~”.","nuance":"It conveys **caution and avoidance of a strong assertion**. It works in speech and writing and sounds slightly formal. In daily conversation, **〜なくもない** sounds more natural than **〜ないでもない**. It is often followed by **が / けど** or used independently at the end of a sentence.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"手伝えないでもない","r":"てつだえないでもない","m":"could help"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"少し","r":"すこし","m":"a little"},{"t":"忙しい","r":"いそがしい","m":"busy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It’s not that I can’t help, but I’m a little busy today.","scene":"A boss asks for overtime help; you soften the refusal."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"言いたい","r":"いいたい","m":"wants to say"},{"t":"こと","r":"こと","m":"thing"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"分からなくもない","r":"わからなくもない","m":"can understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It’s not that I don’t understand what he wants to say.","scene":"You understand a coworker but do not fully agree."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-086||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～ないでもない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないでもない","patternReading":"ないでもない","coreMeaning":"Not exactly not ~ / could also ~ (half-hearted admission)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is **V-ない + でもない** or **V-なく + もない**. The result means “could also” or “not exactly not”. It is used mainly with **verbs**, especially ability verbs such as できる, わかる, and 行ける.","nuance":"〜なくもない is a variant of 〜ないでもない and is often used in conversation. Both forms commonly appear before a clause containing **が / けど** to create contrast, or at the end of a sentence. 〜ないでもない sounds slightly more formal.","structure":[{"lhs":"V-ない","rhs":"+ でもない","note":"できる → できないでもない"},{"lhs":"V-ない → V-なく","rhs":"+ もない","note":"わかる → わからなくもない"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"できなくもない","r":"できなくもない","m":"could do it"},{"t":"けど"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"自信","r":"じしん","m":"confidence"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ない"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It’s not that I can’t do it, but I’m not confident.","scene":"Asked whether you can handle a new task; you answer cautiously."},{"tokens":[{"t":"行かないでもない","r":"いかないでもない","m":"might go"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"気","r":"き","m":"mood"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"進まない","r":"すすまない","m":"not inclined"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It’s not that I won’t go, but I’m not very keen on it.","scene":"You accept a reunion invitation hesitantly."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-086||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～ないでもない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないでもない","patternReading":"ないでもない","coreMeaning":"Not exactly not ~ / could also ~ (half-hearted admission)","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern is often used to **soften a reply**, **gently counter an opinion**, or **admit possibility with hesitation**. The speaker wants to preserve harmony without refusing directly or making a firm commitment.","nuance":"In conversation with superiors or friends, use it to show **willingness** while adding conditions. In writing, it presents a neutral viewpoint.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"その"},{"t":"考え","r":"かんがえ","m":"idea"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"分からないでもない","r":"わからないでもない","m":"can understand"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"賛成","r":"さんせい","m":"agree"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"しない"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It’s not that I don’t understand that idea, but I don’t agree.","scene":"Gently challenging a coworker’s proposal in a meeting."},{"tokens":[{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あれば"},{"t":"行けなくもない","r":"いけなくもない","m":"could go"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"無理","r":"むり","m":"impossible"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If I had the money, I could go, but not right now.","scene":"You want to travel but have financial constraints."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-086||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare","sub":"～ないでもない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないでもない","patternReading":"ないでもない","coreMeaning":"Not exactly not ~ / could also ~ (half-hearted admission)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare patterns that are easily confused with **〜ないでもない** so you can choose the right nuance in speech and writing.","nuance":"The key difference is the degree of hesitation and contextual tone. 〜ないでもない is usually more formal and hesitant, 〜ないことはない is more natural in everyday speech, while 〜に違いない expresses near certainty.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"なら"},{"t":"行けなくもない","r":"いけなくもない","m":"could go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If it’s tomorrow, I might be able to go.","scene":"Giving an evasive reply about attending."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ないことはない","point":"Nearly synonymous, but 〜ないことはない is **more natural and common** in speech. 〜ないでもない sounds slightly formal or intellectual."},{"vs":"〜に違いない","point":"〜に違いない expresses about **95% certainty** through strong inference; 〜ないでもない expresses only **about 50%, cautiously**. They cannot replace each other."}]}},"n2-086||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～ないでもない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないでもない","patternReading":"ないでもない","coreMeaning":"Not exactly not ~ / could also ~ (half-hearted admission)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"This pattern is confusing because of its double negative. Learners may interpret it as “definitely”, but it actually means “not exactly not”. Also distinguish V-ない + でもない from V-なく + もない, and remember that the following clause often uses が/けど to create contrast.","nuance":"If used incorrectly, listeners may think you are making a firm commitment or completely refusing. Pay attention to intonation and the following clause.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"気持ち","r":"きもち","m":"feelings"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"分からなくもない","r":"わからなくもない","m":"can understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It’s not that I don’t understand how he feels.","scene":"A correct pattern in everyday conversation."}],"pitfalls":["❌ Interpreting it as “Definitely ~” → ✅ It means “Not exactly not ~”, only about 50%.","❌ Using 手伝わないでもない in the wrong context → ✅ Use 手伝えないでもない (negative potential form) or 手伝わなくもない.","❌ Confusing 〜ないでもない with 〜ないではいられない → ✅ 〜ないでもない expresses only a hesitant, incomplete admission (about 50%)."],"compare":null}},"n2-086||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～ないでもない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないでもない","patternReading":"ないでもない","coreMeaning":"Not exactly not ~ / could also ~ (half-hearted admission)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture a scale: one side says “Yes” and the other “No”. Japanese places two negatives on the scale, which tips slightly toward “Yes” but not completely. Remember: **the confidence level is about 50%**.","nuance":"Do not use it for a definite assertion. When you see が after this pattern, expect a contrasting idea to follow.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-086||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～ないでもない · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないでもない","patternReading":"ないでもない","coreMeaning":"Not exactly not ~ / could also ~ (half-hearted admission)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common **〜なくもない** phrases with verbs, expressing possibility with hesitation.","nuance":"These phrases appear often in conversation, especially when giving a softened reply.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"それ","r":"それ","m":"that"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"分からなくもない","r":"わからなくもない","m":"can understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It’s not that I don’t understand that.","scene":"A coworker explains a reason, and you partly acknowledge it."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-087||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ないまでも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないまでも","patternReading":"ないまでも","coreMeaning":"Even if it does not reach the level of ~, at least...","slug":"core","definition":"～ないまでも expresses that the high level in the first clause is not reached, but at least a lower level in the second clause is achieved. The first clause usually states an ideal action or goal; the second gives a minimum request, expectation, or evaluation. The pattern shows a compromise between wishes and practical ability.","nuance":"Neutral and slightly formal; it works in both speech and writing. The speaker often combines hope with acceptance of limitations. With せめて or 少なくとも, the minimum level is emphasized.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"全部","r":"ぜんぶ","m":"everything"},{"t":"できないまでも","m":"even if unable"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"せめて","m":"at least"},{"t":"半分","r":"はんぶん","m":"half"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"やって","m":"do"},{"t":"ほしい","m":"want"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if you cannot do everything, please do at least half.","scene":"A manager asks an employee to finish the work."},{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"でないまでも","m":"even if not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"週","r":"しゅう","m":"week"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"一度","r":"いちど","m":"once"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"連絡","r":"れんらく","m":"contact"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"ほしい","m":"want"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if not every day, please contact me at least once a week.","scene":"A mother advises her child studying abroad."},{"tokens":[{"t":"合格","r":"ごうかく","m":"pass"},{"t":"できないまでも","m":"even if unable"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"精一杯","r":"せいいっぱい","m":"with all one's effort"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"する","m":"make"},{"t":"こと","m":"act"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"大切","r":"たいせつ","m":"important"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if I cannot pass, what matters is doing my very best.","scene":"A thought before an exam."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-087||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～ないまでも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないまでも","patternReading":"ないまでも","coreMeaning":"Even if it does not reach the level of ~, at least...","slug":"forms","definition":"Main structure: verb in the ない-form + までも. It can also follow a noun as N + でない + までも, as in 毎日でないまでも. The following part often contains せめて / 少なくとも plus a desired clause.","nuance":"Note that ～ないまでも is a fixed form; do not replace までも with でも. Do not use it with an affirmative form. ～するまでもない has a completely different meaning: “there is no need to go as far as doing.”","structure":[{"lhs":"V（ない形）","rhs":"＋ までも、","note":"Example: できないまでも"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"＋ でないまでも、","note":"Example: 毎日でないまでも"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"全部","r":"ぜんぶ","m":"everything"},{"t":"できないまでも","m":"even if unable"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"せめて","m":"at least"},{"t":"半分","r":"はんぶん","m":"half"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"やって","m":"do"},{"t":"ほしい","m":"want"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if you cannot do everything, please do at least half.","scene":"A manager asks an employee to finish the work."},{"tokens":[{"t":"買えないまでも","r":"かえないまでも","m":"even if cannot buy"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"見る","r":"みる","m":"look"},{"t":"だけでも","m":"even just"},{"t":"楽しい","r":"たのしい","m":"fun"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if I cannot buy it, just looking at it is fun.","scene":"Seeing a beautiful item while walking around town."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-087||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～ないまでも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないまでも","patternReading":"ないまでも","coreMeaning":"Even if it does not reach the level of ~, at least...","slug":"usage","definition":"～ないまでも is used when the speaker does not demand the highest standard, but wants the other person or themselves to reach an acceptable minimum. It can soften a request, acknowledge personal limits, or state that if X is not achieved, Y should still be achieved.","nuance":"The tone is gentle but principled: the speaker does not demand something difficult, but still expects the easier part. It often appears in letters, speeches, and workplace conversations.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"全部","r":"ぜんぶ","m":"everything"},{"t":"できないまでも","m":"even if unable"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"せめて","m":"at least"},{"t":"半分","r":"はんぶん","m":"half"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"やって","m":"do"},{"t":"ほしい","m":"want"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if you cannot do everything, please do at least half.","scene":"A manager asks an employee to finish the work."},{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"でないまでも","m":"even if not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"週","r":"しゅう","m":"week"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"一度","r":"いちど","m":"once"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"連絡","r":"れんらく","m":"contact"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"ほしい","m":"want"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if not every day, please contact me at least once a week.","scene":"A mother advises her child studying abroad."},{"tokens":[{"t":"買えないまでも","r":"かえないまでも","m":"even if cannot buy"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"見る","r":"みる","m":"look"},{"t":"だけでも","m":"even just"},{"t":"楽しい","r":"たのしい","m":"fun"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if I cannot buy it, just looking at it is fun.","scene":"Seeing a beautiful item while walking around town."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-087||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with similar patterns","sub":"～ないまでも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないまでも","patternReading":"ないまでも","coreMeaning":"Even if it does not reach the level of ~, at least...","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish ～ないまでも from similar patterns: ～ないでもない (double negation) and ～ものの (concession). The key difference is the relationship between the two clauses: ～ないまでも contrasts a high level with a lower level, while the other patterns do not require this. ～ないでもない expresses a tentative possibility; ～ものの describes something that has already happened.","nuance":"The key point: ～ないまでも compares two levels, high → low; ～ないでもない is double negation, meaning “not entirely impossible”; ～ものの concedes an established fact.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"全部","r":"ぜんぶ","m":"everything"},{"t":"できないまでも","m":"even if unable"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"せめて","m":"at least"},{"t":"半分","r":"はんぶん","m":"half"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"やって","m":"do"},{"t":"ほしい","m":"want"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if you cannot do everything, please do at least half.","scene":"A manager asks an employee to finish the work."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ないでもない","point":"～ないでもない: “it is not that one cannot” — a hesitant possibility; ～ないまでも: “even if the high level is not reached, the lower level is.” Compare: 'できないでもないが、時間がかかる。' (It is not impossible, but it takes time) vs '全部できないまでも、半分はできる。' (Even if I cannot do everything, I can do half)."},{"vs":"〜ものの","point":"ものの: used when something has happened but the result differs from expectations; ないまでも: used when the goal is not reached and only a minimum is needed. Compare: '買ったものの、使っていない。' (I bought it, but do not use it) vs '買えないまでも、見るだけでも楽しい。' (Even if I cannot buy it, just looking is fun)."}]}},"n2-087||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes / traps","sub":"～ないまでも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないまでも","patternReading":"ないまでも","coreMeaning":"Even if it does not reach the level of ~, at least...","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors include making the second clause something other than a lower level, using the wrong form, and confusing this pattern with another.","nuance":"The biggest mistake is losing the descending degree relationship between the clauses. The first clause must describe something difficult or impossible, while the second must describe something easier or smaller that can be achieved.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"でないまでも","m":"even if not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"週","r":"しゅう","m":"week"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"一度","r":"いちど","m":"once"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"連絡","r":"れんらく","m":"contact"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"ほしい","m":"want"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if not every day, please contact me at least once a week.","scene":"A mother advises her child studying abroad."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 全部食べられないまでも、食べすぎてしまった。 → ✅ 全部食べられないまでも、せめて半分は食べてほしい。","❌ 毎日行かないまでも、忙しかった。 → ✅ 毎日行かないまでも、週に一度は行きたい。","❌ 買えないまでも、財布を忘れた。 → ✅ 買えないまでも、見るだけでも楽しい。"],"compare":null}},"n2-087||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～ないまでも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないまでも","patternReading":"ないまでも","coreMeaning":"Even if it does not reach the level of ~, at least...","slug":"memory","definition":"To remember ～ないまでも, picture a two-step ladder: the upper step is too high to reach in the first clause, but the lower step in the second clause is still reachable. Memory phrase: “If the big goal is impossible, do the smaller one.”","nuance":"Picture a ladder: the upper rung is too high to reach, while the lower rung is within reach and should be achieved at least.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-087||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～ないまでも · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ないまでも","patternReading":"ないまでも","coreMeaning":"Even if it does not reach the level of ~, at least...","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases with ～ないまでも in exams and everyday life include せめて, 少なくとも, and 精一杯. These words emphasize the minimum level the speaker expects.","nuance":"These combinations often appear in questions asking learners to choose a grammar pattern. They help identify the structure quickly: after ないまでも, せめて or 少なくとも often follows.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"全部","r":"ぜんぶ","m":"everything"},{"t":"読めないまでも","r":"よめないまでも","m":"even if cannot read all"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"せめて","m":"at least"},{"t":"目","r":"め","m":"eyes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"通して","r":"とおして","m":"skim"},{"t":"ほしい","m":"want"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if you cannot read it all, please at least skim it.","scene":"A boss asks an employee to read a report."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-088||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～以上は · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～以上は","patternReading":"いじょうは","coreMeaning":"Once ~ happens, one must ~ (duty/inevitable result)","slug":"core","definition":"～以上は means that once something has happened or been decided, one naturally must do something. The focus is the duty or inevitable consequence that follows, not a mere prediction. For example, once you accept a job, you must see it through; once you make a promise, you must keep it.","nuance":"Used in both formal speech and writing. The tone is strong: the speaker sees the following action as mandatory, with no way to back out. Compared with からには, 以上は is slightly more formal and literary.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"社会人","r":"しゃかいじん","m":"working adult"},{"t":"である","r":"である","m":"be"},{"t":"以上は","r":"いじょうは","m":"once one is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"マナー","m":"manners"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"守る","r":"まもる","m":"observe"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"当然","r":"とうぜん","m":"natural"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once you are a working adult, observing good manners is only natural.","scene":"A new employee is reminded about workplace conduct"},{"tokens":[{"t":"引き受けた","r":"ひきうけた","m":"have accepted"},{"t":"以上は","r":"いじょうは","m":"once you have"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"最後","r":"さいご","m":"the end"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"責任","r":"せきにん","m":"responsibility"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持って","r":"もって","m":"take"},{"t":"やり遂げ","r":"やりとげ","m":"see through"},{"t":"なければならない","r":"なければならない","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once you accept a task, you must take responsibility and see it through to the end.","scene":"Discussing the attitude needed when accepting a difficult project"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-088||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"～以上は · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～以上は","patternReading":"いじょうは","coreMeaning":"Once ~ happens, one must ~ (duty/inevitable result)","slug":"forms","definition":"～以上は attaches directly after an established clause. The preceding clause can use a verb in plain form, or a noun / na-adjective with **である**. は may be omitted before a comma: ～以上.","nuance":"The following clause is not a free choice by the speaker, but an obligation or inevitable conclusion. Polite forms are less common because this pattern is often used in writing and formal statements.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"以上（は）","note":"Vた / Vる / Vている are all possible"},{"lhs":"N + である","rhs":"以上（は）","note":"N indicates a status, role, or state"},{"lhs":"Aな語幹 + である","rhs":"以上（は）","note":"Formal; uncommon in ordinary conversation"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"受ける","r":"うける","m":"take"},{"t":"以上は","r":"いじょうは","m":"once you have"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"合格","r":"ごうかく","m":"passing"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"目指す","r":"めざす","m":"aim for"},{"t":"べきだ","r":"べきだ","m":"should"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once you take the exam, you should aim to pass.","scene":"Advising a student who has applied for an exam"},{"tokens":[{"t":"約束した","r":"やくそくした","m":"have promised"},{"t":"以上","r":"いじょう","m":"once you have"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"守ら","r":"まもら","m":"keep"},{"t":"なければならない","r":"なければならない","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once you make a promise, you must keep it.","scene":"Reminding each other to keep promises"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-088||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"～以上は · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～以上は","patternReading":"いじょうは","coreMeaning":"Once ~ happens, one must ~ (duty/inevitable result)","slug":"usage","definition":"～以上は is used when something has become true or has been decided, and an obligatory consequence is then drawn. The following clause often uses **べきだ**, **なければならない**, or **～のは当然だ**.","nuance":"This pattern conveys binding responsibility: once the speaker has crossed an important threshold, there is no backing out. It appears more often in essays, letters, and speeches than in casual conversation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もう"},{"t":"宣言した","r":"せんげんした","m":"have declared"},{"t":"以上は","r":"いじょうは","m":"once you have"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"後","r":"あと","m":"back"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"引けない","r":"ひけない","m":"cannot retreat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once you have declared it, you cannot back down.","scene":"Someone publicly announces their decision to quit"},{"tokens":[{"t":"生きて","r":"いきて","m":"live"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"以上は","r":"いじょうは","m":"as long as one"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"希望","r":"きぼう","m":"hope"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持ち続ける","r":"もちつづける","m":"keep"},{"t":"べきだ","r":"べきだ","m":"should"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"As long as you are alive, you should keep hope alive.","scene":"Encouraging someone after a major setback"},{"tokens":[{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"である","r":"である","m":"be"},{"t":"以上は","r":"いじょうは","m":"once one is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"第一","r":"だいいち","m":"first priority"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"考える","r":"かんがえる","m":"consider"},{"t":"べきだ","r":"べきだ","m":"should"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once you are a student, you should put studying first.","scene":"A parent advises a child over dinner"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-088||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing it from からには","sub":"～以上は · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～以上は","patternReading":"いじょうは","coreMeaning":"Once ~ happens, one must ~ (duty/inevitable result)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare ～以上は with easily confused patterns. The main difference is tone and range of meaning. ～からには is nearly synonymous, but ～以上は is more formal, while ～ばこそ emphasizes a sole cause rather than an obligation.","nuance":"～からには is natural, common, and often used in conversation. ～以上は is written and somewhat stiff. ～ばこそ emphasizes a reason and does not convey a sense of duty.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"来た","r":"きた","m":"have come"},{"t":"以上は","r":"いじょうは","m":"once you have"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"責任","r":"せきにん","m":"responsibility"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持って","r":"もって","m":"take"},{"t":"最後","r":"さいご","m":"the end"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"やるべきだ","r":"やるべきだ","m":"must do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once you have come, you should take responsibility and see it through.","scene":"Discussing responsibility after joining a task"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜からには","point":"The meanings are nearly the same, but からには is natural in speech, while 以上は is formal and somewhat literary. Example: 来たからには一緒にやろう / 来た以上は責任を持ってやる。"},{"vs":"〜ばこそ","point":"ばこそ emphasizes the SOLE CAUSE (precisely because...), and lacks the sense of duty found in 以上は. Example: 学生である以上は勉強すべきだ。 (Do not use: 学生であればこそ勉強すべきだ。)"}]}},"n2-088||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～以上は · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～以上は","patternReading":"いじょうは","coreMeaning":"Once ~ happens, one must ~ (duty/inevitable result)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with ～以上は include omitting である after a noun, using a prediction in the second clause, confusing 以上 with “or more,” and using the pattern for unresolved events. Remember that ～以上は requires responsibility or an inevitable conclusion, not a merely natural event.","nuance":"The most common error is omitting である after a noun. Learners also use 以上は with predictions such as だろう or objective events such as 中止された; both are inappropriate here.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"覚悟した","r":"かくごした","m":"have resolved"},{"t":"以上は","r":"いじょうは","m":"once you have"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"最後","r":"さいご","m":"the end"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"やり抜く","r":"やりぬく","m":"see through"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once I have resolved to do it, I must see it through to the end.","scene":"Talking to oneself before an exam"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 社会人以上は、マナーを守るべきだ。 → ✅ 社会人である以上は、マナーを守るべきだ。 (A noun must be followed by である)","❌ 行く以上は、楽しいだろう。 → ✅ 行く以上は、最後まで楽しむべきだ。 (The second clause should express duty, not prediction)","❌ 雨が降った以上は、試合が中止された。 → ✅ 雨が降った以上は、試合を中止するのはやむを得ない。 (Express a decision or duty, not an automatic event)","❌ 子供以上は、大人が守るべきだ。 → ✅ 大人である以上は、子供を守るべきだ。 (Do not read 以上 as “or more”)"],"compare":null}},"n2-088||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"～以上は · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～以上は","patternReading":"いじょうは","coreMeaning":"Once ~ happens, one must ~ (duty/inevitable result)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember ～以上は with this image: the kanji 以 (take) + 上 (above) suggests that once you have crossed the threshold, you cannot drop back down. からには can cue the idea: “once ..., then you must ...”.","nuance":"Picture a drawbridge: once you cross it, other routes close. That feeling of having no way back captures the spirit of this pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-088||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～以上は · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～以上は","patternReading":"いじょうは","coreMeaning":"Once ~ happens, one must ~ (duty/inevitable result)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases found in JLPT questions and written Japanese include 引き受けた以上, 約束した以上, 社会人である以上, and 生きている以上. These phrases are followed by a clause expressing duty or an inevitable consequence.","nuance":"These phrases appear often in reading passages and essays; memorizing them helps you recognize the pattern quickly.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"約束した","r":"やくそくした","m":"have promised"},{"t":"以上は","r":"いじょうは","m":"once you have"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"必ず","r":"かならず","m":"surely"},{"t":"守る","r":"まもる","m":"keep"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Once you make a promise, you must keep it.","scene":"A familiar reminder about keeping one’s word"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-089||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～ことなしに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことなしに","patternReading":"ことなしに","coreMeaning":"Without ~, one cannot / Without ~ (a necessary condition missing)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ことなしに means that without doing, learning, or experiencing ~, the result in the latter clause cannot be achieved. It stresses that the preceding action is indispensable, not merely skipped. It often translates as “without ~, one cannot ~” or “without ~, ~ is impossible.”","nuance":"This is a very formal, literary pattern used in essays, speeches, literature, and slogans. Compared with the neutral, everyday ずに (without...), ことなしに sounds logical, philosophical, or forceful: without A, do not expect B.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ことなしに","r":"ことなしに","m":"without doing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"成功","r":"せいこう","m":"success"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ない","m":"not exist"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Without effort, there is no success.","scene":"A maxim in an opening speech"},{"tokens":[{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"people"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"交流","r":"こうりゅう","m":"interaction"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ことなしに","r":"ことなしに","m":"without doing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"社会","r":"しゃかい","m":"society"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"成り立たない","r":"なりたたない","m":"cannot exist"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Without interaction among people, society cannot exist.","scene":"A claim in an essay on modern society"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-089||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"～ことなしに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことなしに","patternReading":"ことなしに","coreMeaning":"Without ~, one cannot / Without ~ (a necessary condition missing)","slug":"forms","definition":"The fixed formula is **dictionary-form verb + ことなしに**. The latter clause is usually a judgment, evaluation, or negative statement. The variant **ことなしには** before a negative clause adds emphasis: without this condition, the result is completely impossible.","nuance":"Do not use the ます-form, た-form, or polite form. This is a **formal written style**, common in official documents, books, and speeches. In everyday conversation, Japanese speakers use ずに or ないで instead.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vる","rhs":"ことなしに＋latter clause","note":"Combines with Vる. The latter clause is negative or evaluative."},{"lhs":"Vる","rhs":"ことなしには＋Vない","note":"Emphasizes the condition; the latter clause must be negative."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"基礎","r":"きそ","m":"basics"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"学ぶ","r":"まなぶ","m":"learn"},{"t":"ことなしに","r":"ことなしに","m":"without doing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"応用","r":"おうよう","m":"application"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"できない","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Without learning the basics, you cannot apply them.","scene":"A teacher advising students"},{"tokens":[{"t":"経験","r":"けいけん","m":"experience"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ことなしには","r":"ことなしには","m":"without, cannot"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"真の","r":"しんの","m":"true"},{"t":"判断","r":"はんだん","m":"judgment"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"できない","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Without experience, one cannot make sound judgments.","scene":"An article about work experience"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-089||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～ことなしに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことなしに","patternReading":"ことなしに","coreMeaning":"Without ~, one cannot / Without ~ (a necessary condition missing)","slug":"usage","definition":"～ことなしに often appears in statements expressing **rules or general truths**: the preceding clause is a necessary condition, and the latter clause is an inevitable result. It therefore suits **maxims, life philosophy, and slogans**.","nuance":"This is a formal, **rhetorical** style. The speaker is not merely describing an action but asserting a **truth**: to obtain B, A is mandatory. It is generally not used in chat or casual conversation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"相手","r":"あいて","m":"other party"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"立場","r":"たちば","m":"position"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"理解","r":"りかい","m":"understand"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ことなしに","r":"ことなしに","m":"without doing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"真の","r":"しんの","m":"true"},{"t":"協力","r":"きょうりょく","m":"cooperation"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"生まれない","r":"うまれない","m":"cannot arise"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Without understanding the other party’s position, true cooperation cannot arise.","scene":"A discussion of negotiation skills"},{"tokens":[{"t":"信頼","r":"しんらい","m":"trust"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"築く","r":"きずく","m":"build"},{"t":"ことなしに","r":"ことなしに","m":"without doing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"人間関係","r":"にんげんかんけい","m":"human relations"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"作れない","r":"つくれない","m":"cannot create"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Without building trust, you cannot create good relationships.","scene":"Advice in a life-skills article"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-089||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish from ずに / ことなく","sub":"～ことなしに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことなしに","patternReading":"ことなしに","coreMeaning":"Without ~, one cannot / Without ~ (a necessary condition missing)","slug":"compare","definition":"The three most easily confused patterns are **ことなしに**, **ずに**, and **ことなく**. The key distinction is: **ことなしに** stresses a prerequisite, **ずに** simply means “without doing something,” and **ことなく** stresses that Q still occurs despite not doing P.","nuance":"On tests, if the latter clause contains **ない** and expresses a judgment or conclusion, choose ことなしに. If it merely describes an everyday action (“went to work without eating breakfast”), use ずに. If the result still occurs despite not doing something, use ことなく.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"挨拶","r":"あいさつ","m":"greeting"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ことなしに","r":"ことなしに","m":"without doing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"信頼関係","r":"しんらいかんけい","m":"trusting relationship"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"始まらない","r":"はじまらない","m":"cannot begin"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Without greeting each other, a trusting relationship cannot begin.","scene":"A communication maxim in an article"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～ずに","point":"ずに simply means “without doing” something and works for small daily actions: 食べずに映画を見た. ことなしに requires an important, indispensable result: 努力することなしに成功はない."},{"vs":"～ことなく","point":"ことなく = “P occurs without doing Q, yet the result still happens,” emphasizing a surprising contrast: 努力することなく成功した. ことなしに = “without P, Q cannot happen”: 努力することなしに成功はない."}]}},"n2-089||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common pitfalls","sub":"～ことなしに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことなしに","patternReading":"ことなしに","coreMeaning":"Without ~, one cannot / Without ~ (a necessary condition missing)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make the mistake of using ことなしに for overly ordinary situations, even though this pattern is reserved for an **important judgment** or a **rule-like negative statement** in the latter clause.","nuance":"Ask yourself: is the latter clause something someone is striving to achieve? If it is merely “go to work” or “watch a movie,” use ずに. If it is “success,” “cooperation,” or “society exists,” ことなしに is appropriate.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"準備","r":"じゅんび","m":"preparation"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ことなしに","r":"ことなしに","m":"without doing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"本番","r":"ほんばん","m":"main event"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"迎えられない","r":"むかえられない","m":"cannot face"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Without preparation, you cannot face the actual presentation.","scene":"Advice before an important presentation exam"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食べることなしに、映画を見た。 → ✅ 食べずに映画を見た。（Use ずに for small daily actions）","❌ 朝ごはんを食べることなしに会社へ行った。 → ✅ 朝ごはんを食べずに会社へ行った。（Do not use ことなしに for everyday actions）","❌ 彼は練習することなしに上手になった。 → ✅ 彼は練習することなく上手になった。（The latter clause is affirmative; use ことなく）"],"compare":null}},"n2-089||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～ことなしに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことなしに","patternReading":"ことなしに","coreMeaning":"Without ~, one cannot / Without ~ (a necessary condition missing)","slug":"memory","definition":"Break down each part: **こと** = thing, **なし** = none, **に** = in a state. Together: “in a state of having no thing ~” → without doing ~, the latter clause cannot succeed.","nuance":"This helps you remember that ことなしに is not merely “without doing,” but connects a specific “thing” that serves as the key to the result that follows.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-089||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"～ことなしに · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～ことなしに","patternReading":"ことなしに","coreMeaning":"Without ~, one cannot / Without ~ (a necessary condition missing)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases used with ことなしに in tests and written texts include: **努力する** (make an effort), **信頼を築く** (build trust), and **基礎を学ぶ** (learn the basics).","nuance":"These phrases often appear in topics about **perseverance, society, and study** and have a philosophical tone. Knowing them helps you quickly identify the pattern in reading questions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"effort"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ことなしに","r":"ことなしに","m":"without doing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"成功","r":"せいこう","m":"success"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ない","m":"not exist"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Without effort, there is no success.","scene":"A company slogan"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-090||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"～というわけだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～というわけだ","patternReading":"というわけだ","coreMeaning":"Logical conclusion: “So, that means ~” / “It’s not necessarily ~”","slug":"core","definition":"～というわけだ draws a **logical conclusion** from known information: “so ~” or “that explains it.” The negative form **～というわけではない** denies a conclusion the listener may be making: “it does not necessarily mean ~.” わけ means “reason” or “result,” giving the sentence the sense of a logical consequence.","nuance":"This pattern is common in conversation when you **suddenly understand** something or explain it to someone. It is close to ～はずだ, but **stronger and more objective** because it is based on concrete evidence. It works in speech and writing; formal writing may use ～という理由だ instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"遅れた","r":"おくれた","m":"was delayed"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"遅刻した","r":"ちこくした","m":"was late"},{"t":"というわけだ","r":"というわけだ","m":"so that explains it"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The train was delayed, so he was late—that explains it.","scene":"A colleague explains that the delayed train made him late."},{"tokens":[{"t":"つまり","r":"つまり","m":"in other words"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし","m":"cancellation"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"というわけですか","r":"というわけですか","m":"so, is that it?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"So, the meeting was canceled?","scene":"You confirm with your boss that the meeting was canceled."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-090||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"～というわけだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～というわけだ","patternReading":"というわけだ","coreMeaning":"Logical conclusion: “So, that means ~” / “It’s not necessarily ~”","slug":"forms","definition":"This structure attaches to the **plain form (普通形)**. Nouns and な-adjectives retain their plain form: 学生というわけだ, 静かというわけだ. Verbs and い-adjectives use their corresponding plain forms. Change だ to ですか for a question, です for politeness, or ではない for negation.","nuance":"Form the negative by changing だ to ではない. The polite form uses です, and the question form uses か. In casual speech, ではない may contract to じゃない.","structure":[{"lhs":"V plain form (Vた/Vない)","rhs":"というわけだ","note":"Conclusion about an action done/not done"},{"lhs":"Aい plain form","rhs":"というわけだ","note":"Conclusion about a state"},{"lhs":"Aな plain form (な→だ)","rhs":"というわけだ","note":"Conclusion about a quality"},{"lhs":"N plain form (N＋だ)","rhs":"というわけだ","note":"Conclusion about identity or things"},{"lhs":"Plain form","rhs":"というわけではない","note":"Rejects a conclusion: “not necessarily”"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"練習","r":"れんしゅう","m":"practice"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"practiced"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"skilled"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なった","r":"なった","m":"became"},{"t":"というわけだ","r":"というわけだ","m":"so that explains it"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I practiced every day, so I improved—that explains it.","scene":"You proudly tell a friend about your diligent practice."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"him"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"嫌い","r":"きらい","m":"dislike"},{"t":"というわけではない","r":"というわけではない","m":"not necessarily"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is not that I dislike him.","scene":"You clarify when asked, “Do you dislike him?”"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-090||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"～というわけだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～というわけだ","patternReading":"というわけだ","coreMeaning":"Logical conclusion: “So, that means ~” / “It’s not necessarily ~”","slug":"usage","definition":"～というわけだ is common in conversation when the speaker **draws a conclusion** or **confirms what they heard**. It sounds natural with **つまり** (“in other words”) or **なるほど** (“I see”).","nuance":"Use it to connect an event with an inference. At the end of a question, **というわけ?** shows mild surprise. The negative **というわけではない** usually softens a rejection rather than denying everything.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"なるほど","r":"なるほど","m":"I see"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来ない","r":"こない","m":"will not come"},{"t":"というわけか","r":"というわけか","m":"so that is why?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I see—so that is why he is not coming.","scene":"You realize why someone is absent."},{"tokens":[{"t":"つまり","r":"つまり","m":"in other words"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"来週","r":"らいしゅう","m":"next week"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"延期された","r":"えんきされた","m":"was postponed"},{"t":"というわけですね","r":"というわけですね","m":"so, right?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"So the exam was postponed until next week, right?","scene":"You summarize the changed exam schedule."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-090||compare":{"title":"⚖️ How it differs","sub":"～というわけだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～というわけだ","patternReading":"というわけだ","coreMeaning":"Logical conclusion: “So, that means ~” / “It’s not necessarily ~”","slug":"compare","definition":"The three patterns most easily confused are ～というものだ, ～ということだ, and ～はずだ. Remember: というわけだ = “so, that explains it,” a conclusion from facts; というものだ = “that is what it really is,” the essence; ということだ = “I hear that,” reported information; はずだ = “should be,” a prediction.","nuance":"All three can translate as “is,” but their perspectives differ: というわけだ is **inference from facts**, というものだ is **the nature of something**, ということだ is **reported information**, and はずだ is **a prediction**. Knowing this prevents confusion in speech and writing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"残業","r":"ざんぎょう","m":"overtime"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"多い","r":"おおい","m":"a lot"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"疲れる","r":"つかれる","m":"get tired"},{"t":"というわけだ","r":"というわけだ","m":"so that explains it"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I work a lot of overtime, so I am tired—that explains it.","scene":"You explain to a coworker why you are tired."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"～というものだ","point":"というわけだ = a conclusion from facts; というものだ = the nature of something. Example: 彼が怒ったというわけだ (so he got angry) vs これが愛というものだ (this is true love)."},{"vs":"～ということだ","point":"というわけだ = a logical inference the speaker reaches; ということだ = reported information or hearsay. Example: 彼が来ないというわけだ (so he is not coming) vs 彼は明日来ないということだ (I hear he will not come tomorrow)."},{"vs":"～はずだ","point":"というわけだ = explains why something happened; はずだ = predicts what should happen. Example: 彼が遅刻したというわけだ (so that is why he was late) vs 彼が遅刻するはずだ (he should be late)."}]}},"n2-090||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"～というわけだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～というわけだ","patternReading":"というわけだ","coreMeaning":"Logical conclusion: “So, that means ~” / “It’s not necessarily ~”","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"～というわけだ requires a **logical basis** established beforehand. Learners often misuse it when expressing a direct emotion or when no concrete evidence is available.","nuance":"Without a preceding basis, the sentence can sound abrupt. Always ask yourself: “What am I basing this conclusion on?”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"止まった","r":"とまった","m":"stopped"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"遅刻した","r":"ちこくした","m":"was late"},{"t":"というわけだ","r":"というわけだ","m":"so that explains it"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The train stopped, so I was late—that explains it.","scene":"You explain why you were late at work."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 嬉しいというわけだ。 → ✅ 彼にもらったから、嬉しいというわけだ。 (A preceding basis is needed)","❌ 今すぐ行くわけだ。 → ✅ 理由があるから、今すぐ行くというわけだ。 (Do not use it without a premise or context leading to the conclusion)","❌ 時間がないから行くわけではない。 → ✅ 時間がないから行くわけにはいかない。 (For “cannot do it for a reason,” use わけにはいかない, not わけではない)"],"compare":null}},"n2-090||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"～というわけだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～というわけだ","patternReading":"というわけだ","coreMeaning":"Logical conclusion: “So, that means ~” / “It’s not necessarily ~”","slug":"memory","definition":"Think of **わけ** as “reason.” というわけだ means “that is the reason → so, that means.” Mnemonic: when you see “so, that means,” think わけだ.","nuance":"When you see というわけではない, picture a shield that stops an inference from going too far.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n2-090||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common combinations","sub":"～というわけだ · N2","v2":{"pattern":"～というわけだ","patternReading":"というわけだ","coreMeaning":"Logical conclusion: “So, that means ~” / “It’s not necessarily ~”","slug":"phrases","definition":"These common combinations help you form natural sentences and frequently appear on tests.","nuance":"In conversation, というわけだ often follows つまり, なるほど, or だから.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"つまり","r":"つまり","m":"in other words"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし","m":"cancellation"},{"t":"というわけですね","r":"というわけですね","m":"so, right?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"So the meeting tomorrow was canceled, right?","scene":"You confirm tomorrow’s meeting with a coworker."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}}}}